"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3554],{3554:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"subject":"Physiology","slug":"physiology","questions":[{"bankId":"qb-c3dd57a1cb","n":1,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"“a” wave on JVP is for","options":["Atrial contraction","Atrial filling","Ventricular relaxation","Ventricular filling"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Atrial contraction\\n\\n\\"a\\" wave in JVP represents atrial contraction.\\n\\nWaves of JVP :\\n\\n• a- atrial systole pushing the\xa0blood to the ventricles.\\n• c- isovolumetric contraction of ventricles\xa0causing the tricuspid valve to bulge.\\n• x\'- fall in atrial pressure during ventricular systole due to pull of valve leaflets.\\n• v- venous filling which creates an increase in atrial pressure.\\n• y- passive filling of ventricles by a passive flow of the\xa0blood from the right atrium.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a391011323","n":2,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"A 13-year-old boy presents with involuntary dancing movements of his right hand. He was diagnosed with rheumatic fever. What constitutes the ganglia involved in the presenting complication?","options":["Caudate nucleus and globus pallidus","Globus pallidus and putamen","Globus pallidus and lentiform nucleus","Caudate nucleus and putamen"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Caudate nucleus and putamen\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0Sydenham\'s chorea,\xa0a complication of\xa0rheumatic fever. The choreic movements are caused by the destruction of D2 neurons of the striatum. The neostriatum consists of the\xa0caudate nucleus and putamen.\\n\\nThe image given below shows\xa0Sydenham\'s chorea in a patient.\\n\\nNomenclature of basal ganglia:\\n\\n• Corpus striatum: Caudate nucleus + putamen+ globus pallidus\\n• Lentiform nucleus: Putamen + globus pallidus\\n• Neostriatum: Caudate nucleus + putamen","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d5f2fbb568","n":3,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"A 30-year-old patient with respiratory volume of 500 mL. The intraplueral pressure measured -4 before inspiration and -8 at the end of inspiration. The pulmonary compliance of the patient is","options":["125 ml/cm H2O","150 ml/cm H2O","50 ml/cm H2O","62.5 ml/cm H2O"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 125 ml/cm H2O\\n\\nPulmonary compliance of this patient is 125 mL/cm of H2O.\\n\\nCompliance is developed due to the tendency for tissue to resume its original position after an applied force has been removed.\\n\\nAfter an expiration during quiet breathing, the lungs have a tendency to collapse and the chest wall has a tendency to expand. The interaction between the recoil of the lungs and recoil of the chest can be measured through a spirometer and a curve of airway pressure plotted against volume is the pressure-volume curve of the total respiratory system (Ptr).\\n\\nCompliance of the lung and chest wall is measured as the slope of the Ptr curve, or, as a change in lung volume per unit change in airway pressure (ΔV/ΔP).\\n\\nCompliance = Change in lung volume / Change in pressure\\n\\nCompliance = 500/4\\n\\nCompliance = 125 ml/cm H20","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-84a92c9635","n":4,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"A patient has a blood glucose level of 200 mg/dL and GFR of 90 ml/min. What is the amount of glucose excreted if the transport maximum of the patient is as shown below?","options":["80 mg/min","50 mg/min","40 mg/min","30 mg/min"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 30 mg/min\\n\\nThe amount of glucose excreted in the above scenario is 30 mg/min.\\n\\nCalculation:\\n\\nStep 1: Calculate total filtered glucose:\\n\\nIn this question,\\n\\n• GFR = 90 ml/min\\n• Plasma glucose concentration = 200 mg/dL= 2 mg/ml (1 dL=100 ml).\\n• Total filtered glucose = GFR X Plasma glucose concentration.\\n\\n= 90 ml/min X 2 mg/ml\\n= 180 mg/min.\\n\\nStep 2: Check transport maximum:\\n\\nIn the above question transport maximum is 150 mg/min.\\n\\nStep 3: Calculate the excretion:\\n\\nExcretion = Total filtered glucose - Total reabsorbed glucose (Transport Maximum)\\n\\n= 180-150 mg/min\\n= 30 mg/min","img":false,"images":["/bimg/947f83a1a0044f6977f8.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-102560db79","n":5,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Absolute refractory period is due to:","options":["Opening of calcium channels","Closure of potassium channels","Closure of active gates of sodium channel","Closure of inactive gates of sodium channel"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Closure of inactive gates of sodium channel\\n\\nAbsolute refractory period is due to closure of inactive gates of sodium channel.\\n\\nVoltage-gated sodium channels have 3 states that you can find them in. They can either be closed, open, or inactive.\\n\\nWhen the stimulus causes activation of the open gates, sodium rushes into the cell causing the membrane potential to become more positive. After 0.5 to 1 millisecond, the inactive gate automatically closes and no stimulus, no matter how strong, can excite the nerve. This is called the absolute refractory period.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-aebe157d1c","n":6,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Cellular junctions present in a cardiac muscle are all except:","options":["Zonula occludens","Fascia adherens","Gap junctions","Macula adherens"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Zonula occludens\\n\\nZonula occludens is the other name for the tight junction. It is absent in a cardiac muscle.\\n\\nCardiac muscle cells are connected by\xa0Fascia adherens (a structural analog of zonula adherens),\xa0Gap junctions and\xa0Macula adherens.\\n\\nCardiac muscle fibers are made up of many individual cells connected in series and parallel to one another. The areas where the end of one muscle fiber abuts on another are called intercalated disks. At each intercalated disc, the plasma membrane fuses with one another forming gap junction which provides low resistance bridges for the spread of excitation from one fiber to another permitting the cardiac muscle to function as a syncytium.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0a13a54019","n":7,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Difference in trajectory between inspiratory loop and the expiratory loop in the curve is due to:","options":["Difference in alveolar pressure during inspiration and expiration","Difference in conc. of surfactant during inspiration and expiration","Difference in airway resistance during inspiration and expiration","Inspiration is active and expiration is passive"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Difference in conc. of surfactant during inspiration and expiration\\n\\nThe difference between the inflation and the deflation paths is called\xa0hysteresis.\\n\\nIt\xa0exists because a greater transpulmonary pressure is required to open a previously closed airway, due to a deficit of surfactant at the air-water interface, than to keep an open airway from closing, as then it has abundant surfactant.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/bd0fe3ad858d469c2b6e.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-67990c6894","n":8,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Efferent arteriolar constriction causes all except:","options":["Decrease in GFR","Decreases renal blood flow","Decreases oncotic pressure in glomerular capillaries","Increases hydrostatic pressure in glomerular capillaries"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Decreases oncotic pressure in glomerular capillaries\\n\\nEfferent arteriolar constriction increases oncotic pressure in glomerular capillaries.\\n\\nEfferent arteriolar constriction has a\xa0biphasic response\xa0on GFR depending on the severity of resistance.\\n\\nModerate efferent arteriolar constriction causes increased glomerular hydrostatic pressure and slightly\xa0increased GFR.\\n\\nHowever, in cases of severe efferent arteriolar constriction\xa0(more than threefold increase in efferent arteriolar resistance), the renal blood flow is significantly reduced. This results in increased plasma protein concentration, thereby increasing the glomerular colloid oncotic pressure. As the increase in colloid oncotic pressure exceeds the increase in hydrostatic pressure, the net force for filtration decreases, resulting in decreased GFR.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1ddba9f68b","n":9,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Feed forward control system is employed during the regulation of","options":["Blood volume","pH","Temperature","Blood pressure"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Temperature\\n\\nFeed forward control system is employed during regulation of temperature.\\n\\nFeed forward control\\n\\nThere is a control system in our body when no stimulus is required but still the system anticipates and makes corrective changes.\\n\\nSuch a system is called Feed forward or Anticipatory or Adaptive Control.\\n\\nExamples include\\n\\n• Temperature control – Thermoregulatory responses are initiated by hypothalamus BEFORE the changes in environmental temperature have succeeded in changing the body’s core body temperature\\n• Cephalic phase of gastric secretion- Just thinking about food increases gastric acid production\\n• Thinking about exercise itself increases heart rate & respiratory rate\\n• Role of cerebellum in motor coordination","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-85eb60fddf","n":10,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Given below is the demonstration of tetanization in the gastrocnemius muscle of a frog. Identify the tetanising frequency.","options":["25-20 Hz","30-35 Hz","15-20 Hz","10-15 Hz"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 30-35 Hz\\n\\nTetanizing frequency demonstrated by the muscle is 33Hz.\xa0In the above graph, we know,\\n\\n• The speed of the paper is 1000mm/sec.\\n• The y-axis represents millivolts, the x-axis represents time.\\n• The scale on the x-axis is 10mm.\\n• The area under the graph represents the muscle contraction.\\n\\nContraction period (latent period to maximum force of contraction) includes 3 scales = 30 mm.\\n\\nAs paper speed is 1000mm/sec,\\n\\nContraction period = 30mm = (30/1000) = 30 msec.\\n\\nTetanizing frequency = 1/contraction period = 1000/30 = 33Hz.\\n\\nTetanization\\n\\nThe features of muscle contraction can be demonstrated by eliciting single muscle twitches. The adding together of individual twitch contractions to increase the intensity of overall muscle contraction is Summation.\xa0Summation occurs in two ways:\\n\\n• by increasing the number of motor units contracting simultaneously, which is multiple ﬁber summations.\\n• by increasing the frequency of contraction, which is called frequency summation and can lead to tetanization.\\n\\nWhen the frequency of a contraction reaches a critical level, the successive contractions eventually fuse together, and the whole muscle contraction appears to be completely smooth and continuous, this is called tetanization.\\n\\nTetanization occurs in two ways:\\n\\n• Complete tetanus occurs when no relaxation occurs between stimuli.\\n• Incomplete tetanus when periods of incomplete relaxation take place between the summated stimuli.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/e8e6160958af2e13b5ac.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-8b76735a03","n":11,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Identify the hormone from the picture","options":["Growth hormone","Cortisol","Estrogen","Insulin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Growth hormone\\n\\nThe image depicts the fluctuations of secretion of growth hormone throughout the day.\\n\\n• Growth hormone is found at relatively low levels during the day unless specific triggers such as strenuous exercise are present.\\n• During sleep, on the other hand, large pulsatile bursts of growth hormone secretion occur.\\n\\nStimuli that Affect Growth Hormone Secretion in Humans\\n\\nStimuli that Increase Secretion\\nStimuli that Decrease Secretion\\n\\n• Exercise\\n• Going to sleep\\n• Fasting/Hypoglycemia\\n• Stressful stimuli (including various psychological stresses)\\n• High Protein Meal\\n• Glucagon\\n• Estrogens and androgens\\n• Pyrogen\\n\\n• Glucose\\n• Cortisol\\n• FFA\\n• Medroxyprogesterone\\n• Growth hormone and IGF-I\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nThe early morning surge of Growth Hormone during sleep, as seen in the image, plays a role in the Dawn Phenomenon","img":false,"images":["/bimg/9b880298e4cb28d48512.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-77c841b69e","n":12,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"In the 2 vessels shown below, assume the pressure along both the vessels is the same and both follow linear flow pattern. What will be the amount of blood flow in A compared to B","options":["4 times","32 times","8 times","16 times"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 8 times\\n\\nThe relationship between the flow in a long narrow tube, the viscosity of the fluid, and the radius of the tube is expressed mathematically in the formula called as\xa0Poiseuille\'s law :\\n\\nBlood flow (F) inside the vessel depends on:\\n\\n• Pressure\xa0difference: directly proportional\\n• Viscosity: inversely proportional\\n• Radius: directly proportional to the fourth power\\n• Length of the vessel: inversely proportional\\n\\nHere, the radius is doubled so the flow increases by 2 to the power of 4 = 16.\\n\\nthe length of the vessel is also doubled, so the flow decreases\xa0by\xa0half (\xf72) = 8","img":false,"images":["/bimg/b04566fed0b10c5ec7e1.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-9c5c0961e2","n":13,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Reflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is:","options":["Bezold-Jarisch reflex","Bainbridge reflex","Cushing reflex","J reflex"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bainbridge reflex\\n\\nReflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is\xa0Bainbridge Reflex.\\n\\nThe stretch receptors of the atria that\xa0elicit the Bainbridge reflex transmit their afferent signals\xa0through the vagus nerves to the medulla of the brain.\xa0Then efferent signals are transmitted back through vagal\xa0and sympathetic nerves to increase heart rate and strength\xa0of heart contraction. Thus, this reflex helps prevent\xa0damming of blood in the veins, atria, and pulmonary\xa0circulation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Bezold–Jarisch Reflex:\xa0Activation of chemosensitive vagal C fibers in the cardiopulmonary region causes profound bradycardia, hypotension and a brief period of apnoea followed by rapid shallow breathing.\\n\\nOption C: Cushing Reflex:\xa0When intracranial\xa0pressure\xa0is increased, the blood supply to Rostral\xa0Ventro-Lateral Medulla (RVLM) neurons is compromised.\xa0The local hypoxia and hypercapnia cause an increase in discharge from the neurons, producing hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration.\\n\\nOption D: J Reflex:\xa0The stimulation of the J-receptors (C receptors/juxta-pulmonary capillary receptors) causes apnea followed by rapid breathing, bradycardia, and hypotension.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-806548f399","n":14,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Slow waves are generated by:","options":["Myenteric neurons","Smooth muscle cells","Interstitial cells of Cajal","Parasympathetic neurons"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Interstitial cells of Cajal\\n\\nThe interstitial cells of Cajal undergo cyclic changes in membrane potential due to unique ion channels that periodically open and produce inward (pacemaker) currents that generate slow-wave activity.\\n\\nInterstitial cells of Cajal\xa0are the\xa0pacemaker cells\xa0of the\xa0GIT\xa0that initiate the\xa0basic electrical rhythm\xa0(BER). These\xa0are stellate mesenchymal pacemaker cells with smooth muscle-like features.\\n\\nIn the stomach and the small intestine, these cells are located in the circular smooth muscle layer near the myenteric plexus. In the colon, they are at the submucosal border of the circular muscle layer.\\n\\nThe slow waves usually do not by themselves cause muscle contraction in most parts of the gastrointestinal tract, except perhaps in the stomach. Instead, they mainly excite the appearance of intermittent spike potentials, and the spike potentials in turn actually excite the muscle contraction.\\n\\nNote:\xa0Gastrointestinal stromal tumors\xa0arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0a9d630ec8","n":15,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"The fourth heart sound is caused by:","options":["Closure of aortic and pulmonary valves","Vibrations in the ventricular wall during systole","Ventricular filling","Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ventricular filling\\n\\nThe fourth heart sound is caused by ventricular filling.\\n\\nA fourth sound is\xa0heard immediately before the first sound when atrial pressure is high or the ventricle is stiff in conditions such as ventricular hypertrophy. It is due to ventricular filling and is rarely heard in normal adults.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bfc94f6f30","n":16,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"The function of the Golgi tendon organ is to _________","options":["Sense changes in muscle direction","Sense changes in muscle tension","Sense changes in position of the muscle","Sense changes in length of the muscle"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sense changes in muscle tension\\n\\nGolgi tendon organ sense changes in muscle tension.\\n\\nGolgi tendon reflex (Inverse myotatic/ Inverse stretch reflex)\xa0is\xa0disynaptic,\xa0is the opposite of the stretch reflex.\\n\\nWhen the tension in a contracted muscle is significantly elevated, the inhibitory effect from the Golgi tendon organ leads to instantaneous relaxation of the entire muscle. This effect is called the\xa0lengthening reaction.\\n\\nIt is a protective mechanism to prevent tearing of the muscle or avulsion of the tendon from its attachments to the bone.\\n\\nNote: Muscle spindles senses changes in muscle length (stretch).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-554e5dda20","n":17,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"The oxygen carrying capacity of a 18 year old boy with haemoglobin of 14g/dl is","options":["22","16","14","18"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 18\\n\\nThe oxygen carrying capacity of a boy with haemoglobin 14g/dl is 18.\\n\\nWhen blood is saturated with 100% O2, the haemoglobin is also 100% saturated and each gram of normal haemoglobin contains 1.39 mL of O2.\\n\\nHowever, blood normally contains some inactive haemoglobin derivatives, and the measured value in vivo is thus slightly lower hence, each gram of haemoglobin in vivo contains\xa01.31 mL of O2 (Hufner’s constant).\\n\\nAccording to the above question, when Hb=14g/dl, O2= 1.31*14= 18.34\\n\\nThe oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve\\n\\n• Demonstrates a progressive increase in the percentage of haemoglobin bound with oxygen as blood Po2 increases, which is called the percent saturation of haemoglobin.\\n• Blood leaving the lungs and entering the systemic arteries usually has a Po2 of about 95 mm Hg, the usual oxygen saturation of systemic arterial blood averages 97 per cent.\\n• Conversely, in normal venous blood returning from the peripheral tissues, the Po2 is about 40 mm Hg, and the saturation of haemoglobin averages 75 per cent.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e4ae45e99c","n":18,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Which of the following can be responsible for Bezold-Jarisch reflex?","options":["Serotonin","Histamine","Prostaglandin","Angiotensin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Serotonin\\n\\nSerotonin can be responsible for\xa0Bezold–Jarisch reflex.\\n\\nActivation of chemosensitive vagal C fibers located in the cardiopulmonary region (ex: ventricles, atria, great veins, pulmonary artery, juxtacapillary region of alveoli) causes profound bradycardia, hypotension, and apnea followed by rapid shallow breathing. This response is called Bezold-Jarisch reflex.\\n\\nOther substances that can cause Bezold–Jarisch reflex are capsaicin, phenyl biguanide, and veratridine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3f2c246da7","n":19,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"Which of the following is the constituent of the marked area in the electron microscopic picture of muscle?","options":["Alpha actinin","Actin","Titin","Tropomyosin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Alpha actinin\\n\\nAlpha actinin is the constituent of the marked area.\\n\\nThe image depicts sarcomere and the arrow points towards Z-line. Alpha actinin binds actin to Z-lines.\\n\\nThe orderly arrangement of actin, myosin, and related proteins that produces this pattern.\xa0The area between two Z-lines is called sarcomere.\\n\\nThe thick filaments, which are about twice the diameter of the thin filaments, are made up of myosin, which are lined to form A bands; the thin filaments are made up of actin, tropomyosin, and troponin. The array of thin filaments extends out of the A band and into the less dense staining I bands.\\n\\nThe lighter H bands in the center of the A bands are the regions where, when the muscle is relaxed, the thin filaments do not overlap the thick filaments.\xa0 The Z lines allow for anchoring of the thin filaments.\\n\\nActinin, titin and desmin are additional structural proteins.Titin connects Z-lines to the M line and provides the scaffolding to the sarcomere.\xa0 Desmin adds structure to Z-lines by connecting Z-line to plasma membrane.\\n\\nNote: Some sources say that even titin is a component of Z-disc so if alpha actinin is not given in the options titin is the second best answer.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/b9f100fba6185c59e60a.png"]},{"bankId":"qb-2d516a3bb3","n":20,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2017","text":"X, Y, Z are three permeable ions. X = -50 and Y = -30. At RMP, if there is no net electrogenic transfer, what is the value of Z?","options":["+20","-20","+80","-80"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. +80\\n\\nNo net electro genic transfer at RMP means X + Y + Z = 0\\n\\nSince X = -50 and Y = -30, then Z must be +80 since (-80 +80 = 0)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-cfbb5b252a","n":21,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Baroreceptor provides ________","options":["Negative feedback","Positive feedback","Both negative and positive feedback","Feedforward control"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Negative feedback\\n\\nBaroreceptors provide negative feedback in response to an increase in blood pressure.\\n\\nIncreased baroreceptor discharge inhibits the tonic discharge of sympathetic nerves and excites the vagal innervation of the heart.\\n\\nBaroreceptor reflex\\n\\n• Increased BP → stimulation of baroreceptors → stimulation of NTS → inhibition of RVLM → inhibition of sympathetic system\\n• Stimulation of NTS also stimulates CVC → parasympathetic stimulation\\n• Net effect = sympathetic inhibition and parasympathetic stimulation → decreased HR and BP\\n\\nNTS - Nucleus tractus solitarius\\n\\nCVC - Cardiovagal center\\n\\nRVLM - Rostral ventrolateral medulla\\n\\nHR- Heart rate\\n\\nBP - Blood pressure","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-85d30006a6","n":22,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Calculate the net filtration pressure across the GBM for a patient with Glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient = 6 Glomerular hydrostatic pressure =45 Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure =20 Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure =10 Bowman’s capsule oncotic pressure =0","options":["90 mmHg","60 mmHg","30 mmHg","15 mmHg"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 15 mmHg\\n\\nNet filtration pressure = Hydrostatic pressure in the glomerular capillaries - Pressure in Bowman\'s capsule - Oncotic pressure in the glomerular capillaries.\\n\\nNet filtration pressure=\xa0[PG -PB-\xa0πG+\xa0πB]\\n\\nTherefore,\\n\\nNet filtration pressure\xa0 \xa0 \xa0 = (45-10-20+0)\\n\\n= 25-10\\n\\n= 15 mmHg\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nHere, just the\xa0net filtration pressure is asked and not the GFR.\\n\\nGFR= Kf [PG -PB-\xa0πG+\xa0πB]","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e58d9e0779","n":23,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"GFR is increased by all except","options":["Increased renal blood flow","Efferent arteriolar constriction","Ureteric stone","Decreased oncotic pressure"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ureteric stone\\n\\nUreteric stone causes a reduction in glomerular filtration rate (GFR).\\n\\nFollowing ureteral obstruction, there is a progressive fall in glomerular filtration rate (GFR) due to a reduction in a\xa0single-nephron glomerular filtration rate (SNGFR) and a reduced number of filtering nephrons.\\n\\nAll the other factors/options increase GFR.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6236c109b6","n":24,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Hormone degradation as an important mechanism for regulating local concentrations is seen in _________.","options":["11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase inactivation of glucocorticoids","Thyroid hormone deiodinases inactivating T3","Degradation of retinoic acid by Cyp26b1 in primordial germ cells of males","All of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. All of the above\\n\\nHormone degradation as an important mechanism for regulating local concentrations is seen in all of the above.\\n\\n11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase inactivates glucocorticoids in renal tubular cells, preventing actions through the mineralocorticoid receptor.\\n\\nDuring development, degradation of retinoic acid by Cyp26b1 prevents the primordial germ cells in the male from entering meiosis, which occurs in the female ovary.\\n\\nThe thyroid hormone deiodinases convert T4 to T3 and can inactivate T3. All the three are hence examples of local hormone degradation being utilized as a method for regulation of hormone concentration locally.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b94c87945e","n":25,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"How is an electrical synapse different from a chemical synapse?","options":["No cytoplasmic continuity between presynaptic and postsynaptic membrane","Direction of impulse is unidirectional","Connexons are involved in electrical synapse","Electrical synapse has more latent period than chemical synapse"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Connexons are involved in electrical synapse\\n\\nIn electrical synapses, the cytoplasm of adjacent cells is directly connected by gap junctions (connexons) that allow free movement of ions from the interior of one cell to the interior of the next cell.\\n\\nThe transmission in electrical synapses is bidirectional and there is no synaptic delay. Unlike chemical synapses, electrical synapse has cytoplasmic continuity.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nMost of the synapses used for signal transmission in the central nervous system are chemical synapses.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4c397c56da","n":26,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Identify the ions A and B based on conductance graph of a neuron shown below:","options":["Na, K","K, Na","Na, Ca","Ca, Na"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Na, K\\n\\nChannel A: Na+, Channel B: K+\\n\\nNa+ channels are fast activating and are responsible for depolarization phase of action potential.\\n\\nK+ channels are slow activating and open for prolonged duration than the sodium channels. They are responsible for repolarization phase of action potential. Also, the postassium channels return slowly to their closed state, which is responsible for after-hyperpolarization.\\n\\nThe sequence of events during the action potential\xa0occur in 7 steps as described below.\\n\\n1.\xa0Resting state - Both channels are closed\\n\\n2.\xa0Na+ channels open in response to depolarizing stimulus. Sodium enters into the cell. The membrane reaches threshold potential.\\n\\n3. The sodium influx causes opening of more sodium channels through positive feedback mechanism. Rapid depolarization occurs.\\n\\n4. Sodium conductance decreases.\\n\\n5. Na+ channels close and K+ channels open. Repolarization occurs.\\n\\n6. Sodium channels are reset, and are ready to respond to another depolarizing stimulus (relative refractory period). During this phase, the\xa0potassium channels remain open and slowly return to closed state causing after-hyperpolarization.\\n\\n7. Both channels are closed. The membrane returns to RMP.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/5aa55964b7b23c30e8f5.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-b169751b30","n":27,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Identify the site which is responsible for relieving pain when pressure is applied to the painful area.","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A\\n\\nIt is a cross section of brain stem at pons.\\n\\n• A is a periaqueductal gray matter which also consists of raphe nucleus.\\n• Descending pathways from the mesencephalic periaqueductal gray matter inhibit transmission in nociceptive pathways. This descending pathway includes a synapse in the ventromedial medulla (Raphe nucleus) and the release of endogenous opiates. So periaqueductal gray matter releases endogenous opiates.\\n\\nOther marked structures are\\n\\nB: Corticospinal tract\\n\\nC: Medial lemniscus\\n\\nD: Medial longitudinal fasciculus","img":false,"images":["/bimg/4e2cff268eeb648506bb.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-8a1f0c6a35","n":28,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"In a 100-m dash, most of the energy consumed by skeletal muscles to replenish ATP is derived from _______","options":["Phosphocreatine","Aerobic glycolysis","Oxidation of Fatty acids","None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Phosphocreatine\\n\\nIn a 100-m dash that takes 10 s, 85% of the energy consumed is derived anaerobically by\xa0phosphocreatine, which is hydrolyzed to creatine and phosphate groups with the release of considerable energy.\\nThe ATP concentration in a muscle is low enough (4 millimolar) to sustain contractions for\xa01-2 seconds\xa0after which ATP is dephosphorylated into ADP. The ADP is re-phosphorylated by phosphocreatine for the\xa0next 5 to 8 seconds.\\n\\nADP is re-phosphorylated by\xa03 sources.\\n\\n• Phosphocreatine after 2 seconds upto 10 seconds.\\n• Glycolysis (anaerobic) after 10 seconds upto a minute\\n• Oxidative metabolism - after a minute upto 2-4 hours.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fcf7374aa2","n":29,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"In the homunculus, which of the following has maximum representation?","options":["Low control and skilled movements","More muscle fibres are present","Increased preciseness of movements","None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Increased preciseness of movements\\n\\nIn a homunculus, the more the representation of an area, the more precise the movements are.\\n\\n• The relative sizes of the cortical receiving areas of the sensory homunculus are\xa0proportionate to the use of the part.\\n• The sizes of these areas are\xa0directly proportional to the number of specialized sensory receptors\xa0in each respective peripheral area of the body.\\n• Some body parts are represented by large areas in the somatic cortex—the\xa0lips the greatest\xa0of all, followed by the\xa0face and thumb,\xa0whereas the\xa0trunk and lower part of the body\xa0are represented by relatively\xa0small areas.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0d004cc250","n":30,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Inhibitory control by the hypothalamus is exerted on:","options":["Prolactin only","Prolactin and growth hormone","Growth hormone only","Neither"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Prolactin and growth hormone\\n\\nInhibitory control by the hypothalamus is exerted mainly on prolactin and growth hormone.\\n\\nThe major hypothalamic releasing hormones are:\\n\\n• Thyrotropin releasing hormone (TRH)\\n• Corticotropin releasing hormone (CRH)\\n• Growth hormone releasing hormone (GHRH)\\n• Gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH)\\n\\nThe major hypothalamic inhibitory hormones are:\\n\\n• Prolactin inhibitory hormone or dopamine\\n• Growth hormone inhibitory hormone (GHIH), also called Somatostatin.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bd653a0906","n":31,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Integrin connects with:","options":["Cadherin","Vitronectin","Fibronectin","Selectin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Fibronectin\\n\\nIntegrin connects with fibronectin.\\n\\nIntegrins are transmembrane glycoproteins (integral proteins) that allow cells to attach to extracellular matrix constituents such as laminin, fibronectin and collagen. It helps in linking the intracellular cytoskeleton functionally and structurally with the outside world.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d2b5d2e2aa","n":32,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"On performing a histamine test on an individual with brachial plexus trunk injury, you can expect ________.","options":["Central capillary dilatation → wheal → Surrounding flare","Central capillary dilatation → wheal without surrounding flare","Central capillary dilatation → wheal with exaggerated surrounding flare","Absent triple response"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Central capillary dilatation → wheal without surrounding flare\\n\\nBrachial plexus trunk injury leads to anesthetized skin over the region. On performing a histamine test on such an individual, one can expect central capillary dilatation → wheal without surrounding flare. The incomplete triple response over the anesthetic skin is characteristic of postganglionic lesions.\\n\\nIntradermal injection of histamine usually causes a triple response in the surrounding skin i.e, red reaction→ flare → wheal\\n\\n• Red reaction\xa0is independent of innervation\xa0and is due to histamine-mediated\xa0relaxation of the precapillary sphincters\\n• Flare reaction\xa0(Flush,\xa0Axon reflex) is due to\xa0Substance P\xa0mediated arteriolar dilatation\xa0(antidromic conduction\xa0of afferent impulses via collaterals of the sensory nerve fiber that lead to the release of vasoactive mediators)\\n• Wheal (skin edema)\xa0is due to\xa0histamine-mediated increased capillary permeability\\n• The triple response persists after total sympathectomy\\n• Flare is absent in locally anesthetized skin","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4e9d84d4aa","n":33,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"The diagram below shows the pressure-volume curves for 3 different lungs. Which of the following is the best representation of compliance between the three lungs?","options":["A<B<C","B>A>C","B>C>A","C>A>B"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. B>A>C\\n\\n• The pressure-volume relationship is the fundamental relation between the work of breathing and lung mechanics.\\n• The diagram depicts the change in volume with respect to the change in intrapleural pressure, which is compliance. Here, the change is more (steep curve) with B and least (flat curve) with C.\\n• The “stiffer” lung displays a more horizontal curve (C), meaning that it takes a greater pressure to achieve the same inflation volume compared with a normal lung (A). These lungs are called\xa0less compliant.\\n• Curve (B) is more compliant as it takes lesser pressure to achieve the same inflation volume compared to normal (A).\\n\\nApplied aspect:\\n\\nCompliance is increased in emphysema.\xa0The mechanism is shown in the flow chart below.\\n\\nCompliance is decreased in case of pulmonary edema or fibrosis.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/242a7b8f53a6540e8d81.png"]},{"bankId":"qb-12f1482673","n":34,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"The earliest stage at which erythropoietin is effective in red blood cell formation is?","options":["Burst forming units","Colony forming units","Late erythroblast stage","Normoblast stage"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Burst forming units\\n\\nBFU is the\xa0earliest stage of hematopoiesis where erythropoietin (EPO) acts\\n\\nUnder the influence of EPO, both BFU and CFU grow in semisolid culture media and give rise to colonies of well-hemoglobinized erythroblasts.\\n\\nErythroid burst-forming unit (BFU-E) is the most primitive single lineage committed erythroid progenitor. In addition to EPO, they form the sub-population of Erythroid Colony Forming Units (CFU-E).\\nCFU is derived from burst-forming units.\\n\\nNote: While CFUs have the highest number of receptors for EPO, the earliest stage at which it acts is on the BFU.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c8218f7906","n":35,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"The factors that can cause a person to drink water due to stimulation of thirst mechanism is/are ___________.","options":["Dehydration","Hemorrhage","Prandial Drinking","All of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. All of the above\\n\\nDehydration, hemorrhage, and prandial drinking:\xa0All three can cause increased thirst resulting in water drinking.\\nIncreased osmolality acts via osmoreceptors in the anterior hypothalamus.\\n\\nThe decreased plasma volume, as in hemorrhage, acts via\\n1. Renin-angiotensin system\\n2. Baroreceptors in heart and blood vessels.\\nThe intake of liquids during a meal is known as\xa0prandial drinking. The increase is known as learned or habit response but is not understood completely.\\n\\nThe following factors are thought to play a role\\n1. Increase in plasma osmolality due to food absorption\\n2. Action on hypothalamus by one or more gastrointestinal hormones","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9e3ee4200b","n":36,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"The following Darrow Yannet diagram is suggestive of which condition? Note : The solid line is the original fluid compartment and the colour shaded compartment within the dotted line denotes the change caused by the condition.","options":["Excessive sweating","Adrenal Insufficiency","Diarrhoea","SIADH"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Adrenal Insufficiency\\n\\nIn adrenal insufficiency there is hypo-osmotic volume contraction. Aldosterone deficiency leads to increased sodium loss (loss of hyperosmotic fluid).\\n\\n• The osmolarity of ECF decreases.\\n• ECF volume decreases\xa0because of the loss of volume.\\n• Water shifts into ICF; as a result of the shift,\xa0ICF osmolarity decreases\xa0until it is equal to ECF osmolarity, and\xa0ICF volume increases.\\n• So, loss of hypertonic fluid causes hypotonic volume contraction.\\n\\nPlease note that in the diagram, the horizontal axis represents volume and vertical axis represents osmolarity.\\n\\nThe Darrow Yannet diagrams of various conditions are shown above.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/168fc35ffd3d28de99b4.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-8bb016ed50","n":37,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"The mechanism by which glucose is absorbed in the intestine is:","options":["Primary active transport","Secondary active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Any of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Secondary active transport\\n\\nGlucose is absorbed in the intestine by\xa0secondary active transport.\\n\\nOn the basolateral side, there is active transport of\xa0sodium ions out of the cell\xa0into the interstitial fluid. This\xa0depletes sodium inside the cell. Because\xa0of this Na+ concentration difference, more Na+ binds to\xa0the SGLT on the luminal side. This increases the affinity of SGLT for glucose and glucose binds to it.\\n\\nThen, both the Na+\xa0and glucose are transported together to the interior of the cell. Thus, glucose is absorbed in the intestine by\xa0secondary active transport. The energy for glucose transport is provided indirectly, by the active transport of Na+ out of the cell. The glucose moves out of\xa0the cell into the blood across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion mediated via GLUT.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6aab6bbfe6","n":38,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Weber-Fechner law relates magnitude of sensation to ____.","options":["Strength of muscle contraction","Log of Intensity of stimulus","Amplitude of action potential","Duration of stimulus"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Log of Intensity of stimulus\\n\\nWeber-Fechner Law states that the magnitude of sensation felt is proportionate to the log of the intensity of the stimulus.\\n\\nOther Laws in Physiology:\\n\\n• Doctrine of specific nerve energies\xa0- When the nerve pathways from a particular sense organ are stimulated, the sensation evoked is that for which the receptor is specialized no matter how or where along the pathway the activity is initiated.\\n• Law of projection\xa0- No matter where a particular sensory pathway is stimulated along its course to the cortex, the conscious sensation produced is referred to the location of the receptor. This principle is called the law of projection.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-09fe9e63f1","n":39,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"What is the usual position of the Golgi apparatus in serous acinar cells?","options":["Supranuclear","Basal","Adjacent to nucleus","Apical"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Supranuclear\\n\\nGolgi\xa0complex is located in supranuclear cytoplasm in serous acinar cells. Serous acinar cells are generally found in pancreas and the submandibular salivary glands.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7a195fa87b","n":40,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Which of the following fibres reach the Purkinje cell directly?","options":["Inferior olivary nucleus","Raphe nucleus","Vestibular nucleus","Locus coeruleus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inferior olivary nucleus\\n\\nThe fibres of inferior olivary nucleus (climbing fibres) reach the purkinje cell directly.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nThe input from the Purkinje cells to the deep nuclei is inhibitory.\\n\\nHowever, the deep nuclei output is always excitatory.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-159c132a59","n":41,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Which of the following hormones play a permissive role in the attainment of puberty?","options":["GnRH","Leptin","Insulin","GH"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Leptin\\n\\nThe hormone that plays a permissive role in the attainment of puberty is\xa0leptin.\\n\\nIt now appears that leptin may be the link between body weight and puberty.\xa0Leptin is a satiety-producing hormone secreted by adipocytes.\xa0High leptin levels reduce appetite\xa0and increase energy expenditure.\\n\\nEvidence for which is given by:\\n\\n• Obese ob/ob mice that cannot make leptin are infertile, and their fertility is restored by injections of leptin.\\n• Leptin treatment also induces precocious puberty in immature female mice.\\n• However, the way that leptin fits into the overall control of puberty remains to be determined.\\n\\nLeptin also accelerates GnRH pulsatility in neurons, fastening puberty.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f7ff202a56","n":42,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Which of the following is the diluting segment of the kidney?","options":["PCT","Collecting duct","Ascending thick loop of Henle","Descending loop of Henle"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ascending thick loop of Henle\\n\\nThe ascending thick limb of the\xa0loop of Henle is called the diluting segment.\\n\\nIn the ascending limb, there is the movement of Na+ and Cl– out of the tubular lumen, and the tubular fluid becomes more dilute.\\n\\nWhen the fluid reaches the top of the ascending limb, it becomes hypotonic to plasma.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5d4462861b","n":43,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2018","text":"Which of the following organelles is absent in the area of the cardiac muscle marked:","options":["Mitochondria","Lysosome","Golgi bodies","Endoplasmic reticulum"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lysosome\\n\\nThe area of the cardiac muscle marked is devoid of nuclei. Lysosomes are commonly observed in perinuclear areas. Other organelles are present in abundance throughout the cell.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/2d3789a0d2558b654c1b.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-597d093d87","n":44,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"\\"Vasopressin escape\\" in SIADH results due to:","options":["Downregulation of aquaporin 2 production","Increase in urine osmolarity","Decrease in ADH secretion","Upregulation of aquaporin 2 production"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Downregulation of aquaporin 2 production\\n\\nVasopressin escape in SIADH (Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion) results due to downregulation of aquaporin 2 production.\\n\\nProlonged exposure to elevated levels of vasopressin (ADH) leads eventually to downregulation of aquaporin-2 receptors. This prevents excessive reabsorption of water, despite high serum ADH levels and hence maintains euvolemia. This is known as vasopressin escape.\xa0This helps prevent excessive dilutional hyponatremia in SIADH.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8faa1fec73","n":45,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"A soldier posted in Siachen is brought to you in the ER. In order to rule out hypothermia, how do you best measure his temperature?","options":["Oral","Rectal","Infrared tympanic thermometer","Axillary"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Rectal\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario rectal temperature would be the preferred site to measure the core body temperature.\\n\\nDuring low or high temperatures like in Siachen, the axillary and the oral temperatures are highly misleading and should not be used to measure the core body temperatures\\n\\nInfrared tympanic thermometer measures the temperature of the eardrum which is representative of core body temperature. However, more often the thermometer is not inserted adequately or for safety reasons, they are manufactured in such a way that they do not reach deep into the ear canal, or the ear canal contains cerumen which can disturb the measurement. Hence the infrared thermometer more commonly delivers the temperature of ear canal cerumen and hence not a reliable method of measuring the core body temperature.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-97de3b3341","n":46,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Afferent fibers from baroreceptors for blood pressure control goes to?","options":["Nucleus ambiguous","Rostral-ventro lateral medulla","Nucleus Tractus Solitarius","Caudal ventrolateral medulla"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Nucleus Tractus Solitarius\\n\\nAfferent fiber for blood pressure control goes to the Nucleus Tractus Solitarius (NTS).\\n\\nIncreased BP stimulates the baroreceptors which provide\xa0negative feedback\xa0in response to the increase in blood pressure.\\n\\nBaroreceptor reflex:\\n\\n• Increased BP → stimulation of baroreceptors → stimulation of NTS → excitation of CVLM → inhibition of RVLM → inhibition of sympathetic system\\n• Stimulation of NTS also stimulates CVC → parasympathetic stimulation\\n• Net effect = sympathetic inhibition and parasympathetic stimulation → decreased HR and BP\\n\\nNTS - Nucleus tractus solitarius\\n\\nCVC - Cardiovagal centre\\n\\nCVLM - Caudal ventrolateral medulla\\n\\nRVLM - Rostral ventrolateral medulla\\n\\nHR - Heart rate\\n\\nBP - Blood pressure","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6d7fd14c47","n":47,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"I-125 labelled albumin is used for measurement of:","options":["Plasma volume","Interstitial fluid volume","ICF","ECF"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Plasma volume\\n\\nI-125 labelled albumin is the most commonly used substance for the measurement of plasma volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-22ebbe4dbd","n":48,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"If the lung is allowed to undergo elastic recoil without the chest wall, the resultant volume of the lung would be?","options":["Minimum volume","Zero","Functional residual capacity","Residual volume"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Minimum volume\\n\\nIf lung is allowed to undergo elastic recoil without the chest wall, the resultant volume of the lung would be minimum volume as the opposing force by the chest wall would be non existant.\\n\\nThe yellow line in the above graph denotes the compliance of lungs alone. According to the graph, when the pressure (x axis) \xa0inside the lung is zero, there is some volume of air lesser than FRC still present (y axis) inside the lungs which is less than the functional residual capacity and residual volume which can be called as minimum volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-efbb02f0cd","n":49,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Myosin head detaches from actin by which of the following mechanism?","options":["Binding of ATP","Entry of calcium into sarcoplasmic reticulum","Change in troponin C configuration","Release of ADP and Pi"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Binding of ATP\\n\\nBinding of ATP on the free site of myosin leads to the detachment of myosin head from the thin filament.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-88d468d0ad","n":50,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Pacemaker generating the rhythm for breathing is ________","options":["Pneumotaxic centre","Dorsal group of neurons in the medulla","Pre-Bőtzinger complex","Apneustic centre"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pre-Bőtzinger complex\\n\\nPre-Botzinger complex generates the rhythm for automated breathing.\\n\\n• These are also known as pacemaker cells\xa0located in the medulla.\\n• These are the first set of neurons that\xa0initiate inspiration.\\n• These neurons discharge rhythmically, and they produce rhythmic discharges in the phrenic motor neurons.\\n\\nBreathing is under the control of both the following:\\n\\n• Voluntary control:\xa0Located in the cerebral cortex (via corticospinal tract)\\n• Automatic control:\xa0Driven by pacemaker cells in the medulla (Pre-Botzinger complex). These activate motor neurons via cervical (Phrenic nerve innervating diaphragm) and thoracic spinal cord (external intercostal muscle).\\n\\nThere is a reciprocal innervation to expiratory and inspiratory muscles in that motor neurons supplying expiratory muscles are inactive when motor neurons supplying inspiratory muscles are active, and vice versa.\\n\\nInspiration\\n\\nInitiation\\nPre-Botzinger complex (Pacemaker cells) in Medulla\\n\\nMaintenance\\nApneustic center in lower Pons stimulates\xa0Dorsal group of neurons in the medulla\\n\\nTermination\\nApneustic center inhibited by Vagus and Pneumotaxic center\\n\\nRegulation of expiration: During quiet breathing, expiration is a passive process; there is no firing of neurons. Ventral respiratory group of neurons are involved in forceful expiration like during exercise.\\n\\nMnemonic DIVE:\xa0Dorsal respiratory group for Inspiration and Ventral respiratory group for Expiration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-68d484a796","n":51,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Single breath nitrogen method is used for measuring ____","options":["Dead space volume","FRC","Tidal volume","Diffusion capacity"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Dead space volume\\n\\nAnatomical dead space can be measured by the\xa0single-breath N2 curve (Fowler\'s method).\\n\\n• Anatomical dead space is the respiratory system volume exclusive of alveoli.\\n• Alveolar dead space is the volume of gas not equilibrating with blood i.e. wasted ventilation.\\n• Physiological/total dead space is the sum of anatomical and alveolar dead space.\\n• The equation used for the calculation of total/physiological dead space is the Bohr’s equation.\\n\\nThe patient takes a deep breath of O2 From mid-inspiration, then exhales steadily. The changes in the N2 concentration of expired gas during expiration are plotted.\\n\\nRegion I - Dead Space (DS)\\n\\nFrom I–II is a mixture of DS and alveolar gas.\\n\\nThe transition from III-IV is the closing volume (CV).\\n\\nEnd of IV - Residual Volume (RV).\\n\\nFactors affecting dead space:\\n\\nFactor\\nEffect\\n\\nPosture:\\n\\nUpright\\n\\nSupine\\n\\nIncrease\\n\\nDecrease\\n\\nPosition of airway:\\n\\nNeck Extension\\n\\nNeck Flexion\\n\\nIncrease\\n\\nDecrease\\n\\nAge\\nIncrease\\n\\nArtificial airway\\nDecrease\\n\\nPositive pressure ventilation\\nIncrease\\n\\nDrug anti-cholinergic\\nIncrease\\n\\nPulmonary perfusion:\\n\\nPulmonary embolism\\n\\nHypotension\\n\\nIncrease\\n\\nIncrease\\n\\nPulmonary disease:\\n\\nEmphysema\\n\\nIncrease\\n\\nOption B: Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is measured using body plethysmography.\\n\\nOption C: Tidal volume (TV) is measured using spirometry.\\n\\nOption D: Diffusion Capacity is measured using carbon monoxide diffusion capacity (DLCO).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-939814dcb0","n":52,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Which hormone crosses the plasma membrane for its action?","options":["Epinephrine","Thyroxine","Glucagon","Insulin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Thyroxine\\n\\nThyroxine crosses the plasma membrane for its action.\\n\\nThyroid hormones enter the nucleus and bind to thyroid hormone receptors. The hormone-receptor complex in\xa0the nucleus\xa0binds to DNA via zinc fingers. This increases the expression of a variety of different genes that code for proteins regulating cell function.\xa0 Although thyroxine is an AA derivative, there are transporters to move it into the cell, hence they are intracellular receptors acting on the nucleus.\\n\\nEpinephrine, glucagon, and insulin bind to cell surface receptors.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-86c09daa6b","n":53,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Which is the first-order neuron in the optic pathway?","options":["Rods and cones","Ganglion cells","Cells in lateral geniculate body","Bipolar cells"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Rods and cones\\n\\nPhotoreceptors - rods and cones\xa0are the first-order neurons in the\xa0visual pathway.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-692bdb8d1c","n":54,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Which of the following hormones do not act on the pancreas?","options":["Secretin","Gastrin","Cholecystokinin","GIP"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Gastrin\\n\\nGastrin does not act upon the pancreas. Gastrin stimulates gastric acid secretion and gastric mucosal growth.\\n\\nGI hormones acting on the pancreas include:\\n\\nCCK\\n\\n(Cholecystokinin)\\n\\nSecretin\\n\\nGIP\\n\\n(Gastric inhibitory peptide)\\n\\nStimulates\\n\\n• Pancreatic enzyme secretion\\n• Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion\\n• Gallbladder contraction\\n• Growth of exocrine pancreas\\n\\n• Pepsin secretion\\n• Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion\\n• Biliary bicarbonate secretion\\n• Growth of exocrine pancreas\\n\\n• Insulin release\\n\\nInhibits\\n\\n• Gastric emptying\\n\\n• Gastric acid secretion\\n\\n• Gastric acid secretion\\n\\nGIP is also known as Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b872d1d93b","n":55,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Which of the following hormones is exclusively secreted by adrenal gland but not by the ovaries of females?","options":["Progesterone","DHEA","Estrogen","DHEA-S"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. DHEA-S\\n\\nDHEAS is\xa0exclusively secreted from the adrenals but not by ovaries because ovaries lack the enzyme dehydroepiandrosterone-sulphotransferase needed to form DHEAS.\\n\\n90% of DHEA is synthesised by the adrenals and 10% comes from the ovaries.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-686a8973e1","n":56,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"Which of the following is not an example of feed forward control mechanism?","options":["Temperature Regulation","Cephalic phase of gastric secretion","Cerebellar action in motor coordination","Exercise resulting in heart rate increase"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Exercise resulting in heart rate increase\\n\\nExercise resulting in heart rate increase is not an example of feedforward control mechanism. However, thinking about exercise\xa0and it resulting in a heart rate increase would be an example of feedforward control mechanism.\\n\\nFeedforward control is that which makes an anticipatory or adaptive response without a specific stimulus.\\n\\nTemperature control - Thermoregulatory responses are initiated by the hypothalamus before the changes in environmental temperature changes the core body temperature. Thus, shivering in response to cold environment would be an example of feedforward control.\\n\\nIn the cephalic phase of gastric secretion, the thought of food itself and not food per se produces a gastric secretion.\\n\\nThe cerebellum has a feed-forward inhibition that limits the duration of the excitation produced by any given afferent volley.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ea848d3d67","n":57,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2019 (May & Nov)","text":"You are an intern and want to determine the quantity of IV fluids needed for a patient. How much amount of transient loss/insensible loss per day would you account for in your calculations?","options":["150-200 ml","500-600 ml","200-350 ml","350-400 ml"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 500-600 ml\\n\\n500-600 ml of\xa0transient loss/insensible loss per day would have to be accounted for in the calculation.\\n\\nAlthough water is lost through sweating especially in hot climates or during physical exertion, we tend to lose water at rest as well. Physiologically, water evaporates from the skin and lungs at a rate of about 600 to 700 ml/day. This is known as insensible water loss.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9e2adb1567","n":58,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A 65-year old patient visits his family practitioner for a yearly examination. On examination, his BP was 190/100 mm Hg. His pulse pressure was 90 mm Hg. What would have decreased resulting in this increase in pulse pressure?","options":["Myocardial contractility","Stroke volume","Arterial wall compliance","Cardiac output"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Arterial wall compliance\\n\\nPulse pressure increases with age due to decreased arterial wall compliance.\\n\\nWith advancing age, there is an increase in systolic blood pressure. Whilst diastolic blood pressure falls after 60 years of age due to increased arterial stiffness (decreased compliance). As a result of these two changes, pulse pressure increases (↑PP = ↑SBP - ↓DBP).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-169c776f00","n":59,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"A neuron is receiving 400 inputs from 150 sensory neurons. This is an example of?","options":["Divergence","Convergence","Feedback inhibition","Feedforward excitation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Convergence\\n\\nA single neuron receiving 400 inputs from 150 sensory neurons is an example of\xa0convergence.\\n\\nConvergence means signals from multiple inputs\xa0uniting to excite a single neuron. It can be of 2 types:\\n\\n• Convergence\xa0from a single source\\n• Convergence\xa0from multiple sources\\n\\nThe importance of\xa0convergence is that neurons are almost never excited\xa0by an action potential from a single input terminal.\xa0However, action potentials converging on the neuron\xa0from multiple terminals provide enough spatial summation\xa0to bring the neuron to the threshold required for\xa0discharge.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-01a2c489b9","n":60,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Arrange the following events in the sequence in which they occur, after \'p wave\' in ECG:\\n\\n1. a wave in JVP\\n2. First heart sound\\n3. Rapid filling of ventricles\\n4. T wave in ECG","options":["1-2-3-4","1-2-4-3","3-1-2-4","1-3-2-4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1-2-4-3\\n\\nThe correct sequence of events that occur after the p-wave is 1-2-4-3, ie:-\\n\\na wave in JVP → First heart sound\xa0 → T wave in ECG\xa0 → Rapid filling of ventricles.\\n\\nThe sequence of the events in this question can be better understood with the following diagram:\\n\\n• The p wave in the ECG represents atrial depolarization, which causes contraction of the atria, represented by the \'a\' wave in the JVP.\xa0Atrial contraction causes final filling of the ventricles (fourth phase of ventricular diastole)\\n• Atrial contraction\xa0is followed by the closure of the mitral valve, which causes the first-heart sound.\\n• This is followed by the isovolumetric contraction of the ventricle, which causes c wave in JVP, due to bulging of the tricuspid valve. This is followed by the rapid ejection phase of the ventricle.\\n• The T wave of ECG occurs during ventricular repolarization, which is followed by the opening of the mitral valve, followed by\xa0rapid filling phase of the ventricle\xa0(second\xa0 phase of ventricular diastole).\\n\\nNote: Passive rapid filling occurs as the mitral valve opens just before atrial contraction occurs.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8e58319515","n":61,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Calculate the ECF volume in a volunteer in whom 10 gm of mannitol was injected in venous blood. At equilibrium urinary excretion of mannitol was found to be 10% and the plasma concentration of mannitol was 65 mg/100 ml.","options":["14L","10L","28L","20L"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 14L\\n\\nECF volume of the above volunteer is calculated to be 14 L.\\n\\nThe ECF volume can be calculated using the formula:\\n\\nWhere,\\n\\n• V = ECF volume\\n• Amount injected = 10 gm\\n• Amount of mannitol excreted = 10% of 10 gm = 1gm\\n• Plasma concentration of mannitol = 65 mg/100mL = 0.65 gm/L\\n\\nCalculation:\\n\\nECF Volume = (10-1)/0.65\\n\\n= 13.8 L =\xa0approximately 14 L\\n\\nMannitol cannot cross cell membranes, hence, it will be distributed\xa0in ECF but not in ICF. Thus mannitol is a marker\xa0for ECF volume and therefore calculating the volume of distribution of mannitol gives the value of ECF volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-98a019524c","n":62,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Calculate the respiratory quotient (RQ) in a patient with 50kg body weight, if the CO2 exhaled is 200ml/min and O2 consumed is 250 ml/min.","options":["0.8","1","1.25","1.5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 0.8\\n\\nThe respiratory quotient is 0.8.\\n\\nThe respiratory quotient is the ratio of carbon dioxide production to oxygen utilization.\\n\\nIn the given question, the CO2 production is 200mL/min, while the O2 consumption is 250mL/min.\\n\\nTherefore,\\n\\nRespiratory quotient = 200/250 = 0.8\\n\\nThe respiratory quotient (RQ) is used for determining the metabolic utilization of various foods.\\n\\n• When carbohydrates are metabolized with one molecule of oxygen, one carbon dioxide molecule is released. Therefore, the RQ of carbohydrates\xa0= 1.\\n• When\xa0fats\xa0are oxidized, for every 100 molecules of oxygen consumed, 70 molecules of CO2 are released. Therefore, the RQ of fats is 0.70.\\n• Similarly, the respiratory quotient for proteins is 0.80.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ba003219f1","n":63,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium is mainly due to?","options":["Phosphate","Bicarbonate in the intracellular compartment","Calcium in the extracellular compartment","Intracellular proteins"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Intracellular proteins\\n\\nGibbs-Donnan equilibrium is mainly due to intracellular proteins.\\n\\nDonnan and Gibbs showed that \\"When an ion on one side of a membrane cannot diffuse through the membrane, the distribution of other ions to which the membrane is permeable is affected in a predictable way\\".\\n\\nAccording to this, in the presence of\xa0intracellular proteins, which are negatively charged non-diffusable\xa0ions, the distribution of other ions to which the membrane is permeable is affected in a predictable way (Donnan Effect) so that at equilibrium their concentration ratios are equal.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fcee48df66","n":64,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"In negative feedback, feedback gain of infinity is seen in which of the following?","options":["Blood volume control by kidney","Temperature control in hypothalamus","BP control by barorecptors","Infinite gain is not possible"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Blood volume control by kidney\\n\\nIn negative feedback, feedback gain of infinity\xa0could be seen in\xa0blood volume control by the kidney.\\n\\nWhen the blood volume falls below the equilibrium, the renal–body fluid mechanism causes the blood volume returns back to the equilibrium point resulting in zero error and hence the gain of kidney in its ability to regulate blood volume is infinity (∞).\\n\\nThe degree of effectiveness with which a regulatory system maintains constant conditions is determined by the gain of the negative feedback system.\\n\\nGain= Correction/Error\\n\\nExample: If an external or internal factor led to a decrease in mean blood pressure from 100 mm Hg to 80 mm Hg. Activation of a negative feedback mechanism i.e. baroreflex leads to a correction of blood pressure to 96 mm Hg. So in this case,\\n\\n• Correction = 16 mm Hg\\n• Error = 4 mm Hg\\n• Gain = Correction/Error = 16/4 = 4\\n\\nNote: A gain of 0 would imply a total failure of the regulatory system and a gain of infinity\xa0(∞) would indicate complete restoration of the regulated variable.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fa197366e1","n":65,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"In skeletal muscle, dihydropyridine and ryanodine receptors are coupled by?","options":["Chemically","Electrically","Chemically and then electrically","Mechanically"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Mechanically\\n\\nIn skeletal muscle, dihydropyridine and ryanodine receptors show mechanical coupling involving structural interaction between the two.\\n\\nDihydropyridine receptors (DHPRs) are voltage-gated Ca2+ channels in the T tubule membrane.\\n\\nThe ryanodine receptor (RyR) is a ligand-gated Ca2+ channel with Ca2+ as its natural ligand.\\n\\nSkeletal muscle excitation-contraction (E-C) coupling is thought to occur by a \\"mechanical coupling\\" mechanism involving protein-protein interactions that lead to activation of the sarcoplasmic reticulum ryanodine receptor by the voltage-sensing transverse (T-) tubule dihydropyridine receptor.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d30fa5b6db","n":66,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Intercellular communication in eukaryotes is by?","options":["Lysosome","Peroxisome","Exosome","Genome"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Exosome\\n\\nIntercellular communication in eukaryotes is by\xa0Exosome.\\n\\nIn the last decade, a class of small, heterogeneous, secreted vesicles, broadly termed as\xa0extracellular vesicles\xa0have been identified.\\n\\nThese extracellular vesicles have been implicated as a new and important mediator of cell-cell communication that likely contributes importantly to both normal and pathologic physiology.\\n\\nThese vesicles are enclosed by a lipid bilayer.\\n\\nThey are heterogeneous in size (30-2000 nm diameter), and are generated by at least two distinct mechanisms:\\n\\n• Microvesicles are generated by budding from the plasma membrane of a source cell.\\n• Exosomes are generated from the multivesicular body (MVB).\\n\\nNote: The terms microvesicle and exosome are often lumped together as simply exosomes.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6d03904407","n":67,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Least frequency of slow waves is seen in:","options":["Duodenum","Ileum","Jejunum","Stomach"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Stomach\\n\\nAmong the given options, the least frequency of slow waves is seen in the stomach (4/min).\\n\\nThe Basal electrical rhythm (BER) also called slow-wave, is a rhythmic fluctuation of membrane potential in the gastrointestinal smooth muscle. It is initiated by the interstitial cells of Cajal.\xa0The membrane potential fluctuates between -65mV and -45mV.\\n\\nThe BER is seen in all sites of gastrointestinal smooth muscle except in the esophagus and proximal stomach.\\n\\nThe rate of BER in various sites is as follows:\\n\\n• Stomach: 4/min\\n• Duodenum: 12/min\\n• Distal ileum: 8/min\\n• Cecum: 2/min\\n• Sigmoid colon: 6/min\\n\\nThe BER does not cause contraction of the smooth muscle. However, when the BER is superimposed by the spike potentials, it increases muscle tension.\\n\\nAcetylcholine causes an increase in the number of spike potentials and muscle tension.\xa0Epinephrine causes a decrease in the number of spike potentials and muscle tension.\\n\\nMuscle contraction can occur only when the motor stimulus is provided during the depolarisation part of the BER.\\n\\nThus, BER does not cause muscle contraction by itself, but controls the rate and organization sites of the muscle contractions, as in coordination of the peristaltic activity and setting the rhythm of segmentation.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-dce455aca6","n":68,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Macula densa is formed by which part of the nephron?","options":["PCT","DCT","Afferent arteriole","Bowman\'s capsule"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. DCT\\n\\nMacula densa is formed by specialized tubular epithelial cells of the distal convoluted tubule.\\n\\nThe juxtaglomerular apparatus consists of the following parts:\\n\\n• Macula densa: At the end of the thick ascending limb of Henle, and at the beginning of the distal tubule, the tubular epithelial cells are specialized to form the macula densa. They detect sodium concentration of the fluid in the tubule (chemoreceptor). In response to elevated sodium, the macula densa cells trigger\xa0the contraction of the afferent arteriole, reducing\xa0the flow of blood to the glomerulus and the glomerular filtration rate. This happens as follows: When there is increased sodium, there is increased sodium-potassium ATPase activity which\xa0leads to increased ATP hydrolysis causing more adenosine formation. The adenosine receptors on the macula densa cells stimulate it to release more calcium, so that there is vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole, thereby decreasing the GFR.\\n• Lacis cells: Extraglomerular mesangial cells situated between afferent and efferent arteriole.\\n• Granular cells/JG cells:\xa0Modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole, they secrete renin.\xa0Fall in pressure in afferent arteriole stimulates renin release (mechanoreceptor), which, in turn, increases blood pressure.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-39d37fb20f","n":69,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"The image below shows simple muscle twitch along with the time trace, recorded in a frog gastrocnemius muscle. Calculate the minimum tetanizing frequency. (The time tracer was made with a 100Hz tuning fork).","options":["25 per second","15 per second","17 per second","20 per second"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 20 per second\\n\\nThe tetanizing frequency of the muscle, based on the given diagram, is 20 per second.\\n\\nTetanizing frequency = 1/contraction period. It is the frequency at which individual muscle fibres twitch that fuse and cause sustained muscle contraction, where the contraction period is the time taken from\xa0latent period to maximum force of contraction.\\n\\nIn this case, the time tracer was taken using a 100Hz tuning fork. Which means the tracer recorded 100 waves per second.\\nTherefore, the duration of each wave in time tracer = 0.01 seconds.\\n\\nIn the image given in the question, between the point B (end of latent period) to point C (maximum force of contraction), there are about 5 waves.\\nHence, the duration of contraction = 5 x 0.01 = 0.05 seconds.\\n\\nSubstituting the value in the formula, we get:\\nTetanizing frequency = 1/0.05 sec = 100/5 = 20/sec.\\n\\nTetanization:\\n\\nThe features of muscle contraction can be demonstrated by eliciting single muscle twitches. The adding together of individual twitch contractions to increase the intensity of overall muscle contraction is Summation.\xa0Summation occurs in two ways:\\n\\n• by increasing the number of motor units contracting simultaneously, which is multiple ﬁber summations.\\n• by increasing the frequency of contraction, which is called frequency\xa0summation and can lead to tetanization.\\n\\nWhen the frequency of a contraction reaches a critical level, the successive contractions eventually fuse together, and the whole muscle contraction appears to be completely smooth and continuous, this is called tetanization.\xa0Beyond this point, a further increase in the frequency, will not result in increased force of contraction.\\n\\nTetanization occurs in two ways:\\n\\n• Complete tetanus occurs when no relaxation occurs between stimuli.\\n• Incomplete tetanus when periods of incomplete relaxation take place between the summated stimuli.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/cc14bacf03632ed725b5.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-abf488f1a1","n":70,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"This is the graph depicting the filterability of dextran through the kidney, 1 representing complete filtration and 0 representing no filtration. Choose the substance A, B, C respectively.","options":["Polycationic, Neutral, Polyanionic","Polyanionic, Neutral, Polycationic","Neutral, Polycationic, Polyanionic","Polyanionic, Polycationic, Neutral"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Polycationic, Neutral, Polyanionic\\n\\nIn the given graph, the order of filterability is A>B>C, which likely indicates that the substances are\xa0Polycationic, Neutral, Polyanionic.\\n\\nThe glomerular filtration barrier is negatively charged, which will repel most of the negative charged (anionic substances). Whereas, the cationic substances are easily filtered.\\n\\nHence, the order of filterability would be:\xa0Polycationic > Neutral > Polyanionic, represented by A, B, and C in the graph shown.\\n\\nThe glomerular filtration barrier consists of:\\n\\n• Endothelium of the capillary\\n• Basement membrane:\\n\\n• Lamina densa - electron-dense layer. It contains type IV collagen\\n• Lamina rara interna -\xa0contains glycosaminoglycan heparan sulfate which has a negative charge\\n• Lamina rara externa - contains glycosaminoglycan heparan sulfate which has a negative charge\\n\\n• Layer of epithelial cells (podocytes) surrounding the outer surface of the capillary basement membrane\\n\\nThe capillary endothelium is perforated by thousands of small holes called fenestrae, which measure from 4 nm up to 8nm. Therefore, neutral substances with molecular diameters of less than 4 nm are freely filtered, and filtration of those with diameters of more than 8 nm approaches zero.\\n\\nHowever, in addition to the size of the particles, the filtration also depends mainly on the charge of the particle being filtered. The glomerular capillary wall consists of highly negatively charged proteins called sialoproteins, which repel the negatively charged substances (anions). The property can be studied by using charged dextran.\\n\\nWhen the filtration of the negatively and positively charged dextrans was studied, the results indicated that the negatively charged dextrans in the blood are repelled, with the result that filtration of anionic substances 4 nm in diameter is less than half that of neutral substances of the same size.\\n\\nThis explains, why albumin despite having an effective molecular diameter of approximately 7 nm, has a very low filtration rate.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/dfcc02b8e2050e473347.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-fd81cb75ef","n":71,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Transcutaneous electric nerve stimulation is used to reduce pain. What is the mechanism behind this?","options":["Central inhibition","Desensitization","Gate control theory","Descending inhibition"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Gate control theory\\n\\nTranscutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) used for pain relief is based on the\xa0gate theory of pain.\xa0This method uses electrodes to activate Aα and Aβ fibers near the injury to relieve pain.\\n\\nGate control theory is based on the inhibition of pain pathway in the spinal cord. Various sensory inputs from a particular dermatome reach the dorsal horn of the spinal cord. The pain is transmitted via A-delta and C fibers, whereas, A-beta fibers carry tactile sensations (touch, pressure) and A-alpha fibers carry proprioception.\\n\\nWhen these large-diameter A-beta/A-alpha fibers are stimulated, they inhibit the pain pathway in the spinal cord. This inhibition is thought to result from the local lateral inhibition of interneurons in the spinal cord.\\n\\nGate control theory operates at the\xa0substantia gelatinosa of the\xa0dorsal horn of the spinal cord.\\n\\nThe given image depicts the gate control theory of pain:","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0449413301","n":72,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following curves represents gamma-globin chain production?","options":["Green Curve","Black Curve","Blue Curve","Orange curve"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Orange curve\\n\\nIn the given image, the Orange curve\xa0represents the gamma-globin chain production. Gamma globin chain is produced in the fetal period and drops to undetectable levels 6 months postpartum which is the pattern represented by the Orange curve.\\n\\nHemoglobin in blood has different variants throughout life, in different proportions. The type of hemoglobin essentially depends on the combination of globin chains. Adult hemoglobin is made of 2 alpha and 2 beta chains, whereas fetal hemoglobin is made of 2 alpha and 2 gamma-globin chains. Other combinations of chains can be found in embryonic hemoglobin.\\n\\nHere,\\n\\n• Gamma chain - Orange curve\\n• Alpha chain - Red curve\\n• Beta chain - Green curve\\n• Epsilon chain - Black curve\\n• Delta chain - Blue curve","img":false,"images":["/bimg/7d18db2942fc497905b6.png"]},{"bankId":"qb-97999be9d9","n":73,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following events occur immediately when a person changes his/her posture from supine to standing?","options":["Decrease in firing from baroreceptor","Vagal output to cardiac fibres increase","Mesenteric Vasoconstriction","Increase cardiac contractility"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Decrease in firing from baroreceptor\\n\\nThere is a decreased firing from the baroreceptors.\\n\\n• When a person stands up from a lying down position, it causes pooling of the blood in the legs, due to gravity. This reduces the venous return and causes a decrease in cardiac output.\\n• Reduced cardiac output, leads to a fall in blood pressure, which in turn causes reduced firing from baroreceptors.\\n• As a result, the vagal tone is suppressed leading to increased heart rate.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0787a164cd","n":74,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following increases in COPD?\\n\\n1. FRC\\n2. TLC\\n3. VC\\n4. FEV1/FVC","options":["1 only","1 and 4","1 and 3","1 and 2"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1 and 2\\n\\nIn COPD, the functional residual capacity increases. Total lung capacity may be increased or normal. Hence, both 1 and 2 are correct.\\n\\n• In obstructive lung diseases, both FEV1 and FVC reduces, where the decrease in FEV1 is more than the reduction in FVC. As a result, the ratio of FEV1/FVC also decreases.\\n• The total lung capacity is usually normal in chronic bronchitis, while increased in emphysema.\\n• Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is the volume of air present in the lungs at the end of passive expiration. In COPD, FRC is increased.\\n\\nLung Capacities:\\n\\nThe total lung capacity can be broken down into alternative capacities that help to define functioning lungs.\\n\\n• The vital lung capacity\xa0refers to the maximum amount of air expired from the fully inflated lung or maximum inspiratory level.\\n• The inspiratory capacity is the maximum amount of air inspired from the end-expiratory level.\\n• The functional residual capacity represents the volume of the air remaining in the lungs after expiration of a normal\xa0breath.\xa0Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is expiratory reserve volume + residual volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-166fe94502","n":75,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following is not a cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism?","options":["Parathyroid adenoma","Lithium intake","Vitamin D deficiency","Chronic kidney disease"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Parathyroid adenoma\\n\\nParathyroid adenoma is a benign tumor of the parathyroid gland, which causes primary hyperparathyroidism (not secondary).\\n\\nSecondary hyperparathyroidism\xa0is characterized by high levels of parathyroid hormone, to compensate for the low levels of serum calcium, due to some other underlying condition.\\n\\nCauses of secondary hyperparathyroidism, include:\\n\\n• Chronic kidney disease:\xa0The damaged kidneys are unable to produce sufficient amounts of the active form of vitamin D, 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol. This further causes hypocalcemia and triggers PTH secretion.\\n• Vitamin D deficiency: Causes hypocalcemia, which triggers the increased secretion of PTH.\\n• Lithium: May cause an increased set-point of calcium for parathyroid\xa0hormone suppression, which leads to parathyroid hyperplasia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9e9dbafc46","n":76,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following is not required for calculation of creatinine clearance?","options":["Urine creatinine","Serum creatinine","24 hour urine output","Volume of serum passing through kidney in 24 hours"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Volume of serum passing through kidney in 24 hours\\n\\nThe volume of plasma passing through the kidney in 24 hours is not used in the calculation of creatinine clearance.\\n\\nThe parameters used to calculate creatinine clearance (CCr) are:\\n\\n• Urine creatinine\\n• Serum creatinine\\n• 24hr urine output\\n\\nThe formula used to calculate creatinine clearance (CCr) is:\\n\\nUCr \xd7 V ⁄ PCr\xa0\xa0where,\\n\\nUCr -\xa0Urine creatinine(mg/dl)\\n\\nPCr -\xa0Serum creatinine(mg/dl)\\n\\nV - Urine volume(ml/24 hrs)\\n\\nCreatinine is a metabolite of muscle metabolism and is cleared from the body mostly by glomerular filtration. Therefore, the clearance of creatinine is used to assess GFR.\\n\\nUrine collection for the measurement of creatinine clearance may be subject to practical constraints. Therefore, approximate changes in GFR can be obtained by measuring the\xa0serum creatinine concentration, which is inversely proportional to GFR.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-adbe2e1d44","n":77,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following statements are correct regarding mechanisms of depolarisation, repolarisation, and hyperpolarisation in a skeletal muscle action potential?\\n\\n1. Depolarisation due to influx of Na+ through voltage-gated channels\\n2. Depolarisation due to influx of Na+ through ligand-gated channels\\n3. Repolarisation due to efflux of K+ through a voltage-gated channel\\n4. Repolarisation due to efflux of K+ through a ligand-gated channel\\n5. Hyperpolarisation due to efflux of K+","options":["2 and 4 are correct","1, 3, and 5 are correct","1, 4, and 5 are correct","2, 4, and 5 are correct"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 3, and 5 are correct\\n\\nStatements 1, 3, and 5 are correct.\\n\\nAction potential in skeletal muscle:\\n\\n• Motor-neuron discharge causes end-plate potential, which when reaches to a threshold value, causes action potential in the muscle fibre.\\n• The depolarization of the muscle fibre is caused by the activation of the voltage-gated sodium channels, which causes the influx of sodium.\\n• During repolarization, the sodium channels are closed, and voltage-gated potassium channels open, which causes efflux of potassium.\\n• The voltage-gated potassium channels close slowly. As a result, the potassium efflux continues, leading to hyperpolarization of the membrane.\\n\\nThe steps in an action potential in muscle fibre and the neuron, are similar except for the quantitative differences, such as:\\n\\n• RMP is -80 to -90 mV in skeletal muscle fibres, while it is about -70mV in neurons.\\n• The duration of the action potential in skeletal muscle is 1 to 5 msec, which is about 5 times longer than in the largest myelinated nerve fibre.\\n• The velocity of conduction is about 3-5m/s, which is about 1/13th of the velocity of the nerve fibre.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0d542bd0fe","n":78,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following statements is true about skeletal muscle contraction?\\n\\n1. Extracellular Ca2+ is not important for contraction\\n2. Ca2+ is released from mitochondria\\n3. Ca2+ is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum\\n4. Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels on sarcolemma have no role in EC coupling","options":["2, 3, 4","3 only","1, 2, 4","1, 3, 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 3 only\\n\\nDuring the contraction of the skeletal muscle, the\xa0Ca2+ is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Only statement 3 is correct.\\n\\nExcitation contraction coupling refers to the process by which the membrane depolarization of the muscle fibre (excitation) leads to the contraction of the muscle.\\n\\nThe steps of excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle are as follows:\\n\\n• Motor-neuron discharge causes the release of acetylcholine at the motor endplate, which binds to the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors.\\n• This causes increased Na+ and K+ conductance in the endplate membrane, leading to the generation of end-plate potential, which is a local potential.\\n• When the local endplate potential reaches a threshold for the muscle fibre, and an action potential is generated.\\n• The generated action potential passes inward through the T tubules, leading to the spread of depolarisation.\\n• The depolarization of the muscle fibre causes the release of Ca2+ from terminal cisterns of sarcoplasmic reticulum and diffusion to thick and thin filaments.\\n• The calcium released,\xa0binds\xa0to troponin C, uncovering myosin-binding sites on actin.\\n• The formation of cross-linkages between actin and myosin and sliding of thin on thick filaments causes contraction of the skeletal muscle.\\n\\nSteps in relaxation:\\n\\n• The cytoplasmic calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, by the action of SERCA (sarcoplasmic endoplasmic reticulum calcium ATPase).\\n• Once the calcium concentration is lowered, the interaction between actin and myosin ceases, and the muscle relaxes.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nStatement 1: Incorrect - Extracellular calcium levels play an important role in muscle contraction. When there is a decrease in extracellular calcium as in hypocalcemia, the voltage-gated sodium channels are activated, which causes sodium influx. The increased intracellular sodium concentration causes sustained depolarization, which may lead to tetany.\\n\\nStatement 2: Incorrect -\xa0Ca2+ is not released from mitochondria. It is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\\n\\nStatement 4:\xa0Voltage-gated calcium channels on T-tubules (part of sarcolemma) are activated by a depolarizing stimulus, which causes the release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The increased calcium concentration will eventually lead to muscle contraction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-227230792c","n":79,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of the following types of amino acids are present in the transmembranous part of the membrane receptor?","options":["Amphipathic","Hydrophobic","Hydrophilic","None of the above"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hydrophobic\\n\\nThe transmembranous part of the membrane receptor consists of hydrophobic amino acids.\\n\\nA membrane protein can be either integral protein (spans the thickness of the cell membrane) or peripheral\xa0protein (attached to the peripheral side of the membrane).\xa0The membrane-spanning segment\xa0of integral proteins are usually hydrophobic alpha-helices.\\n\\nThe central core of the cell membrane is hydrophobic. Integral proteins have their\xa0hydrophobic portions usually in the interior of the membrane and the hydrophilic portions towards the surfaces.\xa0The presence of these hydrophobic amino acids in the interior is a special adaptation that allows the protein to remain stable in the hydrophobic milieu of the cell membrane.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0832bdda7a","n":80,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"AIIMS 2020 (May And Nov INI-CET)","text":"Which of these hormones have intracellular receptors?","options":["Thyroxine","Epinephrine","Glucagon","Parathormone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Thyroxine\\n\\nThyroxine hormone act by binding to intracellular nuclear receptors.\\n\\nAll other hormones mentioned in the options bind to receptors on the cell membrane.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-93a96d407b","n":81,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"A 50-year-old male was diagnosed with a submandibular gland tumour and underwent removal of the tumour following which lingual nerve was found to be damaged. Which of the following statements is correct?","options":["Taste sensation of posterior one-third of tongue is lost.","Taste sensation of anterior two-thirds of tongue is lost.","General sensation of posterior one-third of tongue is lost.","General sensation of anterior two-thirds of tongue is lost."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. General sensation of anterior two-thirds of tongue is lost.\\n\\nGeneral sensation from the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue is carried by the\xa0lingual\xa0nerve, which is injured in this case.\\n\\nIn the anterior two-thirds of the tongue,\\n\\n* General sensation is carried by the lingual nerve.\\n\\n* Taste sensation is carried by chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve.\\n\\nIn the posterior one-third of the tongue,\\n\\n* Both general and taste sensations are carried by the glossopharyngeal\xa0nerve.\\n\\nValleculae is supplied by the internal laryngeal branch of the vagus nerve.\\n\\nPearl PF2110: Sensory Innervation of Tongue\\n\\nArea\\n\\nGeneral sensory\\n\\nSpecial sensory\\n\\nAnterior 2/3rds\\n\\nLingual nerve (CN V)\\n\\nChorda tympani (CN VII)\\n\\nPosterior 1/3rd\\n\\nGlossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)\\n\\nGlossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)\\n\\nPosterior most tongue (vallecullae, epiglottis)\\n\\nInternal laryngeal nerve (CN X)\\n\\nInternal laryngeal nerve (CN X)","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a3c4b0fd7f75b6f30c15.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/ba61ed8874c8ee2e8c5f.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-2d0aa45360","n":82,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Fast vibration sense is mediated by ______.","options":["Merkel’s disc","Meissner\'s corpuscle","Pacinian corpuscles","Ruffini’s end organ"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pacinian corpuscles\\n\\nFast vibration sense is mediated by Pacinian corpuscles.\\n\\nPacinian corpuscles\xa0are the largest cutaneous mechanoreceptors. They are present in the\xa0dermis\xa0of\xa0glabrous\xa0and\xa0hairy skin. They are composed of a nerve ending that is encapsulated by concentric layers of connective tissue, giving them an\xa0onion-like appearance.\xa0The sensory nerves from cutaneous mechanoreceptors are myelinated Aα and Aβ fibers.\xa0These receptors respond to\xa0fast vibration\xa0and\xa0deep pressure\xa0and\xa0adapt\xa0very\xa0rapidly\\n\\nOption A: Merkel\'s disc responsible for sensing sustained touch\xa0and pressure\\n\\nOption B: Meissner\'s corpuscles respond to texture and\xa0slow vibration. They are present in dermal papillae\xa0and are used in 2-point discrimination.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Ruffini endings sense skin stretch and fluttering vibration.\\n\\nPearl PF1473: Sensory Receptors\\n\\nPearl PF0517: Slow vs rapidly adapting receptors\\n\\nSlow adapting\\nRapidly adapting receptors\\n\\nAlso called tonic receptors\\nAlso called phasic receptors\\n\\nThey are expanded on free nerve endings.\\nThey are encapsulated nerve endings.\\n\\nResponds to steady stimulus\\nRespond to rate of change of stimulus\\n\\nWith steady stimulus, tonic receptors keep responding steadily.\\nWith steady stimulus, receptor responds initially but stops or adapts rapidly.\\n\\nExamples: Merkel\'s disc, Ruffini\'s end organs, Joint capsule, Muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organs\\nExamples: Pacinian corpuscle, Meissner\'s corpuscles, Hair end organs","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/3101b76ee4fcd61096b0.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-06f3e9556e","n":83,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Identify the correct type of facial nerve palsy in the following patients based on their clinical features: Patient 1 - Deviation of angle of mouth to left, eyelids completely closed and wrinkling of the forehead. Patient 2 - Deviation of angle of mouth to left, the right eyelid remains partially open, and no wrinkling of the forehead.","options":["A-Right LMN facial nerve palsy B-Left UMN facial nerve palsy","A-Right UMN facial nerve palsy B-Right LMN facial nerve palsy","A-Left UMN facial nerve palsy B-Right LMN facial nerve palsy","A-Left LMN facial nerve palsy B-Right UMN facial nerve palsy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. A-Left UMN facial nerve palsy B-Right LMN facial nerve palsy\\n\\nPatient 1 has left UMN facial nerve palsy, and patient 2 has right LMN facial nerve palsy.\\n\\nIn an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion, wrinkling of the forehead and eyelid closure remain intact because the upper facial muscles receive bilateral cortical innervation. In this case, the right side of the face is affected, as evidenced by deviation of the angle of the mouth towards the left (normal) side - a key indicator of facial palsy, since the mouth always deviates towards the normal side due to loss of muscle tone on the affected side.\\n\\nThe first-order neurons originate from the left cerebral hemisphere, cross over to the right facial nerve nucleus in the pons, and synapse there. From this nucleus, second-order neurons of the right facial nerve descend to supply the right side of the face. Hence, a left UMN lesion causes paralysis of the lower half of the face on the right side, while forehead movement is preserved.\\n\\nIn contrast, a lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion affects both the upper and lower facial muscles on the same (ipsilateral) side, leading to loss of forehead wrinkling, incomplete eye closure, and drooping of the mouth. In this patient, since the mouth deviates towards the left, it indicates a right LMN facial nerve palsy.\\n\\nFeatures of facial nerve palsy:\\n\\nFeature\\nUMN\\nLMN\\n\\nSide affected\\nContralateral\\nIpsilateral\\n\\nFacial muscles involved\\nLower\\nBoth upper and lower\\n\\nForehead wrinkling\\nPresent\\nAbsent\\n\\nEmotional incontinence\\nPresent\\nAbsent\\n\\nCorneal reflex\\nNormal\\nAbsent\\n\\nTaste\\nIntact\\nAffected\\n\\nAtrophy of facial muscles\\nAbsent\\nPresent","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0a5ba2c86c1265ffd22d.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-cec252e481","n":84,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"In ORS, sodium is given along with glucose to facilitate___?","options":["Co-transport","Osmosis","Facilitated diffusion","Primary active transport"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Co-transport\\n\\nIn ORS, sodium is given along with glucose to facilitate\xa0co-transport.\\n\\nGlucose co transported with sodium is an example of Secondary Active transport.\\n\\nThe glucose-dependent sodium and water absorption is the principle behind replacing glucose and sodium in a 1:1 molar ratio in the WHO oral rehydration solution (ORS). The low osmolarity of the ORS in the lumen as compared to the blood facilitate absorption of fluid and hence the electrolytes due to the solvent drag.\\n\\nThe\xa0low-osmolality\xa0World Health Organization (WHO) oral rehydration solution (ORS) is now the global\xa0standard of care\xa0in the management of acute gastroenteritis.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nTypes of transport:\\n\\n* Active transport:\\n\\n* Primary\\n\\n* Energy is derived from the transporter itself. Eg Na+ K+ ATPase pump.\\n\\n* Secondary:\\n\\n* Energy is derived indirectly. Eg. SGLT – Sodium linked glucose transport\\n\\n* Passive transport\\n\\n* Simple:\\n\\n* No energy is required.\\n\\n* No carrier molecule is involved.\\n\\n* Eg. O2 exchange in alveoli.\\n\\n* Facilitated:\\n\\n* No energy is required.\\n\\n* Carrier molecule involved.\\n\\n* Eg. Glucose transport by GLUT transporters.\\n\\nPearl PF0069: Composition of reduced osmolarity Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS)\\n\\nThe composition of reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration solution (ORS) is as follows:\\n\\nReduced- osmolarity ORS\\nmmol/L\\n\\nSodium\\n75\\n\\nChloride\\n65\\n\\nGlucose (anhydrous)\\n75\\n\\nPotassium\\n20\\n\\nCitrate\\n10\\n\\nTotal Osmolarity\\n245","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ab1d41c395","n":85,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Oxytocin plays a role in ______.","options":["milk production","milk ejection","milk storage","osmoregulation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. milk ejection\\n\\nOxytocin plays a role in milk ejection.\\n\\nIt stimulates postpartum milk let-down in response to suckling.\\n\\nProlactin is the hormone responsible for milk production.\xa0Osmoregulation is mediated by hormones such as\xa0ADH, Aldosterone, etc.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0c153703e8","n":86,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Rabies virus uses which molecular motor to cause involvement of the central nervous system from the site of the host?","options":["Kinesin","Dynein","Actin","Myosin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Dynein\\n\\nRabies virus uses\xa0dyenin for transport from site of inoculation to the central Nervous system.\\n\\nThe rabies virus P protein\xa0which is involved in viral transcription and replication binds with the LC8 chain of dynein molecule for retrograde transport of the virus to the CNS.\\n\\nAXONAL TRANSPORT\\n\\n* Protein synthesis occurs in the cell body of neuron and proteins and polypeptides are transported to axonal endings by the axoplasmic flow.\\n\\n* Axonal transport can be Orthograde or Retrograde.\\n\\n* Orthograde transport\\n\\n* Can be either fast or slow axonal transport and it occurs along microtubules that run along the length of the axon away from the cell body.\\n\\n* Fast axonal transport occurs at about\xa0400 mm/day, and slow axonal transport occurs at\xa00.5 to 10 mm/day.\\n\\n* Molecular motor involved in\xa0orthograde\xa0transport is\xa0kinesin.\\n\\n* Retrograde transport,\\n\\n* In the opposite direction (from the nerve ending to the cell body), occurs along microtubules at about\xa0200 mm/day.\xa0Synaptic vesicles, nerve growth factor (NGF) and some viruses\xa0(Eg: herpes simplex, rabies, and polio)\xa0are transported back to the cell body.\\n\\n* Molecular motor involed in\xa0retrograde\xa0transport is\xa0dyenin.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e40cdc8967","n":87,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"The major thyroxine-binding protein is _____.","options":["Follistatin","Transthyretin","Thyroxine-binding globulin","Transferrin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Thyroxine-binding globulin\\n\\nThyroxine-binding globulin (TBG)\xa0is the major protein that binds to thyroxine and tri-iodothyronine.\\n\\nThyroid hormones bind to transthyretin and albumin to a much lesser extent than TBG.\\n\\nHormones and their Binding Proteins\\n\\n* T4\xa0 and T3\xa0\xa0bind to thyroxine-binding globulin\xa0 (TBG),\xa0albumin, and thyroxine-binding pre-albumin (TBPA)\\n\\n* Cortisol\xa0binds to cortisol-binding globulin (CBG);\\n\\n* Androgen and estrogen\xa0bind to sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG);\\n\\n* IGF-I and -II\xa0bind to multiple IGF-binding proteins (IGFBPs);\\n\\n* GH\xa0interacts with GH-binding protein (GHBP), a circulating fragment of the GH receptor extracellular domain;\\n\\n* Activin\xa0binds to\xa0follistatin.\\n\\nPearl PF1547: Physiologic effects of thyroid hormone\\n\\nTarget Tissue\\n\\nEffect\\n\\nMechanism\\n\\nHeart\\n\\nChronotropic and inotropic\\n\\nIncreased number of β-adrenergic receptors\\n\\nEnhanced responses to circulating catecholamines\\n\\nIncreased proportion of α-myosin heavy chain (with higher ATPase activity)\\n\\nAdipose tissue\\n\\nCatabolic\\n\\nStimulated lipolysis\\n\\nMuscle\\n\\nCatabolic\\n\\nIncreased protein breakdown\\n\\nBone\\n\\nDevelopmental\\n\\nPromote normal growth and skeletal development\\n\\nNervous system\\n\\nDevelopmental\\n\\nPromote normal brain development\\n\\nGut\\n\\nMetabolic\\n\\nIncreased rate of carbohydrate absorption\\n\\nLipoprotein\\n\\nMetabolic\\n\\nFormation of LDL receptors\\n\\nOther\\n\\nCalorigenic\\n\\nStimulated oxygen consumption by metabolically active tissues (exceptions: testes, uterus, lymph nodes, spleen, anterior pituitary)\\n\\nIncreased metabolic rate","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bb2085719c","n":88,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Which of the following is the correct sequence of muscles used in ejaculation?","options":["Ischiocavernosus contraction-External urethral sphincter relaxation- Bulbospongiosus contraction","Ischiocavernosus relaxation-External urethral sphincter contraction-Bulbospongiosus contraction","Ischiocavernosus relaxation-External urethral sphincter contraction-Bulbospongiosus relaxation","Ischiocavernosus contraction-External urethral sphincter relaxation- Bulbospongiosus relaxation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Ischiocavernosus contraction-External urethral sphincter relaxation- Bulbospongiosus contraction\\n\\nThe correct sequence of muscles used are\xa0Ischiocavernosus contraction-External urethral sphincter relaxation- Bulbospongiosus contraction.\\n\\nThere are 2 stages involved in this process - emission of the sperms into the prostatic urethra and ejaculation.\\n\\n1.Emission:\\n\\n* The sperm produced in the testes is brought into the urethra with\xa0contraction of the vas deferens and the ampulla and reach the seminal vesicles.\\n\\n* Then contractions of the muscular coat of the prostate gland followed by contraction of the seminal vesicles expel prostatic and seminal fluid also into the urethra, forcing the sperm forward.\\n\\n* All these fluids mix in the internal urethra with mucus already secreted by thebulbourethral glands to form the semen.\\n\\n2.Ejaculation:\\n\\n* The filling of the internal urethra with semen elicits sensory signals that are transmitted through the pudendal nerves to the sacral regions of the cord, giving\\nthe feeling of sudden fullness in the internal genital organs.\\n\\n* These sensory signals further exciter rhythmic contraction of the internal genital organs and cause contraction of the ischiocavernosus to further move the semen into the prostatic urethra.\\n\\n* Retrograde ejaculation is prevented by the contraction of the internal urethral sphincter and relaxation of external urethral sphincter to help push the semen into the spongy urethra.\\n\\n* The bulbocavernosus muscles\xa0contracts and compress the bases of the penile erectile tissue and cause rhythmic wave like increases in pressure in the erectile tissue of the penis and the genital ducts and urethra, which move the semen from the urethra to the exterior which is called ejaculation.\\n\\nAn image showing the muscles involved is shown below:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/e6a04785be0f01abe666.png"}]},{"bankId":"qb-12259d51d2","n":89,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Aug 2020","text":"Which of the following statements is true about renal tubular transport?","options":["Maximum reabsorption in distal tubule","Urea is absorbed 100% in PCT","H+ is absorbed in proximal tubule","Amino acid is reabsorbed in proximal tubule"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Amino acid is reabsorbed in proximal tubule\\n\\nGlucose and amino acids(proteins)\xa0are completely reabsorbed in the proximal tubule.\\n\\nOption A: Maximum reabsorption occurs in the proximal tubule\\n\\nOption B: Urea is reabsorbed and secreted. It is reabsorbed in PCT and in the collecting duct. It is secreted in the thin ascending loop of Henle.\\n\\nOption C: H+\xa0is secreted in PCT as well as in collecting ducts.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9ae82d1e07dcf21602bc.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-04cb3c5a19","n":90,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A 32-year-old traveler was lost in the desert. He was found after 2 days. He says that he has not consumed food or water for more than 24 hours. His vitals were normal. Which of the following part of the kidney must have been the site of maximum water reabsorption in this case?","options":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Proximal convoluted tubule\\n\\nThe proximal convoluted tubule is the site of maximum water reabsorption about 67% of the filtered water.\\n\\nThe driving force for water reabsorption is a transtubular osmotic gradient established by reabsorption of solutes.\\n\\nBased on the ultrastructure proximal tubule is divided into three segments:\\n\\n* S1 segment: 1st half of PCT\\n\\n* S2 segment: 2nd half of PCT and 1st half of proximal straight tubule\\n\\n* S3 segment: 2nd half of proximal straight tubule\\n\\nPearl PF1348: Water transport along nephron\\n\\nSegment\\n\\nMechanism of Water Reabsorption\\n\\nProximal tubule(67%)\\n\\nOsmosis, AQ 1\\n\\nLoop of Henle (15%)\\n\\nDescending thin limb only; aquaporin 1 channels\\n\\nEarly distal convoluted tubule(0%)\\n\\nNo water reabsorption\\n\\nLate distal tubule and collecting duct (8-17%)\\n\\nPassive(aquaporin-2) under the influence of ADH\\n\\nPearl PF0563: Summary of Transport Across Proximal and Distal Portions of the Nephron\\n\\nProximal Tubule\\n\\nReabsorption\\n\\nSecretion\\n\\n67% of filtered Na+ actively reabsorbed, not subject to control; Cl– follows passively\\n\\nAll filtered glucose and amino acids reabsorbed by secondary active transport; not subject to control\\n\\nVariable amounts of filtered PO43- and other electrolytes reabsorbed; subject to control\\n\\n65% of filtered H2O osmotically reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\n50% of filtered urea passively reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\nAlmost all filtered K+ reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\nVariable H+ secretion, depending on acid-base status of body\\n\\nOrganic ion secretion; not subject to control\\n\\nDistal tubule\\n\\nReabsorption\\nSecretion\\n\\nVariable Na+ reabsorption, controlled by aldosterone; Cl– follows passively\\n\\nVariable H2O reabsorption, controlled by vasopressin\\n\\nVariable H+ secretion, depending on acid-base status of body\\n\\nVariable K+ secretion, controlled by aldosterone","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/202fa76817a3a8741663.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-91e56aea1f","n":91,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A 65-year-old female patient presents to the OPD with complaints of shortness of breath and tiredness. There is no history of reactive airway disease or heart diseases previously. She has a history of breast cancer for which breast conservation surgery and chemoradiation were done. On examination, there were engorged neck veins and JVP showed rapid x and y descent and Chest Xray is as below.Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Constrictive pericarditis","Cardiac tamponade","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Restricted cardiomyopathy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Constrictive pericarditis\\n\\nThe clinical features and the JVP findings of rapid X and Y descent\xa0 are suggestive of constrictive pericarditis.\\n\\nConstrictive pericarditis:\\n\\n* It is the condition where a thickened, fibrotic, and often calcified pericardium restricts the diastolic filling of the heart.\\n\\n* In developing countries, infectious etiologies like tuberculosis or viral infections are the most common cause of this condition.\\n\\n* Cardiac surgery and radiation therapy are also common causes.\\n\\n* Other causes include connective tissue disease, uremia, neoplasm, or idiopathic.\\n\\n* Clinically the patients present with dyspnea, orthopnea, and fatigue.\\n\\n* JVP findings - There is a\xa0rapid \'x\' and \'y\' descent. The rapid \'y\' descent is more prominent and reflects rapid early ventricular diastolic filling.\xa0This occurs due to the constricting pericardium, increasing the atrial pressures.\\n\\nDiagnosis:\\n\\n* Diagnosis is difficult and is confirmed by multiple investigations\\n\\n* Hallmark findings of pericardial constriction are pericardial thickening, calcification, and abnormal ventricular diastolic function.\\n\\n* The X-ray can be normal or there may be non-specific cardiac enlargement, calcification, and roughened cardiac outline.\\n\\n* Echo can assess the hemodynamic function but it is accurate in identifying pericardial thickening.\\n\\n* CT and MRI are more sensitive investigations.\\n\\n* CT has an edge over MRI as it can detect calcification associated with this condition.\\n\\n* Pericardial thickness of > 4mm along with clinical features of constriction is highly suggestive of this diagnosis.\\n\\n* Cardiac MR may show diastolic septal bounce.\\n\\n* Hepatomegaly and ascites can be seen associated with this condition.\\n\\nConstrictive pericarditis will have prominent y descent, prominent x descent, and Kussmaul\'s sign. Normally, the venous pressure should fall by at least 3 mmHg with inspiration. Kussmaul’s sign is defined by either a rise or a lack of fall of the JVP with inspiration.\\n\\nOther important conditions and their JVP tracings are\\n\\n* In Cardiac tamponade, the jugular venous pressure (JVP) tracing of the patient will have\xa0a\xa0prominent x descent but an absent y descent.\\n\\n* Atrioventricular block will have cannon a waves\\n\\n* Atrial fibrillation will have absent a wave\\n\\n* Tricuspid regurgitation will have accentuated v wave with rapid y descent\\n\\n* Tricuspid stenosis will have prolonged y wave\\n\\nPearl PF0262: JVP waveform and the abnormalities\\n\\n1) The a-wave corresponds to atrial contraction.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProminent a-waves\\nPulmonary hypertension, right atrial thrombus/ mass, and tricuspid stenosis\\n\\nCannon a-waves\\n\\nComplete AV block, ventricular tachycardia, and premature atrial/ junctional/ ventricular beats\\n\\nAbsent a-waves\\nAtrial fibrillation\\n\\n2) The c-wave corresponds to the bulging of tricuspid valve into right atrium during ventricular contraction.\\n\\n3) The x-descent corresponds to atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nAbsent x-descent\\nSevere tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nRapid x-descent\\nCardiac tamponade\\n\\n4) The v-wave corresponds to the venous filling of right atrium.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nDominant v-waves\\nTricuspid regurgitation\\n\\n5) The y-descent corresponds to the\xa0passive emptying of the right atrium into right ventricle.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProlonged y-descent\\nTricuspid stenosis\\n\\nRapid y-descent\\n\\nConstrictive pericarditis and isolated severe tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nAbsent y-descent\\nCardiac tamponade","img":false,"images":["/bimg/42e57338d6c193435733.webp"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/c2e18e9aafa0c2c276c8.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/4b67330931e365caa974.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/b20f84ecccc0884e7460.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9c81c7c78c380c90261c.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/fb30bea9e90eb31522c5.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/754ffb0aba0cd38091d7.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/541059646151c412833d.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-58da33ea00","n":92,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A diabetic patient walks into your clinic and does \\"namaste\\". The image is given below. What could be the probable cause?","options":["Flexor tenosynovitis","Cheiroarthropathy","Dupuytrens contracture","Ankyloses"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cheiroarthropathy\\n\\nThe image shows the namaste or prayer sign\xa0where\xa0the patient is unable to touch the palmer aspects of both hands and fingers together.\xa0The given scenario is suggestive of diabetic cheiroarthropathy.\\n\\nDiabetic cheiroarthropathy/diabetic stiff hand syndrome\xa0is due to limited joint mobility as a result of the periarticular fibrosing syndrome. This is due to the non-enzymatic glycosylation of collagen. It is seen in both diabetes type 1 and type 2. Its prevalence increases with age and poor glycemic control.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nFlexor tenosynovitis or trigger finger occurs due to thickening and fibrosis of the tendon sheath.\\n\\nDupuytren\'s contracture is a result of fibrosis of palmar fascia and flexor tendons.\\n\\nNote: Diabetes is also a known risk factor for Dupuytren\'s contracture but this condition leads to palmar nodules and flexion deformity of fingers. The image below shows Dupuytren\'s contracture:\\n\\nAnkylosis results in limited joint mobility due to joint fusion as seen in osteoarthritis.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/08cac5930036ff110747.webp"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/b05120da0ca79f2cca51.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/4a4b1baa62b6b1c6c0db.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/ed6e4c222df3ef8b6c79.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-b825b5a6fb","n":93,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"A patient presents to the ER with a history of a road traffic accident. On examination his vitals are stable, but he could not speak. He had no other neurological findings, had normal handwriting skills, and could understand what was being asked of him to which he responds by nodding. Where could be the lesion for this clinical condition?","options":["Broca\'s area","Wernicke\'s area","Primary motor cortex","Brainstem"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Broca\'s area\\n\\nBroca’s area is concerned with word formation\xa0and is the most likely area to be involved as only speech production is affected here and\xa0all other examinating findings are normal.\\n\\nBroca’s area is in the inferior frontal lobe.\\n\\nBroca area processes the information received from Wernicke area into a detailed and coordinated pattern for vocalization and then projects the pattern via a speech articulation area in the insula to the motor cortex. This then initiates the appropriate movements of the lips, tongue, and larynx to produce speech.\\n\\nThe probable sequence of events that occurs when a subject names a visual object is shown in the figure and flowchart.\\n\\nPearl PF0236: Brodmann areas\\n\\nArea of the Brain(Brodmann)\\nLocation\\n\\n3, 1 and 2 -\xa0Primary sensory area\\n\\nPostcentral gyrus\\n\\nExtends to the posterior part of the paracentral lobule medially\\n\\n4 -\xa0Primary motor area\\n\\nPrecentral gyrus\\n\\nExtends to the anterior part of the paracentral lobule medially\\n\\n6-\xa0Premotor area\\n\\nAnterior to Area 4\\n\\nIn the posterior parts of superior, middle and inferior frontal gyri\\n\\n5 and 7 -\xa0Sensory association area\\nSuperior parietal lobule\\n\\n8 -\xa0Frontal eye field\\nPosterior part of middle frontal gyrus, anterior to the facial area of the precentral gyrus\\n\\n44 and\xa045 -\xa0Broca’s(Motor speech) area\\nInferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere\\n\\n41 and 42 -\\n\\nPrimary auditory area\\n\\nSuperior surface of the superior temporal gyrus(Heschl’s gyrus)\\n\\n22* -\xa0Wernicke’s(Sensory speech) area\\nSuperior temporal gyrus\\n\\n17 -\xa0Primary visual(striate) area\\nWalls and floor of the posterior part of calcarine sulcus (postcalcarine sulcus) in the occipital lobe\\n\\n18 and\xa019-\\n\\nVisual association area\\n\\nRemaining part of the medial and superolateral surfaces of the occipital lobe\\n\\n28 -\xa0Primary olfactory area\\nAnterior part of the parahippocampal gyrus and uncus\\n\\n43 -\xa0Gustatory(taste) area\\nInferior part of the parietal lobe, posterior to the general sensory area of the mouth\\n\\n*There is uncertainty about the extent of the Wernicke\'s area. Some authorities consider areas 39 and 40 as part of Wernicke\'s area in addition to area 22.\\n\\nPearl PF0027: Aphasias and Related Conditions\\n\\nWernicke\'s\\nBroca\'s\\nGlobal\\nConduction\\nAnterior (nonfluent) transcortical\\nPosterior (fluent) transcortical\\nIsolation\\nAnomic\\nPure Word Deafness\\nPure Alexia\\n\\nComprehension\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nImpaired only for spoken language\\nImpaired only for reading\\n\\nRepetition of Spoken language\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nPreserved\\nEcholalia\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\n\\nNaming\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nPreserved\\n\\nFluency\\nPreserved\\nDecreased\\nDecreased\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nNo purposeful speech\\nPreserved except for word finding pauses\\nPreserved\\nPreserved","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/77dc0c8a91fbe3925ef9.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/43bece79db86db34a6af.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/cf373fa8172cf444ff64.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/6456638725ec6ea01b33.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d61e328e91ee65404aa1.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-48c6046926","n":94,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"Identify this movement that takes place in the intestine?","options":["Segmented movement","Peristalsis","Intersegmental contraction","Diffusion"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Peristalsis\\n\\nThe given image is of peristalsis.\\n\\nPeristalsis is the propulsive movement in GI tract, starting in esophagus and ends in rectum. It follows the law of gut- forward directed towards the rectum, because of intact myenteric plexus.\\n\\nIt begins by the local stretch produced by the food and the wave of contraction propels forward at 2-25cm/s speed. It is independent of external innervation, but autonomic activity may increase or decrease the activity.\\n\\nThe interstitial cells of Cajal, acts as pacemakers of GI tract,They maintain a basal electric rhythm (BER). It doesn’t cause contraction on its own but maintains a muscle tension. It’s function is to coordinate the various motility of GI tract. For example, in the absence of BER, peristalsis waves in the stomach will be irregular and chaotic.\\n\\nPeristalsis:\\n\\nFrom esophagus to rectum.\\n\\nFunction - Propels food forward\\n\\nStimulus - Stretch of food\\n\\nSpeed-2-25cm/s\\n\\nRegulation- sustains by self, autonomic system and interstitial cells of cajal fine tune it.\\n\\nPearl PF0272: Types of gastrointestinal motility\\n\\nPearl PF0557: Mechanism of peristalsis","img":false,"images":["/bimg/bea9749317ee9542ddf3.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/760d26364c218b8071e1.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d4542f64a7b42c1bdda2.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/eb6c57797fe362221794.png"}]},{"bankId":"qb-b25191eeca","n":95,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"In the cardiac muscle, the inhibition of Na+K+ATPase results in __?","options":["Hyperpolarisation and then Depolarisation","Depolarisation","Hyperpolarisation","Repolarisation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Depolarisation\\n\\nThe inhibition of Na+K+-ATPase results in depolarisation.\\n\\nAn energy (ATP)-dependent pump system (Na+/K+-ATPase), is located on the sarcolemma, which pumps Na+ out and K+ into the cell.The Na+/K+-ATPase pump is electrogenic because it extrudes three Na+ for every two K+ entering the cell. By pumping more positive charges out of the cell than into it, the pump creates a negative potential within the cell. This electrogenic potential may be up to −10 mV.\\n\\nIf this pump\xa0stops working (such as when ATP is lost under hypoxic conditions), or if the activity of the pump is inhibited by cardiac glycosides such as digoxin, Na+ accumulates within the cell and intracellular K+ falls,resulting in a less negative (more depolarized) resting membrane potential.\\n\\nInhibition of this pump, therefore, causes depolarization resulting from changes in Na+ and K+ concentration gradients and from the loss of an electrogenic component of the membrane potential.\\n\\nAction potential of myocardium:\\n\\n* Phase 0:\xa0Depolarisation: Influx of Na+ into myocyte.\\n\\n* Phase 1:\xa0Early repolarization: Efflux of\xa0K+ from myocyte.\\n\\n* Phase 2:\xa0Plateau:\xa0Ca2+ enters myocardial cells via voltage regulated L-type Ca2+ channels.\\n\\n* Phase 3: Repolarisation:\xa0Efflux of K+.\\n\\n* Phase 4: Restoration of RMP:\xa0Restoration of Na+ and K+ by Na-K ATPase.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/cdaf7447a3daaeb13e1b.gif"}]},{"bankId":"qb-7d6d1083e1","n":96,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"In the following LV pressure-volume loop diagram, point C denotes ______","options":["Mitral valve closes","Aortic valve opens","Aortic valve closes","Mitral valve opens"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Aortic valve opens\\n\\nThe aortic valve will open at point C.\\n\\nThe volume-pressure curve represents the changes in volume and pressure in the left ventricle during a single cardiac cycle. The relationship between ventricular pressure (Y-axis) and volume (X-axis) throughout the cardiac cycle is represented by this loop.\\n\\n* A - Mitral valve opens\\n\\n* B - Mitral valve closes\\n\\n* C - Aortic valve opens\\n\\n* D - Aortic valve closes\\n\\nPhase I: Period of filling\\n\\nIt begins at a ventricular volume of about 50 ml (end-systolic volume) and a diastolic pressure of 2 to 3 mm Hg. As venous blood flows into the ventricle from the left atrium, the ventricular volume normally increases to about 120 ml, called the end-diastolic volume and the diastolic pressure rising to about 5 to 7 mm Hg.\\n\\nPhase II: Period of isovolumic contraction\\n\\nDuring isovolumic contraction, the volume of the ventricle does not change because all valves are closed. However, the pressure inside the ventricle increases to equal the pressure in the aorta (80 mm Hg) as depicted by point C.\\n\\nPhase III: Period of ejection\\n\\nDuring ejection, the systolic pressure rises even higher because of still more contraction of the ventricle. At the same time, the volume of the ventricle decreases because the aortic valve has now opened and blood flows out of the ventricle into the aorta.\\n\\nPhase IV: Period of isovolumic relaxation\\n\\nAt the end of the period of ejection (point D), the aortic valve closes and the ventricular pressure falls back to the diastolic pressure level. The line labeled “IV” traces this decrease in intraventricular pressure without any change in volume. Thus, the ventricle returns to its starting point, with about 50 ml of blood left in the ventricle and at an atrial pressure of 2 to 3 mm Hg.\\n\\nThe area subtended by this functional volume-pressure diagram (the shaded area, labeled “EW”) represents the net external work output of the ventricle during its contraction cycle.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/512a820f343278d3e18d.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d08d99de89b81bb7781b.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-f9fd462371","n":97,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"Lysosomal enzymes are present in___?","options":["Cytosol","Mitochondria","Endoplasmic reticulum","Nucleus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cytosol\\n\\nLysosomal enzymes (hydrolytic enzymes) are present in the lysosome, which is located in the cytoplasm of the cell. In the given options, cytosol is the best choice.\\n\\nThe interior of lysosomes is more acidic than the rest of the cytoplasm, and external material such as endocytosed bacteria, as well as worn-out cell components, is digested in them. The interior is kept acidic by the action of a proton pump, or H+ ATPase, which uses the energy of ATP to move protons from the cytosol up their electrochemical gradient and keep the lysosome relatively acidic, near pH 5.0.\\n\\nLysosomes contain over 40 types of hydrolytic enzymes, which are all acid hydrolases, and they function best at the acidic pH of the lysosomal compartment. This can be a safety feature; if the lysosomes were to break open and release their contents, the enzymes would not be efficient at the near-neutral cytosolic pH of 7.2 and thus would not be able to digest cytosolic targets they may encounter.\\n\\nWhen a lysosomal enzyme is congenitally absent, the lysosomes become engorged with the material the enzyme normally degrades and lead to lysosomal storage diseases. e.g., Fabry disease caused by a deficiency in α-galactosidase, Gaucher disease\xa0caused by a deficiency in glucocerebrosidase,\xa0and\xa0Tay-Sachs disease caused by the loss of hexosaminidase A.\\n\\nPearl PF1557: Lysosomal storage diseases\\n\\nLysosomal Storage Diseases\\nEnzyme-deficient\\n\\nSphingolipidoses\\n\\nGaucher disease\\nGlucocerebrosidase\\n\\nTay-Sachs disease\\nHexosaminidase A\\n\\nFabry disease\\nα-galactosidase A\\n\\nKrabbe disease\\n\\nGalactocerebrosidase\\n\\n(Galactosylceramidase)\\n\\nNiemann-Pick disease\\nSphingomyelinase\\n\\nMucopolysaccharidoses\\n\\nHurler syndrome\\nα-L-iduronidase\\n\\nHunter syndrome\\nIduronate-2-sulfatase\\n\\nOthers:\\n\\nPompe disease\\nLysosomal acid\xa0α-1,4- glucosidase","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0032496894","n":98,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2020","text":"Which murmur is depicted in the image below?","options":["End diastolic","Pan systolic","Ejection systolic","Mid diastolic"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ejection systolic\\n\\nThe image given above is of an ejection systolic murmur.\\n\\nThis murmur starts after S1, increases in intensity to reach the peak, and ends before A2.\\n\\nEjection systolic murmur(ESM) is most commonly heard in aortic stenosis and in pulmonary stenosis. They are differentiated based on the difference in symptoms, site of murmur, etc.\\n\\nAuscultation of ESM in aortic stenosis.\\n\\n* In the early stages, an\xa0early systolic ejection sound may be heard. It disappears when the valve becomes calcified and rigid.\\n\\n* As the stenosis worsens, the LV systole gets prolonged and there is delayed closure of the aortic valve. This leads to the\xa0narrowing of S2 sound, and in severe cases, it might lead to the\xa0paradoxical splitting of S2.\\n\\n* S4 and S3 sounds may be heard in severe cases when the diastolic and systolic functions of the left ventricle have been reduced.\\n\\n* Murmur: The murmur in aortic stenosis is an ejection systolic murmur. The murmur radiates to the neck and base of the heart. It is augmented by squatting and reduced by the strain of the Valsalva maneuver.\\n\\nPearl PF0028: Dynamic auscultation of murmurs\\n\\nBedside Manoeuvre\\nEffect\\n\\nInspiration (↑venous return to right atrium)\\n↑ intensity of right sided sounds\\n\\nHand grip (↑afterload )\\n↑ intensity of MR, AR and VSD murmurs\\n↓ hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and AS murmurs\\nMVP: delayed onset of click / murmur\\n\\nValsalva-Phase II, standing up (↓ preload)\\n↓ intensity of most murmurs (including AS)\\n↑\xa0intensity of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy murmur\\nMVP: earlier onset of click /murmur\\n\\nRapid squatting (↑venous return,↑preload,\\n↑ afterload)\\n↓intensity of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy murmur\\n↑\xa0intensity of AS, MR and VSD murmurs\\nMVP: later onset of click / murmur","img":false,"images":["/bimg/3ffd79b88f92a3fc6232.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-4804612290","n":99,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"A patient presents with symptoms as shown in the image below: 1) shows the site of the lesion, 2) has loss of proprioception, vibration, and discriminative touch on the same side, and 3) has loss of pain and temperature beginning one or two segments below the side of the lesion on the contralateral side. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Weber\'s syndrome","Dejerine syndrome","Brown Sequard syndrome","Wallenberg syndrome"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Brown Sequard syndrome\\n\\nThe image along with the features above is diagnostic of\xa0Brown Sequard syndrome.\\n\\nHemisection of the spinal\xa0cord causes damage to ascending sensory (dorsal column pathway, ventrolateral spinothalamic tract) and descending motor (corticospinal tract) pathways, which is called the\xa0Brown-S\xe9quard syndrome.\\n\\nThe part marked as 1 is the site of the lesion, at which hemisection of the cord is seen.\\n\\nThe part marked as 2 is the ipsilateral side of the lesion, which has the following features:\\n\\n* Loss of proprioception and vibration\\n\\n* Loss of discriminative touch\\n\\n* Weakness and spastic type of paralysis in certain muscle groups\\n\\nThe part marked as 3 is the contralateral side of the lesion and exhibits the following features:\\n\\n* Loss of pain and temperature beginning one or two segments below the side of the lesion\\n\\nPearl PF0387: Brown Sequard syndrome (Hemisection of spinal cord)\\n\\nPearl PF1464: Medullary syndromes and Weber syndrome\\n\\nSyndrome\\n\\nVessel involved\\n\\nClinical features\\n\\nMedial Medullary syndrome/Dejerine syndrome\\n\\nVertebral artery/Anterior spinal artery\\n\\nC/L loss of conscious proprioception, C/L hemiplegia, I/L tongue paralysis.\\n\\nLateral Medullary syndrome\\n\\nVertebral artery>Posterior inferior cerebellar artery\\n\\nC/L loss of pain/temperature on the body, I/L loss of pain/temperature on face, Ataxia, Nystagmus, Vertigo, Horner\'s syndrome, dysphagia, Ipsilateral bulbar paralysis and loss of gag reflex.\\n\\nWeber\'s syndrome\\n\\nPosterior cerebral artery\\n\\nI/L Oculomotor nerve palsy - ptosis with fixed dilated pupil pointed down and out, C/L hemiplegia, C/L paralysis of the lower face\\n\\nC/L- Contralateral\\n\\nI/L-Ipsilateral","img":false,"images":["/bimg/6ad457b115edb36f7e02.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/5c5274baf7424ba9e804.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-4da24eb95d","n":100,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Following complete ileal and partial jejunal resection, the patient is most likely to have","options":["Folic acid deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Gastric ulcer","Constipation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Vitamin B12 deficiency\\n\\nAfter complete ileal and partial jejunal resection, the patient is likely to have vitamin B12 deficiency.\\n\\nVitamin B12 is absorbed in the ileum. B12 binds to intrinsic factor, a protein secreted by the parietal cells of the stomach, and the complex is absorbed across the ileal mucosa. Hence after ileal resection, there is impaired absorption of vitamin B12.\\n\\nClinical features\xa0of megaloblastic anemia (cyanocobalamin and folate deficiency):\\n\\nSystem\\nClinical features\\n\\nOral\\n\\nGlossitis\\n\\nAngular cheilosis\\n\\nGastrointestinal\\n\\nAnorexia\\n\\nWeight loss\\n\\nDiarrhea\\n\\nConstipation\\n\\nSkin\\nHyperpigmentation\xa0(reversible)\\n\\nHematological\\n\\nThrombocytopenia\\n\\nLeucopenia\\n\\nIneffective erythropoiesis and unconjugated\xa0jaundice\\n\\nCardiovascular\\n\\nAtherosclerosis leading to PVD,\xa0CAD (due to elevated homocysteine)\\n\\nNeurological (only B12)\\n\\nPeripheral neuropathy\\n\\nSubacute combined degeneration (degeneration of the posterior and pyramidal tracts of the spinal cord)\\n\\nPregnancy (only folate)\\n\\nRecurrent fetal loss\\n\\nNeural tube defects\\n\\nOthers\\n\\nMild\xa0fever\xa0in severe cases\\n\\nPsychiatric disturbances\\n\\nPeripheral smear\xa0findings:\\n\\n* Macro-ovalocytes\xa0are seen.\\n\\n* Most macrocytes lack the central pallor of normal red cells and even appear hyperchromic, but the MCHC is not elevated.\\n\\n* Reticulocyte\xa0count is\xa0low.\\n\\n* Nucleated red cell progenitors occasionally appear in the circulating blood when anemia is severe.\\n\\n* Neutrophils are also larger than normal (macro polymorphonuclear) and show\xa0nuclear hypersegmentation, with five or more nuclear lobules.\\n\\nBone marrow\xa0findings:\\n\\n* Markedly\xa0hypercellular\xa0as a result of increased hematopoietic precursors\\n\\n* Sieve-like\xa0pattern of\xa0chromatin\xa0of immature precursors (Megaloblasts)\\n\\n* Granulocytic precursors display dysmaturation in the form of\xa0giant metamyelocytes\xa0and\xa0band forms\\n\\n* Megakaryocytes abnormally large and have bizarre, multilobate nuclei\\n\\n* Derangement in DNA synthesis causes most precursors to undergo apoptosis in the marrow (ineffective hematopoiesis) and leads to\xa0pancytopenia\\n\\nPearl PF1304: Major Sites of Absorption of Hematopoietic Factors\\n\\n* Duodenum + upper jejunum: Iron\\n\\n* Jejunum: Folate\\n\\n* Terminal Ileum: Vitamin B12\\n\\nMnemonic - Dude Is Just Feeling Ill Bro","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/5be7c737c81144e9f3da.png"}]},{"bankId":"qb-1c84d383ee","n":101,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Which of the following cell organelles is associated with killing bacteria and viruses?","options":["Peroxisomes","Lysosomes","Golgi complex","Ribosomes"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lysosomes\\n\\nLysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes and are associated with killing bacteria and viruses.\\n\\nLysosomes \xa0are membrane-bound organelles broken off from the Golgi apparatus, containing roughly 40 different acid hydrolases (enzymes that function best in acidic pH ≤5). These hydrolases include proteases, nucleases, lipases, glycosidases, phosphatases, and sulfatases.\\n\\nLysosomal enzymes provide an intracellular digestive system that digests damaged cellular structures, food particles ingested by the cells, and foreign particles like bacteria and viruses.\xa0The membrane around the lysosomes prevents the hydrolytic enzymes from being in contact with the other substances in the cell and prevents them from being digested.\\n\\nWhen an enzyme is congenitally absent, the cell becomes engorged with the material the enzyme normally degrades. This leads to lysosomal diseases e.g Gaucher disease is caused due to a deficiency of glucocerebrosidase.\\n\\nPearl PF1557: Lysosomal storage diseases\\n\\nLysosomal Storage Diseases\\nEnzyme-deficient\\n\\nSphingolipidoses\\n\\nGaucher disease\\nGlucocerebrosidase\\n\\nTay-Sachs disease\\nHexosaminidase A\\n\\nFabry disease\\nα-galactosidase A\\n\\nKrabbe disease\\n\\nGalactocerebrosidase\\n\\n(Galactosylceramidase)\\n\\nNiemann-Pick disease\\nSphingomyelinase\\n\\nMucopolysaccharidoses\\n\\nHurler syndrome\\nα-L-iduronidase\\n\\nHunter syndrome\\nIduronate-2-sulfatase\\n\\nOthers:\\n\\nPompe disease\\nLysosomal acid\xa0α-1,4- glucosidase","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fc141c0f3a","n":102,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Dec 2021","text":"Which of the following conditions will shift the Oxygen-Hb dissociation curve to the left?","options":["Hypoxia","Hypercarbia","Alkalosis","Increase in 2,3-DPG"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Alkalosis\\n\\nAn increase in the pH (alkalosis), shifts the O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve, to the left.\\n\\nOxygen-Hb dissociation curve (OHDC)\\n\\n* This is a plot of the partial pressure of O2 and the % saturation of Hb with O2.\\n\\n* It is normally sigmoid in shape.\\n\\n* If OHDC is shifted to the right, it means that the affinity of Hb for O2 has become less (which favors O2 delivery) i.e. Higher the P50. (R - Right shift\xa0Releases oxygen)\\n\\nShift to the right is caused by: (Conditions with an increased requirement of O2 - metabolically active tissues)\\n\\n* Hypoxia and Hypercarbia - Metabolically active tissues utilize O2 and tissues produce CO2\\n\\n* ↓ pH - Secondary to increased CO2 production\\n\\n* ↑ in temperature, heat\\n\\n* ↑ in 2,3-DPG - increased anaerobic glycolysis\\n\\n* Growth hormone, thyroid hormone, androgen - by increasing 2,3 DPG\\n\\n* Sickle cell anemia - reduced affinity for O2\\n\\nShift to the left is caused by:\\n\\n* ↑ pH (alkalosis)\\n\\n* ↓ PCO2\\n\\n* ↓ 2,3-DPG\\n\\n* ↓ Temperature\\n\\n* HbF\\n\\n* Carboxyhemoglobin","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0a382afe1de820a7d132.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-196ac9995c","n":103,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"A large \'V\' wave on jugular venous examination is seen in ______ JVP- Jugular venous pulse","options":["Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis","Atrial fibrillation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nA large \'V\' wave on JV examination is seen in tricuspid regurgitation.\\n\\nPearl PF0262: JVP waveform and the abnormalities\\n\\n1) The a-wave corresponds to atrial contraction.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProminent a-waves\\nPulmonary hypertension, right atrial thrombus/ mass, and tricuspid stenosis\\n\\nCannon a-waves\\n\\nComplete AV block, ventricular tachycardia, and premature atrial/ junctional/ ventricular beats\\n\\nAbsent a-waves\\nAtrial fibrillation\\n\\n2) The c-wave corresponds to the bulging of tricuspid valve into right atrium during ventricular contraction.\\n\\n3) The x-descent corresponds to atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nAbsent x-descent\\nSevere tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nRapid x-descent\\nCardiac tamponade\\n\\n4) The v-wave corresponds to the venous filling of right atrium.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nDominant v-waves\\nTricuspid regurgitation\\n\\n5) The y-descent corresponds to the\xa0passive emptying of the right atrium into right ventricle.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProlonged y-descent\\nTricuspid stenosis\\n\\nRapid y-descent\\n\\nConstrictive pericarditis and isolated severe tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nAbsent y-descent\\nCardiac tamponade","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/541059646151c412833d.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-dd70667f78","n":104,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"A new resident to a high-altitude area develops hypoxia. What is the causative factor?","options":["Low hemoglobin levels","Low partial pressure of 02","Low blood lactate levels","High partial pressure of CO2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Low partial pressure of 02\\n\\nHypoxia in high altitude is due to the\xa0low partial pressure of O2.\\n\\nAdaptation at High Altitudes:\\n\\n* The work capacity is reduced at high altitudes and is directly proportional to the decrease in O2 uptake.\\n\\n* Immediate exposure to low PO2 at higher altitudes stimulates the arterial chemoreceptors, and this stimulation increases alveolar ventilation.\\n\\n* This immediate increase in pulmonary ventilation upon rising to a high altitude blows off large quantities of CO2, reducing the PCO2 and increasing the pH of the body fluids causing respiratory alkalosis. This raised pH inhibits the brain stem respiratory center and opposes the increased ventilation. So, respiratory alkalosis will hinder maximal exercise effort.\\n\\n* To compensate for this respiratory alkalosis, kidneys reduce hydrogen ion secretion and increase bicarbonate excretion. This reduces plasma and cerebrospinal fluid bicarbonate concentration, bringing the pH to normal. It removes the inhibitory effect of alkalosis on respiration. Hence fall in blood bicarbonate facilitates the exercise effort.\\n\\n* Lactic acid levels increase which causes extreme fatigue. When there is insufficient oxygen (at high altitudes), most of the pyruvic acid is converted into lactic acid, which diffuses out of the muscle cells into the interstitial fluid and blood.\\n\\nPearl PF1303: Acclimatization to high altitude","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/04d80571b51143b62988.png"}]},{"bankId":"qb-8d2470b247","n":105,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Hemoglobin with iron in ferric form is _______","options":["Methemoglobin","HbA","Fetal hemoglobin","HbS"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Methemoglobin\\n\\nHemoglobin with iron in ferric form is methemoglobin.\\n\\nHemoglobin structure:\\n\\n* Each consists of a tetramer of globin polypeptide chains: a pair of α-like chains\xa0and\xa0a pair of β-like chains.\\n\\n* HbA (α2β2): Major adult hemoglobin\\n\\n* HbF (α2γ2): Fetal Hemoglobin\\n\\n* HbA2 (α2δ2): Minor adult hemoglobin\\n\\n* Each globin chain enfolds a single heme moiety, consisting of a protoporphyrin IX ring complexed with a single iron atom in the ferrous state (Fe2+).\\n\\n* Each heme moiety can bind a single oxygen molecule.\\n\\n* The hemoglobin tetramer is highly soluble, but individual globin chains are insoluble.\\n\\n* Unpaired globin precipitates, forming inclusions that damage the cell and can trigger apoptosis.\\n\\nMethemoglobin is formed when hemoglobin is oxidized to contain iron in the ferric [Fe3+] rather than the normal ferrous [Fe2+] state.\xa0The presence of iron in the ferric state results shift of the oxygen-dissociation curve to the left. This shift leads to increased affinity of the ferrous iron for oxygen and thus impaired oxygen release to the tissue.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9844c933b5","n":106,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"The first heart sound coincides with which phase of the cardiac cycle?","options":["Rapid atrial filling","Aortic ejection","Isovolumetric contraction","Isovolumetric relaxation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Isovolumetric contraction\\n\\nThe first heart sound coincides with the isovolumetric contraction phase of the cardiac cycle.\\n\\nThe time between the closure of the mitral valve and the opening of the aortic valve is the isovolumic contraction period. It begins with the closure of mitral and tricuspid valve\xa0which is responsible for the first heart sound. During this period, the ventricles contract as a closed chamber till the aortic and pulmonary valves open.\\n\\nEvents of the Cardiac Cycle\\n\\nSystole\\n\\n* Isovolumetric Contraction\\nThe ventricular muscle continues to contract, even though the ventricular volume does not change, with all valves closed.\\nIt correlates with the ‘c’ wave of JVP.\\n\\n* Ventricular Ejection\\nWhen the ventricular pressures rise above aortic and pulmonary pressures the valves open, and ventricular ejection begins.\xa0Peak pressures in the left and right ventricles are about 120 and 25 mmHg, respectively.\\n\\nDiastole\\n\\n* Protodiastole\\nBefore the semilunar valve closes, even though ventricular muscle is relaxed, blood flow into great vessels is due to momentum of blood.\\n\\n* Iso-volumetric Relaxation\\nThe ventricular muscle continues to relax, even though the ventricular volume does not change, with all valves closed.\\n\\n* Ventricular Filling\\nIsovolumetric relaxation ends when the ventricular pressure falls below the atrial pressure and the AV valves open, permitting the ventricles to fill.\\n\\n* Atrial Systole\\nDuring the last third of diastole, the atria contract and give additional thrust to the inflow of blood into the ventricles. It correlates with \'a\' wave in JVP.\\n\\nPearl PF0539: Heart sounds\\n\\nHeart Sound\\n\\nFrequency\\n\\nDuration\\n\\nAssociated Event\\n\\nECG Correlation\\n\\nS1\\n\\n25-45 Hz\\n\\n0.15 sec\\n\\nClosure of AV Valves-Onset of Ventricular systole\\n\\nLater half of R wave\\n\\nS2\\n\\n50 Hz\\n\\n0.12 sec\\n\\nClosure of Semilunar Valves-Onset of ventricular diastole\\n\\nLater half of T wave\\n\\nS3\\n\\nLow\\n\\n0.1 sec\\n\\nFirst rapid filling phase of ventricular diastole\\n\\nBetween the T and P wave\\n\\nS4\\n\\nBelow 20 Hz\\n\\n0.1 sec\\n\\nLast rapid filling phase of ventricular diastole due to atrial contraction\\n\\nFollowing P wave\\n\\nPearl PF1467: Cardiac pressure - volume loop\\n\\nThe volume-pressure curve represents the changes in volume and pressure in the left ventricle during a single cardiac cycle. The relationship between ventricular pressure(Y-axis) and volume (X-axis) throughout the cardiac cycle is represented by this loop.\\n\\nPhase I: Period of filling (between A and B)\\n\\nPhase II: Period of isovolumic contraction\xa0(between B and C)\\n\\nPhase III: Period of ejection\xa0(between C and D)\\n\\nPhase IV: Period of isovolumic relaxation\xa0(between D and A)\\n\\nwhere,\\n\\n* A - Mitral valve opens\\n\\n* B - Mitral valve closes\\n\\n* C - Aortic valve opens\\n\\n* D - Aortic valve closes","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f32a442b76424c6a574f.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d08d99de89b81bb7781b.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-c5af6526bc","n":107,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Which among the following is true regarding creatinine clearance?","options":["Equal to inulin clearance","It is less than inulin clearance","It is more than inulin clearance","Equal to renal plasma flow"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. It is more than inulin clearance\\n\\nThe true statement is\xa0creatinine clearance\xa0is more than inulin clearance.\\n\\nCreatinine is partially secreted and never reabsorbed.\xa0Clearance of creatinine (CCr) can also be used to determine GFR; since creatinine is secreted by the tubules, thus the clearance of creatinine will be slightly higher than inulin.\xa0Hence, it overestimates GFR.\\n\\nInulin is completely filtered and never reabsorbed.\xa0Hence, it can be used for GFR estimation.\\n\\nPAH(para amino hippuric acid) at low plasma concentrations is completely filtered and secreted.\xa0Hence, it can be used for the estimation of renal blood flow.\\n\\nPearl PF0562: Formulae in renal physiology\\n\\nTerm\\n\\nEquation\\n\\nClearance rate\\n\\nUs \xd7V / Ps\\n\\nGlomerular filtration rate\\n\\nUinulin \xd7 V / Pinulin\\n\\nClearance ratio\\n\\nCs/Cinulin\\n\\nEffective renal plasma flow\\n\\nERPF=CPAH=UPAH \xd7V / PPAH\\n\\nRenal blood flow\\n\\nRPF / (1− Hematocrit)\\n\\nExcretion rate\\n\\nUs \xd7V\\n\\nReabsorption rate\\n\\n=Filtered load −Excretion rate\\n\\n=(GFR\xd7Ps)−(Us \xd7V)\\n\\nSecretion rate\\n\\nExcretion rate−Filtered load\\n\\nCs - clearance rate of substance “s”\\n\\nEPAH\xa0 - PAH extraction ratio\\n\\nERPF - effective renal plasma flow\\n\\nGFR - glomerular filtration rate\\n\\nP - plasma concentration\\n\\nPAH - para-aminohippuric acid\\n\\nPPAH - renal arterial PAH concentration\\n\\nRBF - renal blood flow\\n\\nRPF - renal plasma flow\\n\\nS - a substance\\n\\nU - urine concentration\\n\\nV - urine flow rate\\n\\nVPAH - renal venous PAH concentration","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a92267d3e1","n":108,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Which of the following clotting factor participates in the extrinsic pathway of coagulation?","options":["Factor VIII","Factor VII","Factor XI","Factor IX"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Factor VII\\n\\nFactor VII\xa0participates in the extrinsic pathway of coagulation.\\n\\nThe prothrombin time (PT) assay\xa0is done to assesses the function of the proteins in the extrinsic pathway- Factors VII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen.\\n\\nFactor VIII,\xa0IX, and XI participate in the intrinsic pathway of\xa0coagulation.\\n\\nFactor VIII\xa0is a protein involved in the\xa0intrinsic pathway and its function is assessed by the partial thromboplastin time (PTT) assay.\\n\\nPearl PF1932: Coagulation Pathways and its Abnormalities\\n\\nTest results\\nInherited causes\\nAcquired causes\\n\\nProlonged PT\\n\\nEvaluates extrinsic pathway factors\\n\\nNormal PT - 12-16 seconds\\n\\nFactor VII deficiency\\n\\nMild vitamin K deficiency\\n\\nLiver disease\\n\\nWarfarin\\n\\nProlonged aPTT\\n\\nEvaluates intrinsic pathway factors\\n\\nNormal aPTT - 26-34 seconds\\n\\nFactors VIII, IX, XI deficiency\\n\\nFactor XII, prekallikrein, or HMW kininogen deficiency\\n\\nvon Willebrand disease\\n\\nHeparin, lupus anticoagulant, acquired von Willebrand disease\\n\\nProlonged PT + prolonged aPTT\\n\\nEvaluates common pathway factors\\n\\nProthrombin (II), fibrinogen (I), factors V and factor X deficiencies\\n\\nDisseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\\n\\nSevere liver disease\\n\\nSevere vitamin K deficiency\\n\\nDirect thrombin inhibitor-argatroban, dabigatran\\n\\nDirect factor Xa inhibitor-rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban\\n\\nFondaparinux","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/fd0dc9f568f21339d3da.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-06b5fc1438","n":109,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Which of the following is an orexigenic hormone?","options":["Melanocyte-stimulating hormone","Cholecystokinin","Ghrelin","Leptin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ghrelin\\n\\nGhrelin is an\xa0orexigenic hormone that increases appetite.\\n\\nGhrelin is produced by\xa0the oxyntic gland\xa0area of the stomach and is primarily involved in the\xa0central control of food intake.\xa0It is also secreted by the pancreas and adrenal glands.\\n\\nFunctions\\n\\n* Increases appetite/food intake (orexigenic)\\n\\n* It\xa0stimulates growth hormone secretion\xa0by directly acting on the anterior pituitary\\n\\n* It\xa0increases gastric acid secretion and gastrointestinal motility\xa0to prepare the body for food intake\\n\\n* It regulates the\xa0distribution and rate of use of energy\\n\\n* It suppresses the action of leptin and increases the production of neuropeptide Y. It also causes lipolysis\\n\\nGhrelin levels\xa0increase during fasting, peaking just before food intake\xa0begins or when the subject is expecting a meal. The levels fall within 1 hour of the start of feeding.\\n\\nMelanocyte-stimulating hormone, cholecystokinin, and leptin are anorexigenic and they decrease the\xa0appetite.\\n\\nPearl PF0555: Neurotransmitters and Hormones that influence Feeding and Satiety Centers in the Hypothalamus\\n\\nIncreased Appetite (Orexigenic)\\n\\nDecreased Appetite (Anorexigenic)\\n\\nNeuropeptide Y\\n\\nAgouti-related protein (AGRP)\\n\\nMelanin-concentrating hormone (MCH)\\n\\nOrexins A and B\\n\\nEndorphins\\n\\nGalanin\\n\\nAmino acids (glutamate and γ aminobutyric acid)\\n\\nCortisol\\n\\nGhrelin\\n\\nGrowth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH)\\n\\nEndocannabinoids\\n\\nMelanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH)\\n\\nLeptin\\n\\nSerotonin\\n\\nNorepinephrine\\n\\nCorticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH)\\n\\nInsulin\\n\\nCholecystokinin (CCK)\\n\\nGlucagon-like peptide\\n\\nCocaine-and amphetamine-regulated transcript (CART)\\n\\nBombesin\\n\\nPeptide YY","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f11d383c8b","n":110,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Which of the following is the principal constituent of serum osmolality?","options":["Potassium","Sodium","Bicarbonate","Hydrogen ions"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sodium\\n\\nThe principal constituent of serum osmolality is sodium.\\n\\nSerum osmolality = 2 sodium + (glucose/18) + (BUN/2.8).\\n\\nThe principal determinants of the osmolality of serum are the concentrations of sodium, glucose\xa0and urea (blood urea nitrogen or BUN).\\n\\nOsmolality is defined as a measure of the osmoles (Osm) of solute per kilogram of solvent (osmol/kg or Osm/kg). The osmolality of the intracellular and extracellular fluids is maintained between 290 and 310 mOsm in each compartment.\\n\\nAn increase in plasma sodium (plasma osmolality) attracts water out of the cell. This leads to shrinkage of the intracellular volume. Hyponatremia (decreased plasma osmolality) allows the flow of water into the cell and expands intracellular volume.\\n\\nPearl PF0501: Extracellular fluid vs Intracellular fluid\\n\\nExtracellular fluid\\nIntracellular fluid\\n\\nNa+\\n142 mEq/L\\n10\xa0mEq/L\\n\\nK+\\n4\xa0mEq/L\\n140\xa0mEq/L\\n\\nCa++\\n2.4\xa0mEq/L\\n0.0001\xa0mEq/L\\n\\nCl-\\n103\xa0mEq/L\\n4\xa0mEq/L\\n\\nHCO3-\\n28\xa0mEq/L\\n10\xa0mEq/L\\n\\nPhosphates\\n4\xa0mEq/L\\n75\xa0mEq/L\\n\\nGlucose\\n90\xa0mg/dL\\n0-20 mg/dL\\n\\nProteins\\n2 g/dl (5mEq/L)\\n16 g/dL (40\xa0mEq/L)\\n\\npH\\n7.4\\n7.0\\n\\nOsmolarity\\n282 mOsm/L\\n281 mOsm/L\\n\\nMajor Cation\\nNa+\\nK+\\n\\nMajor Anion\\nCl-\\nPhosphate\\n\\nBuffer\\nHCO3-\\nProtein\\n\\nMost osmotically active ion\\nNa+\\nK+","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-99c754faed","n":111,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2023","text":"Which of the following statements is true about GFR? GFR- Glomerular filtration rate RPF- Renal Plasma Flow","options":["Increase in sympathetic function increases GFR","Afferent arteriolar constriction increases GFR","Increase in RPF increases GFR","Decrease in RPF increases GFR"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Increase in RPF increases GFR\\n\\nThe true statement about GFR is that the\xa0increase in RPF increases GFR.\\n\\nWith a constant glomerular hydrostatic pressure, an increase in blood flow into the glomerulus tends to increase the GFR and a lower rate of blood flow into the glomerulus tends to decrease the GFR.\\n\\nGFR decreases with sympathetic stimulation as there is afferent arteriolar constriction. Strong activation of the renal sympathetic nerves can constrict the renal arterioles and decrease renal blood flow and GFR.\\n\\nPearl PF0720: Effects of changes in Starling Forces on GFR Renal Plasma Flow, and Filtration Fraction\\n\\nEffect\\n\\nGFR\\n\\nRPF\\n\\nFF(GFR/RPF)\\n\\nConstriction of afferent arteriole (eg. Sympathetic stimulation)\\n\\n↓\\n\\n(caused by ↓PG)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNo change\\n\\nConstriction of efferent arteriole (eg angiotensin II)\\n\\n↑\\n\\n(caused by ↑PG)\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↑\\n\\n(↑GFR/↓RPF)\\n\\nIncreased plasma protein\\n\\n↓\\n\\n(caused by↑π G)\\n\\nNo change\\n\\n↓\\n\\n(↓GFR/unchanged RPF)\\n\\nDecreased plasma protein\\n\\n↑\\n\\n-\\n\\n↑\\n\\nUreteral stone / constriction of the ureter\\n\\n↓\\n\\n(caused by ↑PB)\\n\\nNo change\\n\\n↓\\n\\n(↓GFR/unchanged RPF)\\n\\nNote: PB, Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure; πG, glomerular capillary colloid osmotic pressure; PG, glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure;","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/28cfc0ca35c2d256fc93.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-dd5f1d26ca","n":112,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"A 23-year-old man with hypertension presents at the hospital. His blood pressure upon examination is 150/90, and he is afebrile. Further evaluation reveals normal serum renin levels, leading to a diagnosis of Conn\'s syndrome. All of the following conditions would be seen in Conn\'s syndrome except?","options":["Hypokalemia","Polyuria","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Metabolic acidosis\\n\\nConn\'s syndrome presents with Metabolic alkalosis.\\n\\nPatients with Conn\'s syndrome have primary hyperaldosteronism which results in a modest increase in plasma sodium, usually less than 4-6 mEq/L over the normal range, hypokalemia (due to increased K+excretion), hypertension (due to slight increase in ECF volume), metabolic alkalosis because increased hydrogen ions are secreted in the urine.\\n\\nExcess secretion of aldosterone causes only a modest increase in plasma sodium. When sodium gets reabsorbed, water also accompanies it, resulting in plasma sodium remaining fairly normal.\\n\\nPrimary\xa0hyperaldosteronism is due to\xa0autonomous overproduction of aldosterone with resultant suppression of RAAS. It can occur due to adrenal hyperplasia, adenoma, or carcinoma. It is associated with the following features:\\n\\n* Hypertension (most common)\\n\\n* Hypokalemia\\n\\n* Metabolic alkalosis\\n\\n* Polyuria (due to ANP)\\n\\n* Polydipsia (due to polyuria)\\n\\n* Muscle weakness","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9f663207eb452b30a50d.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-9594a0a1c4","n":113,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"A middle-aged woman presents with easy fatiguability and was diagnosed as iron deficiency anemia. She was advised to take oral iron supplements. Foods containing which of the following should not be taken along with her iron supplements?","options":["Sugars in the diet","Citrus fruits","Ascorbic acid","Tea"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Tea\\n\\nAbsorption of iron is decreased by\xa0tannates found in tea.\\n\\nTea contains compounds called tannins, which are polyphenolic compounds. These tannins have been shown to inhibit the absorption of non-heme iron from plant-based foods. Non-heme iron is the type of iron found in plant foods and is not as easily absorbed by the body as heme iron, which is found in animal-based foods.\\n\\nTannins bind to iron in the gastrointestinal tract, forming complexes that are poorly absorbed by the body. This can lead to decreased absorption of dietary iron, potentially contributing to iron deficiency if tea is consumed in large quantities, particularly with meals. The inhibitory effect of tea on iron absorption is more pronounced when consumed during meals rather than between meals.\\n\\nIn contrast, substances like ascorbic acid (found in fruits like amla) can enhance the absorption of non-heme iron, while certain foods like lime juice and sprouts don\'t significantly affect iron absorption.\\n\\nIron absorption:\\n\\nIncreased by ascorbic acid, citric acid, amino acids and sugars in the diet.\\nDecreased by phosphates, carbonates, oxalates, and tannates.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1e71b5733c","n":114,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"After consuming three cones of ice cream, a man notices that his ear feels colder than the central part of his body. Which area of the brain regulates body temperature?","options":["Caudate nucleus","Amygdala","Thalamus","Hypothalamus"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Hypothalamus\\n\\nThe hypothalamus is responsible for regulating the body temperature.\\n\\nThe hypothalamus is the thermoregulatory centre of the body. It acts as a central command centre for thermoregulation, ensuring that the body maintains a relatively constant core temperature despite external temperature fluctuations.\\n\\n* The reflex responses triggered by cold originate from the posterior hypothalamus,\\n\\n* The reflex responses initiated by warmth primarily stem from the anterior hypothalamus.\\n\\nActivation of the anterior hypothalamus leads to cutaneous vasodilation and sweating, while lesions in this area can result in hyperthermia, with rectal temperatures sometimes rising to 43\xb0C (109.4\xb0F).\\n\\nStimulation of the posterior hypothalamus induces shivering, and animals with lesions in this region experience a decrease in body temperature toward that of the surrounding environment.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5683c22751","n":115,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"Kinesin, Dyenin and Myosin proteins are examples of?","options":["Cell adhesion molecules","Molecular motors","Intracellular molecule","Transcellular molecule"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Molecular motors\\n\\nKinesin, dynein, and\xa0myosin are examples of\xa0molecular motors.\\n\\nThe molecular motors are the force-generating proteins as they move proteins, organelles, and other cell parts (collectively referred to as “cargo”) to all parts of the cell.\\n\\nKinesin:\\n\\n* The conventional form of kinesin is a double-headed molecule that moves its cargo toward the plus (“+”) ends of microtubules.\\n\\n* Some kinesins are associated with mitosis and meiosis.\\n\\n* Other kinesins perform different functions, and may also move cargo to the minus (“–”) end of microtubules.\\n\\nDyneins:\\n\\n* They have two heads, with their neckpieces embedded in a complex of proteins.\\n\\n* Cytoplasmic dyneins are similar to conventional kinesin, except they move particles and membranes to the minus (–) end of the microtubules.\\n\\n* Axonemal dyneins are present with the axonemes of the cilia. An axoneme consists of a unique arrangement of nine outer microtubule doublets and two inner microtubules (\\"9+2\\" arrangement). The coordinated dynein–microtubule interactions within the axoneme are responsible for the ciliary and sperm movement.\\n\\nMyosins:\\n\\n* There are about 18 classes of myosins.\\n\\n* The heads of some myosin molecules (myosin II) bind to actin and produce motion by bending their neck regions.\\n\\n* Some myosin molecules (myosin V) may move the cargo, by walking along microfilaments, one head after the other.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2bef89d619","n":116,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"What cells are present in the center of the lumen of venules?","options":["Lymphocytes","RBCs","Macrophage","Neutrophils"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. RBCs\\n\\nRed blood cells (RBCs) are typically found in the center of central veins, whereas white blood cells (WBCs) are predominantly located in the periphery of these veins.\\n\\nRBCs tend to form a central column within the vessel due to their discoid shape and higher density compared to other blood components. This arrangement displaces leukocytes (white blood cells) towards the vessel\'s wall.\\n\\nThe positioning of leukocytes near the vessel wall facilitates their interaction with endothelial cells, aiding in the immune response to pathogens or injury as well as migration to sites of infection or injury. WBCs play a crucial role in immune surveillance and response to pathogens.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4ebbe16c74","n":117,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"Which of the following glucose transporter is present in skeletal muscles and adipose tissues?","options":["GLUT 4","GLUT 2","GLUT 5","SGLT-1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. GLUT 4\\n\\nGLUT-4 is present in\xa0skeletal muscle cells and adipose tissues.\\n\\nGlucose transporters, also referred to as GLUTs, are transport proteins in the cell membrane responsible for the transfer of glucose into the cells by facilitative diffusion. Facilitative diffusion involves the passage of molecules or ions through the cell membrane by them binding chemically to a carrier/ transport protein that is responsible for their movement across the cell membrane.\\n\\nGLUT-4 is a transport protein present in adipocytes. When there is a rise in blood glucose level, insulin secreted by the pancreatic beta cells binds to its receptors on the plasma membrane of adipocytes. Upon binding of insulin to its receptor, activation of the receptor\'s tyrosine-kinase domain occurs. This activation starts a protein activation cascade leading to the translocation of GLUT4 to the plasma membrane.\\n\\nOnce translocation occurs and GLUT4 is inserted into the plasma membrane, facilitated diffusion of glucose down its concentration gradient into the cell occurs.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: GLUT-2\xa0is found in the liver, pancreatic β cell, small intestine, and kidney. Its function is the rapid uptake or release of glucose.\\n\\nOption C: GLUT-5\xa0is present in the small intestine and is involved in the absorption of fructose.\\n\\nOption D: SGLT-1\xa0is a sodium-dependent unidirectional transporter present in the small intestine and kidney that is involved in the active uptake of glucose against a concentration gradient.\\n\\nPearl PF0018: Glucose transporters and their locations\\n\\nTissue location\\nFunction\\n\\nGLUT 1\\n\\nBrain, RBC, Placenta, kidney, colon\\nBasal glucose uptake\\n\\nGLUT 2\\n\\nLiver, pancreatic beta cell, small intestine, kidney\\nRapid uptake/release of glucose. Low affinity and high km\\n\\nGLUT 3\\n\\nNeurons, kidney, placenta\\nHigh affinity for glucose\\n\\nGLUT 4\\n\\nHeart, skeletal muscle, adipose tissue\\nInsulin-dependent glucose uptake\\n\\nGLUT 5\\n\\nSmall intestine, testis, sperm\\nFructose transporter\\n\\nGLUT 8\\n\\nBlastocyst, testis, brain\\nInsulin-dependent glucose uptake in blastocyst. Transports glucose to Mature spermatozoa.\\n\\nGLUT 9\\n\\nLiver, kidney\\nUrate transporter","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5d481f08dc","n":118,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"Which of the following is a voltage-gated calcium channel present on the T tubule?","options":["Dihydropyridine receptor","SERCA","Ryanodine receptor","Troponin complex"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Dihydropyridine receptor\\n\\nDihydropyridine receptor (DHPR) is a voltage-gated calcium channel present on the T tubule.\\n\\nDihydropyridine receptor (DHPR) is a voltage-gated calcium channel situated on the membrane of T-tubules within muscle cells. It works as a voltage sensor, it detects cell membrane depolarization triggered by an action potential. When depolarization occurs, DHPR undergoes a structural alteration, which then prompts the activation of the ryanodine receptor (RyR). This activation results in the release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, instigating the process of muscle contraction.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nRyanodine receptor (RyR): is present on the membrane of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) in muscle cells. Upon activation by calcium influx through DHPRs, RyRs release stored calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the cytoplasm, thereby initiating muscle contraction.\\n\\nSERCA: is an ATPase enzyme situated on the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane, that actively transports calcium ions from the cytoplasm back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum during muscle relaxation. This process facilitates the reduction of cytoplasmic calcium levels, enabling muscle relaxation after contraction and preparing it for subsequent contraction cycles.\\n\\nTroponin complex acts as a key regulatory switch in the excitation-contraction coupling process, ensuring that muscle contraction occurs only when calcium ions are present and that relaxation occurs when calcium levels decrease.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f17d0463ba","n":119,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2024","text":"Which of the following is the mechanism of action of thiazide diuretics?","options":["Inhibits the Na-CI pump in early distal convoluted tubule","Inhibits the Na-CI pump in late distal convoluted tubule","Inhibits the Na-K-2Cl pump in the descending limb of loop of Henle","Inhibits the Na-K-2Cl pump in the ascending limb of loop of Henle"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inhibits the Na-CI pump in early distal convoluted tubule\\n\\nHydrochlorothiazide is a thiazide-like diuretic. It inhibits Na+-Cl- symporter in the early distal tubule and increases the excretion of sodium and chloride.\\n\\nThiazide diuretics:\\n\\nThey may be used for the treatment of hypertension, heart failure, nephrolithiasis due to idiopathic hypercalciuria, and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.\\n\\nThiazides act on the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). They block the Na+/Cl- co-transporter in the DCT. This results in the loss of sodium, chloride, and water contributing to the diuretic effect. The diuretic effect of thiazide leads to decreased osmotic pressure due to the loss of Na+, Cl-, and water leading to compensatory reabsorption of uric acid thus resulting in hyperuricemia. The intracytoplasmic Na+ concentration also decreases due to the blockade of the co-transporter. To compensate, the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger pumps sodium from the systemic circulation into the epithelial cells in exchange for calcium. The declining calcium levels inside the cell will draw more calcium from the lumen into the systemic circulation. This results in hypercalcemia. There is also increased loss of Mg2+, K+, and H+.\\n\\nMechanisms of antihypertensive action: Decreases plasma and ECF volume by diuresis thereby decreasing cardiac output. Even when the compensatory mechanisms restore the Na+ levels and plasma volume, a low BP is maintained by a reduction in total peripheral resistance. This reduction in total peripheral resistance is due to the persistence of a small sodium and volume deficit even after compensation. The decreased intracellular sodium is responsible for increased compliance of the vessel wall and a reduced response to constrictor stimulus.\\n\\nThiazides are the diuretic of choice for uncomplicated hypertension.\\n\\nAdvantages of thiazide as an antihypertensive are:\\n\\n* It can be given as once a day dose and has a flat dose-response curve\\n\\n* Decreased incidence of postural hypotension and relative freedom from CNS side effects\\n\\n* No tolerance\\n\\n* No fluid retention\\n\\n* Decreased risk of osteoporotic fractures due to its hypocalciuric action\\n\\n* Low cost\\n\\n* Effectiveness in isolated systolic hypertension\\n\\n* Thiazides are ineffective both as antihypertensive and diuretic in patients with chronic renal failure.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b97baec03a","n":120,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A 35-year-old male presents to the emergency department with difficulty breathing and cyanosis after being exposed to phosphine gas in a garage while repairing a vehicle. Despite receiving 100% oxygen, his oxygen saturation remains low and does not improve. What type of hypoxia is most likely responsible for his symptoms?","options":["Histotoxic hypoxia","Anemic hypoxia","Hypoxic hypoxia","Stagnant hypoxia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Histotoxic hypoxia\\n\\nPhosphine causes histotoxic hypoxia by inhibiting mitochondrial function and disrupting cellular respiration at the tissue level.\\n\\nPhosphine causes histotoxic hypoxia, and thus, by preventing the O2 utilization at the tissue level, it acts as a powerful stimulus for peripheral chemoreceptors. Phosphine inhibits cytochrome oxidase (Complex IV) in the electron transport chain. This disrupts oxidative phosphorylation, preventing ATP production despite adequate oxygen availability.\\n\\nIn phosphine poisoning the oxygen is present but cells cannot use it due to blocked cellular respiration. This is why simple oxygen supplementation isn\'t effective as a primary treatment.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Anemic hypoxia occurs due to decreased hemoglobin in the blood capable of carrying oxygen. This can be in the form of a low amount of hemoglobin, defective hemoglobin, or interference with hemoglobin-oxygen binding. Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning is classified as anemic hypoxia as CO preferentially binds to hemoglobin and reduces the oxygen binding. Treatment involves terminating the exposure to CO and oxygen supplementation.\\n\\nOption C: Hypoxic hypoxia refers to a reduced partial pressure of oxygen in the arterial blood. It has multiple causes, like high altitude, hypoventilation, right-to-left shunts, diffusion defects, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch. Chronic emphysema is an example of hypoxic hypoxia, as there is alveolar destruction leading to reduced gas exchange and ventilation-perfusion mismatch. In most cases, supplemental oxygen is helpful in treatment.\\n\\nOption D: Stagnant hypoxia refers to ischemic hypoxia that results from inadequate circulation due to conditions such as heart failure and hypoxia in the setting of shock. In such conditions, administering intravenous fluids can improve the patient\'s condition because it is the circulation that is affected. If the shock is prolonged it can result in acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which can be treated with positive pressure ventilation and not simply by increasing oxygen administration.\\n\\nPearl PF1943: Characteristics of different types of Hypoxia\\n\\nHypoxic\\n\\nAnaemic\\n\\nStagnant\\n\\nHistotoxic\\n\\nEtiology\\n\\n↓ in paO2\\n\\n↓ in available Hb\\n\\nO2 carrying capacity is normal but O2 delivery is decreased\\n\\nNo O2 utilization at tissue level\\n\\nExamples\\n\\nHigh altitude, lung disease\\n\\nAnemia, CO poisoning\\n\\nHeart failure, shock, Haemorrhage\\n\\nCyanide Poisoning\\n\\npaO2\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nDissolved O2 content\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nO2 combined with Hb\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nNormal\\n\\nChemoreceptor stimulation\\n\\n+\\n\\nNot stimulated\\n\\n+++\\n\\n+++\\n\\nAmount of reduced Hb\\n\\n↑\\n\\nTotal Hb↓, HHb↑\\n\\n↑↑↑\\n\\n↓\\n\\nCyanosis\\n\\nCan be present\\n\\nUnlikely\\n\\nCan be present\\n\\nunlikely","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0dcbfd2d21","n":121,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A person is performing weightlifting and attempts to lift the dumbbells overhead. Which of the following changes will be seen in the sarcomere during this process?","options":["M band decreases","I band decreases","A band decreases","M and I bands decrease"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. I band decreases\\n\\nDuring skeletal muscle contraction, sliding of thin filament over thick filament (cross bridge formation) reduces the length of the \'I\' band.\\n\\nKey Changes in the Sarcomere During Contraction:\\n\\n* Overall sarcomere length decreases – causing muscle contraction.\\n\\n* During contraction, the Z lines come closer to each other reducing the length of the sarcomere.\\n\\n* The A band remains constant and the M line becomes prominent.\\n\\n* H-zone (only myosin region) decreases – as actin filaments slide inward.\\n\\n* I-band (only actin region) shortens – due to increased overlap with myosin\\n\\nPearl PF1350: Microanatomy of Muscle Fibres\\n\\n* Each muscle consists of numerous muscle fibres.\\n\\n* Each skeletal muscle fibre is multi nucleated, bonded by sarcolemma, enclosing sarcoplasm, smooth muscle and cardiac muscles are uninucleated.\\n\\n* Within the sarcoplasm, there are several hundreds of nuclei arranged in the periphery just beneath the sarcolemma.\\n\\n* The sarcoplasm also consists of evenly distributed, longitudinal threads called myofibrils.\\n\\n* Each myofibril consists of thick (myosin) and thin (actin) filaments overlapping each other.\\n\\n* The portion of myofibril which consists of thick filaments is known as A band or dark band, and the portion of a myofibril between two A bands consisting of only thin filaments is known I band or Light band.\\n\\n* Thus the A band consists of both thick and thin filaments, where as I band consists of only thin filaments.\\n\\n* In the middle of ‘A’ band, there are only thick filaments with no overlapping thin filaments. This zone is known as ‘H’ zone. In the middle of H zone, there is a dark band known as ‘M’ band.\\n\\n* In the middle of I band, there is a dark ‘Z’ disc or Krause\'s membrane.\\n\\n* The segment of the myofibril between two Z discs is known as the sarcomere.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9d1b3f66ba77a61e6b17.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-77bc07ba05","n":122,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"A renal biopsy of a patient is shown below. Identify the cell marked in the given histological image.","options":["Podocyte","Extraglomerular mesangial cell","Juxtaglomerular (JG) cell","Macula Densa"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Macula Densa\\n\\nThe structure in the histological image is the macula densa cells in the distal convoluted tubule.\\n\\nSpecialized cells at the start of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) form the macula densa. They detect sodium and chloride concentrations of the fluid in the tubule (chemoreceptor). In response to elevated sodium and chloride, the macula densa cells trigger the contraction of the afferent arteriole, reducing the flow of blood to the glomerulus and the glomerular filtration rate.\\n\\nThis happens as follows: When there is increased sodium, there is increased sodium-potassium ATPase activity which leads to increased ATP hydrolysis causing more adenosine formation. The adenosine receptors on the macula densa cells stimulate it to release more calcium, so that there is vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole, thereby decreasing the GFR.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nThe given image constitutes the juxtaglomerular apparatus and has the following parts:\\n\\nOption B: Lacis cells: Extraglomerular mesangial cells situated between afferent and efferent arteriole.\\n\\nOption C: Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells: Modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole, they secrete renin. Fall in pressure in afferent arteriole stimulates renin release (mechanoreceptor), which, in turn, increases blood pressure.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0\xa0Glomerulus is a rounded tuft of anastomosing capillaries. The glomerular capillary wall is the filtering membrane and consists of fenestrated endothelial cells, basement membrane, and visceral epithelial cells called podocytes, which contain foot processes (pedicels) as shown below.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/d33fdf2b9bc71102669b.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/6ec6b607af42ac778b61.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/519fe6e1dc8c7830731c.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a5b006d121421e689878.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-f3097929d6","n":123,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"During spermatogenesis, the process in which sperm cells develop from spermatogonia in the testes, the secretion of FSH (Follicle-Stimulating Hormone) in a male is inhibited by which factor?","options":["Inhibin","Luteinizing hormone","Testosterone","Androgen"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inhibin\\n\\nThe secretion of FSH in a male is inhibited by negative feedback effect of Inhibin secreted by sertoli cells.\\n\\n* GnRH stimulates the anterior pituitary gland to secrete follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH).\\n\\n* FSH stimulates inhibin production by the Sertoli cells. This inhibin inhibits FSH production by directly acting on the anterior pituitary.\\n\\n* LH stimulates Leydig cells to secrete testosterone, which in turn inhibits LH. Testosterone inhibits LH secretion by acting directly on the anterior pituitary and by inhibiting the secretion of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) from the hypothalamus.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/3d3d37187661d6a7d0e4.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-a896a79106","n":124,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"In a patient, after administration of Noradrenaline injection, the heart rate started to decrease. What is the reason behind it?","options":["Stimulation of alpha 2 receptors","Stimulation of beta 1 receptors","Stimulation of baroreceptors","Inhibition of baroreceptors"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Stimulation of baroreceptors\\n\\nThe decrease in heart rate (bradycardia) after noradrenaline (norepinephrine) injection is due to the stimulation of baroreceptors (baroreceptor reflex, also known as the baroreflex).\\n\\nWhen noradrenaline is administered, it causes vasoconstriction via alpha-1 receptors, leading to an increase in blood pressure. This increase in blood pressure activates the baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch, which through the baroreflex mechanism, leads to increased vagal tone and decreased sympathetic outflow to the heart, resulting in a compensatory decrease in heart rate (reflex bradycardia).\\n\\nBaroreceptors are stretch receptors in the walls of the heart and blood vessels. Baroreceptors are stimulated by the stretching of the walls of the respective vessels when there is an increased volume or pressure in the vessel. They are important in the immediate management of blood pressure.\\n\\nThe afferents from the baroreceptors activate the Nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) which in turn activates the caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM). The CVLM, on activation, releases gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) which is inhibitory to the rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM). The RVLM is the major excitatory source of the sympathetic nervous system. As it is inhibited, there is sympathetic inhibition thus reducing the blood pressure.\\n\\nThe NTS also simultaneously activates the vagal motor neurons causing bradycardia.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: (Stimulation of alpha-2 receptors): While alpha-2 receptor stimulation can cause a decrease in sympathetic outflow, this is not the primary mechanism for the heart rate decrease seen with noradrenaline administration. Alpha-2 effects are more relevant for drugs like clonidine.\\n\\nOption B: (Stimulation of beta-1 receptors): Beta-1 stimulation would actually cause an increase in heart rate (tachycardia), not a decrease. While noradrenaline does have some beta-1 activity, its alpha effects predominate.\\n\\nOption D: (Inhibition of Baroreceptor): The baroreceptors are stimulated, not inhibited, by the increase in blood pressure. Inhibition of baroreceptors would actually lead to an increase in heart rate.\\n\\nPearl PF0543: Factors Affecting Heart Rate\\n\\nHeart rate is accelerated by\\n\\n• Decreased activity of arterial baroreceptors\\n• Increased activity of atrial stretch receptors\\n• Inspiration\\n• Excitement\\n• Anger\\n• Pain\\n• Hypoxia\\n• Exercise\\n• Thyroid hormones\\n• Fever\\n\\nThe Heart is slowed by\\n\\n• Increased activity of arterial baroreceptors\\n• Expiration\\n• Grief\\n• Stimulation of pain fibers in trigeminal nerve\\n• Increased intracranial pressure","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/51beafa7308c1b59a5f3.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/c747767d0cf41d0c9ef8.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-25b6bfec73","n":125,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"In the jugular venous pressure waveform, what does the \\"a\\" wave correspond to?","options":["Atrial relaxation","Atrial contraction","Ttricuspid valve bulging into right atria","Venous return during ventricular systole"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Atrial contraction\\n\\nThe ‘a’ wave of the JVP corresponds to\xa0atrial contraction.\\n\\nPearl PF0262: JVP waveform and the abnormalities\\n\\n1) The a-wave corresponds to atrial contraction.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProminent a-waves\\nPulmonary hypertension, right atrial thrombus/ mass, and tricuspid stenosis\\n\\nCannon a-waves\\n\\nComplete AV block, ventricular tachycardia, and premature atrial/ junctional/ ventricular beats\\n\\nAbsent a-waves\\nAtrial fibrillation\\n\\n2) The c-wave corresponds to the bulging of tricuspid valve into right atrium during ventricular contraction.\\n\\n3) The x-descent corresponds to atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nAbsent x-descent\\nSevere tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nRapid x-descent\\nCardiac tamponade\\n\\n4) The v-wave corresponds to the venous filling of right atrium.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nDominant v-waves\\nTricuspid regurgitation\\n\\n5) The y-descent corresponds to the\xa0passive emptying of the right atrium into right ventricle.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProlonged y-descent\\nTricuspid stenosis\\n\\nRapid y-descent\\n\\nConstrictive pericarditis and isolated severe tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nAbsent y-descent\\nCardiac tamponade","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0736cba2145780af5468.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/541059646151c412833d.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-558ffaee4d","n":126,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"Patient presents to the neurology clinic with complaints of difficulty switching from one movement to another. He also reports having uncontrolled hand movements when trying to perform rapid alternating movements, particularly when asked to tap his fingers. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Resting tremor","Intentional tremor","Dysdiadochokinesis","Bradykinesia"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Dysdiadochokinesis\\n\\nThe patient has difficulty switching from one movement to another which is suggestive of Dysdiadochokinesia. It\xa0is the inability to perform rapid alternate movements.\\n\\nIt is a clinical manifestation of cerebellar dysfunction and is most evident when strength and sensation are intact. Focal lesions in the nervous system may produce highly asymmetric dysdiadochokinesia.\\n\\nThe following movement is the method to check for dysdiadochokinesis. The patient is showing normal movement, with no dysdiadochokinesis.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption A: Resting tremor is an involuntary, rhythmic muscle movement that occurs when the affected body part is at rest and diminishes with voluntary movement. It is commonly associated with Parkinson\'s disease and basal ganglia dysfunction.\\n\\nOption B: Intentional tremor is a slow, rhythmic tremor that occurs during purposeful movement and worsens as the target approaches. It is commonly seen in cerebellar disorders, such as multiple sclerosis or stroke.\\n\\nOption D: Bradykinesia is the slowness of voluntary movements, often accompanied by reduced amplitude and difficulty initiating actions. It is a hallmark symptom of Parkinson’s disease and other basal ganglia disorders.\\n\\nPearl PF1419: Functions of cerebellum\\n\\nVestibulo cerebellum\\n\\n• It is concerned with maintainence of balance and equilibirium.\\n• It is responsible for regulating the stability of head and body in space.\\n• It adjusts the tone of the trunk muscle.\\n• Controls the oculur movements and other postural reflexes.\\n\\nSpino cerebellum\\n\\n• Maintains the posture and helps in execution of gross movements.\\n• It controls the interplay between the agonist and antagonist group of muscles.\\n• It is essential for the control of rapid muscular activities like running and talking.\\n\\nCerebro cerebellum (Neocerebellum)\\n\\n• Controls fine, highly precise and coordinated movements.\\n• It is involved in programming of voluntary and skilled movements\\n• It plays a major role in the timing of the motor activities and rapid progression from one movement to the next.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/0b1da2ee7d6bc51f5b7c.webp"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/f478bb568dffc5b17fd6.gif"}]},{"bankId":"qb-6533a230d7","n":127,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"The image shows the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve plotted during different conditions. Which among the following conditions would result in curve D?","options":["Hypothermia","Hypocarbia","Less 2,3 BPG","Acidosis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Acidosis\\n\\nThe oxygen hemoglobin curve denoted by the curve D\xa0will be observed during Acidosis.\\n\\nConditions that cause a right shift (C->D) in the oxygen hemoglobin curve are: (Conditions with the increased requirement of O2 - metabolically active tissues)\\n\\n* Hypoxia and Hypercarbia - Metabolically active tissues utilize O2 and tissues produce CO2\\n\\n* ↓ pH (Acidosis)- Secondary to increased CO2 production.\\n\\n* ↑ in temperature, heat\\n\\n* ↑ in 2,3-DPG - increased anaerobic glycolysis\\n\\nConditions that cause a left shift (B <-C)\xa0 in the oxygen hemoglobin dissociation curve are:\\n\\n* Decreased carbon dioxide (hypocarbia) (Option B)\\n\\n* ↑ pH of the blood\\n\\n* ↓ in the temperature of the body (hypothermia) (Option A)\\n\\n* ↓ in 2,3-DPG (Option C)\\n\\n* Fetal hemoglobin\\n\\nCurve A represents the Myoglobin-O2 binding curve. It is a rectangular hyperbola (unlike a sigmoid curve of Oxy-hemoglobin curve). This is because it only has a single oxygen binding site. It is present in muscle in large amounts and can provide oxygen to muscles even at low blood oxygen levels.\\n\\nPearl PF0528: Factors affecting 2,3 -DPG\\n\\n↑ in 2, 3-DPG\\n↓ in 2, 3-DPG\\n\\n• Anemia\\n• Accilimitization (high altitude)\\n• Thyroid hormone\\n• Growth hormone\\n• Androgens\\n• Exercise\\n\\n• Stored blood\\n• Acidosis\\n• Chronic hypoxia","img":false,"images":["/bimg/19f426617321a7e93017.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/c72586b719f47168861f.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-452e5e11b7","n":128,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"Which electrolyte abnormality will lead to cardiac arrhythmia in the case of severe vomiting?","options":["Hyponatremia","Hypokalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hypokalemia\\n\\nThe electrolyte imbalance most likely to cause arrhythmia in the setting of severe vomiting is hypokalemia.\\n\\nPersistent vomiting results in loss of hydrochloric acid leading to the development of metabolic alkalosis (due to loss of H+) and hypochloremia (due to loss of Cl-). The kidneys try to compensate for alkalosis by secreting Na+ and HCO3-. However, the loss of sodium stimulates the secretion of aldosterone via the RAAS pathway. The aldosterone causes active reabsorption of sodium in exchange for K+ and H+ ions.\\n\\nLoss of potassium ions leads to the development of hypokalemia. Loss of H+ ions in urine lowers the urinary pH resulting in aciduria. It is called paradoxical aciduria, since the body initially lost H+ ions due to loss of HCl, and instead of compensating that loss, the body is losing more H+ ions through urine.\\n\\nPotassium plays a crucial role in cardiac electrical activity, maintaining the resting membrane potential and ensuring proper action potential conduction. Severe hypokalemia disrupts this balance, making the heart more susceptible to arrhythmias, including life-threatening ventricular tachyarrhythmias.\\n\\nIn patients with severe diarrhea, a net loss of bicarbonates (intestinal secretions) leads to metabolic acidosis.\\n\\nPearl PF0290: Causes of Acid-Base Disorders\\n\\nMetabolic acidosis\\n\\nMetabolic alkalosis\\n\\nRespiratory acidosis\\n\\nRespiratory alkalosis\\n\\nKetoacidosis\\n\\nLactic acidosis\\n\\nChronic renal failure\\n\\nSalicylate intoxication\\n\\nMethanol/formaldehyde intoxication\\n\\nEthylene glycol intoxication\\n\\nDiarrhoea\\n\\nRenal tubular acidosis\\n\\nVomiting\\n\\nHyperaldosteronism\\n\\nLoop or thiazide diuretics\\n\\nOpiates, sedatives, anesthesia\\n\\nGuillain- Barre syndrome, Polio, ALS, multiple sclerosis\\n\\nAirway obstruction\\n\\nAdult respiratory distress syndrome, COPD\\n\\nPneumonia, pulmonary embolism\\n\\nHigh altitude\\n\\nPsychogenic","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-734e97dfb0","n":129,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"Which of the following cells is responsible for the removal of small foreign particles trapped in the lungs?","options":["Type II pneumocytes","Dust cells","Type I pneumocytes","Clara cells"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Dust cells\\n\\nDust cells are responsible for the removal of small foreign particles trapped in the lungs.\\n\\nDust cells or pulmonary alveolar macrophages (PAM) originate from the bone marrow and possess active phagocytic abilities. They play a role in engulfing small particles that manage to bypass the mucociliary escalator and reach the alveoli. They secrete substances that attract granulocytes to the lungs and substances that stimulate granulocyte and monocyte formation in the bone marrow. PAM function can also be detrimental—when they ingest large amounts of the substances in cigarette smoke or other irritants, they may release lysosomal products into the extracellular space to cause inflammation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A and C:\xa0Two major types of cells are found in the alveolar epithelium: Type I pneumocytes, the primary lining cells of the alveoli (squamous epithelium), facilitate gas exchange and constitute the majority (95%) of the total surface area due to their flat surface. Additionally, the basement membrane of type I cells and the capillary endothelium are fused, minimizing the distance for gas diffusion and thereby facilitating gas exchange.\\n\\nType II cells, granular pneumocytes that produce surfactant, make up only 5% of the surface area in alveoli. These cells possess superoxide dismutase, making them more resistant to toxic oxygen radicals. As a result, they can survive, proliferate, and differentiate into Type I cells to restore the normal alveolar architecture\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Clara cells, also known as Club\xa0cells, are specialized secretory cells found in the bronchioles that do not possess cilia. They act as progenitor cells and help in bronchial regeneration after injury. They also secrete essential defense markers and contribute to the composition of mucus through the secretion of a nonmucinous material containing carbohydrates and proteins.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/abfc30d55dcb604076d2.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-943c3d166e","n":130,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"Which one of the following is related to the release of bicarbonate in exchange for chloride in the RBC to maintain electrical neutrality?","options":["Haldane effect","Bohr effect","Chloride shift","Haber–Weiss reaction"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Chloride shift\\n\\nThe release of bicarbonate in exchange for chloride in the RBC to maintain electrical neutrality is known as the chloride shift or Hamburger phenomenon.\\n\\nChloride shift:\\n\\n* Upon entering the red blood cell, CO2 is rapidly hydrated to H2CO3 by carbonic anhydrase.\\n\\n* H2CO3 is in equilibrium with H+ and its conjugate base, HCO3-. H+ can interact with deoxyhemoglobin.\\n\\n* whereas HCO3- can be transported outside of the cell via anion exchanger 1 (AE1 or Band 3).\\n\\n* To prevent an imbalance in charge, HCO₃⁻ exits the RBC into plasma, and Cl⁻ enters the RBC via the Band 3 transporter, maintaining electrical neutrality. This is called the chloride shift.\\n\\n* Because of this chloride shift, the Cl – content of the red cells in venous blood is significantly greater than that in arterial blood.\\n\\n* Note that for each CO2 molecule added to a red cell, there is an increase of one osmotically active particles in the cell - either a HCO 3 – or a Cl – ion. Consequently, the red cells take up water and increase in size.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: The Haldane effect is the phenomenon where the binding of oxygen promotes the release of carbon dioxide from hemoglobin, facilitating its removal. This effect allows for efficient gas exchange in the lungs and plays a crucial role in maintaining the balance of oxygen and carbon dioxide in the blood.\\n\\nOption B: A right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve in response to an increase in blood CO2 and hydrogen ions, enhances the release of O2 from the blood in the tissues. This enhances the oxygenation of the blood in the lungs. This is known as the Bohr effect.\\n\\nOption D: Haber–Weiss reaction is a chemical reaction that generates hydroxyl radicals (•OH), which are highly reactive and contribute to oxidative stress in biological systems. It occurs in the presence of superoxide (O₂⁻) and hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) and is catalyzed by iron (Fe\xb3⁺ or Fe\xb2⁺).\\n\\nReaction Steps:\\n\\n* Superoxide reacts with ferric iron (Fe\xb3⁺) to form ferrous iron (Fe\xb2⁺): O2− + Fe3+→O2 + Fe2+\\n\\n* Ferrous iron (Fe\xb2⁺) reacts with hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) in a Fenton-like reaction to produce hydroxyl radicals (•OH): Fe2++H2O2→Fe3++OH−+⋅OH\\n\\n* Overall Haber-Weiss Reaction:\\nO2−+H2O2→O2+OH−+⋅OH","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/c042f4b2a2de1c8bb9eb.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-8f8af72441","n":131,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jan 2025","text":"Which segment of the nephron has the lowest osmolality in the absence of the ADH hormone?","options":["Cortical collecting duct","Medullary collecting duct","Distal convoluted tubule","Descending limb of the Loop of Henle"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cortical collecting duct\\n\\nIn the absence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), the cortical collecting duct has the lowest osmolality.\\n\\nAs filtrate moves through the nephron, it changes concentration. Initially, in the proximal tubule, water and solutes are reabsorbed in equal proportions, keeping the filtrate isotonic (~300 mOsm/L). In the loop of Henle, water is removed in the descending limb, increasing osmolality, while solutes are actively reabsorbed in the ascending limb, diluting the filtrate to ~100 mOsm/L by the time it reaches the early distal convoluted tubule.\\n\\nIn the absence of ADH, the late distal tubule and collecting ducts remain impermeable to water, preventing reabsorption. As a result, in the cortical collecting duct, additional solutes such as sodium and chloride are reabsorbed without water, leading to further dilution of the tubular fluid.\\n\\nThis makes the cortical collecting duct the segment with the lowest osmolality, often reaching values as low as 50–75 mOsm/L, before the filtrate moves into the medullary collecting duct and is excreted as dilute urine.\\n\\nPearl PF2119: Transport proteins involved in the movement of Na+ and CL- across the apical membranes of renal tubular cells\\n\\nSite\\n\\nApical transport\\n\\nFunction\\n\\nProximal tubule(60%)\\n\\nNa+/glucose co transport\\n\\nNa+ uptake , glucose uptake\\n\\nNa+/inorganic phosphate Cotransporter\\n\\nNa+ uptake, phosphate uptake\\n\\nNa+ amino acid Cotransporter\\n\\nNa+ uptake, amino acid uptake\\n\\nNa+/lactate Cotransporter\\n\\nNa+ uptake, lactate uptake\\n\\nNa+/H+ exchanger\\n\\nNa+ uptake , H+ extrusion\\n\\nCl- / base exchanger\\n\\nCl- uptake\\n\\nThick ascending limb(30%)\\n\\nNa+–K+–2Cl- Cotransporter\\n\\nNa+/H+ exchanger\\n\\nNa+ uptake, Cl– uptake, K+ uptake\\n\\nNa+ uptake, H+ extrusion\\n\\nDistal convoluted tubule(7%)\\n\\nNaCl Cotransporter\\n\\nNa+ uptake, Cl– uptake\\n\\nCollecting duct(3%)\\n\\nNa+ channel (ENaC)\\n\\nNa+ uptake","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9ae82d1e07dcf21602bc.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-171701461b","n":132,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"2 medical students are conducting a study to see the effects of acclimatization at high altitudes. Which of the following will not be seen in the same?","options":["Increase Hb","Hypoventilation","Increase myoglobin","Polycythemia"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hypoventilation\\n\\nHypoventilation is not a part of high-altitude acclimatization.\\n\\nA person constantly exposed to high altitude slowly acclimatizes to the low PaO2 so that the deleterious effects of the same can be reduced. This occurs mainly via the following mechanisms:\\n\\n* Increased pulmonary ventilation\\n\\n* Increase RBC and Hb\\n\\n* Increased diffusing capacity of the lungs\\n\\n* Increased vascularity of the peripheral tissues\\n\\n* Increased ability of the tissue cells to use O2, despite low Po2.\\n\\nIncreased pulmonary ventilation: Low PO2 immediately stimulates the arterial chemoreceptors and causes increased alveolar ventilation. The immediate increase may blow off large quantities of CO2, lowering PCO2 and increasing pH which in turn may inhibit the brain stem respiratory centre via peripheral chemoreceptors. But this effect fades away in a few days and the respiratory centre activity returns to normal. Continued exposure to high altitude further increase the ventilation\\n\\nIncrease in red blood cells and hemoglobin concentration: Hypoxia triggers increased RBC production. Hematocrit slowly increases even up to 60%, and blood volume rises and together leading to hemoglobin and myoglobin rise\\n\\nIncreased diffusing capacity after acclimatization: Pulmonary capillary volume expands leading to an increased surface area of\xa0diffusion while an increase of pulmonary volume occurs which also increases the surface area of diffusion. Pulmonary arterial pressure rises leading to the opening of normally poorly perfused areas of the lung whereby diffusion further increases\\n\\nPeripheral circulatory system changes during acclimatization: There is an initial rise in cardiac output to compensate for the low RBC and Hb which later falls to normal once these values rise due to erythropoiesis. Capillary angiogenesis occurs to improve blood flow, especially in the non-pulmonary tissues\\n\\nCellular acclimatization. Changes also occur at the cellular level with an increase in numbers of mitochondria and cellular respiration in organisms living at high altitudes than the ones living at sea level.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3b11e5446e","n":133,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A 25-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department after a car crash. On examination, his BP was 80/40mmHg and pulses were feeble, neck veins distended, and heart sounds were muffled. Which of the following changes will you expect in the jvp of this patient?","options":["Cannon A wave","Absent Y descent","Absent A wave","Absent X descent"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Absent Y descent\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario with hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and distended neck vein suggests a diagnosis of\xa0cardiac tamponade.\xa0The JVP finding most likely to be seen is\xa0absent y descent.\\n\\nCardiac tamponade\xa0is the accumulation of fluid in the pericardial space. It results in the obstruction of the blood flow into the ventricles.\\n\\nThis manifests as\xa0Beck\'s triad\xa0which consists of the following:\\n\\n* Distended neck veins (due to decreased venous return)\\n\\n* Muffled heart sounds (fluid accumulation in the pericardial space)\\n\\n* Hypotension\\n\\nThe physical examination findings in a patient with cardiac tamponade are:\\n\\n* Tachycardia\\n\\n* Hypotension\\n\\n* Elevated JVP\\n\\n* Pulsus paradoxus\\n\\nJugular venous pulse (JVP) findings include:\\n\\n* Prominent \'x\' descent (early systolic) but\\n\\n* Lacks \'y\' descent (early diastolic) due to reduced or absent early diastolic ventricular filling.\\n\\nAn eFAST is reliable and will show fluid in the pericardial space. A chest X-ray will show an enlarged heart shadow.\\n\\nCardiac tamponade can also be treated with open surgery procedures like sternotomy or left thoracotomy. This also allows for the heart to be repaired during the procedure.\\n\\nPearl PF0262: JVP waveform and the abnormalities\\n\\n1) The a-wave corresponds to atrial contraction.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProminent a-waves\\nPulmonary hypertension, right atrial thrombus/ mass, and tricuspid stenosis\\n\\nCannon a-waves\\n\\nComplete AV block, ventricular tachycardia, and premature atrial/ junctional/ ventricular beats\\n\\nAbsent a-waves\\nAtrial fibrillation\\n\\n2) The c-wave corresponds to the bulging of tricuspid valve into right atrium during ventricular contraction.\\n\\n3) The x-descent corresponds to atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nAbsent x-descent\\nSevere tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nRapid x-descent\\nCardiac tamponade\\n\\n4) The v-wave corresponds to the venous filling of right atrium.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nDominant v-waves\\nTricuspid regurgitation\\n\\n5) The y-descent corresponds to the\xa0passive emptying of the right atrium into right ventricle.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nConditions\\n\\nProlonged y-descent\\nTricuspid stenosis\\n\\nRapid y-descent\\n\\nConstrictive pericarditis and isolated severe tricuspid regurgitation\\n\\nAbsent y-descent\\nCardiac tamponade","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/7f0cf2f326d0b0edf1c9.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/541059646151c412833d.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-7860c50355","n":134,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A patient developed hypotension, bradycardia and apnea on injection of an irritant substance. Which of the following physiological reflexes is involved in this scenario?","options":["Bain bridge reflex","Cushing reflex","Bezold-jarisch reflex","None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Bezold-jarisch reflex\\n\\nThe circulatory collapse, apnea in a patient following injection of irritant is due to Bezold-Jarisch Reflex.\\n\\nIt\xa0is the\xa0activation\xa0of chemosensitive vagal C fibres in the cardiopulmonary region (e.g. juxta capillary region of alveoli, ventricles, atria, great veins, and pulmonary artery) that causes profound bradycardia, hypotension and a brief period of apnea followed by rapid shallow breathing\\n\\nIt can be elicited by :\\n\\n* Substances like capsaicin, serotonin, phenyl biguanide, veratridine\\n\\n* Myocardial ischemia and reperfusion as a result of increased production of oxygen radicals\\n\\n* Radiocontrast agents for coronary angiography\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Tachycardia following acute volume overload is due to the Bainbridge Reflex. The stretch receptors of the atria that elicit the Bainbridge reflex transmit their afferent signals through the vagus nerves to the medulla of the brain.\xa0Efferent signals are then transmitted back through vagal and sympathetic nerves to increase heart rate and strength of heart contraction.\\n\\nOption B: Hypertension following a head injury is due to the Cushing Reflex. When the intracranial pressure is increased, the blood supply to RVLM neurons is compromised. The local hypoxia and hypercapnia cause an increase in discharge from the neurons, producing hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration.\xa0The resultant rise in systemic arterial pressure tends to restore the blood flow to the medulla.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e2a1e345fd","n":135,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A patient presents to the emergency department soon after a road traffic accident with intense superficial pain due to injuries. Which nerve fibre is responsible for the conduction of this pain?","options":["A alpha","A beta","A delta","C fibres"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. A delta\\n\\nThe clinical history of acute superficial pain refers to fast pain or first pain. It is transmitted by A delta (δ) fibers.\\n\\nPain can be carried by either Aδ fiber (fast pain/ epicritic pain) or by C fiber (slow pain/protopathic pain)\\n\\nFast pain is a rapid response and mediates the ability to localize the site and intensity of the noxious stimulus. it occurs when glutamate is released following the stimulation of A delta fibers.\\n\\nSlow pain on the other hand can also be burning, dull and poorly localized. It is transmitted\xa0by C nociceptor fibers which release substance P.\\n\\nFeature\\n\\nAδ fibre\\n\\nC fiber\\n\\nStimuli\\n\\nMechanical\\n\\nThermal\\n\\nPolymodal\\n\\nMyelination and tract\\n\\nMyelinated and carried via Neospinothalamic tract\\n\\nUnmyelinated and carried via paleospinothalamic tract\\n\\nConduction velocity\\n\\n12–30 m/s\\n\\n0.5–2 m/s\\n\\nImpulse conduction\\n\\nFast component of pain\\n\\nSlow component of pain\\n\\nNeurotransmitter\\n\\nGlutamate\\n\\nSubstance P\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Aα is seen in muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organs and is associated with proprioception. They have the thickest fibre and fastest connection velocity. They are the Ia and Ib fibres in numerical classification\\n\\nOption B: Aβ is associated with touch and pressure conduction and is seen in conjunction with flower-spray ending. In numerical classification, they correspond to II fibres.\\n\\nOption D: C fibres (dorsal root) are associated with pain and temperature with a conduction velocity of 0.5-2 m/s while C fibres (sympathetic) carry the postganglionic sympathetic function and conduct at a velocity of 0.7-2.3 m/s.\\n\\nPearl PF0506: Types of mammalian nerve fibres (Erlanger and Gasser\'s classification)\\n\\nFiber type\\n\\nFunction\\n\\nFiber diameter(μm)\\n\\nConduction velocity(m/s)\\n\\nSpike duration(ms)\\n\\nAα\\n\\nProprioception, somatic motor\\n\\n12-20\\n\\n70-120\\n\\nAβ\\n\\nTouch, pressure\\n\\n5-12\\n\\n30-70\\n\\n0.4-0.5\\n\\nAγ\\n\\nMotor to muscle spindles\\n\\n3-6\\n\\n15-30\\n\\nAδ\\n\\nPain, temperature\\n\\n2-5\\n\\n12-30\\n\\nB\\n\\nPreganglionic autonomic\\n\\n<3\\n\\n3-15\\n\\n1.2\\n\\nC\\n\\nDorsal root\\n\\nPain, temperature\\n\\n0.4-1.2\\n\\n0.5-2\\n\\n2\\n\\nC Sympathetic\\n\\nPost ganglionic sympathetic\\n\\n0.3-1.3\\n\\n0.7-2.3\\n\\n2","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-00586d8ab5","n":136,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"A study was conducted on a group of people to study the factors that increase and inhibit the secretion of insulin. Which of the following factors inhibit its secretion?","options":["Glucagon","Epinephrine","Gastric inhibitory polypeptide","Acetycholine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Epinephrine\\n\\nEpinephrine inhibits insulin secretion by acting on α-adrenergic receptors of pancreatic β-cells, reducing insulin release during stress or hypoglycemia.\\n\\nAll others- acetylcholine, gastric inhibitory polypeptide, and glucagon stimulate insulin secretion.\\n\\nInsulin is secreted by the pancreatic beta cells in response to increased blood glucose concentration.\xa0It promotes liver uptake, storage, and the utilization of glucose from the blood. If blood glucose levels are high, they cause\xa0glucose\xa0to be rapidly stored in the liver in the form of glycogen. It also promotes the conversion of this excess glucose to fatty acids.\xa0When glucose levels fall, insulin secretion is decreased and activation of enzyme phosphorylase split glycogen to release stored glucose into the circulating blood.\\n\\nMechanism of insulin secretion:\\n\\nGlucose\xa0enters beta cells of the pancreas via\xa0GLUT-2\xa0receptors. Once inside, it is phosphorylated by\xa0glucokinase\xa0(rate-limiting step) to glucose-6-phosphate which is\xa0oxidized to form adenosine triphosphate (ATP). This ATP causes the\xa0closure\xa0of ATP-sensitive\xa0potassium\xa0channels and hence\xa0depolarization. This leads to\xa0opening\xa0voltage-gated\xa0calcium\xa0channels and Ca2+ influx. This stimulates the\xa0fusion\xa0of the insulin-containing vesicles with the cell membrane and the\xa0secretion of insulin\xa0into the extracellular fluid by\xa0exocytosis.\\n\\nPearl PF1549: Factors affecting insulin secretion\\n\\nStimulators\\nInhibitors\\n\\nGlucose\\n\\nMannose\\n\\n2-Deoxyglucose\\n\\nMannoheptulose\\n\\nGlucagon\\n\\nSomatostatin\\n\\nAmino acids - leucine arginine\\n\\nIntestinal hormones - GIP, GLP-1, gastrin, secretin, CCK\\n\\nK+ depletion\\n\\nLeptin\\n\\nAcetylcholine (Parasympathetic stimulation )\\n\\nβ-adrenergic stimulators\\n\\nTheophylline\\n\\nSulfonylureas\\n\\nα-adrenergic stimulators (norepinephrine, epinephrine)β-Adrenergic blockers\xa0 (propranolol)\\n\\nDiazoxide\\n\\nThiazide diuretics\\n\\nPhenytoin\\n\\nAlloxan\\n\\nMicrotubule inhibitors ( Taxanes, Vinca alkaloids)\\n\\nInsulin","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d97aae23d1c7f901bc22.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-7de6e12d3e","n":137,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Identify the hormones 1 and 2 in the following image","options":["1: Testosterone, 2: Inhibin","1: Inhibin, 2: Testosterone","1: Dhea, 2: Testosterone","1: Testosterone, 2: DHEA"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1: Inhibin, 2: Testosterone\\n\\nThe image shows the Hypothalamic pituitary axis and the hormones 1 is Inhibin and 2 is testosterone.\\n\\nHypothalamus stimulates the Anterior pituitary via GnRh to release FSH and LH.\\n\\nLH\xa0stimulates\xa0Leydig cells to secrete testosterone, which in turn inhibits LH by a negative feedback mechanism.\xa0FSH\xa0stimulates\xa0Sertoli cells to secrete inhibin, which in turn inhibits FSH by a negative feedback mechanism.\\n\\nTestosterone inhibits LH secretion by acting directly on the anterior pituitary and by inhibiting the secretion of GnRH from the hypothalamus. Inhibin acts directly on the anterior pituitary to inhibit FSH secretion.\\n\\nFSH also stimulates the secretion of ABP (androgen-binding protein).\xa0FSH and androgens maintain the gametogenic function of the testes.\xa0Hypothalamic lesions in animals and hypothalamic disease in humans lead to atrophy of the testes and loss of their function.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/4426843a4eb9377aebb5.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/1b4b22a86793a15e7ee3.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-31a17df5d7","n":138,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Thyroid hormone acts via which among the following receptor?","options":["Transmembrane receptor linked to tyrosine kinase","Nuclear receptor","Cytoplasmic receptor","G protein coupled receptor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Nuclear receptor\\n\\nThe\xa0thyroid hormone receptors belong to nuclear receptors.\\n\\nThe nuclear receptor is a broad term that includes receptors located in both the nucleus and cytoplasm. This is because even for the receptors that are located in the cytoplasm, the end effect is seen via the nucleus.\\n\\nPearl PF0103: Classification of Hormones by Mechanism of Action\\n\\nA) Hormones that bind to intracellular receptors\\n\\n* Cytoplasmic receptors\\n\\n* Steroids such as glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids and androgens\\n\\n* Nuclear receptors\\n\\n* Thyroid hormones\\n\\n* Vitamins A and D\\n\\nB) Hormones that bind to cell surface receptors\\n\\n* GPCR - G protein-coupled receptors\\n\\n* Gs - stimulation of the 2nd messenger cAMP\\n\\n* Examples: ACTH,\xa0CRH, TSH, LH, FSH hCG, Catecholamines (β receptors), Vasopressin (V2 receptors), Secretin, Somatostatin, and Glucagon.\\n\\n* Gi - inhibition of the 2nd messenger cAMP\\n\\n* Examples: Catecholamines (ɑ2 receptors), Acetylcholine (M2, M4), Somatostatin\\n\\n* Gq - The 2nd messenger is Phospholipase C (IP3 DAG Ca2+)\\n\\n* Examples: Parathyroid hormone, GnRH - Gonadotropin-releasing hormone, GHRH - Growth hormone releasing hormone,\xa0TRH,\xa0Catecholamines (ɑ1 receptors), Vasopressin (V1 receptor), Gastrin, Cholecystokinin and Oxytocin.\\n\\n* cGMP\xa0as the 2nd messenger\\n\\n* Examples: Atrial natriuretic factor, Nitric oxide\\n\\n* Tyrosine kinase receptors\xa0such as those that employ Janus kinase (JAK-STAT pathway) or MAP kinase pathway\\n\\n* Growth hormone, Growth factors (GFs) - eg: IGF, VEGF, FGF, PDGF etc, Erythropoietin, Insulin and Prolactin.\\n\\nNote: Even though the cytoplasmic receptors are present in the cytoplasm, once the hormone-receptor complex is formed it moves to the nucleus for its actions.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f6bf8f452f","n":139,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Which among the following functions is not carried out by the intestinal epithelium?","options":["Secretion of digestive enzymes","Bile salt secretion","Mucous secretion","Absorption of ions"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bile salt secretion\\n\\nBile salts are not secreted by the intestinal epithelium. They are secreted by the liver.\\n\\nThe intestinal epithelium has multiple roles to be performed in the functioning of the gastrointestinal system.\\n\\n* Absorptive function: provides surface area and mechanisms for absorption of digestive products\\n\\n* Secretory function: intestinal digestive enzyme secretion\\n\\n* Protective function: both formation of the physical barrier and chemical barrier via the production of mucous\\n\\nBile salts are secreted by the liver from cholesterol.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5aa391982e","n":140,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Which hormone is responsible for galactokinesis?","options":["Progestrone","Vasopressin","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Oxytocin\\n\\nOxytocin helps in the ejection of milk by myoepithelial contraction also called galactokinesis\\n\\nFunctions of oxytocin are:\\n\\n* Milk ejection (myoepithelial contraction)\\n\\n* ADH like action in high doses (reduces urine output)\\n\\n* Uterine contraction\\n\\nHormones acting on breast\\n\\nFunction\\nDefinition\\nHormone\\n\\nMammogenesis\\nBreast growth and development\\n\\nEstrogen (ductal growth)\\n\\nProgesterone (alveolar growth)\\n\\nLactogenesis\\nInititation of milk secretion\\nProlactin\\n\\nGalactokinesis\\nEjection of milk\\nOxytocin\\n\\nGalactopoiesis\\nMaintanence of lactation\\nProlactin","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-38a1c1fac5","n":141,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Which of the following ECG finding is characteristically found in Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome?","options":["Wide QRS","Diminished AV delay","Short QRS","Prolong PR"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Wide QRS\\n\\nCharacteristic\xa0ECG findings in Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome is Wide QRS interval.\\n\\nWolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome is a form of atrioventricular reentry phenomenon characterized by the presence of accessory conducting pathways between the atria and ventricles that are separate from the AV node and Purkinje tissue.\\n\\nThe tachycardia is usually produced by the conduction of a normal impulse from the ventricles to the atria via the accessory pathway (bundle of Kent). This is the orthodromic pathway.\xa0Less often,\xa0a premature atrial beat travels down the accessory pathway (while AV Node is still in refractory period) leading to premature ventricular activation. This is the\xa0antidromic pathway.\\n\\nThe classic ECG features in WPW are as follows:\\n\\n*\\n\\nA\xa0shortened PR interval\xa0(typically <120 ms)\\n\\n*\\n\\nA slurring and the slow rise of the initial upstroke of the QRS complex (delta wave)\\n\\n*\\n\\nA\xa0widened QRS complex\xa0(total duration >0.12 seconds)\\n\\n*\\n\\nST-segment–T wave (repolarization) changes, generally directed opposite the major delta wave and QRS complex, reflecting altered depolarization\\n\\nPearl PF1816: Wolff Parkinson White Syndrome\\n\\nWolff Parkinson White Syndrome\xa0(WPW) is characterized by an abnormal accessory pathway in the heart leading to arrhythmias.\\n\\nPathology- Born with an accessory pathway, the bundle of Kent which connects the atria and ventricle. The electrical impulses from the SA node reach the ventricles earlier via the accessory pathway as there is no AV nodal delay in this pathway. This leads to the preexcitation of the ventricle and initiation of ventricular contraction. This ventricular contraction is prolonged when the impulse via the AV nodal pathway reaches the ventricles.\\n\\nMost common location of the accessory pathway-\xa0between the left atrium and free wall of the left ventricle\\n\\nECG changes:\\n\\nShortened PR interval- because of the early initiation of ventricular depolarization\\n\\nProlonged QRS duration- because of the AV nodal impulse reaching the ventricles after the aberrant impulse.\\n\\nDelta waves- slurred upstroke of QRS complex\\n\\nThe given ECG shows delta waves marked with red arrows:\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n* Catheter ablation\xa0is the treatment of choice.\\n\\n* Anti-arrhythmic drugs like procainamide, amiodarone, quinidine, propafenone, dofetilide are used in slowing conduction in the accessory pathway (prolong refractory period).\\n\\n* IV procainamide is the drug of choice in a hemodynamically stable WPW presenting with atrial fibrillation.\\n\\n* DC cardioversion is done in\xa0hemodynamically unstable patients.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/a1acf81bd802473f921d.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/9b8fd58a712019edf19f.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/6de39ba9b309f8b74ed1.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-b5d6438878","n":142,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Which of the following has GLUT-2 glucose transporter?","options":["Small intestine","Kidney","Liver","Brain"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Liver\\n\\nLiver is the primary site for GLUT 2 glucose transporter\\n\\nAlthough the kidney and small intestine has GLUT 2, only liver has GLUT 2 on the apical part of the cell.\\n\\nPearl PF0553: Glucose Transporters\\n\\nFunction\\n\\nMajor sites of expression\\n\\nSecondary active transport\\n\\n(Na + -glucose cotransport)\\n\\nSGLT 1\\n\\nAbsorption of glucose\\n\\nSmall intestine, renal tubules\\n\\nSGLT 2\\n\\nAbsorption of glucose\\n\\nRenal tubules\\n\\nFacilitated diffusion\\n\\nGLUT 1\\n\\nBasal glucose uptake\\n\\nPlacenta, blood-brain barrier, brain, red cells, kidneys, colon, many other organs\\n\\nGLUT 2\\n\\nβ-cell glucose sensor; transport out of intestinal and renal epithelial cells\\n\\nβ cells of islets, liver, epithelial cells of small intestine, kidneys\\n\\nGLUT 3\\n\\nBasal glucose uptake\\n\\nBrain, placenta, kidneys, many other organs\\n\\nGLUT 4\\n\\nInsulin-stimulated glucose uptake\\n\\nSkeletal and cardiac muscle, adipose tissue, other tissues\\n\\nGLUT 5\\n\\nFructose transport\\n\\nJejunum, sperm","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ad45b7c490","n":143,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2023","text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding phase Ib of a cystometrogram?","options":["It is due to the elasticity of bladder","It is due to the plasticity the bladder has","The graph is flat due to the contractility of the bladder","Micturition occur at the start of Ib"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. It is due to the plasticity the bladder has\\n\\nThe true statement regarding phase Ib in the cystometrogram is that\xa0segment Ib is due to plasticity\xa0of the badder. Plasticity is the ability of an object to deform its shape without breaking.\\n\\nCystometrogram is a plot of intravesical pressure against the volume of fluid in the bladder. The curve shows three segments:\\n\\n* Ia-\xa0initial slight rise in pressure when the first increments in the volume are produced\\n\\n* Ib-\xa0long, nearly flat segment as further increments are produced\\n\\n* II-\xa0sudden, sharp rise in pressure as the micturition reflex is triggered.\\n\\n* The dotted line-\xa0pressure-volume relation if the micturition had not occurred.\\n\\nThe flatness of segment Ib is a demonstration of the law of Laplace.\xa0The law of Laplace states that the pressure (P) in a spherical viscus is equal to twice the wall tension (T) divided by the radius (R). This is expressed as\xa0P= 2T/R.\xa0In the bladder, the tension increases as the bladder get filled. Since the radius also increases\xa0because of the plasticity of the bladder, there is only a slight increase in pressure\xa0until the bladder is relatively full.\\n\\nThe\xa0first urge\xa0to\xa0void\xa0is felt at a bladder volume of about\xa0150 mL. A marked\xa0sense\xa0of\xa0fullness is felt at a bladder volume of 400ml.\\n\\nMicturition reflex\xa0is the result of sensory signals from the\xa0stretch receptors in the bladder wall, which are conducted to the sacral segments of the cord through the pelvic nerves and then efferent signals conducts back again to the bladder through the parasympathetic nerve fibers.\\n\\nThe micturition reflex pathway is given below.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Elasticity refers to the ability of an object to return to its original state after being deformed by an external force. Elasticity of bladder is responsible for the recoil shrinking of the bladder after micturition.\\n\\nOption C: Contractility is the ability to contract and increase the tension or decrease the length or dimensions. Contractility of the bladder is responsible for the voiding of urine. It doesn\'t contribute to the flat curve in Ib.\\n\\nOption D: Voiding starts at the end of Ib and the start of II.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/418a4dee8ff929b95377.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/601a32b4ad3493b159b7.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-e143a7fccf","n":144,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A 22-year-old medical student has been preparing for exams for several nights with inadequate sleep and irregular meals. Laboratory tests reveal mild hypoglycemia. Which of the following hormones is most likely to show an increase in secretion during this period of fasting, stress, and deep sleep recovery?","options":["Cortisol","Prolactin","Growth hormone","Glucocorticoids"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Growth hormone\\n\\nGrowth hormone increases during fasting, stress, and deep sleep.\\n\\nGrowth hormone is secreted from the somatotropes located in the anterior pituitary. Its main role is the growth of the human body. The\xa0growth hormone levels show\xa0diurnal variation and\xa0variation across different age groups. Adolescents have the highest circulating levels of growth hormone, followed by children and adults. The levels are relatively low during the day and the pulsatile release of this hormone occurs during sleep.\\n\\nStimuli that increase GH secretion:\\n\\n* Hypoglycemia, deoxyglucose, fasting, starvation\\n\\n* Exercise\\n\\n* Protein meal, infusion of arginine\\n\\n* Glucagon\\n\\n* Deep sleep\\n\\n* Estrogens and androgens\\n\\n* Stressful stimuli\\n\\nImportant regulators of growth hormone:\\n\\n* Growth hormone-releasing hormone- stimulates the production of the hormone\\n\\n* Somatostatin- inhibits the release of GH\\n\\n* Ghrelin- increases the GH release\\n\\nActions of growth hormone:\\n\\n* Growth of cartilage, bones, and organs.\\n\\n* Increases amino acid uptake and protein synthesis in the cells and decreases protein breakdown.\\n\\n* Diabetogenic action: Increases the hepatic glucose release and decreases the glucose uptake by muscles and fat.\\n\\n* Increases fat utilization. It is ketogenic and increases circulating free fatty acid levels.\\n\\nPearl PF1545: Factors Regulating GH Secretion\\n\\nStimuli that increase GH secretion\\nStimuli that decrease GH secretion\\n\\n• GHRH\\n• Testosterone, Estrogen\\n• Ghrelin\\n• Glucagon\\n\\n• ↓ Blood glucose\\n• ↓ FFA\\n• ↑ in certain amino acids (arginine)\\n\\n• Fasting or protein deficiency\\n• Exercise\\n• Trauma, stress, excitement\\n• Deep sleep (stages 2&4)\\n\\n• Drugs like Apomorphine, L-Dopa\\n\\n• Somatostatin (GHIH)\\n• Exogenous GH\\n• Somatomedins (IGF-1)\\n• Cortisol\\n\\n• ↑ Blood glucose\\n• ↑ FFA\\n\\n• REM sleep\\n• Obesity\\n• Aging\\n\\nGHRH - Growth hormone releasing hormone\\nGHIH - Growth hormone inhibiting hormone\\nFFA - Free fatty acids","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5884993c2f","n":145,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A 23-year-old chronic alcoholic male presents to the hospital with a history of abdominal pain, dehydration, weakness, and lethargy. Which of the following kidney segments is associated with maximum water reabsorption?","options":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Proximal convoluted tubule\\n\\nThe proximal convoluted tubule is the site of maximum water reabsorption about 67% of the filtered water.\\n\\nThe driving force for water reabsorption is a trans tubular osmotic gradient established by the reabsorption of solutes.\\n\\nProximal convoluted tubule (PCT): Sodium is reabsorbed along with various inorganic and organic substances- chloride, bicarbonate, phosphate, lactate, glucose, and amino acids. In the early PCT, the main sodium channels used for reabsorption are Na+-glucose cotransporter (SGLT), Na+-H+ exchanger, Na+-amino acid cotransporter, Na+-phosphate cotransporter. In the distal PCT, the high chloride concentration in the lumen favors NaCl reabsorption, driven by the Cl− gradient.\\n\\nBased on the ultrastructure proximal tubule is divided into three segments:\\n\\n* S1 segment: 1st half of PCT\\n\\n* S2 segment: 2nd half of PCT and 1st half of proximal straight tubule\\n\\n* S3 segment: 2nd half of proximal straight tubule\\n\\nThe dilution of urine in water diuresis mainly occurs in the distal parts of the nephron. The proximal convoluted tubule absorbs water and solutes in equal proportions, while the descending limb of the loop of Henle is permeable to only water. As a result, the urine is concentrated as it enters the ascending limb.\\n\\nThe ascending limb of the loop of Henle is impermeable to water. There is reabsorption of solutes and thus, urine gets diluted. In the absence of vasopressin seen in water diuresis, the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct also remain impermeable to water, while sodium continues to get reabsorbed. Urine becomes hypo-osmolar to plasma. Thus, a large volume of dilute urine is excreted.\\n\\nIn chronic alcoholics, the production and release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), also known as vasopressin, can be impaired. Alcohol inhibits the secretion of ADH from the pituitary gland. This leads to a decreased ability to concentrate urine and increases diuresis, resulting in excessive loss of water. Consequently, the kidneys reabsorb less water, causing dehydration and electrolyte imbalances. Over time, chronic suppression of ADH can lead to persistent polyuria and the risk of developing chronic dehydration and electrolyte disturbances, such as hyponatremia.\\n\\nPearl PF0564: Water transport along nephron\\n\\nSegment\\n\\nMechanism of Water Reabsorption\\n\\nProximal tubule(67%)\\n\\nOsmosis, AQ 1\\n\\nLoop of Henle (15%)\\n\\nDescending thin limb only; aquaporin 1 channels\\n\\nEarly distal convoluted tubule(0%)\\n\\nNo water reabsorption\\n\\nLate distal tubule and collecting duct (8-17%)\\n\\nPassive(aquaporin-2) under the influence of ADH\\n\\nPearl PF0563: Summary of Transport Across Proximal and Distal Portions of the Nephron\\n\\nProximal Tubule\\n\\nReabsorption\\n\\nSecretion\\n\\n67% of filtered Na+ actively reabsorbed, not subject to control; Cl– follows passively\\n\\nAll filtered glucose and amino acids reabsorbed by secondary active transport; not subject to control\\n\\nVariable amounts of filtered PO43- and other electrolytes reabsorbed; subject to control\\n\\n65% of filtered H2O osmotically reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\n50% of filtered urea passively reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\nAlmost all filtered K+ reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\nVariable H+ secretion, depending on acid-base status of body\\n\\nOrganic ion secretion; not subject to control\\n\\nDistal tubule\\n\\nReabsorption\\nSecretion\\n\\nVariable Na+ reabsorption, controlled by aldosterone; Cl– follows passively\\n\\nVariable H2O reabsorption, controlled by vasopressin\\n\\nVariable H+ secretion, depending on acid-base status of body\\n\\nVariable K+ secretion, controlled by aldosterone","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/202fa76817a3a8741663.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-94d90611c6","n":146,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"A histology slide of the adrenal gland is provided below. Which of the following hormones is secreted from the zone marked as D?","options":["Aldosterone","Epinephrine","Glucocorticoids","Androstenedione"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Epinephrine\\n\\nIn the above image, the structure marked D is the adrenal medulla. Epinephrine is secreted from the adrenal medulla.\\n\\nAdrenal Medulla secretes:\\n\\n* Epinephrine\\n\\n* Norepinephrine\\n\\n* Dopamine\\n\\nOther structures marked are as follows:\\n\\nA – Zona glomerulosa (just underneath the eosinophilic connective tissue)\\n\\nB - Zona fasciculata\\n\\nC – Zona reticularis (cells arranged in the form of irregular cords giving rise to the reticular pattern)\\n\\nPearl PF1509: Secretion of Adrenal Hormones by the different Zones of the Adrenal Cortex and Adrenal Medulla\\n\\nAdrenal Cortex\\n\\nAdrenal Medulla\\n\\nZona glomerulosa(15%)\\n\\nMineralocorticoids\\n\\nAldosterone\\n\\nDeoxycorticosterone\\n\\nNorepinephrine\\n\\nEpinephrine\\n\\nDopamine\\n\\nZona fasiculata (50%)\\n\\nGlucocorticoids\\n\\nCortisol\\n\\nCorticosterone\\n\\nZona reticularis(7%)\\n\\nAndrogens:\\n\\n• DHEA\\n• Androstenedione","img":false,"images":["/bimg/9ef04de4d5dcd551bb96.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-e21070fea1","n":147,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"Calcium binds to which of the following proteins during smooth muscle contraction?","options":["Calmodulin","Actin","Myosin","Troponin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Calmodulin\\n\\nCalcium binds to calmodulin during smooth muscle contraction.\\n\\nIncreased cytoplasmic Ca2+ binds to and activates calcium-binding proteins (calmodulin, troponin) or activates protein kinases (or phospholipase C) to bring about physiological effects.\\n\\nCalmodulin is structurally and functionally related to troponin. In place of troponin, smooth muscle cells contain a large amount of the regulatory protein calmodulin; it is different in the manner in which it initiates contraction. Calmodulin initiates contraction by activating the myosin light chain kinase. Troponin, on the other hand, initiates contraction by causing a conformational change in the tropomyosin molecule which then uncovers the active sites on the actin filament.\\n\\nThe sequence of events in smooth muscle contraction:\\n\\n* Binding of acetylcholine to muscarinic receptors\\n\\n* Increased influx of Ca2+ into the cell\\n\\n* Activation of calmodulin-dependent myosin light chain kinase\\n\\n* Myosin phosphorylation\\n\\n* Increased myosin ATPase activity and binding of myosin to actin\\n\\nActin, myosin, and tropomyosin are present in both skeletal muscle and smooth muscle.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption B: Actin is a protein that makes up microfilaments. Microfilaments are present in the outer zone of the cytoplasm and provide support to the cell membrane. In muscle cells, a specialized arrangement of actin provides the mechanism for muscle contraction.\\n\\nOption C: Myosin is the major contractile protein found in muscle cells. Its specialized arrangement with actin is essential for muscle contraction. It does not play a role in axonal transport.\\n\\nOption D: Troponin molecules are small globular units located at intervals along with the tropomyosin molecules. Each of the three troponin subunits has a unique function:\\n\\n* Troponin T binds the troponin components to tropomyosin.\\n\\n* Troponin I inhibits the interaction of myosin with actin.\\n\\n* Troponin C contains the binding sites for the Ca 2+ that help to initiate contraction.\\n\\nPearl PF0509: Sarcomere","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/2e0916a44f09312ab98c.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-0026edb761","n":148,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"What is the mechanism of action of inhibin?","options":["Stimulating estradiol","Inhibits FSH","Inhibits LH","Stimulates FSH"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Inhibits FSH\\n\\nInhibin acts by inhibiting the production of FSH.\\n\\nThe inhibins are produced by the granulosa cells of the ovarian follicle, as well as other tissues, including the pituitary, the brain, the adrenal gland, the kidney, the bone marrow, the corpus luteum, and the placenta. Inhibin A is produced by the corpus luteum, whereas inhibin B is mainly produced by granulosa cells of the follicle.\\n\\nFSH specifically stimulates the granulosa cells to produce inhibins. It stimulates inhibin production by the Sertoli cells. This inhibin inhibits FSH production by directly acting on the anterior pituitary.\\n\\nJust before ovulation, after the granulosa cells acquire LH receptors, LH also stimulates the production of inhibin by granulosa cells. LH stimulates Leydig cells to secrete testosterone, which in turn inhibits LH. Testosterone suppresses LH release by:\\n\\n* inhibiting GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus\\n\\n* directly acting on the anterior pituitary","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/967e0aca02b1756390d1.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-1a8f0fb96b","n":149,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"Which of the following ion channels in peripheral chemoreceptors is oxygen-sensitive?","options":["Na+","K+","Ca2+","Cl-"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. K+\\n\\nPotassium channels in the peripheral chemoreceptors are oxygen-sensitive.\\n\\nType I glomus cells (carotid and aortic body) have O2-sensitive K+ channels, whose conductance is reduced in proportion to the degree of hypoxia.\\n\\nThis reduces the K+ efflux, depolarizing the cell and causing Ca2+ inﬂux.\\n\\nHypoxia induces vasoconstriction by inhibition of oxygen-sensitive potassium channels in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle cell membranes. This leads to depolarization of the cell membrane and activation of calcium channels causing the influx of calcium ions. The Ca2+ influx triggers action potentials and consequent excitation of the afferent nerve endings and\xa0constriction of small arteries and arterioles.\\n\\nWhen the concentration of O2 in the alveoli decreases below normal the adjacent blood vessels constrict. This effect is opposite to the effect observed in systemic vessels, which dilate rather than constrict in response to low O2 levels.\\n\\nThis is because the low oxygen concentrations can :\\n\\n* Stimulate the release of or increase sensitivity to vasoconstrictor substances such as endothelin or reactive oxygen species.\\n\\n* Decrease the release of vasodilators such as nitric oxide from the lung.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-97388f74eb","n":150,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"Which of the following is not a function of secondary messengers?","options":["They transmit signals from cell surface receptors to intracellular receptors.","Direct activation of DNA replication","Regulation of cellular processes such as metabolism and gene expression","Integration of multiple signaling pathways within the cell"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Direct activation of DNA replication\\n\\nDirect activation of DNA replication is not a function of secondary messengers.\\n\\nSecondary messengers are intracellular molecules that relay and amplify signals from extracellular stimuli, such as hormones, neurotransmitters, or growth factors. They act as intermediaries between cell surface receptors and the internal signalling pathways that execute the cellular response. Secondary messengers are crucial for translating external signals into appropriate cellular actions.\\n\\nThey carry the message from the cell surface receptor to other parts of the cell, initiating a series of actions that lead to a response and help control different cellular activities, including metabolism, growth, and how cells respond to external signals.\\n\\nTypes of Secondary Messengers:\\n\\n* Cyclic AMP (cAMP): Increases activity in the cell by activating other proteins.\\n\\n* Cyclic GMP (cGMP): Similar to cAMP, it activates proteins and affects various functions like muscle relaxation.\\n\\n* Inositol Triphosphate (IP3): Helps release calcium from inside the cell, which can influence many cellular activities.\\n\\n* Diacylglycerol (DAG): Works with IP3 to activate other proteins that affect cell functions.\\n\\n* Calcium Ions (Ca\xb2⁺): Act as a messenger to control various processes such as muscle contractions and neurotransmitter release.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e78d04edb8","n":151,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2024","text":"Which of the following will increase the GFR?","options":["Efferent arteriole dilation","Afferent arteriole constriction","Decrease in bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure","Increase in glomerular plasma flow"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Decrease in bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure\\n\\nA decrease in Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure causes an increase in glomerular filtration rate (GFR).\\n\\nA decrease in Bowman\'s Capsule Hydrostatic Pressure increases GFR because it reduces the opposing force to filtration, thus enhancing the net filtration pressure and increasing the amount of filtrate formed.\\n\\nFactors that can increase the GFR are as follows:\\n\\n* Increase in glomerular capillary filtration coefficient Kf: This coefficient depends on the hydraulic conductivity and surface area of the glomerular capillaries.\\n\\n* Decrease in Bowman\'s capsule hydrostatic pressure: Conditions with urinary tract obstruction can lead to an increase in this pressure and a fall in GFR.\\n\\n* Decrease in glomerular colloid osmotic pressure: Accumulation of plasma proteins that are not filtered from the blood contributes to this pressure, and an increase reduces further filtration and GFR. It is directly proportional to the following factors: Arterial plasma colloid osmotic pressure.\\n\\n* Filtration fraction : An increase in filtration fraction concentrates the plasma proteins and raises the glomerular colloid osmotic pressure which in turn decreases the GFR.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Efferent Arteriole Dilation reduces the resistance to blood leaving the glomerulus. This leads to a decrease in glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure. A reduction in hydrostatic pressure lowers the driving force for filtration, resulting in a decreased GFR.\\n\\nOption B: Afferent Arteriole Constriction decreases blood flow into the glomerulus. This lowers the glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure. Lower pressure reduces the filtration rate across the glomerular capillary wall, leading to a decrease in GFR.\\n\\nOption C: Increase in Glomerular Plasma Flow: If glomerular plasma flow increases but the efferent arteriole doesn\'t constrict, the extra blood flow is quickly carried away. This prevents a significant rise in the pressure inside the glomerular capillaries. As a result, the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) doesn\'t significantly increase, because the necessary increase in filtration pressure doesn\'t occur.\\n\\nPearl PF0561: Factors Affecting GFR\\n\\nDeterminants\\n\\nPhysiological/Pathophysiological Causes\\n\\n↓Kf\xa0(filtration coefficient)→ ↓GFR\\n\\nRenal disease, diabetes mellitus, hypertension\\n\\n↑PB\xa0→ ↓GFR\\n\\nUrinary tract obstruction (e.g., kidney stones)\\n\\n↑πG → ↓GFR\\n\\n↓ Renal blood flow\xa0increased plasma proteins\\n\\n↓PG\xa0→ ↓GFR\\n\\na) ↑afferent arteriolar resistance (RA\xa0)→ ↓PG\\n\\n↑ Sympathetic activity, (e.g., norepinephrine, endothelin)\\n\\nb) ↓systemic arterial pressure(AP\xa0)→ ↓PG\\n\\nArterial pressure\\n\\nc) ↓efferent arteriolar resistance (RE )→ ↓PG\\n\\n↓ Angiotensin II (drugs that block angiotensin II formation)\\n\\nPG :\xa0Glomerular hydrostatic pressure\\n\\nπB :\xa0Colloid osmotic pressure of the proteins in Bowman’s capsule\\n\\nPB :\xa0Hydrostatic pressure in Bowman’s capsule\\n\\nπG :\xa0Colloid osmotic pressure of glomerular capillary plasma proteins","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5ed87952e1","n":152,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A 28-year-old woman travels from Delhi to Leh (3500 feet) on vacation. She develops a headache, lightheadedness, nausea, fatigue, and shortness of breath shortly after arriving at the airport. Which of the following best describes the initial physiological disturbance and the body\'s immediate compensatory reaction?","options":["Decreased alveolar PO2 stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors, leading to hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis","Increased barometric pressure causes hyperoxia, triggering central chemoreceptors to decrease ventilation","Decreased alveolar ventilation","Shift of the O2-Hb dissociation curve to the right"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Decreased alveolar PO2 stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors, leading to hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis\\n\\nDuring the process of acclimatisation to high altitudes, respiratory alkalosis and hyperventilation\xa0occur as a result of stimulation of peripheral chemoreceptors by decreased alveolar PO₂.\\n\\nThe symptoms of chest tightness and breathlessness following rapid ascent indicate acute mountain/altitude sickness, which can be prevented by gradual ascent with sufficient time for acclimatisation.\\n\\nFollowing are the physiological changes that occur during acclimatisation:\\n\\n* Initially there is hyperventilation leading to respiratory alkalosis. There is\xa0shift\xa0of the oxygen–haemoglobin dissociation curve to the left. But following this, there is also an increase in red blood cell 2,3-DPG, which tends to decrease the O₂ affinity of haemoglobin. Finally, the net effect is a small increase in P50, and this decrease in O₂ affinity makes more O₂ available to the tissues. But this increase in P50 is also limited because when the arterial PO₂ is markedly reduced, the decreased O₂ affinity interferes with O₂ uptake by haemoglobin in the lungs.\\n\\n* Increased erythropoietin secretion, leading to an increase in circulating red blood cells\\n\\n* Increased number of mitochondria\\n\\n* Increased myoglobin to facilitate the movement of O₂ into the tissues.\\n\\n* Increased tissue cytochrome oxidase\\n\\n* Increased sympathetic tone\\n\\n* Renal suppression of carbonic anhydrase and excretion of dilute alkaline urine bring the pH of the blood to normal.\\n\\n* Decreased maximal exercise capacity (by ~1% for every 100 m gained above 1500 m)\\n\\nOn the ascent to high altitudes, the low partial pressure of oxygen leads to hypoxia and systemic arteriolar dilatation. Consequently, the capillary pressure rises with increased transudation of fluid into the brain tissue, and the following symptoms are seen:\\n\\n* Headache\\n\\n* Irritation\\n\\n* Insomnia\\n\\n* Breathlessness\\n\\n* Nausea and vomiting\\n\\nTreatment involves administration of oxygen, descent to a lower altitude, and administration of acetazolamide. Acetazolamide counteracts hypoxia by stimulating respiration and reducing the formation of cerebrospinal fluid, thereby reducing intracranial tension.\\n\\nPortable hyperbaric chambers are increasingly being used in the treatment of high-altitude illnesses when rapid descent is not possible. These chambers are available in armed forces hospitals in high-altitude areas,","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8907655d6a","n":153,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A 46-year-old patient presents with headache, elevated blood pressure tachycardia. Laboratory tests reveal increased creatinine, elevated renin levels. A drug is administered that inhibits the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) pathway, resulting in reduced blood pressure, improved heart rate control, and slowed renal damage. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which RAAS inhibition exerts these effects?","options":["Inhibition of aldosterone receptors to reduce sodium and water retention","Blockade of angiotensin II receptors, preventing vasodilation and aldosterone release (ARB)","Direct inhibition of renin secretion by juxtaglomerular cells","Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme, reducing angiotensin II formation and decreasing vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion (ACE Inhibitor)"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme, reducing angiotensin II formation and decreasing vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion (ACE Inhibitor)\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario of a patient presenting with headache, elevated blood pressure tachycardia. Laboratory tests reveal increased creatinine, and elevated renin levels describe a typical case where the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) is activated, with the administered drug inhibiting RAAS with reduced renal damage. accurately describes the mode of action of ACE Inhibitor drugs, which work by inhibiting\xa0angiotensin-converting enzyme, reducing angiotensin II formation, and decreasing vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion.\\n\\nACE inhibitors such as captopril or ARBs can be considered as a treatment of choice in hypertensive patients with diabetes because of the renoprotective action. It benefits diabetic patients by reducing the risk of occurrence and progression of diabetic nephropathy. They are also recommended in patients with CKD irrespective of diabetes status, as they can delay the progression of nephropathy.\\n\\nThe adverse effects of ACE inhibitors are:\\n\\n* Hyperkalemia\\n\\n* Hypotension - A sudden fall in blood pressure can occur following the first dose of ACE inhibitors.\\n\\n* Cough\\n\\n* Angioedema\\n\\n* Teratogenicity\\n\\n* Dysgeusia\\n\\n* Acute renal failure can occur with ACE inhibitors in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis.\\n\\n* Skin rash\\n\\n* Extremely rare but reversible side effects include neutropenia.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption A: ACE inhibitors do not inhibit aldosterone receptors; this is the mode of action of Mineralocorticoid (Aldosterone) Receptor Antagonists like Spironolactone.\\n\\nOption B: Angiotensin Receptor Blocker does not prevent vasodilation; rather, it prevents vasoconstriction by blocking angiotensin receptors.\\n\\nOption C: ACE inhibitors do not cause direct inhibition of renin secretion by juxtaglomerular cells; this is done by Renin Inhibitor drugs like Aliskiren.\\n\\nPearl PF0565: Factors that affect renin secretion\\n\\nStimulatory\\n\\n* Increased sympathetic activity via renal nerves\\n\\n* Increased circulating catecholamines\\n\\n* Prostaglandins\\n\\n* Na+ depletion\\n\\n* Diuretics\\n\\n* Upright posture\\n\\n* Hemorrhage\\n\\n* Hypotension\\n\\n* Dehydration\\n\\n* Cardiac failure\\n\\n* Cirrhosis\\n\\n* Constriction of the renal artery or aorta\\n\\n* Various psychological stimuli\\n\\nInhibitory\\n\\n* Increased Na+ and Cl- reabsorption across macula densa\\n\\n* Increased afferent arteriolar pressure\\n\\n* Angiotensin II\\n\\n* ADH (vasopressin)\\n\\nPearl PF1028: European society of cardiology guidelines for treatment of hypertension\\n\\nIndication\\n\\nInitial treatment of choice\\n\\nHypertension alone or hypertension with diabetes/ cerebrovascular disease\\n\\nACEi/ARB + CCB/diuretic (A+C or A+D)\\n\\nHypertension with coronary artery disease\\n\\nACEi/ ARB + beta blocker/CCB (A+B or A+C)\\n\\nor\\n\\nbeta-blocker + diuretic (B+D)\\n\\nor\\n\\nCCB+ diuretic/ beta-blocker (C+B or C+D)\\n\\nHypertension with chronic kidney disease\\n\\nACEi/ARB + CCB/diuretic (A+C or A+D)\\n\\nHypertension with heart failure and reduced ejection fraction\\n\\nACEI/ ARB + diuretic + beta-blocker (A+D+B)\\n\\nPregnancy\\n\\nLabetalol, Nifedipine, methyldopa\\n\\nACEi – Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors\\n\\nARB – Angiotensin II receptor blockers\\n\\nCCB – Calcium Channel Blockers\\n\\nPearl PF1654: Drugs used in heart failure\\n\\nDrugs used in acute decompensated heart failure:\\n\\nIntravenous vasodilators\\n\\nNitroglycerine\\n\\nSodium nitroprusside\\n\\nDecrease preload and afterload\\n\\nNesiritide\\nRecombinant human BNP\\n\\nIntravenous positive inotropes\\n\\nDobutamine\\n\\nDopamine\\n\\nEpinephrine\\n\\nNorepinephrine\\n\\nβ1 receptor-mediated stimulation of cardiac output\\n\\nEnoximone\\n\\nMilrinone\\n\\nPDE 3/4 inhibitors\\n\\nLevosimendan\\n\\nPimobendan\\n\\nCombined Ca2+ sensitizers and PDE3 inhibitors\\n\\nDrugs used for rapid symptom relief\\n\\nBumetanide\\n\\nFurosemide\\n\\nTorasemide\\n\\nLoop diuretics- used for symptomatic treatment of severe heart failure and acute decompensation.\\n\\nMorphine\\nOpioid - to calm the patient and decreases cardiac O2 consumption\\n\\nDrugs used in chronic heart failure:\\n\\nDrugs\\nUses\\n\\nACE inhibitors\\nUsed as first-line therapy\\n\\nARBs\\nUsed in case of intolerance to ACE inhibitors\\n\\nBeta-blockers\\nUsed as first-line therapy\\n\\nSpironolactone\\n\\nEplerenone\\n\\nMineralocorticoid receptor antagonists - first-line drugs for symptomatic heart failure.\\n\\nSGLT-2 inhibitors\\nUsed as 1st line therapy- reduces mortality and morbidity\\n\\nSacubitril/Valsartan\\nNeprilysin inhibitor/ARB(ARNI)\\n\\nIvabradine\\nInhibitor of If\xa0current in the sinoatrial node","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a49adcf54b","n":154,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A conscious 35-year-old male patient was brought to casualty with a history of a road traffic accident and a head injury. When the doctor asked questions about the incident, his answers were irrelevant to the questions, but his speech was clear. What could be the potential site of injury?","options":["Superior temporal gyrus","Inferior frontal gyrus","Brainstem","Precentral gyrus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Superior temporal gyrus\\n\\nFluent aphasia is seen in the lesions of the posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus due to the involvement of the Wernicke\'s area. The speech is full of jargon and neologisms that make little sense.\\n\\nAphasia is a language disorder that develops after brain damage. Wernicke\'s aphasia is marked by the ability to speak fluently but with difficulty understanding language. The patient cannot understand words or recognise symbols, whether they are auditory, tactile or visual. People with this condition often don\'t recognise that their speech is unclear, and they may experience significant agitation or paranoia as a result.\\n\\nLesions in the categorical hemisphere lead to\\n\\n* Broca\'s aphasia is caused by an injury in the inferior frontal gyrus. It causes\xa0nonfluent aphasia.\xa0Affected individuals have slow speech and difficulty in generating verbal or written words. For example, a patient with Broca\'s aphasia may say \\"walk dog\\" instead of \\"I want to take the dog for a walk\\". Speech therapy in Broca\'s aphasia is more successful than in Wernicke’s aphasia.\\n\\n* Wernicke\'s aphasia is caused by a lesion in the posterior end of the superior temporal gyrus. It produces a type of\xa0fluent aphasia in which speech itself is normal, but it is full of jargon and neologisms that make little sense. The patient also fails to comprehend the meaning of spoken or written words, so other aspects of the use of language are compromised.\\n\\n* Anomic aphasia –\xa0When a lesion damages the angular gyrus in the categorical hemisphere without affecting Wernicke\'s or Broca\'s areas, there is no difficulty with speech or the understanding of auditory information. But there is trouble understanding written language or pictures because visual information is not processed and transmitted to the Wernicke area.\\n\\n* Global aphasia—In this, both understanding and speaking are impaired. It is also non-fluent aphasia.\\n\\n* Acalculia—impairment of mathematical ability. Occurs due to an impairment to the parietal lobe, particularly the angular gyrus.\\n\\nConduction aphasia is seen in lesions of the arcuate fasciculus, the bundle of nerve fibres connecting the Wernicke and Broca\'s areas. Repetition of words is impaired and paraphasias are observed, though not as much as in Wernicke aphasia.\\n\\nDysarthria, in contrast to aphasia, is a motor dysfunction due to disruption in innervation to the face, tongue or soft palate that results in slurred speech but intact fluency and comprehension.\\n\\nPearl PF1819: Aphasias and Related Conditions\\n\\nComprehension\\nRepetition of Spoken language\\nNaming\\nFluency\\n\\nWernicke\'s\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\n\\nBroca\'s\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nDecreased\\n\\nGlobal\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nDecreased\\n\\nConduction\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\n\\nAnterior (nonfluent) transcortical\\nPreserved\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nImpaired\\n\\nPosterior (fluent) transcortical\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\n\\nIsolation\\nImpaired\\nEcholalia\\nImpaired\\nNo purposeful speech\\n\\nAnomic\\nPreserved\\nPreserved\\nImpaired\\nPreserved except for word finding pauses\\n\\nPure Word Deafness\\nImpaired only for spoken language\\nImpaired\\nPreserved\\nPreserved\\n\\nPure Alexia\\nImpaired only for reading\\nPreserved\\nPreserved\\nPreserved","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0b724e5deb59cce0e982.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/d65fd30d8782f5d9347b.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-0821ebcd97","n":155,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A man falls into a deep sleep with one arm under his head. Upon waking, he is unable to move the arm, though he still feels tingling and pain. The reason for the loss of motor function while maintaining pain sensation is?","options":["B fibers are more susceptible to pressure than A fibers","A fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers","C fibers are more sensitive to pressure than A fibers","Motor nerves are more affected by sleep than sensory nerves"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers\\n\\nIn the above scenario, the patient has developed paresis (partial paralysis), but the pain sensation is intact (no numbness). This is because A fibers that carry motor, touch, and pressure sensations are more susceptible to pressure changes/compression than C fibers that carry pain sensations.\\n\\nThe compression of a large-diameter nerve fiber leads to a reversible physiologic conduction block, or neuropraxia. The susceptibility of the nerve fibers to pressure is A>B>C.\xa0This is responsible for Saturday night or Sunday morning paralysis.\\n\\nPearl PF0507: Relative susceptibility of nerve fibres to conduction block by various agents\\n\\nSusceptibility to\\n\\nMost susceptible\\n\\nIntermediate\\n\\nLeast susceptible\\n\\nHypoxia\\n\\nB\\n\\nA\\n\\nC\\n\\nPressure\\n\\nA\\n\\nB\\n\\nC\\n\\nPearl PF0506: Types of mammalian nerve fibres (Erlanger and Gasser\'s classification)\\n\\nFiber type\\n\\nFunction\\n\\nFiber diameter(μm)\\n\\nConduction velocity(m/s)\\n\\nSpike duration(ms)\\n\\nAα\\n\\nProprioception, somatic motor\\n\\n12-20\\n\\n70-120\\n\\nAβ\\n\\nTouch, pressure\\n\\n5-12\\n\\n30-70\\n\\n0.4-0.5\\n\\nAγ\\n\\nMotor to muscle spindles\\n\\n3-6\\n\\n15-30\\n\\nAδ\\n\\nPain, temperature\\n\\n2-5\\n\\n12-30\\n\\nB\\n\\nPreganglionic autonomic\\n\\n<3\\n\\n3-15\\n\\n1.2\\n\\nC\\n\\nDorsal root\\n\\nPain, temperature\\n\\n0.4-1.2\\n\\n0.5-2\\n\\n2\\n\\nC Sympathetic\\n\\nPost ganglionic sympathetic\\n\\n0.3-1.3\\n\\n0.7-2.3\\n\\n2","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a26be594d0","n":156,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A patient presents with contralateral weakness of the arm and leg, along with ipsilateral facial weakness. The NCCT brain shows evidence of intracerebral hemorrhage. What is the most likely location of the bleed? NCCT-Non contrast CT","options":["Basal ganglia","Midbrain","Pons","Medulla"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pons\\n\\nThe clinical presentation of contralateral weakness of the arm and leg and ipsilateral facial weakness along with intracerebral hemorrhage in NCCT is a typical of a lesion in the pons.\\n\\nBrainstem syndromes cause crossed hemiplegia with ipsilateral cranial nerve palsies and contralateral hemiplegia. Pontine syndromes occur due to the involvement of pontine branches of the basilar artery and anterior inferior cerebellar artery. They are\\n\\nCNS lesion\\nEffects\\n\\nFoville’s Syndrome/ Dorsal pontine syndrome\\n\\n• Lateral gaze palsy\\n• Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy\\n• Contralateral hemiparesis\\n\\nMillard–Gubler Syndrome/Ventral pontine syndrome\\n\\n• Ipsilateral abducens nerve weakness\\n• Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy\\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\\n\\nIn Foville\'s syndrome, there is a dorsal pontine injury (lesion in the abducens nucleus) that causes lateral/ horizontal gaze palsy. In Millard-Gubler syndrome, there is a ventral pontine injury (lesion in the abducens fascicle) that causes lateral rectus weakness.\\n\\nLocked-in syndrome occurs when a pontine stroke causes quadriplegia and paralysis of all voluntary muscles except vertical movements of the eyes and blinking. The vertical gaze nucleus, the rostral interstitial nucleus of medial longitudinal fasciculus, is located in the midbrain and is hence preserved.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Basal ganglia → causes contralateral hemiplegia but does not cause ipsilateral cranial nerve palsy.\\nOption B: Midbrain → may cause oculomotor (III) palsy + contralateral weakness (Weber syndrome), but not isolated facial weakness.\\nOption C: Medulla → classically causes crossed findings (CN IX, X, XII) but not facial nerve involvement.\\n\\nPearl PF1846: Medullary syndromes and Weber syndrome\\n\\nSyndrome\\n\\nVessel involved\\n\\nClinical features\\n\\nMedial Medullary syndrome / Dejerine syndrome\\n\\nVertebral artery/Anterior spinal artery\\n\\n• C/L loss of conscious proprioception\\n• C/L hemiplegia\\n• I/L tongue paralysis\\n\\nLateral Medullary syndrome / Wallenberg syndrome\\n\\nVertebral artery>Posterior inferior cerebellar artery\\n\\n• C/L loss of pain/temperature on the body\\n• I/L loss of pain/temperature on face\\n• Ataxia\\n• Nystagmus\\n• Vertigo\\n• Horner\'s syndrome\\n• dysphagia\\n• Ipsilateral bulbar paralysis\\n• Loss of gag reflex\\n\\nWeber\'s syndrome\\n\\nPosterior cerebral artery\\n\\n• I/L Oculomotor nerve palsy - ptosis with fixed dilated pupil pointed down and out\\n• C/L hemiplegia\\n• C/L paralysis of the lower face\\n\\nC/L- Contralateral\\n\\nI/L-Ipsilateral","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6fce3dba84","n":157,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A patient presents with symmetrical leg weakness and areflexia. He developed diarrhoea two weeks ago. He was given a diagnosis of Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome. What is the underlying cause?","options":["Demyelination of spinal cord","Demyelination of A and B fibers","Demyelination of B fibers","Cord inflammation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Demyelination of A and B fibers\\n\\nThe underlying cause of Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome is demyelination of A and B fibres.\\n\\nErlanger and Gasser classified nerve fibres into types A, B, and C, with type A fibres further subdivided into α, β, γ, and δ fibres.\\n\\n* Type A fibers are the typical large- and medium-sized myelinated fibers of spinal nerves.\\n\\n* Type B fibers are smaller fibres with minimal myelination. They transmit impulses of the ANS, including the bladder.\\n\\n* Type C fibers are small unmyelinated nerve fibers that conduct impulses at low velocities.\\n\\nMotor fiber type\\nInnervation\\n\\nAɑ\\nSkeletal muscle\\n\\nAɣ\\nMuscle spindle\\n\\nB\\nPreganglionic sympathetic\\n\\nC\\nPostganglionic sympathetic\\n\\nSensory nerve fiber\\nNumerical class\\nInnervation\\n\\nAɑ\\nIa\\nMuscle spindle – proprioception\\n\\nAβ\\nIb, II\\nGolgi tendon organ, skin mechanoreceptors – touch, pressure\\n\\nAδ\\nIII\\nSkin mechanoreceptors, thermoreceptors, and nociceptors – touch, cold, fast pain\\n\\nC\\n\\nIV\\nSkin mechanoreceptors, thermoreceptors, and nociceptors – slow\xa0pain, temperature\\n\\nErlanger and Gasser classification of pain fibres(Shown above):\\n\\nGuillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome (GBS) is an acute autoimmune disease that causes fulminant polyradiculoneuropathy. It is a T-cell-mediated immune response.\\n\\nGBS is usually associated with a history of upper respiratory infection (URI) or acute gastroenteritis (AGE) 1-3 weeks prior. The causative organisms may be\xa0Campylobacter jejuni, cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, and Mycoplasma pneumoniae.\\n\\nIt presents with the following features:\\n\\n* Ascending motor paralysis\xa0(lower limbs > upper limbs)\\n\\n* Sensory deficits may or may not be seen\\n\\n* Deep tendon reflexes—absent\\n\\n* Transient bladder dysfunction—severe cases\\n\\n* Autonomic manifestations—loss of vasomotor control, postural hypotension, and cardiac dysrhythmias.\\n\\nHistological examination shows inflammation of peripheral nerves, manifested as perivenular and endoneurial infiltration by lymphocytes, macrophages, and a few plasma cells. On electron microscopy, an early effect on myelin sheaths is seen. There is\xa0segmental demyelination induced by the actions of activated macrophages.\\n\\nThere are several types of Guillain–Barr\xe9 syndrome:\\n\\n* Demyelinating\\n\\n* Acute Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy (AIDP)—most\xa0common\\n\\n* Axonal\\n\\n* Acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN)\\n\\n* Acute motor-sensory axonal neuropathy (AMSAN)\\n\\n* Regional GBS syndromes:\xa0Miller–Fisher syndrome (MFS)\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A and D: Spinal cord inflammation/demyelination point to conditions like transverse myelitis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8c11a4443b","n":158,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A young adult engages in strenuous exercise outside in cold weather and develops breathing difficulties (dyspnoea), which do not occur at normal temperatures. What is the most likely physiological cause?","options":["Sympathetic stimulation leads to histamine release","Cold-induced airway hyperresponsiveness causing reflex bronchoconstriction","Increased vagal tone causing bronchodilation","Mast cells activated due to cold weather and exercise"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Mast cells activated due to cold weather and exercise\\n\\nThe patient\'s symptoms are classic for exercise-induced asthma (EIA), a condition where exercise triggers bronchospasm due to mast cell activation.\\n\\nExercise-induced asthma usually begins after the end of the exercise, and spontaneous recovery within a few minutes of stopping exercise is seen. It is worse in cold, dry climates than in hot, humid conditions. Prior to exercise, β2-agonists and antileukotrienes are useful. Regular treatment with inhaled corticosteroids, which reduces the population of surface mast cells required for this response, is the most effective method.\\n\\nInhaled beta-2 agonists act like bronchodilators, relaxing the airway muscles and relieving wheezing and shortness of breath. Taking them 15-30 minutes before exercise can significantly prevent EIA symptoms. Trigger avoidance (e.g., cold, dry air) and proper warm-up techniques can further reduce the risk of EIA episodes.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Sympathetic stimulation causes bronchodilation via β₂-adrenergic receptors.\\n\\nOption B: While cold air is a trigger, the underlying physiological cause involves mast cell activation, not simply a reflex.\\n\\nOption C: Increased vagal tone causes bronchoconstriction, not bronchodilation. It would worsen the symptoms.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a4142aecf3","n":159,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"A young female patient presents with ptosis which reportedly worsens in the evening and improves in the morning. What is the most probable cause?","options":["Decreased calcium influx at presynaptic membrane","Increased sodium influx at postsynaptic membrane","Antibody against Ach receptor at postsynaptic membrane","Increased endplate potential at postsynaptic membrane"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Antibody against Ach receptor at postsynaptic membrane\\n\\nThe most probable cause in the given case is myasthenia gravis, which is due to antibodies against the Ach receptor at the postsynaptic membrane.\\n\\nIntermittent ptosis is considered one of the earliest signs of myasthenia gravis. Pyridostigmine is the preferred treatment for symptomatic patients\xa0at this stage of the disease.\\n\\nMyasthenia\xa0gravis is an autoimmune neuromuscular junction disorder that is characterised by\xa0weakness\xa0and\xa0fatigability\xa0of skeletal muscles. It is due to the autoantibody-mediated destruction of the\xa0acetylcholine receptors\xa0(AChRs) at the postsynaptic membrane.\xa0This consequentially leads to a\xa0decreased transmission\xa0at the\xa0neuromuscular\xa0junction. Failure of transmission results in muscle weakness. Hence, the goal is to increase the duration of action of acetylcholine by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase. The most commonly used anticholinesterase drug is pyridostigmine.\\n\\nFemales are more commonly affected than males. It occurs in individuals of all ages, but the highest incidence is seen in women during their twenties and thirties, and in men during their fifties and sixties.\\n\\nClinical features of myasthenia gravis:\\n\\n* Muscle weakness and fatiguability increase with repeated use or late in the day and may improve following rest or sleep. Limb weakness is proximal and asymmetrical\\n\\n* Cranial muscles, particularly the lids and extraocular muscles, are involved early in the course – diplopia, ptosis.\\n\\n* Facial weakness – snarling expression when smiling\\n\\n* Weakness in chewing\\n\\n* Speech with nasal timbre due to the weakness of the palate and tongue\\n\\n* Difficulty in swallowing\\n\\n* Deep tendon reflexes are intact\\n\\n* Myasthenic crisis – acute exacerbation of muscle weakness, especially intercostal and diaphragmatic muscles, resulting in ventilatory failure. It is most commonly due to infection and anticholinesterase medication overuse.\\n\\nMyasthenia gravis is associated with thymoma and other autoimmune diseases such as Hashimoto\'s thyroiditis, Graves\' disease, neuromyelitis optica, Systemic lupus erythematosus, etc.\\n\\nDiagnosis:\\n\\n* Ice-pack test – application of an ice pack over the eyes improves ptosis (decreases the activity of acetylcholinesterase; this increases the amount of acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft and increases transmission).\\n\\n* Antibodies associated with myasthenia gravis: Anti-AChR antibodies, Anti-MuSK antibodies, Anti-LRP4 antibodies\\n\\n* A repetitive nerve stimulation test shows a decremental response.\\n\\n* Edrophonium chloride\xa0or Tensilon test – improvement\xa0of symptoms occurs on injecting edrophonium. Edrophonium is an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor. This increases the amount of acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft and improves transmission.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n* Symptomatic treatment: Anticholinesterase agents – Pyridostigmine is the initial therapy of choice.\\n\\n* Chronic immunomodulating therapy: Glucocorticoids (prednisone) and immunosuppressive drugs (azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil, cyclosporine) are implemented in patients significantly symptomatic on pyridostigmine.\\n\\n* Rapid immunomodulating treatment: IVIG and therapeutic plasma exchange —Used to tide over severe illness (myasthenic crisis) and as \'bridge\' therapy before surgery or starting chronic immunomodulating treatment.\\n\\n* Surgical approach: Thymectomy","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1fc7e497dd","n":160,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"Identify the true statement regarding the given nerve action potential curve.","options":["The threshold point is at A","Na+ influx leads to depolarization between point C to D","The absolute refractory period corresponds to the interval from point B to point D","The membrane conductance for potassium is maximum at point E"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. The absolute refractory period corresponds to the interval from point B to point D\\n\\nThe correct statement regarding the given nerve action potential curve is that point B to D is the refractory period.\\nThe sequence of events occurring during a nerve action potential is as shown below:\\n\\n* Point A represents the resting state where\xa0both channels\xa0are\xa0closed.\\n\\n* Na+\xa0channels open in response to depolarizing stimulus resulting in Na+ influx (Option A). The initiation of action potential occurs only after a certain potential is reached, which is called the threshold potential represented by point B.\\n\\n* After this, the sodium influx causes the\xa0opening\xa0of more\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0through a\xa0positive feedback\xa0mechanism.\xa0Rapid depolarization\xa0occurs to reach point C.\\n\\n* At point C,\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0close\xa0and\xa0K+\xa0channels\xa0open, causing\xa0repolarization due to\xa0K+\xa0efflux. (Option B)\\n\\n* Point D represents continued repolarization due to K+ efflux.\\n\\n* At point E,\xa0Na+\xa0channels are\xa0reset\xa0and are ready to respond to another depolarizing stimulus. During this phase, the\xa0K+\xa0channels remain open and\xa0slowly return\xa0to the\xa0closed\xa0state, causing\xa0after-hyperpolarization. As a stronger stimulus is required to reach the threshold potential, the excitability of nerve fibre is reduced at point E.\\n\\n* As\xa0both channels close, the membrane returns to resting membrane potential back to point A.\\n\\nPoint B-D is a part of the refractory period. During this period, the application of even a stronger stimulus will not elicit an action potential.\\n\\nThe refractory period is divided into an absolute refractory period and a relative refractory period. During the absolute refractory period, the nerve will not be excited no matter how strong a\xa0stimulus is given. However, during the relative refractory period, stronger than normal stimuli can cause excitation.\\n\\nPearl PF0017: Resting membrane potential (RMP) in tissues\\n\\nTissue\\n\\nRMP(mV)\\n\\nNeuron\\n\\n-70\\n\\nSkeletal muscle\\n\\n-90\\n\\nMyocardial muscle\\n\\n-90\\n\\nSmooth muscle\\n\\n-20 to -65","img":false,"images":["/bimg/f8498ea12152098f53f8.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/eb538fbdcaee5f34e3cf.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-9247420d1a","n":161,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"In malignant hyperthermia, a genetic mutation in the ryanodine receptor (RyR1) causes a potentially fatal reaction to certain anaesthetics. What is the primary ionic abnormality caused by this receptor?","options":["Excessive calcium influx from extracellular fluid","Reduced ATP binding to actin-myosin complex","Excessive calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum in skeletal muscle","Defective sodium channel leading to muscle hyperexcitability"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Excessive calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum in skeletal muscle\\n\\nThe primary ionic abnormality caused by mutation in the RyR1 receptor is excessive calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in skeletal muscle.\\n\\nMalignant hyperthermia is a genetic condition in which mutated RyR1 receptors are present in the skeletal muscles. In the presence of certain triggers, this mutated RyR1 causes severe dysfunction in the RyR1 channels. This causes abnormal calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum, leading to excessive cytoplasmic calcium concentration, resulting in sustained muscle contraction, rigidity, increased heat production, acidosis, hyperkalaemia, and increased end-tidal CO₂. Patients with positive family histories and patients with congenital myopathies are at increased risk for the development of this condition.\\n\\nThe process of sequestering excess Ca\xb2⁺ consumes large amounts of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) and generates excess heat (hyperthermia). The muscle cell is damaged by ATP depletion and high temperatures, and cellular constituents \\"leak\\" into the circulation, including potassium, myoglobin, creatine, phosphate, and creatine kinase.\\n\\nThe key clinical features are\\n\\n* Succinylcholine-induced masseter muscle rigidity (causing trismus) or generalized muscle rigidity\\n\\n* Tachycardia\\n\\n* Hypercarbia (increased CO₂ production) and reduced oxygen saturation\\n\\n* Hyperthermia (rise of 1\xb0 Celsius can occur every 5 minutes)\\n\\n* Arrhythmia\\n\\n* Hypertension\\n\\n* Myoglobinuria and myoglobinaemia indicate muscle damage\\n\\n* Metabolic derangements like hyperkalemia and mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis\\n\\nWhen volatile anaesthetics or succinylcholine are used, malignant hyperthermia should be suspected in case of an unexpected rise in end-tidal CO₂ (ETCO₂). This is considered to be one of the earliest and most sensitive indicators of malignant hyperthermia. It can be supported by taking arterial blood gas reports with metabolic acidosis.\\n\\nTreatment is to discontinue the triggering agents, i.e., succinylcholine, followed by administration of 100% oxygen. Dantrolene sodium is the drug of choice. It is given rapidly at a dose of 2.5 mg/kg intravenously and can be repeated every 5-10 minutes till the attack subsides. Metabolic acidosis and hyperkalaemia need to be corrected. Hyperthermia must be controlled by cooling the patient. Continuous monitoring in intensive care is required for 24 to 48 hours.\\n\\nDantrolene is a direct-acting skeletal muscle relaxant that acts on the RyR1 (ryanodine receptor) calcium channels in the sarcoplasmic reticulum of skeletal muscles and inhibits calcium-induced calcium release. This results in the uncoupling of membrane depolarisation and muscle contraction, thus inhibiting muscle contraction.\\n\\nDrugs triggering malignant hyperthermia:\\n\\n* Succinylcholine\\n\\n* Halothane\\n\\n* Isoflurane\\n\\n* Sevoflurane\\n\\nPearl PF1601: Malignant hyperthermia key features\\n\\nPathophysiology:\\n\\n* Prolonged opening of functionally altered ryanodine receptors, leading to uncontrolled release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum resulting in sustained muscle contraction.\\n\\nDrugs known to trigger malignant hyperthermia:\\n\\n* Inhaled anesthetics: Halothane, Isoflurane, Sevoflurane, Desflurane, Ether, Methoxyflurane\\n\\n* Depolarising muscle relaxant:\xa0Succinylcholine\\n\\nClinical signs:\\n\\n* Increased metabolism:\xa0Increased EtCO2, decreased SpO2, metabolic acidosis\\n\\n* Increased sympathetic activity:\xa0Tachycardia,\xa0hypertension, arrythmia\\n\\n* Muscle damage: Masseter spasm, generalised\xa0rigidity, hyperkalemia, rhabdomyolysis, myoglobinuria\\n\\n* Hyperthermia: Fever, sweating\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n* Discontinuation of all anesthetic agents\\n\\n* Hyperventilation with 100% O2\\n\\n* Dantrolene: 2.5 mg/kg intravenously every 5 min until the episode is terminated (upper limit - 10 mg/kg).\\n\\n* Correction of acid-base, electrolyte abnormality\\n\\n* Cooling the patient","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fb677c6752","n":162,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"Which among the following best describes neuropraxia?","options":["Damaged axon and nerve sheath","Damaged axon, intact nerve sheath","Intact axon, damaged nerve sheath","Intact axon and nerve sheath"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Intact axon, damaged nerve sheath\\n\\nNeurapraxia causes temporary damage to the myelin sheath while leaving the nerve intact and is a transient condition.\\n\\nNeurapraxia is a peripheral nervous system disorder characterised by a temporary loss of motor and sensory function caused by nerve conduction blockages. It is typically caused by a blunt neural injury\xa0to muscle fibres and skeletal nerve fibres, resulting in prolonged pressure buildup on the nerve. As a result of this pressure, ischaemia develops, and a neural lesion forms, which results in oedema. This lesion causes a complete or partial blockage of action potential conduction over a segment of a nerve fibre, resulting in a reduction or loss of function in parts of the neural connection downstream from the lesion, causing muscle weakness.\\n\\nNeurapraxia is the least severe type of peripheral nerve injury. Tinel\'s sign is negative in neuropraxia.\\n\\nIn neurapraxia, there is a temporary failure of nerve function without physical axon disruption. Degeneration does not occur, and there is the return of normal axonal function over hours to months, often in the 2-4 week range. Tinel\'s sign is negative.\\n\\nTinel\'s sign is used to assess nerve regeneration following injury. The nerve is percussed along its course to elicit a tingling sensation. The point of tingling marks the site of newly sprouting axons.\\n\\nA neuroma is an overgrowth of fibrous tissue and randomly sprouting axons following nerve injury. A neuroma may form following axonotmesis and neurotmesis. In neurotmesis, neuroma does not bridge the break in continuity and is called end neuroma. The Tinel sign is positive and nonprogressive. In axonotmesis, the neuroma formed bridges the continuity and is called neuroma in continuity. The Tinel sign is positive and progressive.\\n\\nAccording to Seddon\'s classification, peripheral nerve injuries can be classified as\\n\\n* Neurapraxia – The\xa0temporary failure of nerve function without physical axon disruption. Degeneration does not occur, and there is the return of normal axonal function\xa0over hours to months, often in the 2-4 week range.\\n\\n* Axonotmesis – It is the disruption of axons and myelin with intact surrounding connective tissue. Axons degenerate proximally and distally from the area of injury. Distal degeneration is called Wallerian degeneration. Recovery may occur after 18 months.\\n\\n* Neurotmesis – It\xa0is the disruption of axons and endoneurial tubes, and degeneration of proximal and distal axons occurs.\\n\\nPearl PF1196: Seddon\'s classification of nerve injuries\\n\\nSeddon\'s\xa0classification includes 3 groups\\n\\n1. Neurapraxia\\n2. Axonotmesis\\n3. Neurotmesis\\n\\nNeurapraxia:\\n\\nReversible physiologic conduction block. Occurs due to mechanical pressure causing segmental demyelination.\\n\\nE.g.,Saturday night palsy, torniquet, and crutch palsy\\n\\nAxonotmesis:\\n\\nAxonal interruption. Conduction is lost but the nerve is in continuity and neural tubes are intact.\\n\\nE.g.,Closed fractures and dislocations\\n\\nNeurotmesis:\\n\\nDivision of the nerve trunk.\xa0Endoneurial tube destruction also occurs.\\n\\nE.g., Complete section of nerve in open fractures","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a215386bac","n":163,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"Which hormone is responsible for the development of the structure labeled as 1 in the given image?","options":["Oxytocin","Estrogen","Prolactin","Progesterone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Estrogen\\n\\nThe structure labeled as 1 in the given image is the lactiferous duct, which develops under the influence of estrogen.\\n\\nEstrogen stimulates the hypertrophy and proliferation of the ductal system and the stroma. It also causes hypertrophy of the uterus during pregnancy and relaxes the pelvic ligaments and pubic symphysis to aid in parturition.\\n\\nProgesterone (Option D) leads to lobular growth and alveolar budding and causes the alveolar cells to become secretory. It also inhibits uterine contractions and is thus responsible for maintaining pregnancy. It stimulates fallopian tube secretions, which provide nutrition to the developing morula and blastocyst.\\n\\nProlactin (Option C) a hormone from the anterior pituitary, stimulates the secretion of milk from the alveolar cells. Suckling stimulates this secretion. Prolactin levels are high in pregnancy. However, there is limited milk production prior to delivery because estrogen and progesterone inhibit this process. Thus, after delivery, as their levels fall, the lactogenic action of prolactin takes over.\\n\\nMilk is secreted continuously into the alveoli but is only ejected into the ducts during suckling. When the baby suckles, oxytocin is released from the posterior pituitary. It leads to milk ejection due to the contraction of the myoepithelial cells that surround the alveoli. This process is known as the milk letdown reflex.\\n\\nChanges in breast\\n\\nHormone\\n\\nDevelopment of lactiferous ducts\\n\\nEstrogen\\n\\nDevelopment of secretory alveoli\\n\\nProgesterone\\n\\nContraction of myoepithelial cells\\n\\nOxytocin\\n\\nSecretion of milk from\xa0 secretory alveoli\\n\\nProlactin","img":false,"images":["/bimg/18e236fedd16565b2fc3.jpg"],"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/259fff410462c9ed4e1c.webp"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/2c9c551e70888782a2fe.webp"}]},{"bankId":"qb-436599a87f","n":164,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE July 2025","text":"Which one of the following is increased in hypothalamic hamartoma?","options":["Prolactin","Estrogen","Inhibin","GnRH"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. GnRH\\n\\nHypothalamic hamartoma is caused by increased release of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH).\\n\\nHypothalamic hamartoma, a neuronal migration defect, is the most common cause of organic central precocious puberty. The onset of breast development before the age of 8 years in females and the onset of testicular development (>4 mL) before the age of 9 years in males are suggestive of central precocious puberty.\\n\\nIt is a congenital malformation that consists of ectopically located neural tissue characterized by\\n\\n* Early-onset of puberty\\n\\n* Seizures associated with unnatural crying or laughing episodes are known as gelastic seizures.\\n\\nTuberculous meningitis, tuberous sclerosis, and astrocytomas can also cause\xa0central precocious puberty.\\n\\nHypothalamic hamartomas are congenital non-progressive lesions in the hypothalamus that occur during foetal development. Two anatomical subtypes are recognised depending on the location of the mass-like lesion. The first type is the intrahypothalamic lesion that connects with the posterior hypothalamus and third ventricle; they appear near the mammillary bodies and are usually associated with gelastic seizures. The second type is the parahypothalamic or pedunculated lesion located near the anterior hypothalamus, tuber cinereum, or pituitary stalk and attaches only to the floor of the third ventricle. It is usually associated with signs of precocious puberty caused by increasing the release of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). These lesions typically develop a disabling course presenting with multiple seizure types, cognitive decline, and psychiatric symptoms","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1bf28ebd4a","n":165,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"A mother complains of breast cramps during breastfeeding her child. Which hormone is responsible for this?","options":["Progestrone","Estrogen","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Oxytocin\\n\\nOxytocin helps in the ejection of milk by myoepithelial contraction which is felt as cramps.\\n\\nFunctions of oxytocin are:\\n\\n* Milk ejection (Myoepithelial contraction)\\n\\n* ADH like action in high doses (reduces urine output)\\n\\n* Uterine contraction\\n\\nHormones acting on breast\\n\\nFunction\\nDefinition\\nHormone\\n\\nMammogenesis\\nBreast growth and development\\n\\nEstrogen (Ductal growth)\\n\\nProgesterone (Alveolar growth)\\n\\nLactogenesis\\nInititation of milk secretion\\nProlactin\\n\\nGalactokinesis\\nEjection of milk\\nOxytocin\\n\\nGalactopoiesis\\nMaintanence of lactation\\nProlactin","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c52f0dad28","n":166,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"A young patient sustained a head injury following a road traffic accident. His family members complain that his behavior has changed following the incident. He is extremely curious and tries touching everything. He forgets everything rapidly, and he tries to keep everything in his mouth. He even tried to eat the plates. They have also noticed him squeezing his genitals. Which of the following structures is most likely to be damaged in this patient?","options":["Gray matter around aqueduct of Sylvius","Amygdala","Lateral nuclei of hypothalamus","Anterior Cingulate gyri"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Amygdala\\n\\nIncreased curiosity, hyperorality, memory loss, and increased sex drive are the features of Kluver Bucy syndrome. This occurs due to the damage to the\xa0amygdala.\\n\\nFeatures of Kluver Bucy syndrome are:\\n\\n* Fearlessness\\n\\n* Increased curiosity\\n\\n* Memory loss\\n\\n* Patient tries to keep everything in the mouth, even the solid objects that are not meant for eating\\n\\n* Increased sex drive\\n\\nFunctions of amygdala:\\n\\nIt receives neuronal signals from\xa0the neocortex of the temporal, parietal, and occipital lobes, especially from the auditory and visual association areas. It transmits signals to the same cortical areas, thalamus, hypothalamus, and hippocampus. Stimulation of the amygdala leads to alterations in arterial pressure, heart rate, gastrointestinal motility, piloerection, and abnormal tonic circling and clinic movements. It also has role in olfaction.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A-\xa0Gray matter around the aqueduct of Sylvius is the punishment center. Stimulation of punishment centers leads to the inhibition of reward and pleasure centers completely. Punishment and fear will be more prominent than pleasure and reward.\\n\\nOption C- Lateral nuclei of hypothalamus primarily control hunger and feeding behavior. Stimulation of this area gives a sense of reward\\n\\nOption D- Damage to the anterior cingulate gyri\xa0releases the rage centers of septum and hypothalamus from the prefrontal inhibitory influence. Therefore the patient becomes very aggressive.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fcb94ba8e0","n":167,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"As filtrate flows through PCT, the concentration of all of the following decreases except:","options":["Aminoacid","Glucose","Chloride","Bicarbonate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Chloride\\n\\nAs the filtrate flows through the proximal convoluted tubule, the concentration of the chloride increases.\\n\\n* The concentration of chloride in the early part of the pCT is ≈105 mEq/L.\\n\\n* As the sodium is reabsorbed, it preferentially carries glucose, amino acids, and bicarbonate.\\n\\n* This leaves behind the solute, that has a higher concentration of the chloride, which as it reaches the second half of the PCT is ≈140 mEq/L.\\n\\nPearl PF0563: Summary of Transport Across Proximal and Distal Portions of the Nephron\\n\\nProximal Tubule\\n\\nReabsorption\\n\\nSecretion\\n\\n67% of filtered Na+ actively reabsorbed, not subject to control; Cl– follows passively\\n\\nAll filtered glucose and amino acids reabsorbed by secondary active transport; not subject to control\\n\\nVariable amounts of filtered PO43- and other electrolytes reabsorbed; subject to control\\n\\n65% of filtered H2O osmotically reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\n50% of filtered urea passively reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\nAlmost all filtered K+ reabsorbed; not subject to control\\n\\nVariable H+ secretion, depending on acid-base status of body\\n\\nOrganic ion secretion; not subject to control\\n\\nDistal tubule\\n\\nReabsorption\\nSecretion\\n\\nVariable Na+ reabsorption, controlled by aldosterone; Cl– follows passively\\n\\nVariable H2O reabsorption, controlled by vasopressin\\n\\nVariable H+ secretion, depending on acid-base status of body\\n\\nVariable K+ secretion, controlled by aldosterone","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-50ffe3c306","n":168,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"Corticospinal tract crosses at ?","options":["Midbrain","Pons","Upper medulla","Lower medulla"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Lower medulla\\n\\nThe corticospinal tract crosses over at lower medulla.\\n\\nCorticospinal fibres have the following course as shown below:\\n\\nCerbral cortex\xa0→ Corona radiata → Posterior limb of the internal capsule → Crus cerebri of cerebral peduncles\xa0→ Ventral part of the pons\xa0→ Pyramids of medulla oblongata (most fibers decussate here)\xa0→ Anterior and lateral white columns of the spinal cord (as anterior and lateral corticospinal tracts, respectively)\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nMost fibers (80% fibers) decussate (cross) in the medullary pyramids in the lower medulla and descend in the lateral white matter of the spinal cord to form the lateral corticospinal tract. They control the movements of the contralateral limbs.\\n\\nThe remaining 20% make up the ventral corticospinal tract. These fibers remain uncrossed until reaching the spinal cord where axons terminate bilaterally on spinal interneurons antecedent to motor neurons. These fibers control the movements of the axial musculature.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/37f3f53e4f707b6eeea1.jpg"}]},{"bankId":"qb-20d1ca6619","n":169,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"What is the reason that hemoglobin cannot pass through the glomerulus in a normal physiological state?","options":["It is a large molecule bounded with another protein.","It gets attracted to the positive charge of the glomerular filtration barrier.","Both A and B","None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. It is a large molecule bounded with another protein.\\n\\nThe haemoglobin (Hb) cannot pass through the glomerulus in a normal physiological state because it is a large molecule bound with another protein.\\n\\nThe Hb molecule released into the blood from RBCs is immediately bound\xa0by a protein called haptoglobin\xa0(size: 12.4 nm). It forms the haemoglobin-haptoglobin complex which\xa0is large enough to be prevented from crossing the\xa0glomerular filtration barrier.\\n\\nFree haemoglobin undergoes extensive glomerular filtration, which is blocked by Haptoglobin. The filtration of Hb leads to renal tubule iron deposition (brown) and hemoglobinuria. This is prevented by the sequestration of Hb by haptoglobin.\\n\\nAnother reason is because of the negative charge of Hb. Both the Hb and glomerular filtration barrier\xa0are\xa0negatively charged. Hence Hb is repelled\xa0away from the filtration barrier.\\n\\nThe glomerular filtration barrier consists of:\\n\\n* The endothelium of the capillary\\n\\n* A\xa0basement membrane\xa0- contains type IV collagen, glycosaminoglycan heparan sulfate which has a negative charge.\\n\\n* A layer of epithelial cells (podocytes) surrounding the outer surface of the capillary basement membrane.\\n\\nThe capillary endothelium is perforated by thousands of small holes called fenestrae, which measure from 4 nm up to 8nm. Therefore, neutral substances with molecular diameters of less than 4 nm are freely filtered, and filtration of those with diameters of more than 8 nm approaches zero.\\n\\nIn addition to the size of the particles, the filtration also depends mainly on the charge of the particle being filtered. The glomerular capillary wall consists of highly negatively charged proteins called sialoproteins, which repel the negatively charged substances (anions). The glomerular filtration barrier is negatively charged, which will repel most of the negative charges (anionic substances). Whereas, the cationic substances are easily filtered.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fad66fa2b4","n":170,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"Which of the cells secrete surfactant in lungs?","options":["Type 1 pneumocyte","Alveolar macrophage","Dust cells","Type 2 pneumocyte"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Type 2 pneumocyte\\n\\nType 2 pneumocytes\xa0secrete surfactant in the lungs.\\n\\nAlveolar Lining Epithelial Cells\\n\\nTwo types\\n\\n* Type I cells – The primary lining cells of the alveoli (squamous epithelium); Helps in gas exchange\\n\\n* Type II cells\xa0 – Granular pneumocytes produce surfactant\\n\\nType II cells make 60% of epithelial cells in alveoli but type I cells form 95% of the total surface area because of its flat surface.\\n\\nType II cells\xa0are\xa0stem cells which help in replenishing damaged Type 1 pneumocytes in conditions like drug-induced pulmonary toxicity by amiodarone, bleomycin etc.\\n\\nOther cells –\xa0Pulmonary alveolar macrophages (PAMs/type 3 alveolar cells), lymphocytes, plasma cells, neuroendocrine cells (APUD cells-Amine precursor uptake and decarboxylation), and mast cells.\\n\\nClinical correlate :\\n\\n* Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS)\xa0in preterm neonates is due to insufficient surfactant production.\\n\\n* Lung maturity\xa0of the fetus can be assessed by determining the ratio of lecithin to sphingomyelin\xa0(L:S ratio)\xa0in the amniotic fluid. A ratio of\xa0>2\xa0indicates maturity.\\n\\n* Shake test/Bubble test/Clement test: Formation of stable foam in the presence of surfactant.\\n\\n* Phosphatidylglycerol levels in Amniotic fluid.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/ff59920cf0e50e9fd587.png"}]},{"bankId":"qb-ef3b643abc","n":171,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"Which of the following hormones regulate serum calcium levels?","options":["Calcitonin","Parathormone","Vit D","All the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. All the above\\n\\nCalcitonin, parathormone and Vit D regulate serum calcium levels.\\n\\nSummary of hormones regulating calcium levels\\n\\nFeatures\\nPTH\\nVitamin D\\nCalcitonin\\n\\nStimulus Receptors\\n↓ Ca osteoblast\\n\\n↓ Ca,P,\\n\\n↑ PTH osteoblast\\n\\n↑ serum Ca osteoclast\\n\\nBone\\n↑ resorption\\n↑resorption\\n↓ resorption\\n\\nKidney\\n↑ Ca reabsorption↓P reabsorption\\n\\n↑ Ca reabsorption\\n\\n↑ P reabsorption\\n\\nIntestine\\n↑ Ca absorption\\n↑ Ca absorption\\n\\nSerum calcium\\n↑\\n↑\\n↓\\n\\nSerum phosphorus\\n↓\\n↑\\n↓","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-be5377ae69","n":172,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE Jun 2021","text":"Which of the following reflex is responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension?","options":["Bezold-Jarisch reflex","Bainbridge reflex","Cushing reflex","J reflex"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bainbridge reflex\\n\\nReflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is\xa0Bainbridge Reflex.\\n\\nThe stretch receptors of the atria that\xa0elicit the Bainbridge reflex transmit their afferent signals\xa0through the vagus nerves to the medulla of the brain.\xa0Then efferent signals are transmitted back through vagal\xa0and sympathetic nerves to increase heart rate and strength\xa0of heart contraction. Thus, this reflex helps prevent\xa0damming of blood in the veins, atria, and pulmonary\xa0circulation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Bezold–Jarisch Reflex:\xa0Activation of chemosensitive vagal C fibers in the cardiopulmonary region causes profound bradycardia, hypotension and a brief period of apnoea followed by rapid shallow breathing.\\n\\nOption C: Cushing Reflex:\xa0When intracranial\xa0pressure\xa0is increased, the blood supply to Rostral\xa0Ventro-Lateral Medulla (RVLM) neurons is compromised.\xa0The local hypoxia and hypercapnia cause an increase in discharge from the neurons, producing hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration.\\n\\nOption D: J Reflex:\xa0The stimulation of the J-receptors (C receptors/juxta-pulmonary capillary receptors) causes apnea followed by rapid breathing, bradycardia, and hypotension.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-467b9bf43f","n":173,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"A 12-year-old girl is undergoing pubertal changes. Her tanner stage is stage 4 for breast development and pubic hair scale. Which of the following pubertal changes is not due to estrogen?","options":["Vaginal cornification","Pubic and axillary hair growth","Cervical mucus production","Menstruation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pubic and axillary hair growth\\n\\nPubarche or development of axillary and pubic hair is not due to estrogen. It is due to testosterone (in both the sexes).\\n\\nPuberty in females :\\n\\n* Involves the beginning of menstrual cycles (menarche), breast development (thelarche), and an increase in adrenal androgen secretion (adrenarche).\\n\\n* Estradiol induces the development of secondary sex characteristics, including the breasts and reproductive tract, and increased fat in the hips.\\n\\n* Estrogens also regulate the growth spurt at puberty, vaginal cornification and cervical mucus production, induce closure of the epiphyses, have a positive effect in maintaining bone formation, and can antagonize the degrading actions of parathyroid hormone on bone.\\n\\nIn young girls before puberty the vaginal epithelium is thin and comprised of only basal and parabasal layers.\xa0During the reproductive years, the vaginal epithelium thickens and develops a distinct cornified layer due to the influence of estrogen. Cervical mucous production and menstruation are also changes occuring under the influence of estrogen and also progesterone.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f88b8768ad","n":174,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with a six-month history of recurrent, large-volume, watery diarrhea. She reports experiencing multiple episodes per day, often waking her at night. She has also noted significant weight loss and episodes of muscle weakness. Laboratory findings reveal hypokalemia. Imaging studies of the abdomen show a mass in the tail of the pancreas. Which pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor is the most likely cause of her symptoms?","options":["VIPoma","Glucagonoma","Insulinoma","Gastrinoma"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. VIPoma\\n\\nThis patient with recurrent, large-volume, watery diarrhea with laboratory evidence of hypokalemia and a mass in the pancreatic tail is most likely afflicted by a VIPoma.\\n\\nVasoactive intestinal peptide-secreting tumours (VIPomas) are\xa0 pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours that secrete excessive amounts of vasoactive intestinal peptides (VIP)\xa0which are responsible for the\xa0secretory\xa0watery\xa0diarrhoea\xa0and other symptoms. The patient commonly presents with severe intermittent watery diarrhoea leading to\xa0dehydration,\xa0and weakness from fluid and\xa0electrolyte losses.\\n\\nElectrolyte abnormalities\xa0in VIPoma include:\\n\\n* Metabolic acidosis\xa0and\xa0hypokalemia-\xa0due to a large amount of GI loss and bicarbonate wasting\\n\\n* Hypochlorhydria- due to the direct gastric acid inhibitory effect of VIP\\n\\n* Hypomagnesemia- due to severe diarrhoea\\n\\n* Hyperglycemia-\xa0due to the increased glycogenolytic activity of VIP\\n\\n* Hypercalcemia- due to dehydration, coincidental MEN1 syndrome, or secretion by the tumor of a calcitrophic peptide\\n\\nVasoactive intestinal peptide-secreting tumor (VIPoma) is also referred to as Verner–Morrison syndrome/\xa0WDHA syndrome:\\n\\nWDHA stands for:\\n\\n* WD - Watery\xa0Diarrhea\\n\\n* H – Hypokalemia\\n\\n* A\xa0–\xa0Achlorhydria\\n\\nMetastases commonly occurs in the liver, lymph nodes, bones, and kidney.\\n\\nDiagnosis can be made by the demonstration of an\xa0elevated plasma VIP level\xa0(upto 250 to 500 pg/ml) and a history of\xa0large-volume diarrhea\xa0(> 5L/day). A CT scan localizes most VIPomas.\xa0EUS\xa0is the most\xa0sensitive\xa0imaging method.\\n\\nWhile suspecting a case of VIPoma it is essential to conduct a VIP assay so as to confirm the presence of other tumors of\xa0neural crest origin\xa0such as neuroblastoma or pheochromocytoma.\\n\\nThe\xa0medical management\xa0of VIPoma:\\n\\n*\\n\\nInitial medical management is symptomatic with correction of fluids and electrolyte imbalance.\\n\\n*\\n\\nSomatostatin analogs like\xa0octreotide\xa0and\xa0lanreotide\xa0inhibit secretion of VIP and are used for symptomatic control.\\n\\n*\\n\\nGlucocorticoids\xa0are used in patients\xa0refractory\xa0to somatostatin analogs.\\n\\n*\\n\\nInterferon-alpha\xa0has also been used in patients in patients not responding to the above medications.\\n\\nThe\xa0surgical management\xa0of VIPoma:\\n\\n* Complete resection is the treatment of choice for primary tumors and is usually a\xa0distal pancreatectomy.\xa0If the tumor cannot be excised completely, surgical debulkingmay be done\\n\\n* Radiofrequency ablation\xa0and cryoablation\\n\\n* Hepatic artery embolization\xa0is a palliative treatment measure in patients with\xa0unresectable\xa0hepatic metastases.\\n\\nPearl PF0939: Pancreatic Endocrine Neoplasms\\n\\nEndocrine neoplasm\\n\\nSyndrome\\n\\nGlucagonoma\\n\\nMild diabetes mellitus, a characteristic skin rash (necrolytic migratory erythema), and anemia\\n\\nInsulinoma\\n\\nClinically significant hypoglycemia\\n\\nGastrinoma\\n\\nPeptic ulceration often unresponsive to therapy and in unusual locations such as the jejunum\\n\\nSomatostatinoma\\n\\nDiabetes mellitus, cholelithiasis, steatorrhea, and hypochlorhydria\\n\\nVIPoma\\n\\nWatery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria, or WDHA syndrome\\n\\nPancreatic polypeptide-secreting endocrine tumors\\n\\nPresent as mass lesions as even high plasma levels of this hormone fail to cause symptoms\\n\\n* Islet hyperplasia (previously known as nesidioblastosis) is seen in maternal diabetes, Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome , and rare mutations in the β-cell K+-channel protein or sulfonylurea receptor.\\n\\n* In maternal diabetes, the fetal islets respond to hyperglycemia by increasing their size and number. In the postnatal period, these hyperactive islets may be responsible for serious episodes of hypoglycemia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0ce14c3511","n":175,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Cervical dilation and delivery on uterine contraction is similar to which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Milk ejection by oxytocin","Water retention by ADH","FSH secretion by anterior pituitary","None of the above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Milk ejection by oxytocin\\n\\nMilk ejection by oxytocin follows a stimulation mechanism similar to uterine contraction in delivery (cervical dilatation).\\n\\nThe physiological actions of oxytocin are:\\n\\n* Contraction of the pregnant uterus: Cervical dilatation transmits signals to the hypothalamus and stimulates the secretion of oxytocin, which acts on the smooth muscles of the uterus and contracts it.\\n\\n* Milk ejection from breasts: Suckling reflex transmits signals to the hypothalamus which causes release of oxytocin which acts on the myoepithelial cells of the breast. This results in ejection of milk.\\n\\nThe action of oxytocin are examples of positive feedback mechanism.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b580c857cd","n":176,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"If the diameter of an artery was found to be reduced to one-third. By what value does the resistance of the artery increase?","options":["9 times","81 times","27 times","18 times"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 81 times\\n\\nThe resistance increases by 81 times.\\n\\nThe relationship between the flow in a long narrow tube, the viscosity of the fluid, and the radius of the tube is expressed by the Poiseuille-Hagen formula.\\n\\nAirflow can be derived from\xa0Poiseuille- Hagen formula:\\n\\nQ =π\xa0ΔPr4/8ηL\\n\\nWhere\\n\\n* Q is the flow rate\\n\\n* π is pie symbol\\n\\n* ΔP is the pressure difference\\n\\n* r is radius\\n\\n* η is viscosity\\n\\n* L is length\\n\\nAlso,\\n\\nFlow rate (Q) = pressure difference (ΔP)/ resistance (R).\\n\\nTherefore, substituting this in Poiseuille- Hagen formula,\\n\\nΔP/ R =\xa0π\xa0ΔPr4/8ηl\\n\\n1/R =\xa0π\xa0r4/8ηl\\n\\nR\xa0=\xa08ηl/\xa0π\xa0r4\\n\\nTherefore it can be derived that\xa0airway resistance\xa0is\xa0inversely proportional\xa0to the\xa0radius\xa0by the\xa0power of four.\\n\\nIn the given question, the diameter is reduced to one-third. So, the radius is also reduced to one-third. Hence, vessel resistance will increase by 34 or 81 times.\\n\\nWith\xa0Poiseuille- Hagen formula we might feel that the smallest airways are the major site of resistance. However, the contribution by the smallest airways is very little to the overall total resistance. This is because, the airway branches in each generation exist in\xa0parallel\xa0rather than in series. As the parallel branching increases, the resistance decreases. (Total cross-sectional diameter of small airways put together is high). Thus the overall resistance of the small airways is very small. Also,\xa0airflow velocity decreases substantially as the effective cross-sectional area increases (i.e., the flow becomes laminar).\\n\\nOther factors affecting airway resistance :\\n\\n* Lung volume, as lung volume increases the resistance decreases and vice versa. Lung volume increases the transairway pressure thus increasing the caliber of the airway.\\n\\n* Airway mucus, edema, and\xa0contraction\xa0of bronchial smooth muscle increase the airway resistance. Its clinical application is seen in asthma, bronchitis, and emphysema\\n\\n* The\xa0density\xa0and\xa0viscosity\xa0of inspired gas also contribute to airway resistance. A gas with more density imparts more resistance.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/0f3b6b33eda89e5d15c9.png"}]},{"bankId":"qb-1fbffec377","n":177,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Insulin acts on glucose metabolism by______","options":["↑ facilitated diffusion of glucose","↓ permeability of glucose across cell membrane","↑ reabsorption of glucose from renal tubules","↑ glucose reabsorption from intestine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. ↑ facilitated diffusion of glucose\\n\\nInsulin stimulates the entry of glucose into cells via facilitated diffusion by increasing the number of glucose transporters (GLUTs) in the cell membrane.\\n\\nThis type of insulin-dependent glucose transport via GLUT 4 is observed in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and adipose tissues.\\n\\nPearl PF0553: Glucose Transporters\\n\\nFunction\\n\\nMajor sites of expression\\n\\nSecondary active transport\\n\\n(Na + -glucose cotransport)\\n\\nSGLT 1\\n\\nAbsorption of glucose\\n\\nSmall intestine, renal tubules\\n\\nSGLT 2\\n\\nAbsorption of glucose\\n\\nRenal tubules\\n\\nFacilitated diffusion\\n\\nGLUT 1\\n\\nBasal glucose uptake\\n\\nPlacenta, blood-brain barrier, brain, red cells, kidneys, colon, many other organs\\n\\nGLUT 2\\n\\nβ-cell glucose sensor; transport out of intestinal and renal epithelial cells\\n\\nβ cells of islets, liver, epithelial cells of small intestine, kidneys\\n\\nGLUT 3\\n\\nBasal glucose uptake\\n\\nBrain, placenta, kidneys, many other organs\\n\\nGLUT 4\\n\\nInsulin-stimulated glucose uptake\\n\\nSkeletal and cardiac muscle, adipose tissue, other tissues\\n\\nGLUT 5\\n\\nFructose transport\\n\\nJejunum, sperm","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ec86aed066","n":178,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"What is the affinity of carbon monoxide to hemoglobin in comparison to oxygen?","options":["100","4","250","150"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 250\\n\\nCO binds to hemoglobin with an affinity that is 250 times that of O2 to form carboxyhemoglobin.\\n\\nCO also\xa0increases the O2 affinity of remaining free hemoglobin sites (causes left shift\xa0of the O2 - hemoglobin dissociation curve).\\n\\n* Curve A - Myoglobin O2-dissociation curve\\n\\n* Curve B - Normal Hb O2-dissociation curve\\n\\n* Curve C-\xa0CO binds to hemoglobin and prevents\xa0O2 binding\\n\\n* Curve D - Anemia\\n\\nCarbon monoxide (CO) reacts with hemoglobin to form\xa0carbon monoxyhemoglobin\xa0(carboxyhemoglobin or COHb).\xa0The affinity of hemoglobin for CO is\xa0210 times\xa0its affinity for O2, and COHb liberates CO very slowly. An additional difficulty is that when COHb is present, the dissociation\xa0curve of the remaining HbO2 shifts to the left, decreasing the amount of O2 released.\\n\\nTreatment of CO poisoning consists of ventilation with oxygen since oxygen hastens the dissociation of carboxyhemoglobin.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/bimg/151818a35e4268666ea6.png"}]},{"bankId":"qb-4370a94422","n":179,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following enzymes is responsible for the conversion of T4 to T3, in the peripheral tissue?","options":["Iodothyronine deiodinase I","Iodothyronine deiodinase III","Iodothyronine deiodinase II","Iodotyrosine deiodinase"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Iodothyronine deiodinase I\\n\\nPeripheral T3 conversion from T4 is catalyzed by the enzyme iodothryronine deiodinase I. T4 undergoes deiodination in extrathyroidal tissue to form T3, which is the active form of the hormone. It is expressed predominantly in the peripheral tissues.\\n\\nIodothyronine deiodinase II: Iodothyronine deiodinase type II (DIO2) is an enzyme responsible for the activation of T4 to T3 in certain specific tissues, such as the hypothalamus and pituitary gland.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2d40ae9d66","n":180,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following is correct regarding oxytocin?","options":["It is responsible for galactokinesis","It can be given by oral route","It is secreted by anterior pituitary","In higher doses, it exerts diuretic like action"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. It is responsible for galactokinesis\\n\\nOxytocin is responsible for galactokinesis (milk ejection reflex).\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\n* It can not be given by oral route because it has emotional side effects.\\n\\n* It is secreted by the posterior pituitary.\\n\\n* It does not have diuretic-like action at higher doses.\\n\\nWHO guidelines for active management of the third stage of labor (AMTSL)\xa0recommends\xa0oxytocin\xa0administration\xa0immediately\xa0(within 1 minute)\xa0after the\xa0delivery of the fetus.\xa0\xa0Oxytocin 10 IU is given IM or IV. It should be given after the delivery of the fetus and\xa0before the delivery of the placenta.\\n\\nMilk ejection by oxytocin\xa0follows a stimulation mechanism similar to\xa0uterine contraction in delivery\xa0(cervical dilatation).\\n\\nThe physiological actions of oxytocin are:\\n\\n* Contraction\xa0of the pregnant\xa0uterus: Cervical dilatation transmits signals to the hypothalamus and stimulates the secretion of oxytocin, which acts on the smooth muscles of the uterus and contracts it.\\n\\n* Milk ejection\xa0from breasts: Suckling reflex transmits signals to the hypothalamus which causes the release of oxytocin which acts on the myoepithelial cells of the breast. This results in ejection of milk.\\n\\nThe action of oxytocin is an example of positive feedback mechanism.\\n\\nAMTSL\xa0 guidelines recommended by\xa0WHO\xa0:\\n\\n* Immediate\xa0administration of prophylactic\xa0uterotonic\xa0(Oxytocin).\xa0Oxytocin 10 IU\xa0is given IM or IV.\\n\\n* Controlled cord traction\xa0during placental delivery (in cesarean section and in vaginal birth where skilled birth attendants are present)\\n\\n* Delayed cord clamping\\n\\n* Postpartum abdominal uterine tonus assessment\xa0for early identification of uterine atony is recommended for all women. Continuous monitoring of uterine tone is done every 15 minutes for 2 hours. If uterine atony is detected, a fundal massage is performed.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3537b73af2","n":181,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following is involved as a secondary messenger in the mechanism of gastric acid secretion?","options":["cGMP","cAMP","CCK-B receptor","Ach"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. cAMP\\n\\ncAMP is the\xa0secondary messenger involved in the mechanism of gastric acid secretion.\\n\\ncGMP is not involved in gastric acid secretion.\xa0Ach acts on the synapses, and CCK-B receptors interact with gastrin to contribute to gastric acid secretion. They also act via secondary messengers such as IP3 and increased intracellular Ca2+.\\n\\nPearl PF0546: Stimuli for gastric secretion\\n\\nStimuli\\n\\nMechanism\\n\\nApplied aspects\\n\\nVagal stimulation\\n\\nDirect pathway\\n\\nVagus nerve innervates parietal cells and stimulates H+ secretion directly.\\n\\nThe neurotransmitter at these synapses is Ach, the receptor on the parietal cells is muscarinic (M3)\\n\\nThe second messengers for CCK are IP3 and increased intracellular [Ca2+].\\n\\nAtropine, a cholinergic muscarinic antagonist, inhibits H+ secretion by blocking the direct pathway, which uses ACh as a neurotransmitter. However, it does not block H+ secretion completely because it does not inhibit the indirect pathway, which uses GRP as a neurotransmitter\\n\\nIndirect pathway\\n\\nVagus nerve innervates G cells and stimulates gastrin secretion, which then stimulates H+ secretion.\\n\\nThe neurotransmitter at these synapses is GRP (not ACh).\\n\\nVagotomy eliminates both direct and indirect pathways.\\n\\nGastrin\\n\\nReleased in response to eating a meal (small peptides, distension of the stomach, vagal stimulation).\\n\\nStimulates H+\xa0secretion by interacting with the cholecystokinin B (CCKB) receptor on the parietal cells.\\n\\nThe second messenger for gastrin on the parietal cell is IP3/Ca2+.\\n\\nHistamine\\n\\nReleased from enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the gastric mucosa and diffuses to the nearby parietal cells.\\n\\nStimulates H+ secretion by activating H2 receptors on the parietal cell membrane.\\n\\nThe H2\xa0receptor is coupled to adenylyl cyclase via a Gs protein.\\n\\nThe second messenger for histamine is cAMP.\\n\\nH2\xa0receptor–blocking drugs, such as cimetidine, inhibit H+ secretion by blocking the stimulatory effect of histamine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-87c0e0cba7","n":182,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"FMGE June 2022","text":"Which of the following would help in explaining the mechanism of action of ANP (atrial natriuretic peptide)?","options":["cAMP","cGMP","IP3-DAG","JAK/STAT"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. cGMP\\n\\nAtrial natriuretic peptide\xa0(ANP)\xa0increases intracellular cGMP by activating the enzyme guanylyl cyclase.\\n\\nANP\xa0is\xa0secreted by the heart\xa0from atrial storage granules in response to an increase in intravascular volume. It\xa0decreases\xa0aldosterone secretion and decreases blood pressure.\\n\\nOption A: cAMP is increased by activation of adenyl cyclase.\xa0Norepinephrine\xa0is a hormone\xa0that\xa0acts on intracellular adenylyl cyclase.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Gi of\xa0G-protein coupled receptors\xa0(GPCR) act via the IP3-DAG (phospholipase C) pathway.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Tyrosine kinase\xa0receptors act via the JAK/STAT pathway.\\n\\nPearl PF1611: Mechanisms of Action of Hormones\\n\\nTypes of hormone receptors\\n\\nHormones that bind to intracellular receptors\\n\\nCytoplasmic receptors\\nGlucocorticoids\\n\\nMineralocorticoids\\n\\nAndrogens\\n\\nNuclear receptors\\nThyroid hormones\\n\\nVitamins A and D\\n\\nHormones that bind to cell surface receptors\\n\\n1. G-protein coupled receptors (GPCR)\\n\\nGs: stimulation of second messenger cAMP\\nACTH, CRH\\n\\nTSH\\n\\nFSH, LH, hCG\\n\\nVasopressin (V2 receptors)\\n\\nCatecholamines (β receptors)\\n\\nSomatostatin\\n\\nGlucagon\\n\\nSecretin\\n\\nGi:\xa0inhibition of second messenger cAMP\\nCatecholamines (ɑ2 receptors)\\n\\nAcetylcholine (M2, M4)\\n\\nSomatostatin\\n\\nGq:\xa0second messenger is phospholipase\xa0C or cGMP\\n\\nPhospholipase C (IP3 DAG pathway):\\n\\nGnRH, GHRH, TRH\\n\\nCatecholamines (ɑ1 receptors)\\n\\nVasopressin (V1 receptor)\\n\\nGastrin\\n\\nCholecystokinin\\n\\nOxytocin\\n\\nParathyroid hormone\\n\\ncGMP:\\n\\nAtrial natriuretic peptide\\n\\nNitric oxide\\n\\n2. Tyrosine kinase receptors\\n\\nThrough\xa0JAK-STAT or MAP kinase pathway\\nGrowth factors like IGF, VEGF, FGF, PDGF\\n\\nGrowth hormone\\n\\nErythropoietin\\n\\nInsulin\\n\\nProlactin\\n\\nNote: Even though the cytoplasmic receptors are present in the cytoplasm, once the hormone-receptor complex is formed it moves to the nucleus for its actions.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a5ad8843c8","n":183,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 23-year-old man wants to become a fighter pilot in the Indian Air Force. He is made to undergo tests for positive-G in a flight simulator. Which of the following physiological effects is not seen in this individual during exposure to positive-G?","options":["Blackout","Decreased cardiac output","Increased hydrostatic pressure in lower limb veins","Increased cerebral arterial pressure"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Increased cerebral arterial pressure\\n\\nExposure to positive G-forces (+Gz) (e.g., during rapid upward acceleration in fighter aircraft) causes blood to shift toward the lower parts of the body due to gravity.\\n\\nPhysiological effects:\\n\\n• Pooling of blood in lower limbs → increased hydrostatic pressure in leg veins.\\n• Reduced venous return to the heart → decreased cardiac output.\\n• Reduced cerebral perfusion → visual disturbances (grey-out/blackout) and possible loss of consciousness.\\n\\nTherefore, cerebral arterial pressure decreases, not increases.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-742496f49f","n":184,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 26-year-old man touches a hot metal surface and immediately withdraws his hand, experiencing a burning pain sensation. The receptor responsible for detecting this stimulus is most likely mediated by which of the following ion channels?","options":["TRPM8","TRPV1","TRPA1","Piezo channels"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. TRPV1\\n\\nTRPV1 (Transient Receptor Potential Vanilloid 1) is a nociceptive ion channel present on sensory nerve endings that detects noxious heat and burning pain.\\n\\n• Activated by temperatures > 43\xb0C, capsaicin (from chili peppers), and acidic conditions.\\n• Opening of the channel allows Na⁺ and Ca\xb2⁺ influx, leading to depolarization of pain fibers (C fibers) and perception of burning pain.\\n\\nOther channels:\\n\\nA. TRPM8: Detects cold temperatures and menthol.\\n\\nC. TRPA1: Detects chemical irritants and environmental toxins.\\n\\nD. Piezo channels: Mechanoreceptors involved in touch and pressure sensation.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-49ece72e53","n":185,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 28-year-old trekker ascends rapidly from sea level to a high-altitude base camp at 4,500 meters. Over the next several days, his body undergoes physiological acclimatization. Which of the following changes is most directly responsible for the early rightward compensation that helps maintain tissue oxygen delivery despite a falling arterial PO2?","options":["Decreased 2,3-BPG levels, increasing hemoglobin\'s affinity for oxygen","Hyperventilation-driven respiratory alkalosis, causing a leftward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve, followed by renal compensation","Increased renal erythropoietin secretion, causing an immediate rise in hemoglobin concentration within hours","Decreased 2,3-BPG levels and a leftward-shifted curve, improving oxygen loading in the lungs at the cost of tissue unloading"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hyperventilation-driven respiratory alkalosis, causing a leftward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve, followed by renal compensation\\n\\n• The earliest acclimatization response to high altitude hypoxia is hyperventilation, triggered by peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies) sensing the fall in arterial PO2. This hyperventilation causes a respiratory alkalosis (low PaCO2, high pH), which initially shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left (increasing hemoglobin\'s affinity for O2, favoring better loading in the relatively hypoxic alveoli) — though this transiently impairs O2 unloading at the tissues.\\n• Over subsequent days, renal compensation (bicarbonate excretion) corrects the pH, while a separate, slightly delayed adaptation occurs: increased red cell 2,3-BPG production, which shifts the curve back to the right, restoring better tissue oxygen unloading despite low PaO2.\\n• Option A (Decreased 2,3-BPG levels, increasing hemoglobin\'s affinity for oxygen) and Option D (Decreased 2,3-BPG levels and a leftward-shifted curve, improving oxygen loading in the lungs at the cost of tissue unloading) incorrectly state that 2,3-BPG decreases with altitude (it actually increases).\\n• Option C (Increased renal erythropoietin secretion, causing an immediate rise in hemoglobin concentration within hours) is incorrect because erythropoietin-driven increases in red cell mass/hemoglobin take days to weeks to manifest significantly, not hours — making it a later, not early, compensatory mechanism.\\n\\nQuick reference — Time course of high-altitude acclimatization:\\n\\n| Mechanism | Onset | Effect\\n\\n| --------- | ----- | ------\\n\\n| Hyperventilation (chemoreceptor-driven) | Minutes–hours | ↓ PaCO2, respiratory alkalosis, ↑ PaO2\\n\\n| Renal bicarbonate excretion | Days | Corrects pH toward normal, permits further hyperventilation\\n\\n| ↑ 2,3-BPG in RBCs | Days | Rightward shift of O2-Hb curve, improves tissue O2 unloading\\n\\n| ↑ Erythropoietin → ↑RBC mass | Days to weeks | ↑ Hemoglobin concentration, ↑O2-carrying capacity\\n\\n| ↑ Pulmonary vasoconstriction (hypoxic) | Hours–days | Can contribute to high-altitude pulmonary edema if excessive\\n\\n| ↑ 2,3-DPG, ↑capillary density, ↑ mitochondria (chronic) | Weeks–months | Long-term tissue-level adaptation","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-345e4df88a","n":186,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 29-year-old goes on a month-long trek across the Himalayas. Which of the following physiological adaptations would you expect to see in him at high altitudes following acclimatization? 1. Erythropoietin secretion increases 2. Tissue myoglobin decreases 3. Tissue content of cytochrome oxidase increases 4. pH of blood increases","options":["1, 3 &amp; 5","2, 3, &amp; 5","1, 3, 4","1, 2, 3, 4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 3, 4\\n\\nAt high altitude, decreased atmospheric oxygen causes hypoxia, leading to several acclimatization responses to improve oxygen delivery and utilization.\\n\\nKey adaptations:\\n\\n• ↑ Erythropoietin (EPO) secretion → increased RBC production → improved oxygen-carrying capacity.\\n• ↑ Mitochondrial oxidative enzymes such as cytochrome oxidase, enhancing tissue oxygen utilization.\\n• Respiratory alkalosis occurs due to hyperventilation, causing an increase in blood pH initially.\\n\\nIncorrect statement:\\n\\n• Tissue myoglobin actually increases, not decreases, to improve oxygen storage in muscle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c683be7bfb","n":187,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 34-year-old woman presents with palpitations, heat intolerance, unintentional weight loss, and anxiety over the past two months. Exam reveals warm, moist skin, fine resting tremor, brisk reflexes, lid retraction, and mild proptosis. Her heart rate is 118/min and irregularly irregular, and an ECG confirms atrial fibrillation. Echocardiography shows preserved ejection fraction with increased cardiac output. Her physician explains the arrhythmia and hemodynamic profile to a medical student, who proposes several possible mechanisms. Which of the following statements correctly describes the cardiovascular pathophysiology in this patient\'s condition?","options":["The tachyarrhythmia is best explained by decreased density and sensitivity of myocardial β-adrenergic receptors, which paradoxically lowers the threshold for ectopic atrial activity","Increased sarcolemmal Ca\xb2⁺-ATPase activity together with decreased phospholamban expression enhances sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium cycling, contributing to the increased chronotropy and contractility seen in this condition","Her elevated cardiac output is primarily due to increased systemic vascular resistance, which increases afterload and secondarily raises heart rate via baroreceptor-mediated reflex tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation is a rare and incidental finding in this condition, occurring no more frequently than in the general population, and should prompt a search for an alternative cardiac etiology"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Increased sarcolemmal Ca\xb2⁺-ATPase activity together with decreased phospholamban expression enhances sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium cycling, contributing to the increased chronotropy and contractility seen in this condition\\n\\n• This patient\'s presentation, including the ophthalmopathy with periorbital edema, exophthalmos, and lid retraction, is characteristic of Graves disease-associated hyperthyroidism.\\n• Thyroid hormone (T3) directly alters cardiac gene expression:\\n\\n• ↑ SERCA2 (Sarcoplasmic Reticulum Ca\xb2⁺-ATPase) expression/activity\\n• ↓ Phospholamban (PLB) expression\\n\\n• Normally, phospholamban inhibits SERCA2. Therefore:\\n\\n• ↓ Phospholamban + ↑ SERCA2 → Faster reuptake of Ca\xb2⁺ into the sarcoplasmic reticulum → Faster calcium cycling\\n\\n• This results in:\\n\\n• ✅ Increased contractility (positive inotropy)\\n• ✅ Faster relaxation (positive lusitropy)\\n• ✅ Increased heart rate (positive chronotropy)\\n\\n• Additionally, thyroid hormone also:\\n\\n• ↑ β₁-adrenergic receptor expression\\n• ↑ Sensitivity to catecholamines\\n• ↑ Cardiac output\\n• ↓ Systemic vascular resistance\\n\\n• Hyperthyroidism is characteristically associated with tachycardia and increased cardiac output, not bradycardia, which is instead a feature of hypothyroidism.\\n• Atrial fibrillation is a recognized and clinically important arrhythmic complication of hyperthyroidism, related to the increased adrenergic sensitivity and metabolic demand on the heart.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-257be174a9","n":188,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes total thyroidectomy for papillary thyroid cancer. Following, surgery the patient develops perioral numbness and spasm of the muscles of her hand. The latter change is due to a change in the resting membrane potential of cells. Which of the below mentioned conditions will have a similar effect on the RMP of a cell?","options":["SIADH","Conn’s syndrome","A bolus injection of insulin","Multiple myeloma"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Conn’s syndrome\\n\\nTetany after thyroidectomy occurs due to hypocalcemia.\\n\\nLow extracellular Ca\xb2⁺ decreases the threshold potential of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels, making nerves and muscles hyperexcitable, leading to carpopedal spasm and tetany.\\n\\nAdditionally, associated alkalosis can worsen tetany because alkalosis increases binding of Ca\xb2⁺ to albumin, thereby decreasing free ionized calcium, which is the physiologically active form. Thus neuromuscular excitability further increases.\\n\\nSo the sequence is:\\n\\n• Thyroidectomy → parathyroid damage/removal\\n• ↓ PTH → hypocalcemia\\n• Alkalosis may further ↓ ionized Ca\xb2⁺\\n• ↓ extracellular free Ca\xb2⁺ lowers Na⁺ channel threshold\\n• Increased neuromuscular excitability → tetany/carpopedal spasm.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-67493a2bf1","n":189,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 48-year-old man with diabetes undergoes stress testing. He walks for 7 minutes without chest pain. Peak heart rate is 132/min, BP increases from 95 to 112 mmHg. Which of the following parameters was most likely decreased during peak stress?","options":["Cardiac output","Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure","Systemic systolic blood pressure","Total systemic vascular resistance"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Total systemic vascular resistance\\n\\nDuring exercise or stress testing, increased metabolic activity in skeletal muscles causes local vasodilation due to accumulation of metabolites (adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺).\\n\\nPhysiological changes during exercise:\\n\\n• ↑ Heart rate\\n• ↑ Cardiac output\\n• ↑ Systolic blood pressure\\n• ↑ Venous return\\n• ↓ Total systemic vascular resistance (SVR) due to vasodilation in active muscles\\n\\nEven though sympathetic tone increases, the metabolic vasodilation in skeletal muscles predominates, causing an overall decrease in SVR.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-83f0c0a93a","n":190,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 48-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus undergoes an exercise treadmill test for evaluation of intermittent chest discomfort. He has no ECG changes during testing. During peak exercise, his heart rate increases from 70/min to 132/min, and his mean arterial pressure rises from 95 mm Hg to 112 mm Hg. Which of the following hemodynamic changes is most likely to occur during peak exercise compared to rest?","options":["Decreased cardiac output","Increased left ventricular end-diastolic pressure","Increased pulmonary artery systolic pressure","Decreased total systemic vascular resistance"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Decreased total systemic vascular resistance\\n\\nDuring dynamic exercise, several coordinated cardiovascular changes occur:\\n\\n• ↑ Cardiac output (↑ HR + ↑ stroke volume)\\n• ↑ Systolic blood pressure\\n• ↓ Total systemic vascular resistance (TSVR) due to vasodilation in active skeletal muscles\\n• Blood flow is redistributed to muscles and skin\\n\\nDespite increased cardiac output, TSVR falls because metabolic vasodilation predominates.\\n\\nWhy not other options?\\n\\nA. Decreased cardiac output → Opposite; CO increases during exercise\\n\\nB. Increased LVEDP → May remain normal or slightly increase, not a key change\\n\\nC. Increased pulmonary artery pressure → May increase slightly, but not the primary expected change","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2430563c13","n":191,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 52-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus managed with diet and exercise undergoes treadmill stress testing for complaints of intermittent fatigue. He reports no chest pain during the test, but stops after 6 minutes due to exertional Vitals: o Resting heart rate: 72/min; peak stress heart rate: 138/min o Resting blood pressure: 120/75 mm Hg; peak stress: 135/82 mm Hg. • ECG shows normal sinus rhythm without ischemic changes. • Physical exam and echocardiogram at rest are unremarkable. During exercise, which of the following hemodynamic parameters is most likely to be decreased compared to the resting state?","options":["Cardiac output","Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure","Systemic systolic blood pressure","Total systemic vascular resistance"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Total systemic vascular resistance\\n\\nDuring dynamic exercise, cardiac output rises due to ↑ heart rate and stroke volume, while arterioles in exercising skeletal muscle dilate to increase blood flow. This widespread vasodilation leads to a decrease in total systemic vascular resistance (SVR).\\n\\nA. Cardiac output – Increases significantly with exercise.\\n\\nB. Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure – Usually unchanged or mildly increased due to enhanced venous return and ventricular compliance.\\n\\nC. Systemic systolic blood pressure – Increases due to increased cardiac output despite lower SVR.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7a61c6dfe1","n":192,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 55-year-old man with hypertension is prescribed verapamil. Which skeletal muscle feature explains its resistance to this medication?","options":["Calmodulin-independent excitation-contraction coupling","Decreased troponin affinity to calcium","Elaborate T-tubular system","Little dependence on extracellular calcium influx"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Little dependence on extracellular calcium influx\\n\\nSkeletal muscle contraction is relatively resistant to calcium channel blockers such as verapamil because it relies primarily on intracellular calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum rather than extracellular calcium influx. In contrast, cardiac and smooth muscle require extracellular calcium entry through L-type calcium channels, making them sensitive to calcium channel blockade.\\n\\n• In skeletal muscle, depolarization of the T-tubule activates the dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR).\\n• DHPR is mechanically coupled to the ryanodine receptor (RyR1) on the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\\n• This direct coupling triggers massive calcium release from intracellular stores.\\n• Therefore, skeletal muscle contraction has minimal dependence on extracellular calcium influx, explaining its resistance to verapamil.\\n\\nWhy not the others?\\n\\n• A. Calmodulin-independent excitation-contraction coupling → True for skeletal muscle, but this does not explain resistance to calcium channel blockers.\\n• B. Decreased troponin affinity to calcium → Not the mechanism responsible for verapamil resistance.\\n• C. Elaborate T-tubular system → Facilitates excitation-contraction coupling but does not explain the lack of dependence on extracellular calcium.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a14ec04883","n":193,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 59-year-old man presents with progressively worsening proximal muscle weakness over several weeks. He reports difficulty rising from a chair and climbing stairs, which improves with continued activity. He also complains of dry mouth, erectile dysfunction, and intermittent diplopia. On examination, there is reduced strength in the hip flexors and diminished deep tendon reflexes. After brief repetitive muscle contraction, both muscle strength and reflexes improve significantly. Which of the following underlying conditions is most likely associated with this patient’s disorder?","options":["Campylobacter jejuni infection","Giant cell arteritis","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Small cell lung carcinoma"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Small cell lung carcinoma\\n\\nThis is a classic presentation of Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS):\\n\\n• Proximal muscle weakness\\n• Improves with activity (facilitation)\\n• Hyporeflexia that improves after exercise\\n• Autonomic symptoms (dry mouth, erectile dysfunction)\\n• Ocular involvement (milder than myasthenia gravis)\\n\\nPathophysiology:\\n\\n• Autoantibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels\\n• ↓ Acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junction\\n\\nStrongly associated with paraneoplastic syndrome of small cell lung carcinoma\\n\\nWhy not other options?\\n\\nA. Campylobacter jejuni → Associated with Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome (ascending paralysis, areflexia, no facilitation)\\n\\nB. Giant cell arteritis → Headache, jaw claudication, vision loss\\n\\nC. Medullary thyroid carcinoma → MEN2 syndrome, calcitonin secretion","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5c87c9106b","n":194,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 6-year-old child presents with recurrent infections and defective complement activation. Investigations reveal impaired function of proteins localized to cholesterol- and sphingolipid-rich microdomains of the plasma membrane (lipid rafts). Which of the following types of proteins is most characteristically associated with these membrane rafts?","options":["Mannose-binding protein","GTP-binding intracellular proteins","GPI-anchored proteins","Ion channels"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. GPI-anchored proteins\\n\\nLipid rafts are cholesterol- and sphingolipid-rich microdomains of the plasma membrane that preferentially contain GPI-anchored proteins, which play key roles in immune signaling and complement regulation.\\n\\n• Lipid rafts act as platforms for cell signaling and immune responses\\n• Important examples: CD55 (DAF) and CD59 → complement regulation\\n• Defects can lead to complement-mediated damage (e.g., PNH)\\n\\nDifferentiation:\\n\\nA. Mannose-binding protein → soluble protein (not membrane raft–bound)\\n\\nB. GTP-binding proteins → intracellular signaling (cytosolic)\\n\\nD. Ion channels → not specifically enriched in lipid rafts","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-210f83f8a6","n":195,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 62-year-old man develops sudden massive blood loss following trauma. His mean arterial pressure falls to 35 mmHg. Within seconds, he develops marked sympathetic activation with intense peripheral vasoconstriction and a sharp rise in heart rate. This powerful pressure response is mediated by which of the following mechanisms?","options":["Carotid body chemoreceptor stimulation","Baroreceptor reflex from carotid sinus","Direct ischemia of the vasomotor center","Activation of renin–angiotensin system"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Direct ischemia of the vasomotor center\\n\\nWhen mean arterial pressure (MAP) falls to very low levels (<40 mmHg), the brainstem experiences ischemia, leading to activation of the central nervous system ischemic response.\\n\\nMechanism:\\n\\n• Reduced cerebral perfusion causes CO₂ accumulation and decreased O₂ in the medullary vasomotor center.\\n• This strongly stimulates the sympathetic nervous system, producing:\\n• Intense peripheral vasoconstriction\\n• Marked increase in heart rate\\n• Rapid rise in blood pressure\\n• This response is one of the most powerful short-term mechanisms for restoring blood pressure during severe shock.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2e2c5396be","n":196,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A 72-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital for treatment of unstable angina. Cardiac catheterization shows occlusion that has caused a 50% reduction in the diameter of the left circumflex artery. Resistance to blood flow in this vessel has increased by what factor relative to a vessel with no occlusion?","options":["64","16","8","32"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 16\\n\\nAccording to Poiseuille’s law, vascular resistance (R) is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the vessel radius (r):\\n\\nIf the diameter is reduced by 50%, the radius also becomes half of the original.\\n\\nThus, resistance increases 16-fold.\\n\\nSmall decreases in vessel radius dramatically increase resistance, which significantly reduces blood flow.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":2}]},{"bankId":"qb-f36bef03cc","n":197,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A deep-sea diver develops euphoria, impaired judgment, and altered consciousness at increased depth due to exposure to an inert gas under high pressure. This phenomenon is known as:","options":["Helium narcosis","Oxygen toxicity","Nitrogen narcosis","Decompression sickness"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Nitrogen narcosis\\n\\nAt greater diving depths, the partial pressure of nitrogen increases in the breathing gas mixture. Nitrogen dissolves in neuronal lipid membranes and depresses central nervous system activity, producing effects similar to alcohol intoxication. Clinical features of nitrogen narcosis:\\n\\n• Euphoria\\n• Impaired judgment\\n• Slowed reaction time\\n• Confusion and altered consciousness\\n\\nThese symptoms usually occur at depths >30 meters (≈100 ft) and resolve when the diver ascends and nitrogen partial pressure decreases.\\n\\nWhy other options are incorrect:\\n\\nA. Helium narcosis: Helium is used in deep diving precisely because it does not cause narcosis like nitrogen.\\n\\nB. Oxygen toxicity: Occurs at very high oxygen partial pressures, causing seizures or lung injury.\\n\\nD. Decompression sickness: Caused by nitrogen bubble formation during rapid ascent, leading to joint pain (“the bends”), neurologic deficits, and embolic phenomena.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-644b4fed79","n":198,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A neurophysiology question states that the extracellular concentration of potassium is 5 mmol/L and the intracellular concentration is 50 mmol/L. Using a Nernst slope of 61 mV (at 37\xb0C, monovalent ion, log10 form), what is the approximate equilibrium potential for potassium?","options":["+61 mV","-61 mV","+6.1 mV","-6.1 mV"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. -61 mV\\n\\nThe Nernst equation for a monovalent ion at 37\xb0C is:\\n\\nE = 61 log ([Ion]out / [Ion]in)\\n\\nFor potassium:\\n\\nEₖ = 61 log (5/50)\\n\\nEₖ = 61 log (0.1)\\n\\nEₖ = 61 \xd7 (−1)\\n\\nEₖ = −61 mV\\n\\nA 10-fold higher intracellular K⁺ concentration gives a potassium equilibrium potential of approximately −61 mV.\\n\\nWhy not the others?\\n\\n• A. +61 mV: Concentrations reversed (50 outside, 5 inside)\\n• C. +6.1 mV / D. −6.1 mV: Would result from a much smaller concentration gradient","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f33a532805","n":199,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A patient with spinal cord injury shows loss of recurrent inhibition of alpha motor neurons, leading to increased muscle tone. Dysfunction of which of the following interneurons is most likely responsible for this finding?","options":["Ia inhibitory interneuron","Golgi tendon organ neuron","Renshaw cell","Basket cell"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Renshaw cell\\n\\nRenshaw cells are inhibitory interneurons in the spinal cord that provide recurrent inhibition to α-motor neurons.\\n\\n• An α-motor neuron activates a muscle fiber and simultaneously sends a collateral branch to Renshaw cells.\\n• Renshaw cells release inhibitory neurotransmitters (mainly glycine).\\n• This inhibits the same α-motor neuron and neighboring motor neurons, preventing excessive muscle contraction.\\n\\nClinical significance:\\n\\n• Loss of Renshaw cell inhibition → increased α-motor neuron activity → muscle hypertonia/spasticity.\\n• Tetanus toxin blocks inhibitory neurotransmitter release from Renshaw cells, leading to muscle rigidity and spasms.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e75b01f2fe","n":200,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A researcher engineers a cell line lacking functional signal recognition particle (SRP). Which immediate consequence on protein localization would be expected for proteins normally destined for secretion?","options":["Proteins would be correctly translocated into the RER lumen via an SRP-independent backup pathway","Proteins would accumulate in the cytosol due to failure of ribosome-RER targeting","Proteins would be retained in the Golgi due to failure of COPII vesicle formation","Proteins would be degraded by lysosomal enzymes due to failure of mannose-6-phosphate tagging"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Proteins would accumulate in the cytosol due to failure of ribosome-RER targeting\\n\\nSRP traffics the polypeptide-ribosome complex from cytosol to RER. Absent or dysfunctional SRP → ribosome-mRNA-polypeptide complex never reaches RER → translation either continues in cytosol (for proteins lacking proper insertion) or the protein accumulates untranslocated, mislocalized in the cytosol rather than entering the secretory pathway.\\n\\n| Component | Function | If SRP absent\\n\\n| --------- | -------- | -------------\\n\\n| SRP | Targets polypeptide-ribosome complex to RER | Fails — no targeting\\n\\n| RER | Site of secretory protein synthesis | Bypassed\\n\\n| Result | Normal secretory pathway entry | Cytosolic protein accumulation","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-658d47b25c","n":201,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"A virus exploits the neuron\'s intracellular transport machinery to travel from a peripheral nerve terminal back to the cell body, moving at approximately 200 mm/day along microtubules. Which motor protein mediates this movement, and which direction does it represent?","options":["Kinesin — orthograde transport","Dynein — retrograde transport","Kinesin — retrograde transport","Myosin — orthograde transport"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Dynein — retrograde transport\\n\\nRetrograde axonal transport carries materials from the nerve terminal back to the neuronal cell body along microtubules and is mediated by the motor protein dynein. This transport pathway is exploited by several neurotropic viruses, including Rabies, to reach the central nervous system after entering peripheral nerves.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Kinesin — orthograde transport: Kinesin mediates anterograde (cell body → axon terminal) transport.\\n• C. Kinesin — retrograde transport: Kinesin does not mediate retrograde movement.\\n• D. Myosin — orthograde transport: Myosin functions primarily on actin filaments, not microtubule-based long-distance axonal transport.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f33c11add5","n":202,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All are correct statements about muscle tension curves, except:","options":["Passive tension increases with muscle stretch","Active tension is highest at optimal length","Total tension equals sum of passive and active tension","Passive tension is highest at resting muscle length"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Passive tension is highest at resting muscle length\\n\\nThe muscle length–tension relationship describes how muscle force varies with sarcomere length.\\n\\n• Active tension: Produced by actin–myosin cross-bridge formation.\\n• Maximum at optimal sarcomere length (ideal overlap of actin and myosin).\\n• Passive tension: Produced by elastic elements of muscle (titin, connective tissue).\\n• Increases progressively as the muscle is stretched beyond resting length.\\n• Total tension:\\n• Total tension = active tension + passive tension\\n\\nPassive tension is not highest at resting length; it is minimal at resting length and increases with stretch.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f917cd0287","n":203,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All are features of postganglionic lesions, except:","options":["Positive histamine test","Good prognosis","Distal to dorsal root ganglion","Surgical repair is possible"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Positive histamine test\\n\\nA postganglionic lesion refers to nerve damage distal to the dorsal root ganglion (DRG), affecting the peripheral nerve after the sensory neuron cell body.\\n\\nFeatures of postganglionic lesions:\\n\\n• Occur distal to the dorsal root ganglion.\\n• Better prognosis because peripheral nerves can regenerate.\\n• Surgical repair may be possible in cases of nerve injury.\\n\\nHistamine test:\\n\\n• Used to assess integrity of sensory nerve fibers.\\n• In postganglionic lesions, the axon reflex is lost, so the flare response is absent → negative histamine test.\\n\\nTherefore, positive histamine test is not seen in postganglionic lesions, making it the incorrect statement.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-371c50afe7","n":204,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following are examples of primary active transport except:","options":["Na⁺-K⁺-ATPase","Proton pump","SGLT","Ca\xb2⁺-ATPase"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. SGLT\\n\\nPrimary active transport directly utilizes energy from ATP hydrolysis to move substances against their electrochemical gradient. In contrast, secondary active transport uses the energy stored in an ion gradient (usually Na⁺) that was generated by primary active transport.\\n\\n• SGLT (Sodium-Glucose Linked Transporter) does not directly use ATP.\\n• It transports glucose against its concentration gradient by utilizing the Na⁺ gradient created by the Na⁺-K⁺-ATPase.\\n• Therefore, SGLT is a secondary active transporter, not a primary active transporter.\\n\\n| Primary Active Transport | Secondary Active Transport\\n\\n| ------------------------ | --------------------------\\n\\n| Direct ATP use | Indirect ATP use\\n\\n| Na⁺-K⁺-ATPase | SGLT\\n\\n| H⁺-K⁺-ATPase | Na⁺-amino acid cotransporter\\n\\n| Ca\xb2⁺-ATPase | Na⁺-Ca\xb2⁺ exchanger","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7ff4897394","n":205,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following are true regarding Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase structure, except:","options":["ATP binding site is located intracellularly","K⁺ binding site is on the extracellular side","Ouabain binds to the cytoplasmic domain","Phosphorylation site is intracellular"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Ouabain binds to the cytoplasmic domain\\n\\nThe Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump is a membrane-bound enzyme that maintains the electrochemical gradients of sodium and potassium across the cell membrane.\\n\\n• ATP binding site: located on the intracellular side.\\n• Phosphorylation site: also on the intracellular domain of the pump.\\n• K⁺ binding site: located on the extracellular side.\\n• Ouabain (cardiac glycoside) binding site: located on the extracellular surface of the pump.\\n\\nFunction:\\n\\n• Pumps 3 Na⁺ out of the cell and 2 K⁺ into the cell using ATP hydrolysis.\\n\\nTherefore, the incorrect statement is that ouabain binds to the cytoplasmic domain, because it actually binds to the extracellular side of the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c5178b36f7","n":206,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following increase the rate of diffusion across a membrane, except:","options":["Increased membrane thickness","Increased temperature","Greater lipid solubility","Higher surface area"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Increased membrane thickness\\n\\nThe rate of diffusion across a membrane follows Fick’s law of diffusion, which states that diffusion is directly proportional to the surface area, concentration gradient, and permeability, and inversely proportional to the membrane thickness.\\n\\nFactors that increase diffusion:\\n\\n• Higher temperature → increases molecular kinetic energy.\\n• Greater lipid solubility → substances cross lipid membranes more easily.\\n• Larger surface area → more area available for diffusion.\\n\\nIncreased membrane thickness decreases diffusion because molecules must travel a longer distance.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-dee3cbe63d","n":207,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"All of the following statements about gap junctions are true except:","options":["They are composed of connexin proteins arranged into connexons","They allow direct passage of ions and small molecules (&lt;1 kDa) between adjacent cells","They are present in cardiac muscle and are responsible for electrical coupling at intercalated discs","They require ATP hydrolysis to actively transport molecules between cells"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. They require ATP hydrolysis to actively transport molecules between cells\\n\\n• Gap junctions are channels of passive, non-ATP-dependent diffusion — they allow direct intercellular passage of ions, second messengers, and small molecules (generally <1 kDa) down their electrochemical or concentration gradients, without requiring energy expenditure.\\n• Each gap junction channel is formed by the alignment of two connexons (hemichannels), each composed of six connexin subunits, from adjacent cells.\\n• They are essential for electrical coupling and synchronized contraction in cardiac muscle (at intercalated discs) and smooth muscle, as well as in other tissues like the lens, liver, and neurons (electrical synapses).\\n• The statement that they require ATP hydrolysis for transport is false — this describes active transport mechanisms (e.g., ATPase pumps), not gap junction-mediated diffusion.\\n\\nQuick reference — Gap Junction Essentials:\\n\\n| Feature | Detail\\n\\n| ------- | ------\\n\\n| Structural protein | Connexin (6 connexins → 1 connexon/hemichannel; 2 connexons → 1 complete channel)\\n\\n| Transport mechanism | Passive diffusion (no ATP required)\\n\\n| Size limit | Permits molecules <1 kDa (ions, cAMP, IP3, small metabolites)\\n\\n| Key locations | Cardiac muscle (intercalated discs), smooth muscle, lens fibers, hepatocytes, neurons (electrical synapses), beta cells of pancreas\\n\\n| Functional role | Electrical coupling, metabolic coupling, synchronized contraction\\n\\n| Regulation | Closed by ↑intracellular Ca\xb2⁺, ↓pH (acidosis); important in limiting injury spread after cell damage","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-27798fd528","n":208,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Counter current mechanism is found in: i. Eye ii. Testis iii. Gut iv. Kidney v. Lungs","options":["i, ii are correct","ii, iii, iv are correct","iii, iv, v are correct","ii, iii, iv, v are correct"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. ii, iii, iv, v are correct\\n\\nA countercurrent mechanism occurs when two fluids flow in opposite directions, allowing efficient exchange of heat, gases, or solutes.\\n\\nSites where countercurrent mechanisms occur:\\n\\n• Testis: Countercurrent heat exchange in the pampiniform plexus helps maintain testicular temperature lower than body temperature for spermatogenesis.\\n• Gut: Countercurrent blood flow in intestinal villi facilitates efficient nutrient absorption and exchange.\\n• Kidney: Countercurrent multiplier (Loop of Henle) and countercurrent exchanger (vasa recta) concentrate urine.\\n• Lungs: Countercurrent heat and moisture exchange occurs in the respiratory passages.\\n\\nEye does not have a classical countercurrent exchange system.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b3dab9f045","n":209,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"During exercise, skeletal muscle blood flow increases primarily due to:","options":["Sympathetic vasodilation","Local metabolic vasodilation","Increased parasympathetic tone","Increased blood viscosity"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Local metabolic vasodilation\\n\\nDuring exercise, skeletal muscle blood flow increases mainly due to local metabolic vasodilation in active muscles.\\n\\n• Contracting muscles produce metabolic by-products such as:\\n• CO₂\\n• H⁺ (lactic acid)\\n• K⁺\\n• Adenosine\\n• ↓ O₂\\n• These metabolites cause local arteriolar dilation, greatly increasing muscle perfusion.\\n\\nAlthough sympathetic activity increases during exercise, the local metabolic effects override sympathetic vasoconstriction in active muscles.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9c198926ed","n":210,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"During isometric (static) exercise, which of the following hemodynamic changes is most characteristic?","options":["Decrease in total peripheral resistance","Decrease in diastolic blood pressure","Increase in both systolic and diastolic blood pressure","Increase in stroke volume with fall in heart rate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Increase in both systolic and diastolic blood pressure\\n\\nDuring isometric (static) exercise (e.g., handgrip, weight holding), sustained muscle contraction compresses blood vessels within the muscle, increasing total peripheral resistance (TPR). Hemodynamic effects:\\n\\n• Sympathetic activation\\n• Increased heart rate and cardiac output\\n• Increased total peripheral resistance\\n\\nAs a result, both systolic and diastolic blood pressure rise.\\n\\nContrast with dynamic exercise:\\n\\n• Dynamic exercise (running, cycling): ↓ TPR due to vasodilation → SBP ↑ but DBP normal or ↓.\\n• Isometric exercise: ↑ TPR → both SBP and DBP increase.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7025f9c0bd","n":211,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Given below is the demonstration of tetanisation in the gastrocnemius muscle of a frog. Identify the tetanising frequency.","options":["20-25 Hz","30-35 Hz","15-20 Hz","10-15 Hz"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 20-25 Hz\\n\\nTetanization occurs when muscle stimuli are delivered at a frequency high enough that individual muscle twitches fuse, producing a sustained contraction (tetanus).\\n\\nFor frog gastrocnemius muscle:\\n\\n• A single twitch lasts about 0.04–0.05 seconds (40–50 ms).\\n• When stimuli are given before the muscle completely relaxes, temporal summation occurs.\\n• At around 20–25 Hz, stimuli are frequent enough to produce complete tetanus (fused contraction).\\n\\nKey concepts:\\n\\n• Low frequency: Separate twitches\\n• Moderate frequency: Incomplete tetanus (wave summation)\\n• High frequency: Complete tetanus","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-2772e4e485","n":212,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"How muscle spindle able to detect the length while muscle is contracting?","options":["Nuclear bag fibers","Nuclear chain fibers","Golgi tendon organ","Other muscle receptor"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Nuclear bag fibers\\n\\nThe muscle spindle is a proprioceptive receptor that detects muscle length and the rate of change in length (stretch reflex).\\n\\nMuscle spindles contain intrafusal fibers of two types:\\n\\n• Nuclear bag fibers → detect dynamic changes in muscle length, especially during active contraction or rapid stretch.\\n• Nuclear chain fibers → detect static muscle length.\\n\\nMechanism during contraction:\\n\\n• Gamma motor neurons stimulate intrafusal fibers to maintain spindle sensitivity while the muscle shortens.\\n• Nuclear bag fibers help the spindle continue sensing changes in muscle length even during contraction.\\n\\nOther receptors:\\n\\n• Golgi tendon organ senses muscle tension, not muscle length.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6ddb1069e1","n":213,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"In a neuron, an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) is generated following activation of inhibitory synapses. This hyperpolarization of the postsynaptic membrane occurs due to movement of which of the following ions?","options":["Sodium (Na⁺) influx","Calcium (Ca\xb2⁺) influx","Potassium (K⁺) efflux","Chloride (Cl⁻) efflux"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Potassium (K⁺) efflux\\n\\nInhibitory postsynaptic potentials (IPSPs) cause hyperpolarization of the postsynaptic membrane, making it less likely to generate an action potential. This occurs primarily due to potassium efflux or chloride influx.\\n\\nKey points:\\n\\n• Opening of K⁺ channels → K⁺ moves out → hyperpolarization\\n• Opening of Cl⁻ channels → Cl⁻ moves in → hyperpolarization\\n• Both mechanisms increase negativity inside the cell\\n• Common inhibitory neurotransmitters: GABA, glycine\\n\\nWhy options are incorrect:\\n\\n• A. Na⁺ influx and B. Ca\xb2⁺ influx cause depolarization (excitatory)\\n• D. Cl⁻ efflux would depolarize, not hyperpolarize","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2af636bb71","n":214,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Infinite feedback gain is a characteristic feature of which of the following physiological control systems?","options":["Baroreceptor reflex","Chemoreceptor reflex","Thermoregulation","Renal–body fluid mechanism"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Renal–body fluid mechanism\\n\\nInfinite feedback gain refers to a control system that can completely correct a disturbance and return the regulated variable exactly to normal, even after significant changes.\\n\\nThe renal–body fluid mechanism controlling arterial blood pressure demonstrates near-infinite feedback gain because:\\n\\n• The kidneys regulate sodium and water excretion.\\n• Changes in blood pressure alter renal excretion of salt and water (pressure natriuresis/diuresis).\\n• Over time, this mechanism can fully restore blood pressure to normal.\\n\\nOther mechanisms (baroreceptor reflex, chemoreceptor reflex, thermoregulation) have finite feedback gain and provide short-term or partial correction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5c5c760410","n":215,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Perception of pain in an area that lacks sensation is known as:","options":["Anesthesia dolorosa","Hyperalgesia","Paresthesia","Allodynia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Anesthesia dolorosa\\n\\nAnesthesia dolorosa refers to pain perceived in an area that is otherwise numb or lacks normal sensation. It occurs due to damage to sensory pathways, especially after nerve injury or central nervous system lesions.\\n\\n• Loss of normal sensory input (anesthesia)\\n• Simultaneous abnormal spontaneous pain signals from damaged nerves\\n\\nClinical examples:\\n\\n• Trigeminal nerve injury after procedures for trigeminal neuralgia\\n• Thalamic lesions or other central sensory pathway injuries\\n\\nOther terms:\\n\\nB. Hyperalgesia: Increased response to a painful stimulus\\n\\nC. Paresthesia: Abnormal sensations like tingling or “pins and needles”\\n\\nD. Allodynia: Pain due to a normally non-painful stimulus","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f54288c548","n":216,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Prolonged exposure to high partial pressure of oxygen causes pulmonary capillary endothelial damage, leading to alveolar inflammation and edema. This phenomenon is known as:","options":["Paul Bert effect","Lorrain–Smith effect","Bohr effect","Haldane effect"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lorrain–Smith effect\\n\\nLorrain–Smith effect refers to pulmonary oxygen toxicity caused by prolonged exposure to high partial pressures of oxygen (hyperoxia).\\n\\n• Excess oxygen leads to formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS).\\n• ROS cause damage to pulmonary capillary endothelium and alveolar epithelium.\\n• This results in alveolar inflammation, pulmonary edema, and decreased lung compliance.\\n\\nClinical settings:\\n\\n• Prolonged oxygen therapy\\n• Hyperbaric oxygen exposure\\n• Diving or high-pressure environments\\n\\nOther effects:\\n\\nA. Paul Bert effect: CNS oxygen toxicity → seizures due to high oxygen pressure.\\n\\nC. Bohr effect: Effect of CO₂ and pH on hemoglobin oxygen affinity.\\n\\nD. Haldane effect: Oxygenation of hemoglobin reduces CO₂ carrying capacity.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2f8376d3bd","n":217,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Regarding the speed of axonal transport, All of the following statements are correct except:","options":["Fast anterograde (orthograde) transport occurs at a speed of approximately 200–400 mm/day","Retrograde axonal transport occurs at a speed of approximately 100–300 mm/day","Slow anterograde axonal transport occurs at a speed of approximately 1–5 mm/day","Retrograde axonal transport is slower than slow anterograde transport"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Retrograde axonal transport is slower than slow anterograde transport\\n\\nAxonal transport moves materials between the neuronal cell body and axon terminals along microtubules using motor proteins.\\n\\nTypes of axonal transport:\\n\\n●\xa0 Fast anterograde (orthograde) transport:\\n\\n• Speed: \\\\~200–400 mm/day\\n• Motor protein: Kinesin\\n• Carries synaptic vesicles, mitochondria, enzymes\\n\\n●\xa0Retrograde transport:\\n\\n• Speed: \\\\~100–300 mm/day\\n• Motor protein: Dynein\\n• Carries endocytosed materials, growth factors, toxins, viruses back to the cell body\\n\\n●\xa0Slow anterograde transport:\\n\\n• Speed: \\\\~1–5 mm/day\\n• Carries cytoskeletal proteins (actin, tubulin)\xa0Statement D is incorrect because retrograde transport is faster than slow anterograde transport.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e6c7c72164","n":218,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Researchers are studying ion movement across the membrane of a resting skeletal muscle cell. The resting membrane potential (RMP) is measured at −70 mV. The equilibrium potentials of the major ions are given below. If selective ion channels are opened while all concentration gradients remain unchanged, which of the following ions would be expected to move out of the cell?","options":["Magnesium and calcium","Magnesium and chloride","Potassium and chloride","Potassium only"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Potassium only\\n\\nThe direction of ion movement depends on the relationship between the membrane potential (Vm) and the equilibrium potential (Eion).\\n\\nRule:\\n\\n• If Vm > Eion (membrane is more positive than the ion\'s equilibrium potential), positive ions move out of the cell.\\n• If Vm < Eion, positive ions move into the cell.\\n• For Cl⁻ (a negative ion), the direction is opposite because of its negative charge.\\n\\nGiven:\\n\\n• Vm = −70 mV\\n\\nPotassium (K⁺)\\n\\n• EK = −90 mV\\n• Vm (−70) is more positive than EK (−90).\\n• Therefore, K⁺ moves out of the cell.\\n\\nSodium (Na⁺)\\n\\n• ENa = +60 mV\\n• Vm is much more negative than ENa.\\n• Na⁺ moves into the cell.\\n\\nCalcium (Ca\xb2⁺)\\n\\n• ECa = +125 mV\\n• Strong electrochemical gradient drives Ca\xb2⁺ into the cell.\\n\\nMagnesium (Mg\xb2⁺)\\n\\n• EMg = 0 mV\\n• Vm is more negative than EMg.\\n• Mg\xb2⁺ would move into the cell.\\n\\nChloride (Cl⁻)\\n\\n• ECl = −75 mV\\n• Vm is slightly more positive than ECl.\\n• Because chloride is negatively charged, it moves into the cell (opposite to the electrical driving force for cations).\\n\\nThus, only potassium leaves the cell.\\n\\nWhy the other options are incorrect?\\n\\n• A. Magnesium and calcium: Both would move into the cell.\\n• B. Magnesium and chloride: Magnesium enters; chloride tends to move into the cell under these conditions.\\n• C. Potassium and chloride: Potassium exits, but chloride does not.\\n\\nHigh-yield pearl:\\n\\nThe driving force for an ion is: Driving force = Vm − Eion\\n\\n• Positive driving force → Cations move out\\n• Negative driving force → Cations move in\\n• Anions move in the opposite direction","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-7f54c93433","n":219,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Sequentially arrange the steps involved in excitation-contraction coupling of smooth muscle cells. 1. Myosin phosphorylation 2. Increased influx of Ca2+ into the cell 3. Activation of calmodulin-dependent myosin light chain kinase 4. Binding of myosin to actin","options":["2-1-3-4","2-1-4-3","3-2-1-4","2-3-1-4"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 2-3-1-4\\n\\nIn smooth muscle excitation–contraction coupling, contraction is regulated by Ca\xb2⁺-dependent phosphorylation of myosin rather than troponin (as in skeletal muscle).\\n\\nCorrect sequence:\\n\\n1.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Increased influx of Ca\xb2⁺ into the cell (via voltage-gated or receptor-operated channels).\\n\\n2.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Ca\xb2⁺ binds to calmodulin, activating calmodulin-dependent myosin light chain kinase (MLCK).\\n\\n3.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 MLCK phosphorylates myosin light chains.\\n\\n4.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Phosphorylated myosin binds to actin, initiating cross-bridge cycling and contraction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2e2fe81e34","n":220,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"The serotonergic cell bodies of the analgesia pathway projecting to the dorsal horn of the spinal cord are located in which part of the brain?","options":["Periaqueductal gray area","Rostral ventromedial medulla","Locus ceruleus","Nucleus raphe"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Nucleus raphe\\n\\nThe descending pain inhibitory (analgesia) pathway modulates pain transmission at the dorsal horn of the spinal cord.\\n\\nKey components of this pathway:\\n\\n1.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Periaqueductal gray (PAG) in the midbrain receives pain signals.\\n\\n2.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 PAG activates neurons in the nucleus raphe magnus (raphe nuclei) in the medulla.\\n\\n3.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Serotonergic neurons from the nucleus raphe descend to the dorsal horn of the spinal cord.\\n\\n4.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 These neurons stimulate enkephalin-releasing interneurons, which inhibit pain transmission from primary afferent neurons.\\n\\nImportant neurotransmitters:\\n\\n• Serotonin (5-HT) from raphe nuclei\\n• Enkephalins in the spinal cord\\n\\nOther structures:\\n\\n• Locus ceruleus: provides noradrenergic modulation of pain.\\n• PAG: initiates the descending analgesic pathway but is not the source of serotonergic projections.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-409e989cd2","n":221,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"The solid line represents a normal individual, and the dotted line represents this patient. Which of the following conditions is most likely causing this patient’s findings?","options":["Acute gastrointestinal hemorrhage","Diabetes insipidus","Hypertonic saline infusion","Primary polydipsia"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Primary polydipsia\\n\\nThe diagram represents changes in body fluid compartments (ECF and ICF) comparing a normal individual (solid line) with the patient (dotted line).\\n\\nIn primary polydipsia (excess water intake):\\n\\n• Plasma osmolality decreases (dilution of solutes).\\n• Water distributes into both ECF and ICF, causing expansion of both compartments.\\n• Solute concentration decreases in both compartments due to dilution.\\n\\nInterpretation of the graph:\\n\\n• Decreased concentration of solutes (↓ osmolarity)\\n• Increased total body water → expansion of both ICF and ECF volumes","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-9aba26faf5","n":222,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Upon reactivation of VZV, the virus is transported through the nerve axon to the skin. Which of the following proteins is most likely involved in the transport process leading to disease recurrence?","options":["Dynein","Kinesin","Lamin","Selectin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Kinesin\\n\\nDuring reactivation of varicella-zoster virus (VZV), the virus travels from the dorsal root ganglion along the axon to the skin.\\n\\nAxonal transport mechanisms:\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0Anterograde transport (cell body → axon terminal/skin): mediated by kinesin along microtubules.\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0Retrograde transport (axon terminal → cell body): mediated by dynein.\\n\\nDuring VZV reactivation, the virus moves anterogradely toward the skin, causing herpes zoster (shingles).\\n\\nOther proteins:\\n\\nC. Lamin: structural protein of the nuclear lamina.\\n\\nD. Selectin: adhesion molecule involved in leukocyte rolling during inflammation.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2cab0ec6c3","n":223,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"When the membrane potential moves from +30 mv to -20 mv the sodium channel is predominantly seen in which of the following states?","options":["A","B","C","None"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. C\\n\\nVoltage-gated Na⁺ channels exist in three functional states during an action potential:\\n\\n1. Closed (resting) state – activation gate closed, inactivation gate open (ready to open).\\n2. Open (activated) state – both gates open → rapid Na⁺ influx during depolarization.\\n3. Inactivated state – activation gate open but inactivation gate closes, preventing Na⁺ entry.\\n\\nWhen the membrane potential moves from +30 mV toward −20 mV (early repolarization), most Na⁺ channels are in the inactivated state.\\n\\nIn the figure:\\n\\n• A: Closed (resting)\\n• B: Open\\n• C: Inactivated","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-b87e8243c0","n":224,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following equations is used to determine the pH of a buffer solution?","options":["Nernst equation","Goldman equation","Gibbs–Donnan equation","Henderson–Hasselbalch equation"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Henderson–Hasselbalch equation\\n\\nThe Henderson–Hasselbalch equation is used to calculate the pH of a buffer solution based on the ratio of a weak acid and its conjugate base.\\n\\nThis equation explains how blood pH is maintained by the balance between bicarbonate (kidneys) and CO₂ (lungs).\\n\\nOther equations:\\n\\nA. Nernst equation: Calculates equilibrium potential of ions across membranes.\\n\\nB. Goldman equation: Determines membrane potential considering multiple ions.\\n\\nC. Gibbs–Donnan equation: Describes ion distribution across membranes with impermeant charged molecules.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-1096183ccc","n":225,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle?","options":["Absence of gap junctions","Each fiber functions independently","Innervated by a single nerve ending per fiber","Fibers are connected via many gap junctions and contract as a syncytium"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Fibers are connected via many gap junctions and contract as a syncytium\\n\\nSingle-unit (visceral) smooth muscle functions as a functional syncytium because the individual muscle fibers are electrically coupled by numerous gap junctions.\\n\\n• Many gap junctions connect adjacent cells.\\n• Electrical activity spreads from cell to cell, so the muscle contracts as a coordinated unit (syncytium).\\n• Usually innervated by a few nerve fibers, not one nerve per cell.\\n• Often shows spontaneous rhythmic activity.\\n\\nExamples:\\n• Gastrointestinal tract\\n• Uterus\\n• Urinary bladder\\n• Small blood vessels\\n\\nContrast with multiunit smooth muscle:\\n• Fibers act independently\\n• Few or no gap junctions\\n• Each fiber has individual innervation (e.g., iris, ciliary muscle).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f7c13586fd","n":226,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following is correct about the recruitment of fast fatiguable motor unit?","options":["Recruited first","Recruited last","During relaxation","For the less demanding tasks"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Recruited last\\n\\nMotor units are recruited according to the Henneman size principle, which states that motor neurons are activated from smallest to largest depending on the force required.\\n\\nRecruitment order:\\n\\n1.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Slow (Type I) motor units – small neurons, fatigue-resistant, used for low-force, sustained activities.\\n\\n2.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Fast fatigue-resistant (Type IIa) – moderate force and endurance.\\n\\n3.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Fast fatigable (Type IIb/IIx) – large neurons producing high force but fatigue quickly.\\n\\nTherefore, fast fatigable motor units are recruited last, only during high-intensity or powerful muscle contractions.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ba081d65e6","n":227,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following is the unlikely diagnosis for the Darrow-Yannet diagram shown below (dotted lines abnormal)?","options":["Right heart failure","Cirrhosis","Nephrotic syndrome","Diuretic abuse"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Diuretic abuse\\n\\nHyperosmotic Volume Contraction\\n\\n1. Initial Event\\n\\n• Loss of hypertonic fluid\\n• Leads to:\\n\\n• ↓ ECF volume\\n• Reduced ECF osmolarity\\n\\n2. Fluid Shift\\n\\n• ECF becomes hypotonic relative to ICF\\n• Water moves ECF → ICF (osmosis)\\n\\n| Parameter | Change\\n\\n| --------- | ------\\n\\n| ECF volume | ↓ (contraction)\\n\\n| ICF volume | High\\n\\n| Osmolarity (both compartments) | Low","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-ce6428e99f","n":228,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following junctions is/are calcium dependent? A. Desmosomes B. Tight junctions C. Adherens junctions D. Gap junctions","options":["Only A","A &amp; C","Only C","B &amp; C"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A & C\\n\\nCalcium-dependent cell junctions are those that use cadherins, which require Ca\xb2⁺ for proper adhesion between cells.\\n\\nCalcium-dependent junctions:\\n\\n• Desmosomes → mediated by desmogleins and desmocollins (cadherins)\\n• Adherens junctions → mediated by E-cadherin\\n\\nCalcium-independent junctions:\\n\\n• Tight junctions → formed by claudins and occludins\\n• Gap junctions → formed by connexins (connexons) for cell-to-cell communication","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-061a2ff95e","n":229,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following muscle filaments has a fixed length and does not change during contraction or relaxation?","options":["Actin (thin filament)","Myosin (thick filament)","Titin","All of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. All of the above\\n\\nAccording to the sliding filament theory of muscle contraction, the length of individual filaments remains constant during contraction and relaxation; instead, the degree of overlap between filaments changes.\\n\\nFilaments with fixed length:\\n\\n●\xa0Actin (thin filament): slides over myosin but its length remains unchanged.\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0Myosin (thick filament): also maintains a constant length.\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0Titin: an elastic structural protein that stabilizes the sarcomere and connects myosin to the Z-disc; its structure does not shorten during contraction.\\n\\nDuring contraction:\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Sarcomere length decreases\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Z-lines move closer\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 I-band and H-zone decrease\\n\\n●\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 A-band remains constant","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0bccc22679","n":230,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following proteins connects the lines marked in the following image?","options":["Actinin","Titin","Desmin","Myosin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Titin\\n\\nTitin is the largest protein in the human body and acts as a molecular spring within the sarcomere. It extends from the Z line to the M line, anchoring the thick (myosin) filament and maintaining its central position during muscle contraction and relaxation.\\n\\n• The marked lines represent the Z discs and M line of the sarcomere.\\n• Titin spans from the Z disc to the M line, connecting these structures and providing passive elasticity.\\n• It prevents overstretching and helps the sarcomere return to its resting length after contraction.\\n\\n| Protein | Function\\n\\n| ------- | --------\\n\\n| Titin | Connects Z line to M line; molecular spring\\n\\n| α-Actinin | Anchors actin filaments at the Z disc\\n\\n| Desmin | Intermediate filament linking adjacent Z discs of neighboring myofibrils\\n\\n| Myosin | Major thick filament responsible for force generation","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-0a82400125","n":231,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 All Qbank Qs Excluding Pyqs","text":"Which of the following statements regarding skeletal is/are incorrect? 1. Skeletal muscle contraction is initiated by calcium binding to troponin T. 2. Skeletal muscle fibers contain a single nucleus. 3. Skeletal muscle contractions are voluntary. 4. Gap junctions are present in skeletal muscle.","options":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1, 2 and 4 only\\n\\nLet\'s evaluate each statement:\\n\\n1. Skeletal muscle contraction is initiated by calcium binding to troponin T. ❌ Incorrect\\n\\n• Calcium binds to troponin C (TnC), not troponin T (TnT).\\n• Troponin T attaches the troponin complex to tropomyosin, while troponin I inhibits actin-myosin interaction.\\n• Calcium binding to TnC shifts tropomyosin away from the myosin-binding sites on actin, initiating contraction.\\n\\n2. Skeletal muscle fibers contain a single nucleus. ❌ Incorrect\\n\\n• Skeletal muscle fibers are multinucleated due to fusion of myoblasts during development.\\n• The nuclei are located peripherally beneath the sarcolemma.\\n\\n3. Skeletal muscle contractions are voluntary. ✅ Correct\\n\\n• Skeletal muscle is supplied by the somatic nervous system, making its contractions voluntary.\\n\\n4. Gap junctions are present in skeletal muscle. ❌ Incorrect\\n\\n• Skeletal muscle fibers function independently and lack gap junctions.\\n• Gap junctions are characteristic of cardiac muscle (intercalated discs) and single-unit smooth muscle.\\n\\nTherefore, the incorrect statements are 1, 2, and 4.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-14f8e79a10","n":232,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Basics Of Cell Cytoskeleton","text":"A 25-year-old patient presents with a history of recurrent sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and chronic cough. He also mentions that he has had several ear infections and episodes of pneumonia over the past few years. On physical examination, you note digital clubbing. A chest X-ray is obtained. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Kartagener syndrome","Cystic fibrosis","DiGeorge syndrome","Down syndrome"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Kartagener syndrome\\n\\nRecurrent sinusitis, bronchiectasis, chronic cough, and ear infections suggest primary ciliary dyskinesia. Kartagener syndrome is the classic triad of bronchiectasis, sinusitis, and situs inversus due to defective dynein arms. Impaired mucociliary clearance causes repeated respiratory infections and clubbing. The other options do not explain chronic sinopulmonary disease from ciliary dysfunction.\\n\\nDynein arm defect in cilia\\n\\n↓\\n\\nImpaired ciliary movement\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓ Mucociliary clearance\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMucus + pathogens accumulate\\n\\n↓\\n\\nRecurrent infections\\n\\n↓\\n\\nChronic inflammation\\n\\n↓\\n\\nBronchiectasis + lung damageSignet-Ring Sign, Bronchiectasis.\xa0The bronchi (red arrows) are larger than their corresponding arteries (white arrows), the reverse of the normal pattern in which the bronchus is smaller than its corresponding vessel.\\n\\n| Feature | Kartagener | Cystic Fibrosis\\n\\n| ------- | ---------- | ---------------\\n\\n| Defect | Ciliary movement | CFTR chloride channel\\n\\n| Mucus | Normal but not cleared | Thick, sticky\\n\\n| Situs inversus | Common | Rare\\n\\n| Fertility | ↓ (immotile sperm) | ↓ (thick secretions)","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-5f0b099122","n":233,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Basics Of Cell Cytoskeleton","text":"Identify the type of receptor given below.","options":["Sodium glucose co transporter-1","Organic anion transporter-1","GLUT-4","P-glycoprotein pump transporter"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. P-glycoprotein pump transporter\\n\\nP-glycoprotein = ATP-dependent efflux pump → causes multidrug resistance (MDR)\\n\\n“Tumor cells pump out drugs using P-gp → chemotherapy failure.”\\n\\nThe diagram shows an ATP-dependent efflux pump converting ATP → ADP + Pi, indicating an active transporter using primary active transport. P-glycoprotein is an ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter that expels drugs against a concentration gradient.Drug enters cell\\n\\n↓\\n\\nP-glycoprotein recognizes substrate\\n\\n↓\\n\\nATP hydrolysis\\n\\n↓\\n\\nDrug pumped OUT of cell\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓ Intracellular drug concentration\\n\\n↓\\n\\nDrug resistance\\n\\n| Transport Type | Energy | Direction | Example\\n\\n| -------------- | ------ | --------- | -------\\n\\n| Simple diffusion | ❌ No ATP | Down gradient | O₂, CO₂\\n\\n| Facilitated diffusion | ❌ No ATP | Down gradient | GLUT-4\\n\\n| Primary active transport | ✅ ATP | Against gradient | Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, P-gp\\n\\n| Secondary active transport | ❌ Direct ATP | Uses gradient | SGLT\\n\\nGLUT-4 is facilitated diffusion and does not use ATP. SGLT-1 and OAT-1 are secondary active transporters driven by ion gradients, not direct ATP hydrolysis.\\n\\n| Option | Type | Energy | Direction | Key Feature\\n\\n| ------ | ---- | ------ | --------- | -----------\\n\\n| A. SGLT-1 | Secondary active | Indirect | Into cell | Na⁺-glucose cotransport\\n\\n| B. OAT-1 | Secondary active | Indirect | Into cell | Organic anion uptake\\n\\n| C. GLUT-4 | Facilitated diffusion | No ATP | Into cell | Insulin-dependent\\n\\n| D. P-gp | Primary active | ATP | Out of cell | Drug efflux","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-b7608d7334","n":234,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Basics Of Cell Cytoskeleton","text":"Identify the type of transport across the cell membrane represented below?","options":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Primary active transport","Secondary active transport"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Facilitated diffusion\\n\\nThe diagram shows solute movement through specific carrier proteins without ATP use, indicating passive transport. Facilitated diffusion uses membrane carriers to move substances down their concentration gradient. It is selective and saturable but energy independent. Active transport would require ATP or ion gradients, which are not shown.\\n\\nHigh concentration (outside)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nSubstrate binds to carrier protein\\n\\n↓\\n\\nCarrier changes conformation\\n\\n↓\\n\\nSubstrate released inside\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMoves down concentration gradient\\n\\n(No ATP used)\\n\\n| Option | Mechanism | Correct/Incorrect | Explanation\\n\\n| ------ | --------- | ----------------- | -----------\\n\\n| A. Simple diffusion | Direct lipid bilayer passage | ❌ Incorrect | No protein involved\\n\\n| B. Facilitated diffusion | Carrier/channel mediated passive transport | ✅ CORRECT |\\n\\n| C. Primary active transport | Uses ATP directly | ❌ Incorrect | e.g., Na⁺-K⁺ pump\\n\\n| D. Secondary active transport | Uses ion gradient | ❌ Incorrect | e.g., Na⁺-glucose cotransport","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-3483bc8de8","n":235,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Basics Of Cell Cytoskeleton","text":"The image below depicts which of the following types of cell-to-cells signaling?","options":["Paracrine","Autocrine","Endocrine","Merocrine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Paracrine\\n\\nCell secretes signaling molecule\\n\\n↓\\n\\nDiffusion through extracellular fluid\\n\\n↓\\n\\nActs on nearby target cells\\n\\n↓\\n\\nLocal physiological effect","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-2bd3e97743","n":236,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Basics Of Cell Cytoskeleton","text":"Tight junction is located at which part of the cell?","options":["Apical","Apicolateral","Basal","Basolateral"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Apicolateral\\n\\nTight junctions sit like a “sealant ring” at the apicolateral border, preventing leakage between cells and maintaining polarity. Tight junctions (zonula occludens) are located at the apicolateral border of epithelial cells, forming the most apical component of the junctional complex. Two Key Functions:\\n\\n1.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Barrier function → blocks paracellular transport\\n\\n2.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Fence function → maintains polarity (apical vs basolateral domains)\\n\\n| Junction | Location | Function | Key Proteins\\n\\n| -------- | -------- | -------- | ------------\\n\\n| Tight junction | Apicolateral | Barrier, polarity | Claudin, Occludin\\n\\n| Adherens junction | Below tight | Cell adhesion | Cadherins\\n\\n| Desmosome | Lateral | Mechanical strength | Desmoglein\\n\\n| Gap junction | Lateral | Communication | Connexin\\n\\nAdherens junctions and desmosomes lie more basally.\\n\\nClinical correlations:\\n\\n1. Leaky gut / diarrhea\\n\\n• Loss of tight junction integrity → ↑ permeability\\n\\n2. Cholera toxin\\n\\n• Affects ion transport → overwhelms junctional barrier\\n\\n3. Blood-brain barrier\\n\\n• Tight junctions are extremely well-developed","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2ec193ddc9","n":237,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Basics Of Cell Cytoskeleton","text":"Which of the following are present in the intercalated disc of cardiac muscle? 1. Fascia adherens 2. Desmosome (Macula adherens) 3. Gap junction 4. Tight junction (Zonula occludens)","options":["1, 2, 3","3 only","2, 3, 4","1, 2, 3, 4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 3\\n\\nIntercalated discs are specialized junctions that connect adjacent cardiac muscle cells, providing both mechanical strength and electrical coupling required for synchronized cardiac contraction. They contain–\\n\\n• Fascia adherens: anchors actin filaments and transmits contractile force.\\n• Desmosomes (macula adherens): provide strong mechanical attachment, preventing separation of cardiomyocytes during contraction.\\n• Gap junctions: allow rapid passage of ions between cells, enabling electrical coupling and synchronized depolarization.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. Gap junction only: Ignores the mechanical junctions essential for maintaining tissue integrity.\\n• C. Tight junction included: Tight junctions (zonula occludens) are absent in cardiac intercalated discs.\\n• D. Includes tight junctions: Incorrect because tight junctions are characteristic of epithelial cells, not cardiac muscle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fb4c6d1899","n":238,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Basics Of Cell Cytoskeleton","text":"Which of the following transport mechanism doesn’t require ATP? Select the correct answer","options":["1, 2, 3","1, 2, 3, 4","2 only","3 &amp; 4 only"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 3\\n\\nThe image shows four different transport mechanisms across cell membranes:\\n\\n1. Simple diffusion through membrane: This shows direct diffusion of molecules through the lipid bilayer from high concentration to low concentration. This is also passive transport that doesn\'t require ATP.\\n2. Simple diffusion through channels: This shows molecules moving through a protein channel from high concentration to low concentration. This is passive transport that follows the concentration gradient without requiring ATP. Channels have gates that simply open or close without configurational changes, allowing molecules to pass according to their concentration gradient.\\n3. Paracellular transport: This shows molecules moving between cells through the spaces between adjacent cells, not through the cells themselves. This is passive movement along the concentration gradient and doesn\'t require ATP.\\n\\n| Mechanism | ATP Required | Example | Key Feature\\n\\n| --------- | ------------ | ------- | -----------\\n\\n| Simple diffusion | ❌ | O₂, CO₂ | No protein needed\\n\\n| Facilitated diffusion | ❌ | GLUT-4 | Uses carrier\\n\\n| Secondary active | ❌ (indirect) | SGLT | Uses Na⁺ gradient\\n\\n| Primary active | ✅ | Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase | Direct ATP use\\n\\n| Type | Example\\n\\n| ---- | -------\\n\\n| Symport | SGLT (Na⁺ + glucose)\\n\\n| Antiport | Na⁺/Ca\xb2⁺ exchanger\\n\\nOther Option:\\n\\n1. Active transport: This shows fewer molecules on one side and more on the other side, indicating movement against the concentration gradient (from low to high concentration). This requires energy in the form of ATP to power the transport proteins.","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-7ce3ee9ce5","n":239,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Body Fluid Compartments","text":"Two compartments each contain 1 L of water and are separated by a semipermeable membrane permeable to water and glucose but impermeable to fructose. Which statement is true at equilibrium?","options":["Water is more in compartment 1","Glucose is more in compartment 2","Fructose concentration becomes equal in both compartments","Water level becomes equal in both compartments"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Water is more in compartment 1\\n\\n• Glucose is permeable and diffuses until its concentration becomes equal on both sides.\\n\\n• Fructose cannot cross the membrane.\\n• Therefore, fructose acts as the effective osmole.\\n\\n• Initially, compartment 1 contains more fructose.\\n\\n• Water moves toward compartment 1 by osmosis.\\n• Hence, compartment 1 gains water and its volume increases.\\n\\n• Water levels do not become equal.\\n\\n• Glucose concentration becomes equal because it diffuses freely.\\n• Fructose concentration remains unequal because it cannot diffuse.\\n\\n• The direction of water movement is determined by the impermeant solute.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-8206d22b72","n":240,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Body Fluid Compartments","text":"Which parameter is required to calculate extracellular fluid (ECF) volume using a tracer method?","options":["Creatinine clearance","Inulin clearance","Amount of tracer distributed / concentration","Total body water"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Amount of tracer distributed / concentration\\n\\nTo measure any fluid compartment (like ECF), we use:\\n\\nThis is the indicator dilution principle\\n\\nTracer dilution principle states: volume = amount of tracer injected \xf7 equilibrium concentration. A marker that distributes only in ECF is used, and after equilibration its plasma concentration is measured. This ratio directly gives ECF volume. Clearance values and total body water are not used for this calculation.\\n\\nKnown amount of tracer injected\\n\\n↓\\n\\nTracer distributes uniformly in target compartment (ECF)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nSample taken → concentration measured\\n\\n↓\\n\\nApply formula:\\n\\nVolume = Amount / Concentration","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":2}]},{"bankId":"qb-2e3b2a6bb6","n":241,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Miscellaneous","text":"Consider the following pairs with respect to muscle channelopathies. Which of the pairs given below are correctly matched?","options":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4 only","1, 2 and 4 only","2 and 3 only"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 2 and 4 only\\n\\n• 1. Sodium → Paramyotonia congenita ✓\\n\\n• Caused by mutations in the SCN4A gene, encoding the skeletal muscle voltage-gated Na⁺ channel.\\n• Produces paradoxical worsening of stiffness with repeated activity.\\n\\n• 2. Calcium → Malignant hyperthermia ✓\\n\\n• Malignant hyperthermia is associated with abnormal Ca\xb2⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, classically due to RYR1 mutations.\\n• Excess intracellular Ca\xb2⁺ → sustained muscle contraction → hypermetabolism, hypercapnia, hyperthermia, and rigidity.\\n• Strictly speaking, the relevant channel is the ryanodine receptor (RyR1), a Ca\xb2⁺-release channel.\\n\\n• 3. Ryanodine receptor → Becker disease ✗\\n\\n• Becker muscular dystrophy is caused by mutations in the DMD gene, resulting in abnormal/deficient dystrophin.\\n• RyR1 mutations are associated with malignant hyperthermia and certain congenital myopathies, not Becker dystrophy.\\n\\n• 4. Chloride → Thomsen disease ✓\\n\\n• Thomsen disease is autosomal dominant myotonia congenita, caused by CLCN1 (Cl⁻ channel) mutations.\\n• Defective Cl⁻ conductance → membrane hyperexcitability → myotonia.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-3ea5edf3c2","n":242,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Miscellaneous","text":"Identify the points labelled A, B, and C on the strength–duration curve shown in the image below.","options":["A. Rheobase, B. Chronaxie, C. Utilization time","A. Chronaxie, B. Utilization time, C. Rheobase","A. Utilization time, B. Rheobase, C. Chronaxie","A. Rheobase, B. Utilization time, C. Chronaxie"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A. Rheobase, B. Chronaxie, C. Utilization time\\n\\nThe strength–duration curve describes the relationship between the minimum stimulus strength and the duration of stimulation required to excite a nerve or muscle. The three key parameters are rheobase, chronaxie, and utilization time, which are used to assess tissue excitability and detect nerve injury or denervation.\\n\\nWhy the labels are correct:\\n\\n• A. Rheobase: Minimum stimulus strength of very long duration required to produce an action potential.\\n• B. Chronaxie: Minimum time required to excite a tissue using a stimulus of 2 \xd7 rheobase.\\n• C. Utilization time: Minimum duration required for a rheobase-strength stimulus to produce excitation.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-814e0ff6a6","n":243,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Miscellaneous","text":"In a patient with pulmonary embolism causing complete obstruction of blood flow. Which of the following point on V/Q curve corresponds with given condition?","options":["Point A","Point B","Point C","Point D"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Point D\\n\\nIn pulmonary embolism with complete obstruction of blood flow, the affected lung region receives normal ventilation but zero perfusion, creating alveolar dead space. The V/Q ratio approaches infinity (V/Q → ∞).\\n\\nThe formula: V/Q = Ventilation/Perfusion = Normal/0 = ∞\\n\\nAt Point D:\\n\\n• PO₂ ≈ 150 mmHg (approaches inspired air PO₂)\\n• PCO₂ ≈ 0 mmHg (no CO₂ delivered from blood)\\n• Alveolar gas resembles humidified inspired air\\n• No gas exchange occurs despite normal ventilation\\n\\nPulmonary embolism\\n\\n↓\\n\\nBlood flow blocked (Perfusion = 0)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nAlveoli still ventilated\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNo gas exchange possible\\n\\n↓\\n\\nAlveolar gas ≈ Inspired air\\n\\n↓\\n\\nV/Q → ∞ (Dead space)\\n\\nThis represents pure dead space ventilation where air enters and exits the alveoli without participating in gas exchange, essentially wasting ventilation.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-13cadfa556","n":244,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Miscellaneous","text":"Sour taste is mediated by:","options":["TRPV1 Channel","Metabotropic Receptor","GPCR T1R1","GPCR T1R3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. TRPV1 Channel\\n\\nSour taste = H⁺ ions → activate ion channels (mainly TRP family like TRPV1) → depolarization\\n\\nSour taste is mediated by proton-sensitive ion channels activated by H⁺ from acids. TRPV1-related channels respond to acidity and depolarize taste receptor cells. This mechanism is ion channel–based, not GPCR mediated. T1R receptors are involved in sweet and umami, not sour taste.\\n\\nAcidic food (↑ H⁺)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nH⁺ interacts with taste receptor cell\\n\\n↓\\n\\nActivation of TRPV1 / inhibition of K⁺ channels\\n\\n↓\\n\\nDepolarization\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNeurotransmitter release\\n\\n↓\\n\\nGustatory nerve activation","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-c0d3271b7e","n":245,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Miscellaneous","text":"Which of the following is an example of a feedforward mechanism?","options":["Vasoconstriction after exposure to cold","Increase in heart rate on standing from supine position","Increase in cardiac output in response to anemia","Increased vasopressin secretion during dehydration"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Vasoconstriction after exposure to cold\\n\\nFeedforward = anticipatory control\\n\\nBody acts BEFORE a significant change occurs (like a smart thermostat that predicts winter, not waits for freezing). Feedforward control anticipates a disturbance and activates a response before core variables change. Cold exposure directly triggers cutaneous vasoconstriction via hypothalamic thermoregulatory pathways to prevent heat loss.\\n\\nexamples:\\n\\n• Salivation before food (cephalic phase)\\n• ↑ HR before exercise (anticipatory response)\\n• Insulin release on sight of food\\n\\n1. Feedback Mechanism (Reactive)\\n\\n• Responds after change occurs\\n• Maintains homeostasis\\n\\nExample: ↑ ADH in dehydration, Blood glucose regulation, Calcium homeostasis, Blood pressure control\\n\\n2. Feedforward Mechanism (Anticipatory)\\n\\n• Activated before actual disturbance\\n• Based on prediction / external cues\\n\\nExample: Cold exposure → vasoconstriction BEFORE core temp drops\\n\\nThe other options are feedback responses correcting an already altered physiologic state. Thus cold-induced vasoconstriction is a classic feedforward mechanism.\\n\\nB) Increase in heart rate on standing\\n\\n• Due to baroreceptor reflex\\n• Trigger: ↓ BP after standing\\n\\nThis is negative feedback\\n\\nC) Increase in cardiac output in anemia\\n\\n• Trigger: ↓ O₂ delivery\\n• Body responds after deficit occurs\\n\\nFeedback mechanism\\n\\nD) Increased vasopressin (ADH) in dehydration\\n\\n• Trigger: ↑ plasma osmolality\\n\\nNegative feedback","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9ff21c0cbf","n":246,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Miscellaneous","text":"Which of the following mechanisms is seen in the baroreceptor reflex?","options":["Feedforward","Positive feedback","Negative feedback","Adaptive control regulation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Negative feedback\\n\\nThe baroreceptor reflex maintains arterial pressure through negative feedback. A rise in BP increases baroreceptor firing, which reduces sympathetic outflow and lowers heart rate and vascular tone. A fall in BP produces the opposite response. The system continuously counteracts deviations from normal pressure.\\n\\n↑ Blood pressure\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↑ Stretch of baroreceptors\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↑ Firing via CN IX (carotid) & CN X (aortic)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMedulla (CV center)\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓ Sympathetic + ↑ Parasympathetic\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓ Heart rate + ↓ contractility + vasodilation\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓ Blood pressure (returns to normal)\\n\\n| Option | Mechanism | Correct/Incorrect | Explanation\\n\\n| ------ | --------- | ----------------- | -----------\\n\\n| A. Feedforward | Anticipatory response | ❌ Incorrect | Baroreflex responds AFTER change\\n\\n| B. Positive feedback | Amplifies change | ❌ Incorrect | Would worsen BP instability\\n\\n| C. Negative feedback | Opposes change | ✅ CORRECT |\\n\\n| D. Adaptive control | Long-term adjustment | ❌ Incorrect | Baroreflex is short-term regulation","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-8ad7c00f0d","n":247,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"A patient was brought to the hospital after a road traffic accident. He had severe muscle injury and his serum K+ level was found to be 5.5 mEq/L. What change will happen to the resting membrane potential in this patient?","options":["No change","Becomes more negative","Becomes more positive","First becomes more positive, then negative"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Becomes more positive\\n\\nHyperkalemia reduces the K⁺ gradient across the cell membrane, decreasing outward K⁺ diffusion. This makes the resting membrane potential less negative (partial depolarization). As extracellular K⁺ rises, the membrane shifts toward threshold. Hence the resting membrane potential becomes more positive.\\n\\n↑ Extracellular K⁺\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓ Concentration gradient\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↓ K⁺ efflux from cell\\n\\n↓\\n\\nLess positive charge leaving\\n\\n↓\\n\\nInside becomes less negative\\n\\n↓\\n\\nRMP shifts toward zero (more positive)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7eba31c84b","n":248,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"A skeletal muscle biopsy is examined under an electron microscope before and during contraction. As actin filaments slide over myosin filaments, which of the following changes is expected in the sarcomere?","options":["A band shortens while the I band remains unchanged","H zone and I band decrease in length, while the A band remains constant","A band decreases in length, while the H zone remains unchanged","Z lines move farther apart"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. H zone and I band decrease in length, while the A band remains constant\\n\\nAccording to the sliding filament theory, muscle contraction occurs because thin (actin) filaments slide inward over thick (myosin) filaments. The filaments themselves do not shorten; instead, the degree of overlap between them increases.\\n\\nAs contraction occurs:\\n\\n• H zone decreases or disappears because the region containing only myosin becomes smaller.\\n• I band decreases because the region containing only actin shortens.\\n• A band remains constant because the length of the myosin thick filament does not change.\\n• Z lines move closer together, resulting in shortening of the sarcomere.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. A band shortens while the I band remains unchanged: Incorrect. The A band remains constant, while the I band shortens.\\n• B. H zone and I band decrease in length, while the A band remains constant✅: Correct.\\n• C. A band decreases in length, while the H zone remains unchanged: Incorrect. The opposite occurs: A band remains unchanged, whereas the H zone decreases.\\n• D. Z lines move farther apart: Incorrect. During contraction, Z lines move closer together, shortening the sarcomere.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1bd8336be3","n":249,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"All the following are properties of ventricular muscle fibers: 1. All or none law 2. Length-tension relation 3. Tetanus 4. Long refractory period","options":["1 only","1 and 2","1, 2, and 3","1, 2, and 4"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1, 2, and 4\\n\\nAll-or-None Law\\n\\n• Cardiac muscle behaves as a functional syncytium\\n• Once threshold reached → entire ventricle contracts maximally\\n• No graded contraction like skeletal motor units\\n\\nLength–Tension Relationship\\n\\n• Preload ↑ → sarcomere length ↑ → force ↑\\n• Mechanism:\\n\\n• Optimal actin-myosin overlap\\n• ↑ Ca\xb2⁺ sensitivity of myofilaments\\n\\nThis is the Frank-Starling law: “The heart pumps what it receives”\\n\\nVentricular muscle behaves like a syncytium with unique electrical discipline.\\n\\nIt contracts as a unit, obeys Frank–Starling law, and absolutely refuses tetanus.\\n\\nCardiac action potential (with plateau)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nProlonged Ca\xb2⁺ influx (L-type channels)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nLong absolute refractory period\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNo re-excitation possible\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNo summation → No tetanus\\n\\n• Cardiac AP duration: \\\\~200–300 ms\\n• Contraction duration: overlaps with refractory period\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n• Second stimulus cannot trigger contraction\\n• Prevents sustained contraction (which would be fatal)\\n\\n| Option | Property | Present in Ventricular Muscle? | Explanation\\n\\n| ------ | -------- | ------------------------------ | -----------\\n\\n| 1 | All-or-none law | ✅ YES | Entire myocardium contracts as a syncytium via gap junctions\\n\\n| 2 | Length-tension relation | ✅ YES | Basis of Frank-Starling mechanism (↑ filling → ↑ force)\\n\\n| 3 | Tetanus | ❌ NO | Prevented due to long refractory period\\n\\n| 4 | Long refractory period | ✅ YES | Due to plateau phase (Ca\xb2⁺ influx)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9548be64b9","n":250,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"During the action potential, at which point in the given image are both Na⁺ and K⁺ conductance at their lowest?","options":["A","B","E","F"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A\\n\\nAt the resting membrane potential (Point A), both voltage-gated Na⁺ and K⁺ channels are closed, so Na⁺ and K⁺ conductance are at their lowest. The resting membrane potential is maintained primarily by K⁺ leak channels and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, not by voltage-gated ion channels.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• B. B (Threshold): Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels begin to open, increasing Na⁺ conductance.\\n• C. E (Repolarization): K⁺ conductance is high due to opening of voltage-gated K⁺ channels, while Na⁺ channels are inactivated.\\n• D. F (After-hyperpolarization): K⁺ channels remain open longer than necessary, so K⁺ conductance is still elevated.\\n\\nExam Pearls:\\n\\n• Point A (Resting membrane potential): Lowest voltage-gated Na⁺ and K⁺ conductance\\n• Depolarization: Rapid ↑ Na⁺ conductance due to opening of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels\\n• Peak of action potential: Na⁺ channels become inactivated; K⁺ channels begin opening\\n• Repolarization & after-hyperpolarization: ↑ K⁺ conductance due to delayed closure of voltage-gated K⁺ channels\\n• Resting membrane potential (\\\\~–70 mV): Maintained mainly by K⁺ leak channels and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, not voltage-gated channels.","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-d5d28352f8","n":251,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"Identify the correct labeling (A, B, C, D) in the given image?","options":["Z line, A band, H zone, I Band","A band, Z line, H zone, M Band","A band, I Band, Z line, H zone","Z line, A band, M Band, H zone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Z line, A band, H zone, I Band\\n\\n• A is the Z line: The light line marking the boundary between sarcomeres\\n• B (A band): The darker, thicker region representing myosin filaments\\n• C (H zone): The lighter area between the A bands\\n\\nMuscle contraction begins\\n\\n↓\\n\\nActin slides over myosin\\n\\n↓\\n\\nZ lines move closer\\n\\n↓\\n\\nI band shortens\\n\\n↓\\n\\nH zone disappears\\n\\n↓\\n\\nA band remains constant","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-8606577a71","n":252,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"Identify the correct order of End plate potential at the neuromuscular junction:","options":["Ca2+ entry in Axon Terminal → Ach Release → Na+ channel opens → Action potential","Na+ channel opens → Action potential → Ca2+ entry in Axon Terminal → Ach Release","Ach Release → Na+ channel opens → Action potential → Ca2+ entry in Axon Terminal","Action potential → Ca2+ entry in Axon Terminal → Ach Release → Na+ channel opens"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Ca2+ entry in Axon Terminal → Ach Release → Na+ channel opens → Action potential\\n\\nCa\xb2⁺ entry in Axon Terminal → ACh Release → Na⁺ channel opens → Action potential\\n\\nThe Full Sequence\\n\\n| Step | Event | Location\\n\\n| ---- | ----- | --------\\n\\n| 1 | Motor nerve AP arrives | Axon terminal\\n\\n| 2 | Ca\xb2⁺ enters via VGCC | Axon terminal\\n\\n| 3 | ACh released into synaptic cleft | Presynaptic\\n\\n| 4 | ACh binds nicotinic receptors | Motor end plate\\n\\n| 5 | Na⁺ channels open → Na⁺ influx | Postsynaptic membrane\\n\\n| 6 | End plate potential generated | Motor end plate\\n\\n| 7 | If EPP reaches threshold → muscle AP | Muscle fiber\\n\\nKey Conceptual Point\\n\\nThe question asks specifically about end plate potential — this is a postsynaptic event that begins with ACh binding, NOT with the muscle action potential.\\n\\nThe EPP is the graded potential at the motor end plate caused by Na⁺ influx. It is what leads to the muscle action potential, so the action potential comes last in this sequence.\\n\\nWhy Other Options Fail\\n\\nB — Starts with Na⁺ channel opening before ACh release. Na⁺ channels open because ACh binds — can\'t happen first.\\n\\nC — Starts with ACh release, skipping Ca\xb2⁺ entry. ACh cannot be released without the Ca\xb2⁺ trigger — this is the step that couples electrical signal to vesicle fusion.\\n\\nD — Starts with action potential. This confuses the muscle AP with the nerve AP. The sequence being asked is what happens after the nerve AP arrives — the nerve AP is the trigger, not part of the EPP sequence itself.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-24335b7273","n":253,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"If a simple muscle twitch has a latent period of 10 milliseconds, a contraction period of 40 milliseconds, and a relaxation period of 50 milliseconds, what is the tetanizing frequency?","options":["20HZ","40HZ","25HZ","50HZ"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 25HZ\\n\\nTetanizing frequency = frequency at which next stimulus arrives before relaxation completes → sustained contraction (tetanus).\\n\\n• Latent period = 10 ms\\n• Contraction period = 40 ms\\n• Relaxation period = 50 ms\\n• 1 / contraction period = 1 / 40 ms = 1 / 0.040 s = 25 Hz ✔\\n• The latent period (10 ms) and relaxation period (50 ms) are pure distractor data here\\n\\n| Phase | Duration | Event\\n\\n| ----- | -------- | -----\\n\\n| Latent | 10 ms | Excitation–contraction coupling\\n\\n| Contraction | 40 ms | Cross-bridge cycling\\n\\n| Relaxation | 50 ms | Ca\xb2⁺ reuptake","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8b1ac2b6a9","n":254,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"In a nerve, why is it impossible to generate a new action potential if a second stimulus is applied during the depolarization phase of the current action potential?","options":["Sodium channels are closed","Sodium channels are opened","Potassium channels are closed","Neurons are hyperpolarized"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sodium channels are opened\\n\\nDuring the absolute refractory period (which corresponds to the depolarization phase and early repolarization of the action potential), voltage-gated sodium (Na⁺) channels are already open or in the inactivated state and cannot be reopened by another stimulus. Therefore, no second action potential can be generated, regardless of the strength of the stimulus.\\n\\nWhy not the other options?\\n\\n• A. Sodium channels are closed: Incorrect. During depolarization, sodium channels are open initially and then become inactivated, which is the actual reason for the refractory period.\\n• C. Potassium channels are closed: Incorrect. Voltage-gated potassium channels open during repolarization, not during the early depolarization phase.\\n• D. Neurons are hyperpolarized: Incorrect. Hyperpolarization occurs during the relative refractory period, when a stronger-than-normal stimulus can generate another action potential.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e57edabfee","n":255,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"In the adjoining image of the muscle fiber action potential, the phase marked B is due to which of the following?","options":["Efflux of potassium","Influx of potassium","Sodium efflux","Calcium influx"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Efflux of potassium\\n\\nResting membrane potential (-90 mV)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nStimulus reaches threshold\\n\\n↓\\n\\nVoltage-gated Na⁺ channels open\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNa⁺ influx → Depolarization\\n\\n↓\\n\\nNa⁺ channels inactivate\\n\\n↓\\n\\nVoltage-gated K⁺ channels open\\n\\n↓\\n\\nK⁺ efflux → Repolarization (Phase B)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nReturn to resting potential\\n\\nK⁺ efflux cause repolarization\\n\\n• Intracellular K⁺ concentration is high\\n• When K⁺ channels open → K⁺ moves outward\\n• This:\\n\\n• Removes positive charge\\n• Restores negative membrane potential\\n\\n| Phase | Channel Activity\\n\\n| ----- | ----------------\\n\\n| Depolarization | Fast Na⁺ channels open\\n\\n| Repolarization | Na⁺ channels inactivate + K⁺ channels open\\n\\n| Hyperpolarization | Continued K⁺ efflux\\n\\n| Feature | Skeletal Muscle | Cardiac Muscle (Ventricular) | Smooth Muscle\\n\\n| ------- | --------------- | ---------------------------- | -------------\\n\\n| AP duration | Very short (2–5 ms) | Long (200–300 ms) | Variable, slow\\n\\n| Depolarization ion | Na⁺ influx | Na⁺ influx | Ca\xb2⁺ influx\\n\\n| Plateau phase | ❌ Absent | ✅ Present | ❌ (but slow waves may exist)\\n\\n| Repolarization | K⁺ efflux | K⁺ efflux | K⁺ efflux\\n\\n| Main ion for contraction | Ca\xb2⁺ (from SR) | Ca\xb2⁺ (ECF + SR) | Ca\xb2⁺ (mainly ECF)\\n\\n| Refractory period | Short | Very long | Variable\\n\\n| Tetanus | ✅ Possible | ❌ Not possible | ✅ Possible (sustained contraction)\\n\\n| Control | Voluntary | Involuntary | Involuntary\\n\\n| Special feature | Fast response | Plateau prevents tetany | Slow, sustained tone","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-7070f543ee","n":256,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"The motor supply of the muscle spindle is which of the following?","options":["Alpha neuron","Gamma neuron","Beta neuron","Delta neuron"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Gamma neuron\\n\\nMuscle Spindle-\\n\\n• A stretch receptor embedded within skeletal muscle\\n• Detects:\\n\\n• Muscle length\\n• Rate of stretch\\n\\nCritical for:\\n\\n• Muscle tone\\n• Stretch reflex (deep tendon reflex)\\n\\nTwo key components:\\n\\n| Component | Function\\n\\n| --------- | --------\\n\\n| Intrafusal fibers | Sensory detection\\n\\n| Extrafusal fibers | Muscle contraction\\n\\n| Neuron Type | Target | Function\\n\\n| ----------- | ------ | --------\\n\\n| Alpha motor neuron | Extrafusal fibers | Muscle contraction\\n\\n| Gamma motor neuron | Intrafusal fibers | Adjust spindle sensitivity\\n\\nMuscle stretch\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMuscle spindle activated (Ia afferents)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nSignal to spinal cord\\n\\n↓\\n\\nAlpha motor neuron activation\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMuscle contraction (reflex)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nGamma motor neuron adjusts spindle tension\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMaintains sensitivity during contraction\\n\\n| Feature | Alpha Motor Neuron | Gamma Motor Neuron\\n\\n| ------- | ------------------ | ------------------\\n\\n| Target | Extrafusal fibers | Intrafusal fibers\\n\\n| Function | Muscle contraction | Spindle sensitivity\\n\\n| Role in reflex | Effector | Modulator\\n\\nGamma Loop\\n\\nDescending input\\n\\n↓\\n\\nGamma motor neuron activates spindle\\n\\n↓\\n\\nSpindle stretch sensitivity ↑\\n\\n↓\\n\\nIa afferent firing ↑\\n\\n↓\\n\\nAlpha motor neuron activation\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMuscle tone maintained\\n\\nThis loop explains baseline muscle tone\\n\\nClinical Correlation\\n\\n• UMN lesions → ↑ gamma activity → spasticity\\n• LMN lesions → ↓ tone (loss of gamma influence)","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-b3d0393edb","n":257,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"True statement regarding the given nerve action potential curve is?","options":["Threshold point is at A","At point E, the nerve is more excitable","Point C to D is due to opening of Na+ and closure of K+ channels","Point B to D is refractory period"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Point B to D is refractory period\\n\\nPoints B to D span depolarization through early repolarization, corresponding to the absolute refractory period. During this phase, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are inactivated and a new action potential cannot be generated. Excitability is reduced until repolarization progresses. Hence B to D represents the refractory period.\\n\\n| Option | Statement | Correct/Incorrect | Explanation\\n\\n| ------ | --------- | ----------------- | -----------\\n\\n| A | Threshold at A | ❌ Incorrect | A = resting potential, threshold at B\\n\\n| B | At E nerve is more excitable | ❌ Incorrect | E = hyperpolarization → less excitable\\n\\n| C | C–D due to Na⁺ opening & K⁺ closure | ❌ Incorrect | Opposite: Na⁺ closes, K⁺ opens\\n\\n| D | B–D is refractory period | ✅ CORRECT | Covers absolute + most of refractory phase","img":true},{"bankId":"qb-2287e8fc4b","n":258,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"Which ion is most responsible for the resting membrane potential (RMP):","options":["K+","Ca2+","Na+","Cl-"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. K+\\n\\nResting membrane potential is primarily determined by potassium permeability across the cell membrane. High resting K⁺ conductance through leak channels allows outward diffusion, making the inside negative. The membrane potential closely approximates the K⁺ equilibrium potential. Na⁺ and Ca\xb2⁺ have minimal resting permeability compared to K⁺.\\n\\nHigh intracellular K⁺\\n\\n↓\\n\\nK⁺ diffuses out via leak channels\\n\\n↓\\n\\nLoss of positive charge from inside\\n\\n↓\\n\\nCell interior becomes negative\\n\\n↓\\n\\nElectrical gradient opposes further K⁺ loss\\n\\n↓\\n\\nEquilibrium reached → Resting membrane potential","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-ade7c59a62","n":259,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"Which of the following actin myosin cross bridges pair correctly represents the point (B-C) shown on length-tension relationship in the sarcomere curve?","options":["[Option image — not included in this edition]","[Option image — not included in this edition]","[Option image — not included in this edition]","[Option image — not included in this edition]"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. [image not included in this edition]\\n\\nThe length-tension relationship describes how muscle force varies with sarcomere length. Point B-C on the graph represents the plateau phase where maximum active tension (100%) is generated. At B–C (Plateau phase):\\n\\n• Optimal overlap between actin and myosin\\n• Maximum number of cross-bridges formed\\n• No interference, no overstretching\\n\\nResult: Maximum active tension\\n\\nAt this optimal length (2.0-2.2 μm):\\n\\n• All myosin heads can interact with actin binding sites\\n• No interference from overlapping actin filaments\\n• Maximum number of cross-bridges form simultaneously\\n• This produces peak contractile force\\n\\nOption B correctly shows this optimal arrangement - complete overlap between actin and myosin without actin-actin interference, allowing maximum cross-bridge formation and force generation.","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-093ed3f676","n":260,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"Which of the following contributes to the latent period in a single muscle twitch? a. Excitation-contraction coupling b. Opening of sodium channels on NMJ c. Transmission of impulse from the site of stimulus to NMJ d. Pumping of calcium back to the sarcoplasmic reticulum","options":["a and b","a, b, c","only b","a, b, c, d"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. a, b, c\\n\\nThe latent period of a muscle twitch is the short delay (≈2–5 ms) between stimulus application and start of contraction.\\n\\nThis period includes all events before actual cross-bridge cycling begins. The latent period represents the time between stimulus and onset of contraction and includes electrical and coupling events before tension develops. It covers impulse transmission to the NMJ, opening of Na⁺ channels with depolarization, and excitation–contraction coupling with Ca\xb2⁺ release. Pumping Ca\xb2⁺ back into the SR occurs during relaxation, not latency. Hence a, b, and c contribute to the latent period.\\n\\nStimulus applied\\n\\n↓\\n\\nImpulse travels along motor nerve (c)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nAcetylcholine release at NMJ → Na⁺ channels open (b)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nEnd plate potential → Muscle action potential\\n\\n↓\\n\\nT-tubule depolarization\\n\\n↓\\n\\nCa\xb2⁺ release from SR (Excitation-Contraction Coupling) (a)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nActin-myosin interaction begins → Contraction starts\\n\\nLatent period reflects 3 physiological delays:\\n\\n1. Neural delay\\n\\n• Nerve conduction time\\n\\n2. Synaptic delay (\\\\~0.5 ms)\\n\\n• ACh release, diffusion, receptor binding\\n\\n3. E-C coupling delay\\n\\n• T-tubule depolarization\\n• DHPR–RyR interaction\\n• Ca\xb2⁺ release\\n\\nMost important contributor: Excitation–contraction coupling","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9c12ae3b9f","n":261,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"Which of the following statements about the nerve action potential curve is correct?","options":["Threshold point is at A","At point E, the nerve is more excitable","Point C to D is due to opening of Na+ and closure of K+ channels","Point B to D is refractory period"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Point B to D is refractory period\\n\\nSegment B to D spans depolarization and early repolarization when voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are inactivated. During this interval, a second action potential cannot be generated, defining the absolute refractory period.\\n\\nExcitability only begins to return after further repolarization. Hence B to D represents the refractory period.","img":true,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"imgMissing","count":1}]},{"bankId":"qb-46cd4ebc9d","n":262,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"General Physiology \xb7 Nerve Muscle Physiology","text":"Which one of the following ion is released by the ryanodine receptor (RyR) leading to malignant hyperthermia?","options":["Na+","K+","Cl-","Ca2+"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Ca2+\\n\\nRyanodine receptors are Ca\xb2⁺ release channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum of skeletal muscle. In malignant hyperthermia, defective RyR causes uncontrolled Ca\xb2⁺ release into the cytosol. Excess intracellular Ca\xb2⁺ leads to sustained muscle contraction and hypermetabolism. Thus Ca\xb2⁺ is the ion responsible.\\n\\nNormal E–C Coupling-\\n\\nAction potential in muscle\\n\\n↓\\n\\nT-tubule depolarization\\n\\n↓\\n\\nDHP receptor activation\\n\\n↓\\n\\nRyanodine receptor (RyR) opens\\n\\n↓\\n\\nCa\xb2⁺ released from SR\\n\\n↓\\n\\nCa\xb2⁺ binds troponin\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMuscle contraction\\n\\nIn Malignant Hyperthermia\\n\\nTrigger (e.g., halothane, succinylcholine)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nDefective RyR (genetic mutation)\\n\\n↓\\n\\nUncontrolled Ca\xb2⁺ release\\n\\n↓\\n\\nSustained muscle contraction\\n\\n↓\\n\\n↑ ATP consumption\\n\\n↓\\n\\nHeat production ↑↑\\n\\n↓\\n\\nMuscle rigidity + hyperthermia\\n\\nTreatment: Dantrolene\\n\\nMechanism:\\n\\n• Blocks RyR\\n• ↓ Ca\xb2⁺ release","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-18e585bece","n":263,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A lesion in which lobe will lead to impairment of hand-eye coordination?","options":["Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe","Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Parietal lobe\\n\\nA lesion in the parietal lobe will lead to\xa0impairment of hand-eye coordination.\\n\\nThe somatic sensory area and related parts of the posterior parietal lobe project to the premotor cortex. Some of these neurons are concerned with aiming and manipulating the hands at an object, whereas other neurons are concerned with hand-eye coordination. Neurons in this posterior parietal cortex contribute to the descending pathways involved in motor control.\\n\\nThere are two parietal lobes, the dominant side, and the non-dominant side.\\n\\n• The functions of the dominant side of the parietal lobe are calculation, language, planned movement, appreciation of size, shape, weight, and texture. A lesion here causes dyscalculia, dysphasia, dyslexia, apraxia, agnosia, homonymous hemianopia.\\n• The function of the non-dominant side is spatial orientation\xa0and constructional skills. Its lesion causes spatial disorientation, constructional apraxia, neglect of the non-dominant side, dressing apraxia, and homonymous hemianopia.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: The frontal lobe is responsible for personality, emotional response, social behavior. Any lesion in the frontal lobe causes disinhibition, antisocial behavior, impaired memory, incontinence, anosmia.\\n\\nOption C: The temporal lobe has two lobes and is divided as dominant and non-dominant.\\n\\n• The dominant temporal lobe has important functions like auditory perception, speech, language, verbal memory, smell. Lesions lead to dysphasia, dyslexia, poor memory, complex hallucinations (smell, sound, vision), impaired face recognition (Prosopagnosia) and homonymous hemianopia.\\n• The nondominant temporal lobe auditory perception, music, tone sequences, non-verbal memory (faces, shapes, music). Lesions here leads to poor non-verbal memory, loss of musical skills, complex hallucinations, and homonymous hemianopia.\\n\\nOption D: The occipital lobe is responsible for the analysis of vision. A lesion in occipital lobe mainly affects vision leading to homonymous hemianopia, visual agnosia, impaired face recognition (prosopagnosia), visual hallucinations (lights, lines and zigzags).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9e4f11ffa1","n":264,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"A patient presented with severe watery diarrhea resembling that of rice gruel stool. Damage of which structure is likely to have resulted in this diarrhea?","options":["Zona occludens","Gap junction","Adherent junction","Hemidesmosomes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Zona occludens\\n\\nThe given clinical picture of a patient having rice gruel stools is suggestive of the diagnosis of ‘cholera’. Vibrio cholerae in addition to the cholera toxin (CT) produces some additional toxins such as,\\n\\n• Zonula occludens toxin, which alters the intestinal permeability by acting on intestinal epithelial cell tight junctions (which are also called\xa0zonula occludens)\\n• Accessory cholera enterotoxin.\\n\\nZonula occludens:\\n\\n• Also known as tight junctions\\n• Characteristically surround the apical margins of the cells in epithelia such as the intestinal mucosa, the walls of the renal tubules, and the choroid plexus.\\n• Also important to the endothelial barrier function and endothelium-dependent vasodilation.\\n• Made up of ridges- half from one cell and a half from another cell- which adhere so strongly that there is no space between the cells.\\n• Three families of transmembrane proteins are involved:\\n\\n1. Occludin\\n2. Junctional adhesion molecule\\n3. Claudins\\n\\n• Paracellular pathway - these tight junctions permit the passage of ions, solutes, and intracellular signaling molecules in between adjacent cells.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nB) Gap junctions:\\n\\n• The gap between the two cells in these types of junctions is about 4nm.\\n• These junctions are formed by units called connexons, which are present in the membrane of each cell.\\n• Each connexon is made up of six protein subunits called connexins.\\n• They permit substances to pass between the cells, without entering the ECF.\\n• The pore diameter is around 0.8 to 1.4 \xa0nm, which permits the passage of ions, sugars, amino acids, and other solutes which weigh up to 1000 Da.\\n• They also permit rapid propagation of electrical activity from cell to cell, as well as the exchange of various chemical messengers and intracellular signaling molecules.\\n\\nC) Adherent junctions:\\n\\n• Adherent junctions or Zonula adherens is a continuous structure on the basal side of the zonula occludens.\\n• It contains cadherins.\\n\\nD) Hemidesmosomes:\\n\\n• They look like half-desmosomes.\\n• They attach cells to the underlying basal lamina and are connected intracellularly to intermediate filaments.\\n• They contain integrins.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-564d97e8d3","n":265,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"After reaching an equilibrium state during exercising, it was found that the body was consuming oxygen at the rate of 1500 ml/min. Calculate the MET score if the baseline oxygen consumption at rest is 250 ml/min.","options":["5","6","1/5","1/6"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 6\\n\\nThe MET score in the given scenario is 6.\\n\\nOne metabolic equivalent (MET) is defined as the amount of oxygen consumed while sitting at rest.\\n\\nNormally, one MET= 3.5 ml O2 /kg body weight/min\\n\\nIn the above question, the baseline O2\xa0consumption at rest is given as 250 ml/min. According to the definition of one MET, we can deduce that the given 250ml/min is one MET.\\n\\nO2\xa0consumption at reaching equilibrium during exercise= 1500ml/min\\n\\nMET score= O2\xa0consumption during exercise/ one MET\\n\\n= 1500/250\\n\\n= 6\\n\\nThe MET concept represents a simple, practical, and easily understandable procedure for expressing the energy cost of physical activities as a multiple of the resting metabolic rate. If the MET score is low, it means that the activity requires less oxygen compared to an activity that has a higher MET score. Eg. Gardening requires around 3-7 METS compared to that jogging that may need around 9-11 METS. We can classify activities as light, moderate or heavy, depending on MET scores. It can also be used to identify the functional capacity of a patient.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d315de0d76","n":266,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"All of the following cause a right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve, except ___.\\n\\nHbF- Fetal hemoglobin\\n\\n2,3 BPG-\xa02,3 Bisphosphoglyceric acid","options":["Increased HbF","Raised 2,3 BPG","Acidosis","Increased CO2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Increased HbF\\n\\nIncreased HbF (fetal hemoglobin) causes a left shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve.\\n\\nOxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve:\\n\\nThis is a plot of the partial pressure of O2\xa0and the percentage saturation of Hb with O2.\xa0It is normally\xa0sigmoid in shape.\xa0If the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is shifted to the right, it means that the affinity of Hb for O2\xa0has become less, which favors O2\xa0delivery. This means a higher P50, which implies that a higher PO2 is required to maintain 50% saturation (R - Right shift Releases oxygen).\xa0If the curve is shifted to the left, it means that affinity has increased for O2 ( which favors O2 binding ) i.e. lower P50 value ( L-Left shift Leashes oxygen ).\\n\\nCauses of the right shift of the\xa0oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve:\\n\\n• Hypoxia and Hypercarbia - metabolically active tissues utilize O2 and tissues produce CO2\\n• ↓ pH - Secondary to increased CO2 production\\n• ↑ in temperature\\n• ↑ in 2,3-DPG - increased anaerobic glycolysis\\n• Growth hormone, thyroid hormone, androgen - by increasing 2,3 DPG\\n• Sickle cell anemia - reduced affinity for O2\\n• Exercise\\n\\nCauses of the left shift of\xa0oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve:\\n\\n• ↑ pH (alkalosis)\\n• ↓ PCO2\\n• ↓ 2,3-DPG\\n• ↓ Temperature\\n• HbF\\n• Carboxyhemoglobin\\n\\nBohr\'s effect- The decrease in the O2\xa0affinity of hemoglobin when the pH of the blood falls","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-187b2326fb","n":267,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"All of the following increases gastric acid secretion except:","options":["Gastrin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Serotonin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Serotonin\\n\\nAll of the above increase gastric acid secretion except serotonin.\\n\\nSerotonin is localized in the enterochromaffin cells of the gastrointestinal (GI) mucosa and within neurons of the GI tract. Serotonin inhibits gastric acid secretion. It stimulates the production and release of gastric and colonic mucus. Serotonin acts as an enteric neurotransmitter and increases intestinal motility.\\n\\nThe main factors that stimulate gastric secretion are acetylcholine, gastrin, and histamine.\xa0Parasympathetic stimulation releases Ach which stimulates the secretion of\\n\\n• pepsinogen by peptic cells\\n• hydrochloric acid by parietal cells\\n• mucus by mucous cells.\\n\\nBoth gastrin and histamine strongly stimulate acid secretion by parietal cells\xa0but have little effect on other cells.\\n\\nThe stomach mucosa has two important types of tubular glands:\\n\\n• The oxyntic (gastric) glands secrete hydrochloric acid, pepsinogen, intrinsic factor, and mucus.\\n• The pyloric glands secrete mainly mucus for protection of the pyloric mucosa from the stomach acid. They also secrete the hormone gastrin.\\n\\nNote- Serotonin released from the small intestine triggers vomiting by stimulation of 5HT3 receptors. Therefore,\xa05HT3 antagonists like ondansetron are used as antiemetics.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b69ff83cef","n":268,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"All of the following ions have decreased concentration on the luminal side of proximal convoluted tubule except :","options":["Amino acids","Glucose","Chloride","Bicarbonate"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Chloride\\n\\nThe concentration of chloride on the luminal side is more when compared to sodium, glucose, and amino acids.\\n\\nIn the first half of the proximal tubule, sodium is reabsorbed by co-transport along with glucose, amino acids, and other solutes. As sodium is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, it preferentially carries glucose, amino acids,\xa0bicarbonate along with it.\xa0Thus, their concentrations decrease markedly along the length of the proximal tubule, and a solution having a higher concentration of chloride\xa0is left behind. The concentration of chloride here is around 105 mEq/L.\\n\\nIn the second half of the proximal tubule, sodium gets re-absorbed along with chloride since there is only a little glucose and amino acids yet to be re-absorbed. The second half of the proximal tubule has a concentration of chloride ≈140 mEq/L. This higher chloride concentration favors its own diffusion from the lumen into the renal interstitial fluid.\\n\\nAlthough the amount of sodium in the tubular fluid decreases markedly along the proximal tubule, the concentration of sodium and the total osmolarity remain relatively constant. This is because the proximal tubule is highly permeable to water and water reabsorption increases to keep pace with the sodium reabsorption.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2f7290cde9","n":269,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"An individual is in an environment of a temperature of 47\xb0C. What is the mechanism of heat loss?","options":["Radiation","Conduction","Convection","Sweating"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Sweating\\n\\nThe mechanism of heat loss in the given case where the surrounding temperatures are higher than the body temperature is sweating.\\n\\nDifferent methods of heat loss:\\n\\n• Radiation is heat loss in the form of infrared rays.\\n• Conduction is heat loss that occurs by direct contact with an object.\\n• Convection is heat loss resulting from air movement.\\n\\nWhen the temperature of the surroundings is more than that of the skin, the body gains heat by both radiation and conduction. Under these conditions,\xa0the heat can be lost from the body only by\xa0evaporation\xa0or\xa0sweating.\\n\\nWhen the body temperature is greater than the temperature of the surroundings, heat can be lost by radiation and conduction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2fc1dc6fa2","n":270,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Beta hCG is structurally similar to which biochemical moiety?","options":["LH","FSH","ACTH","Oxytocin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. LH\\n\\nBeta hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) has biological activity similar to that of luteinizing\xa0hormone (LH).\\n\\nhCG is a glycoprotein\xa0produced by syncytiotrophoblasts.\xa0It is made up of\xa0α and\xa0β subunits. Though the\xa0α subunit of hCG is similar to the\xa0α subunits of LH, FSH, and TSH,\xa0the\xa0β subunit has structural similarity to LH only. Hence, LH is a better answer than FSH.\\n\\nhCG is primarily luteinizing and luteotrophic. It appears to act on the same receptor as that of LH. The function of hCG is to prevent regression of the\xa0corpus luteum.\xa0It can be detected in blood as early as 6 days after conception and forms the basis of various tests used to identify pregnancy.\\n\\nIts level peaks at gestational week 9 and then declines gradually but is present in lower concentration till the end of pregnancy. But hCG is not specific for pregnancy as it is secreted in a variety of gastrointestinal\xa0and genital tumors in both males and females.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-917b93dd56","n":271,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"If the contractility of the heart is decreased, which of the following is seen\\n\\nESV: End-systolic volume\\n\\nEDV: End-diastolic volume","options":["End diastolic volume and pressure curve shifts to left without change in slope","Slope changes in ESV pressure curve","End systolic volume and pressure curve shifts to left without change in slope","Slope changes in EDV and pressure curve"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Slope changes in ESV pressure curve\\n\\nIf the contractility of the heart is decreased, slope changes in the ESV pressure curve are seen.\\n\\nTo answer this question, we will first understand the volume-pressure diagram and the end-systolic pressure-volume relationship (ESPVR) during cardiac cycle.\\n\\nVolume-pressure diagram during the cardiac cycle\\n\\nThe volume pressure diagram plots the changes in the pressure and volume in the left ventricle that occurs during the cardiac cycle. The x-axis shows the changes in ventricular volume, while the y-axis shows the changes in ventricular pressure.\\n\\nIt has the following phases.\\n\\nPhase I- This phase is called the period of filling and extends between points A and\xa0B. Point A is at the end of systole when the ventricular volume is about 50 mL. This volume is called end-systolic volume. The pressure at this point is about 2 to 3 mm Hg. These volume and pressure go on increasing as the blood from the atrium flows to the ventricle during diastole. At the end of diastole, marked by point B, the ventricle will have a volume of around 120 mL. This volume is called end-diastolic volume. The pressure is slightly raised to 5-7mm Hg.\\n\\nPhase II- It is the phase of isovolumetric contraction, and extends between points B and\xa0C. At the end of diastole (point B), a slight increase in the ventricular pressure closes the mitral valve, and contraction starts. The ventricle contracts while both mitral and aortic valves are closed. Therefore, the pressure within the ventricle slowly rises without any changes in the volume.\\n\\nPhase III- It is the phase of ejection and extends between point C and point D. Point C is the place where the aortic valve opens. The pressure within the left ventricle rises slowly during isovolumetric contraction until it is sufficient to open the aortic valve. Once the aortic valve opens, blood within the left ventricle is ejected into the aorta. This decreases the volume of the left ventricle, while increasing the pressure within the ventricle.\\n\\nPhase IV- It is the period of isovolumetric relaxation. It extends between points D and A. At the end of the ejection (Point D), the aortic valve closes. However, the ventricular relaxation continues despite closed aortic and mitral valves. Therefore, the ventricular pressure continues to fall while the volume remains the same. The ventricle returns to its starting point, with about 50 ml of blood left in the ventricle and pressure of about 2 to 3 mm Hg\\n\\nEnd-systolic pressure-volume relationship (ESPVR)\\n\\nESPVR is the maximum pressure that can be developed by the ventricle at the end-systolic volume. It is determined by the line intersecting the upper left corners of the loops. This line can be extended down to the x-axis. The slope of this line is called as ESPVR slope.\\n\\nNow coming to the question, when the contractility of the heart is decreased, the force of ejection decreases leading to decreased stroke volume, and increased end-systolic volume. Therefore, the volume-pressure diagram shifts to the right. If the diagram shifts to the right, then ESPVR also changes, leading to a change in the slope of the ESPVR curve.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A and D- End diastolic volume and pressure curve (EDPVR) mainly assesses the compliance of the ventricle, rather than contractility. The slope of this curve increases when the compliance decreases, and vice versa.\\n\\nOption C- ESPVR shifts to right with change in slope.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-556f593129","n":272,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Pacinian corpuscle is stimulated by which of the following?","options":["Pain","Temperature","Touch","Pressure"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Pressure\\n\\nPacinian corpuscle is stimulated by pressure.\\n\\nPacinian corpuscles are the largest cutaneous mechanoreceptors. They are present in the dermis of glabrous and hairy skin. They are composed of a nerve ending which is encapsulated by concentric layers of connective tissue that give it an onion-like appearance. These receptors respond to fast vibration and deep pressure and adapt very rapidly. The sensory nerves from cutaneous mechanoreceptors are myelinated Aα and Aβ fibers.\\n\\nImage shows histology of Pacinian corpuscle.\\n\\nOther mechanoreceptors:\\n\\n• Meissner\'s corpuscles are\xa0present mainly in the dermis of non-hairy skin particularly in the fingertips, lips, and other locations where tactile discrimination is well developed.\xa0They have small receptive fields and can be used for two point discrimination. Meissner corpuscles are rapidly adapting receptors that sense point discrimination, precise location, tapping, and flutter. These are\xa0encapsulated nerve endings of a large myelinated sensory nerve (Aβ type).\\n\\nThe image shows the histology of Meissner\'s corpuscle in the dermis.\\n\\n• Merkel discs\xa0are seen in the epidermis of glabrous skin. They are slowly adapting receptors and have very small receptive fields. These receptors detect vertical indentations of the skin. They are\xa0expanded dendritic endings.\\n• Ruffini corpuscles are capsulated, enlarged, dendritic endings. They are located in the dermis of glabrous and hairy skin, and in joint capsules. They are slowly adapting receptors. Ruffini corpuscles detect stretch and joint rotation.\\n\\nPain and temperature sensations arise from receptors located on unmyelinated sensory neurons present throughout the glabrous skin, hairy skin, and deep tissue. Impulses from nociceptors are transmitted via thinly myelinated Aδ fibers and unmyelinated C fibers.\\n\\nActivation of Aδ fibers is responsible for first pain (also called fast pain). It is a rapid response and helps in the discrimination of pain or the ability to localize the site and intensity of the noxious stimulus. It is mediated by the release of glutamate. An example is pinprick.\\n\\nActivation of C fibers is responsible for delayed second pain(also called slow pain). It is a dull, intense, diffuse, and unpleasant feeling associated with a noxious stimulus. . Second pain is mediated by the release of a combination of glutamate and substance P. Example is visceral pain.\\n\\nTypes of nociceptors :\\n\\n• Mechanical nociceptors respond to strong pressure (eg, from a sharp object).\\n• Thermal nociceptors are activated by skin temperatures above 45\xb0C or by severe cold (<20\xb0C).\\n• Chemically sensitive nociceptors respond to chemicals such as bradykinin, histamine, high acidity, and environmental irritants.\\n• Polymodal nociceptors respond to combinations of these stimuli.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-16e450b190","n":273,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Sodium iodide symporter is present in all of the following except:","options":["Thyroid gland","Placenta","Salivary gland","Pituitary gland"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Pituitary gland\\n\\nSodium iodide symporter is present in all of the options given above except the pituitary gland.\\n\\nSodium iodide symporter is expressed mainly in the thyroid gland and is responsible for iodine uptake in the follicular cells of the thyroid gland.\\n\\nSodium iodide symporter (NIS)\\n\\nThe NIS is mainly present in the basolateral membranes of the thyrocytes. The NIS transports two Na+ ions and one I- ion into the cell against the concentration gradient. The energy for this transport is derived from the Na+-K+ ATPase pump. Na+-K+ ATPase pumps sodium outside the cell and causes low intracellular sodium concentration. This causes sodium to enter the cell by facilitated diffusion. Along with sodium, iodide ions also enter the cell. Since the energy to transport sodium and iodide ions in NIS is derived from Na+-K+ ATPase pump, this is called secondary active transport.\\n\\nThe NIS is also expressed in:\\n\\n• Salivary glands\\n• Gastric mucosa\\n• Placenta\\n• Ciliary body of the eye\\n• Choroid plexus\\n• Mammary glands\\n• Certain cancers are derived from these tissues.\\n\\nThe significance of these extrathyroidal NIS is not known.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9f25df9b2e","n":274,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"The extracellular concentration of chloride is 100mmol/L and the intracellular concentration is 10 mmol/L. What is the equilibrium potential of chloride?","options":["+ 10 mV","- 10 mV","- 60 mV","+ 60 mV"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. - 60 mV\\n\\nThe equilibrium potential of chloride in the given question is -60 mV.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4c06335761","n":275,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"The formula for urea clearance is U*V/P, where U is","options":["Urine volume","Serum urea in mg /100ml","Urine urea in g/24h","Urine urea in mg /100ml"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Urine urea in mg /100ml\\n\\n‘U’ in the urea clearance stands for urine concentration of urea in mg /100ml.\\n\\nThe formula for renal clearance of a substance is,\\n\\nClearance (C) = U\xa0\xd7 V / P,\\n\\nwhere\\n\\nU = Urine concentration of the substance in mg / ml\\n\\nV = Urine flow rate in ml/min\\n\\nP = Plasma concentration of substance in mg /ml.\\n\\nRenal clearance of a substance is the volume of plasma that is completely cleared of the substance by the kidneys per unit of time.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-831b3e183e","n":276,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"The latent period, contraction period, and relaxation period of a skeletal muscle are 10 ms, 40 ms, and 50 ms respectively. Calculate the tetanizing frequency.\\n\\nms- millisecond","options":["100 per second","50 per second","20 per second","25 per second"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 25 per second\\n\\nThe tetanizing frequency of the given skeletal muscle is 25 per second.\\n\\nIn the question, it is given that the contraction period is 40 msec (40 msec is 0.04 seconds)\\n\\nTetanizing frequency = 1/contraction period\\n\\nTherefore, tetanizing frequency = 1/40 millisecond = 1/0.04 per second = 1000/40 per second = 25 per second.\\n\\nWhen there is a repeated stimulus to a muscle fiber before relaxation, it produces an additional contraction of the muscle. The second contraction adds to the first contraction and the total strength of contraction increases. This phenomenon is known as a\xa0summation of contractions.\\n\\nWith repeated stimulation, contraction occurs repeatedly before any relaxation has occurred. This causes the individual responses to fuse into one continuous contraction. Such a response is called tetanus (tetanic contraction).\\n\\nTetanizing frequency is the minimum frequency required for producing tetanus.\\n\\nTetanus is of two types.\\n\\n• Complete tetanus- when no relaxation occurs between stimuli\\n• Incomplete tetanus- when periods of incomplete relaxation occur between the summated stimuli.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-df902ed9ec","n":277,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"The relaxation of the intestinal segment distal to the segment with the bolus of food during peristalsis is because of","options":["VIP","Substance P","Dopamine from adrenergic fibres","Acetylcholine from myenteric fibres"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. VIP\\n\\nThe relaxation of the intestinal segment distal to the segment of a bolus of food during peristalsis is due to Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide (VIP).\\n\\nPeristaltic contractions in the small intestine are designed to propel the food towards the large intestine.\\n\\nThe food bolus in the small intestine is sensed by enterochromaffin cells of the intestinal mucosa, which release 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT)(serotonin). The 5-HT then binds to the receptors on intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs), which when activated, initiate the peristaltic reflex in that segment of the small intestine. Behind the bolus, excitatory transmitters like acetylcholine, Substance P, neuropeptide Y are released in the circular muscle, while these pathways are inhibited in the longitudinal muscle; thus this segment of small intestine (behind the bolus) contracts and lengthens.\\n\\n(Recall that contraction of circular muscle decreases the diameter of that small intestinal segment whereas contraction of the longitudinal muscle decreases the length of that segment. To prevent the conflict that would occur if circular and longitudinal are contracted at the same time, they are reciprocally innervated)\\n\\nIn front of the bolus, inhibitory pathways like vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) and nitric oxide (NO) are activated in the circular muscle, while excitatory pathways are activated in the longitudinal muscle;thereby relaxing and shortening this segment of small intestine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8006221217","n":278,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"The stroke volume depends on end-diastolic volume. This is best represented as","options":["Length-force relationship","Length- velocity relationship","Length-tension relationship","Length-viscosity relationship"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Length-tension relationship\\n\\nThe relationship between end-diastolic volume and stroke volume is best represented by length-tension relationship.\\n\\nThis is explained by the ‘Frank-Starling law of heart’ which states that ‘ the volume of blood ejected by the ventricle depends on the volume present in the ventricle at the end of the diastole’. The length of the myocytes depend on the volume (and hence tension) present at the end of the diastole, which in turn, depends on the volume returned to the heart, or the venous return.\\n\\n• The Frank-Starling law governs the normal ventricular function and ensures that the cardiac output equals venous return, within physiological limits.\\n• There is a curvilinear relationship between stroke volume or cardiac output and the ventricular end-diastolic volume.\\n• Agents that increase the contractility of the cardiac muscle have a positive inotropic effect and produce increased stroke volume and thereby increased cardiac output for a given end-diastolic volume. (Eg: Digoxin)\\n• Agents that decrease the contractility of the cardiac muscle have a negative inotropic effect and produce decreased stroke volume (and cardiac output) for a given end-diastolic volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-706a70f51d","n":279,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"What is the duration of short term memory?","options":["3-6 Hours","12-18 hours","30-300 seconds","3-4 seconds"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 30-300 seconds\\n\\nShort-term memory lasts for seconds to minutes, hence of the given 4 options, 30-300\xa0seconds is the most appropriate answer.\\n\\nMemory is usually divided into two forms: Explicit or declarative memory which is associated with consciousness or awareness. Implicit or nondeclarative memory does not involve awareness and its retention does not usually involve processing in the hippocampus.\\n\\nExplicit and many forms of implicit memory involve\\n\\n• Short-term memory and\\n• Long-term memory.\\n\\nShort-term memory:\\n\\nIt lasts seconds to minutes; the encoding process for short-term explicit memory involves the hippocampus. It lasts only till the person continues to think about the number or facts.\\n\\nWorking memory is a form of short-term memory that keeps information available, usually for very short periods of time, until the individual plans action based on it.\\n\\nLong-term memory:\\n\\nIt stores memories that can be recollected after years or even for a lifetime. These memories are stored in various parts of the neocortex. The various parts of the memories such as, visual, auditory, olfactory, etc., are localised to the cortical regions concerned with these functions. All these parts of the memories are brought together by long-term changes in the strength of transmission at relevant synaptic junctions so that all the components are brought to consciousness when that particular memory is recalled.\\n\\nNeural basis of memory:\\n\\nThe basis of memory is the alteration in the strength of the selected synaptic connections. Second messenger systems contribute to the changes in the neural circuits which are required for learning and memory. Alterations in the cellular membrane channels also contribute to it.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-335ec1d8bd","n":280,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Which among the following has the highest airway resistance?","options":["Alveolar duct","Bronchi","Respiratory bronchioles","Small bronchioles"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bronchi\\n\\nAmong the given options, bronchi\xa0(medium-sized airway) offer maximum resistance.\\n\\nThe best answer here is bronchi, as all the other options suggest smaller airways, while bronchi are the only medium-sized airway.\\n\\nAirflow can be derived from\xa0Poiseuille-Hagen formula:\\n\\nQ =π\xa0ΔPr4/8ηL\\n\\nWhere\\n\\n• Q is the flow rate\\n• π is pie symbol\\n• ΔP is the pressure difference\\n• r is radius\\n• η is viscosity\\n• L is length\\n\\nAlso,\\n\\nFlow rate (Q) = pressure difference (ΔP)/ resistance (R).\\n\\nTherefore, substituting this in Poiseuille- Hagen formula,\\n\\nΔP/ R =\xa0π\xa0ΔPr4/8ηl\\n\\n1/R =\xa0π\xa0r4/8ηl\\n\\nR =\xa08ηl/\xa0π\xa0r4\\n\\nTherefore it can be derived that\xa0airway resistance is inversely proportional to the radius by the\xa0power of four.\\n\\nWith\xa0Poiseuille- Hagen formula we might feel that the smallest airways are the major site of resistance. However, the contribution by the smallest airways is very little to the overall total resistance. This is because, the airway branches in each generation exist in parallel rather than in series. As the parallel branching increases, the resistance decreases. (Total cross-sectional diameter of small airways put together is high). Thus the overall resistance of the small airways is very small. Also,\xa0airflow velocity decreases substantially as the effective cross-sectional area increases (i.e., the flow becomes laminar).\\n\\nOther factors affecting airway resistance :\\n\\n• Lung volume, as lung volume increases the resistance decreases and vice versa. Lung volume increases the transairway pressure thus increasing the caliber of the airway.\\n• Airway mucus, edema, and contraction of bronchial smooth muscle increase the airway resistance. Its clinical application is seen in asthma, bronchitis, and emphysema\\n• The density and viscosity of inspired gas also contribute to airway resistance. A gas with more density imparts more resistance.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3dedfc5730","n":281,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Which of the following ions in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle is responsible for the resorption of divalent ions?","options":["H+","HCO3-","K+","Na+"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. K+\\n\\nPotassium (K+) in the thick ascending limb of loop of Henle is responsible for the resorption of divalent ions such as, calcium, magnesium, etc.\\n\\nThis resorption is driven by the lumen-positive potential difference across the cells of the thick ascending limb, created by the potassium leak into the lumen.\\n\\nMechanism of resorption in thick ascending limb:\\n\\n• The luminal membrane contains a Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter.\\n• There is net resorption of Na+, K+ and Cl- in the thick ascending limb as follows:\\n• All three ions are transported into the cell on the cotransporter.\\n• Na+ is extruded from the cell by the Na+-K+ ATPase in the basolateral membrane.\\n• K+ and Cl- diffuse through channels in the basolateral membrane, down their respective electrochemical gradients.\\n• Most of the K+ leaves the cell across the basolateral membrane; the remaining portion of K+ however diffuses back into the lumen.\\n• This K+ leak into the lumen generates a positive electrochemical potential difference in the lumen.\\n• This lumen-positive potential difference drives the resorption of ions such as sodium(Na+), magnesium (Mg2+), calcium (Ca+),etc.,","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-aa0e3a7f6a","n":282,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Which of the following is not involved in renal auto-regulation?","options":["Tubuloglomerular feedback","Myogenic reflex","Renin-angiotensin system","Renal sympathetic system fibres"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Renal sympathetic system fibres\\n\\nRenal sympathetic system fibres is not involved in the autoregulation of renal blood flow, because a denervated kidney also maintains the auto regulation of renal blood flow as that of a normal kidney.\\n\\nTubulo glomerular feedback, myogenic reflex, and renin-angiotensin system are all involved in the autoregulation of renal blood flow.\\n\\nTubulo glomerular feedback:\\n\\n• When the renal artery pressure increases, the renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate (GFR) increases\\n• This increased GFR results in increased delivery of solute and water to the early distal tubule, particularly the macula densa\\n• The macula dense, then secretes a vasoactive substance that constricts afferent arteriole via a paracrine mechanism\\n• This vasoconstriction then reduces renal blood flow and GFR back to normal\\n\\nMyogenic reflex:\\n\\n• The increase in renal arterial pressure stretches the walls of the afferent arterioles, which then contracts\\n• This contraction increases the afferent arteriolar resistance\\n• This increase in resistance then compensates for the increase in blood pressure, thereby keeping the renal blood flow constant.\\n\\nRenin-angiotensin system:\\n\\n• Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor of both afferent and efferent arterioles\\n• However efferent arterioles are more sensitive to angiotensin II than afferent arteriole\\n• Thus low levels of angiotensin II produce an increase in GFR by constricting efferent arterioles\\n• High levels of angiotensin II decreases GFR by constricting afferent and efferent arterioles, thereby decreasing the renal blood flow","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-206dd306d2","n":283,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Which of the following jugular venous pressure (JVP) waves corresponds to the isovolumetric contraction phase of the cardiac cycle?","options":["a wave","c wave","x descent","y descent"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. c wave\\n\\nThe c wave of jugular venous pressure wave corresponds to the isovolumetric contraction phase in the cardiac cycle.\\n\\nDuring isovolumetric contraction, the ventricles start contracting with valves closed. This leads to bulging of the tricuspid valve. This bulging increases pressure in the right atrium which is transmitted to the right internal jugular vein. This appears as a\xa0c wave in jugular venous pressure (JVP) tracing.\\nJVP measured in the internal jugular vein (IJV) is a reflection of the right atrial pressure. IJV is preferred because it is not valved and is in direct line with superior vena cava and right atrium.\\n\\nThe waveforms seen in a normal JVP are:\\n\\n• a wave corresponds to atrial contraction and occurs just after the P wave (on ECG) and just before the first heart sound.\\n• c wave represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular systole.\\n• x descent corresponds to the atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.\\n• v wave represents atrial filling during ventricular systole and peaks at the second heart sound.\\n• y descent represents passive emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle after tricuspid valve opening.\\n\\nThe Wiggers diagram given below correlates JVP with other aspects of cardiac cycle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fc66aafbe6","n":284,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2021","text":"Which of the following sensory receptors are responsible for tactile discrimination?","options":["Merkel disc","Ruffini end organ","Pacinian corpuscle","Meissner’s corpuscle"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Meissner’s corpuscle\\n\\nMeissner’s Corpuscles are responsible for discriminative touch.\\n\\nMeissner’s corpuscles are encapsulated receptors found in the dermis of the non-hairy skin, most commonly on the fingertips, lips, and other locations where tactile discrimination is very good. Their field of reception is very small and hence used for two-point discrimination. These are rapidly adapting receptors that encode\\n\\n• Point discrimination\\n• Precise location\\n• Tapping\\n• Flutter","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-455d71136b","n":285,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"2,3-BPG causes the unloading of oxygen to tissues by _________.","options":["Increasing ATP production","Binding to beta subunit of Hb","Inhibiting taut structure","Decreasing ATP production"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Binding to beta subunit of Hb\\n\\n2,3-Bisphosphoglycerate (BPG) causes the unloading of oxygen to tissues by binding\xa0to the beta subunit of Hb.\\n\\n2,3 BPG is formed from glycolysis. It is found in high amounts in red blood cells and in trace amounts in most other cells. 2,3 BPG facilitates increased oxygen delivery by reducing the affinity of\xa0hemoglobin for oxygen.\\n\\nHemoglobin exists in 2 forms in the blood. The deoxyhemoglobin form is called the taut or T form. This hemoglobin has a low affinity for oxygen. The oxyhemoglobin form is called the relaxed or R form. This hemoglobin has a high affinity for oxygen.\\n\\n2,3 BPG binds more selectively to deoxyhemoglobin and stabilizes the taut conformation of hemoglobin (Option C).\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A and D:\xa0The process of unloading oxygen by 2,3 BPG is independent of ATP production.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8ea2f428af","n":286,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"A and B in the graph below represent which of the following ions, in terms of permeability for action potential generation?","options":["A is Na+, B is K+","A is Na+, B is Ca2+","A is K+, B is Ca2+","A is Ca2+, B is K+"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A is Na+, B is K+\\n\\nIn the given graph, showing action potential generation in terms of ion permeability, wave A represents\xa0Na+ and B represents\xa0K+.\\n\\nThe sequence of events during the action potential\xa0occurs in 7 steps as described below.\\n\\n1.\xa0Resting-state -\xa0Both channels are closed\\n\\n2.\xa0Na+ channels open\xa0in response to depolarizing stimulus. Sodium enters the cell. The membrane reaches\xa0threshold potential.\\n\\n3. The sodium influx causes the\xa0opening of more\xa0Na+ channels\xa0through a\xa0positive feedback\xa0mechanism.\xa0Rapid depolarization\xa0occurs.\\n\\n4. Sodium conductance decreases.\\n\\n5. Na+ channels close and\xa0K+ channels open.\xa0Repolarization\xa0occurs.\\n\\n6.\xa0Sodium\xa0channels are\xa0reset, and are ready to respond to another depolarizing stimulus (relative refractory period). During this phase, the\xa0potassium\xa0channels remain open and\xa0slowly return to closed\xa0state causing\xa0after-hyperpolarization.\\n\\n7. Both channels are closed. The membrane returns to\xa0RMP.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/942077fa66a943d5bc34.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-f8d1fca86a","n":287,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"A woman has vomiting whenever she eats spicy food. Arrange the sequence of events during vomiting.\\n\\n1. LES is open and UES is closed.\\n2. Strong contractions in the stomach.\\n3. Inspiration against a closed glottis.\\n4. Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter.\\n5. LES opens and UES opens.\\n6. Reverse peristalsis in the small intestine.\\n\\nLES: Lower esophageal sphincter\\n\\nUES: Upper esophageal sphincter","options":["6, 4, 2, 5, 1, 3","6, 4, 2, 3, 1, 5","4, 6, 2, 1, 3, 5","4, 6, 2, 5, 3, 1"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 6, 4, 2, 3, 1, 5\\n\\nThe correct sequence of vomiting is 6, 4, 2, 3, 1, 5.\\n\\n6.\xa0Reverse peristalsis in the small intestine.\\n4. Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter.\\n2. Strong contractions in the stomach.\\n3. Inspiration against a closed glottis.\\n1. LES is open and UES is closed.\\n5. LES opens and UES opens.\\n\\nThe sensory signals that initiate vomiting are located in the pharynx, esophagus, stomach, and upper portions of the small intestines.\xa0Excessive distention or irritation of the duodenum provides a strong stimulus for vomiting. Area postrema constitutes the vomiting centre.\\n\\nIn the early stage, reverse peristalsis begins often minutes before vomiting. It begins far down in the ileum and travels up the intestine. This process pushes a large share of lower small intestine contents to the duodenum and stomach. Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter occurs during this process.\\n\\nAt the onset of vomiting, strong intrinsic contractions are seen in the duodenum and stomach. Once the vomiting centre has been stimulated, inspiration occurs against the closed glottis. It\xa0prevents\xa0the flow of vomitus into the lungs. The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is open and the\xa0upper esophageal sphincter\xa0(UES) is closed at this stage.\\n\\nThe muscles of the abdominal wall contract, building intra-gastric pressure. Finally LES relaxes completely and UES also relaxes allowing the expulsion of the gastric contents.\\n\\nNote: The esophageal smooth muscle functions as LES at the gastro-esophageal junction. It normally remains tonically constricted. Receptive relaxation of LES occurs when the peristaltic wave passes down the esophagus.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-80aba37bd2","n":288,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Arrange the following steps of ventricular action potential in the correct order.\\n\\nA. Opening of voltage-gated calcium channels\\nB. Repolarizing K+ currents\\nC. Opening of voltage-gated sodium channels\\nD. Transient outward flow of potassium","options":["B, A, C, D","D, C, B, A","C, D, A, B","A, B, C, D"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. C, D, A, B\\n\\nThe correct sequence for ventricular action potential is as follows:\\n\\nC. Opening of voltage-gated sodium channels\\nD. Transient outward flow of potassium\\nA. Opening of voltage-gated calcium channels\\nB. Repolarizing K+ currents\\n\\nThe events of action potential\xa0occur in the following sequence:\\n\\n• Phase 0:\xa0Voltage-gated Na+\xa0channels open\xa0in response to depolarizing stimulus (rapid depolarization phase)\\n• Phase 1:\xa0Inactivation of voltage-gated Na+\xa0channels and transient outward flow of K+\xa0causes rapid repolarization (rapid repolarization phase)\\n• Phase 2: Opening of slow voltage-gated Ca\xb2⁺ channels causes the influx of calcium into the cells and results in the plateau phase (plateau phase)\\n• Phase 3:\xa0Opening of various K+\xa0channels results in the efflux of K+\xa0from the cells and results in slow repolarization (slow repolarization phase).\\n• Phase 4: Return to resting membrane potential","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6bd6ce426f","n":289,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Consider the following statements about sympathetic, parasympathetic, and motor nerves. Which of the following statements are true?\\n\\n1. Sympathetic efferents are distributed all over the body/ extensively whereas parasympathetic distribution is limited.\\n2. Sympathetic fibers travel long distances after leaving the sympathetic chain, before finally synapsing and then a shorter distance to effector organs.\\n3. Parasympathetic fibers travel a shorter distance to their respective ganglia which are located close to the target organs.\\n4. Motor neurons are located in the ventral horn of the spinal cord from where the axons come out and divide into several branches, each of which supplies an individual muscle fiber.","options":["1,3","1,4","2,3","2,4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1,4\\n\\nThe true statements are 1,4.\\n\\nSympathetic efferents are distributed\xa0extensively all over the body whereas parasympathetic distribution is limited to head, neck, and trunk regions.\\n\\nSympathetic fibers travel short distances to the sympathetic ganglionic chain, before finally synapsing and then have a long course to supply the target organs.\xa0Parasympathetic fibers travel a longer distance to their respective ganglia which are located close to the target organs.\\n\\nMotor neurons are located in the ventral horn of the spinal cord from where the axons come out and divide into several branches, each of which supplies an individual muscle fiber. A motor unit comprises a motor neuron and a group of skeletal muscle fibers it innervates.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9050932dc7","n":290,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Countercurrent mechanism is present in which of the following parts of the body?\\n\\n1. Eye\\n2. Testes\\n3. Kidney\\n4. Gut\\n5. Lungs","options":["1 and 2 are correct","1,2,3 are correct","1,2,3,4,5 are correct","2,3,4,5 are correct"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 2,3,4,5 are correct\\n\\nThe countercurrent mechanism is present in the\xa0testis, kidney, gut, and lungs.\xa0 When the inflow runs parallel to, counter to, and in close proximity to the outflow for some distance it is called as the counter current mechanism.\\n\\nSpermatogenesis requires a temperature considerably lower than that of the interior of the body. The testes are kept cool by heat exchange in a countercurrent manner between the spermatic arteries and veins.\\n\\nThe concentration of the urine by the kidney requires an osmolality gradient. This gradient is produced by the loop of Henle\xa0as counter-current multipliers\xa0and maintained by vasa recta as counter-current exchangers.\\nThe functioning of the loop of Henle depends upon:\\n\\n1. The high permeability of the thin descending limb to water ( via aquaporin-1)\\n2. Active transport of Na+\xa0and Cl-\xa0from the thick ascending limb, thereby diluting the tubular fluid.\\n3. The inflow\xa0of tubular fluid from proximal tubule and outflow to the distal tubule.\\n\\nThe countercurrent mechanism is used in the villi to decrease the oxygen availability for local metabolic reactions of the gut. Hence this will increase the blood flow in the intestine during gastrointestinal activity.\\n\\nCountercurrent exchange of oxygen is seen in the lungs as well.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7836031cb9","n":291,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Fluid leaving the proximal convoluted tubule in the absence of antidiuretic hormone is:","options":["Isotonic","Hypotonic","Hypertonic","Depends on fluid intake"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Isotonic\\n\\nFluid leaving the proximal\xa0convoluted tubule\xa0(PCT) in the absence of antidiuretic\xa0hormone (ADH) is isotonic.\\n\\nThe PCT is the site of maximum water reabsorption\xa0under normal physiological conditions. It\xa0reabsorbs around\xa060%\xa0of the filtered NaCl and water and other nutrients such as glucose and amino acids. Isosmotic reabsorption occurs in the PCT, so the amount of solute and water reabsorbed are coupled and proportional to each other. Here, sodium is reabsorbed along with the water. The large amounts of sodium\xa0and water reabsorption are essential in maintaining the ECF volume.\\n\\nIn addition to the isosmotic reabsorption, the water reabsorption in the PCT also occurs through aquaporin-1 which are\xa0active water channels.\xa0Reabsorption of sodium and water in PCT is not affected by ADH levels. Thus even in the absence of ADH, fluid is isotonic in PCT.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-947d7a8c07","n":292,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"How is cerebral perfusion pressure calculated?","options":["Systolic blood pressure + Intracranial pressure","Systolic blood pressure - intracranial pressure","Mean arterial pressure/intracranial pressure","Mean arterial pressure - intracranial pressure"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Mean arterial pressure - intracranial pressure\\n\\nCerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) is calculated as mean arterial pressure (MAP) - intracranial pressure (ICP).\\n\\nHowever, ICP values are not readily available. In this case, CPP may be calculated as MAP - central venous pressure (CVP), and if CVP > ICP.\\n\\nThe brain depends on continuous perfusion for oxygen and glucose delivery, thus its survival. The blood flow to the brain is normally about 55 mL per 100 g of brain tissue. If the cerebral flow reduces to less than 20 mL per minute, the brain undergoes ischemia.\\n\\nBlood flow depends on cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP). Normally the CPP is 75-105 mmHg, MAP is 90-110 mmHg and ICP is 5-15 mmHg.\\n\\nAccording to Monro Kellie doctrine, the cranium is a rigid box containing a nearly incompressible brain. In cases of head trauma, there is an expansion of cranial content, in the form of hematomas and brain swelling. This increase in cranial pressure is accommodated initially by the exclusion of fluid components, venous blood, and cerebrospinal fluid. Further increase in the cranial pressure leads to an uncontrolled rise in ICP resulting in a compromise in cerebral perfusion and eventually cerebral herniation.\\n\\nAcute rise in ICP causes\xa0Cushing reflex, a triad\xa0of widened pulse pressure (increasing systolic, decreasing diastolic) bradycardia, and irregular respirations.\\n\\nThus, in cases of head injury with intracranial bleeding, cranial decompression is performed to avoid further complications due to the raised ICP.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-89a9b1cd58","n":293,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"If the pH is reduced from 7.4 to 7.3 with the PO2 being constant at 100 mmHg, which line would PCO2 follow?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1\\n\\nIf the pH is reduced from 7.4 to 7.3 with the alveolar PO2 being constant at 100 mmHg, PCO2\xa0would follow line 1.\\n\\nThis concept can be understood in three steps.\\n\\nStep 1- Effect of\xa0CO2 on ventilation, when the alveolar PO2 is kept constant.\\n\\nWhen the alveolar PO2\xa0(PaO2) is kept constant, an\xa0increased amount of inspired CO2\xa0\xa0leads to increase in\xa0the ventilation. The relation is a straight line.\\n\\nThe below graph shows the effect of CO2\xa0on ventilation when alveolar PaO2\xa0is kept constant at 100mm Hg.\\n\\nStep 2. The shift of the above graph when PO2\xa0changes.\\n\\nWhen PO2 changes, the relation between the amount of CO2 inhaled\xa0and ventilation still remains in a straight line. However, the slopes of the lines\xa0change. The slope increases when alveolar PO2 decreases and vice versa. In other words, the lines move towards the left when PO2\xa0decreases. However, the point of intersection of all these lines remains the same.\\n\\nThe below graph shows the relation between inspired CO2 and ventilation when alveolar PO2\xa0is maintained at 40mm Hg, 55mm Hg, and 100 mm Hg. As the alveolar PO2 has\xa0decreased, the lines have shifted to the left. But they are all intersecting at the same point on the x-axis.\\n\\nStep 3. The shift of the above graph when pH\xa0changes.\\n\\nWhen pH decreases, as in acidosis, the CO2\xa0response curve is similar to the above. The only difference is they are shifted to the left and have different points on the intersection on x- axis.\\n\\nThe below graphs show CO2\xa0response curves when pH is 7.4, 7.3, and PO2\xa0is 40mm Hg, 55mm Hg, and 100mm Hg. The lines have shifted to the left and have a different point\xa0of intersection.\\n\\nIn the question image, only line 1 has been shifted to the left and has a different point of intersection\xa0on x-axis compared to other lines. Therefore, line 1 is the answer.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/d0bd9475c48690925205.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-234599efdf","n":294,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Macula densa is made of specialized cells located at:","options":["Junction of thick descending limb and DCT","Junction of thick descending limb and PCT","Junction of thick ascending limb and DCT","Junction of DCT and collecting duct"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Junction of thick ascending limb and DCT\\n\\nMacula densa are the specialized cells present at the distal end of the thick ascending loop of Henle,\xa0where it reaches the glomerulus. Distal convoluted tubule starts from macula densa.\\n\\nMacula forms the part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus.\\n\\nSignals from the renal tubules affect filtration in its glomerulus. As the rate of flow through the ascending limb of the loop of Henle and the first part of the distal tubule increases, glomerular filtration in the same nephron decreases, and vice versa.\xa0This is known as tubuloglomerular feedback which maintains the constancy of the load delivered to the distal tubule.\\n\\nThe sensor is the macula densa. The Na+ and Cl−\xa0in the fluid enter the macula densa cells via theNa+–K–2Cl− cotransporter in their apical membranes. The increased Na+ increases the Na+-K+\xa0ATPase activity causing ATP hydrolysis and the formation of adenosine. This acts on adenosine A1 receptors on the macula densa cells, causing the release of Ca2+\xa0into the vascular smooth muscle in the afferent arterioles, leading to vasoconstriction and decreased GFR.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5ce91b74e4","n":295,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Match the following molecules with their carriers in plasma:\\n\\nTransporter\\nOrganic Substance\\n\\n1. Thyroxine\\na. Hemopexin\\n\\n2. Fatty acid\\nb. Prealbumin\\n\\n3. Hemoglobin\\nc. Albumin\\n\\n4. Heme\\nd. Haptoglobin","options":["1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a","1-a, 2-b, 3-c 4-d","1-a, 2-d, 3-b 4-c","1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a\\n\\nMolecules\\nCarriers\\n\\nThyroxine\\nPrealbumin\\n\\nFree fatty acid\\nAlbumin\\n\\nHemoglobin\\nHaptoglobin\\n\\nHeme\\nHemopexin\\n\\nPlasma\xa0contains various components like ions, inorganic and organic molecules which help in the transport of substances across various parts of the body. Albumin, globulin, and fibrinogen fractions are important plasma proteins. Some of the plasma proteins act as antibodies and are also concerned with blood clotting. Where some others are non-specific carriers of hormones, drugs, and other solutes. Majority of\xa0plasma proteins are synthesized in the liver.\xa0The above table shows some of the proteins synthesized by the liver and the molecules they carry.\\n\\nThyroxine is carried by thyroid-binding pre-albumin which is also known as transthyretin.\\n\\nAlbumin is a carrier protein as well as an osmotic regulator which carries hormones, amino acids, vitamins, and steroids too.\\n\\nHemopexin\xa0binds with heme, particularly for heme recycling.\\n\\nHaptoglobin binds to free hemoglobin and prevents its excretion in urine.Hence, haptoglobin levels decrease in the presence of free hemoglobin as seen in hemolytic anemia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-32806604f3","n":296,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Match the following molecules with their carriers in plasma:\\n\\nTransporter\\nOrganic substance\\n\\n1. Thyroxine\\na. Hemopexin\\n\\n2. Vitamin B12\\nb. Prealbumin\\n\\n3. Hemoglobin\\nc. Transcobalamin\\n\\n4. Heme\\nd. Haptoglobin","options":["1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a","1-a, 2-b, 3-c 4-d","1-a, 2-d, 3-b 4-c","1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a\\n\\nThe correct pairs are as follows:\\n\\n1. Thyroxine - B. Pre-albumin\\n2. Vitamin B12 - C. Transcobalamin\\n3. Hemoglobin - D. Haptoglobin\\n4. Heme - A. Hemopexin\\n\\nThyroxine is carried by thyroid-binding prealbumin,\xa0which is also known as transthyretin.\\n\\nVitamin B12 binds to transcobalamin II\xa0in the ileal cells\xa0and is secreted into the plasma. Transcobalamin II delivers vitamin B12 to the liver and other cells of the body, including rapidly proliferating cells in the bone marrow and the gastrointestinal tract.\\n\\nHemopexin\xa0binds to porphyrins, particularly heme, for heme recycling.\\n\\nHaptoglobin binds to free hemoglobin and prevents its excretion in urine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7cdae0fe87","n":297,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Match the given cellular components with their respective marker enzymes.\\n\\nCellular components\\nMarker enzymes\\n\\n1. Mitochondria\\nA. Catalase\\n\\n2. Peroxisomes\\nB. Galactosyl transferase\\n\\n3. Golgi complex\\nC. Glucose-6-phosphatase\\n\\n4. Endoplasmic reticulum\\nD. ATP synthase","options":["1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B","1-D,2-A,3-B,4-C","1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D","1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1-D,2-A,3-B,4-C\\n\\nThe correctly matched answer is\xa01-D,2-A,3-B,4-C.\\n\\nCellular components\\nMarker enzymes\\n\\n1. Mitochondria\\nD. ATP synthase\\n\\n2. Peroxisomes\\nA. Catalase\\n\\n3. Golgi complex\\nB. Galactosyl transferase\\n\\n4. Endoplasmic reticulum\\nC. Glucose-6-phosphatase\\n\\nEach organelle has some specific enzymes are called marker enzymes. After centrifugation, the separated organelles are identified by the detection of marker enzymes in the sample.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1c62ed43b1","n":298,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Myosin ceases to be in contact with actin filaments as a result of:","options":["Dissociation of ADP and Pi","Cessation of calcium release from sarcoplasm","Conformational change in Troponin T","Attachment of ATP to myosin head"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Attachment of ATP to myosin head\\n\\nMyosin ceases to be in contact with actin filaments as a result of\xa0the attachment of ATP to the myosin head.\\n\\nMuscle contraction occurs by sliding of thin filament over thick filament. The sequence of events are as follows:\\n\\n• \xa0Troponin I is bound to actin and tropomyosin and covers the sites where actin interacts with myosin heads in a resting muscle. The myosin head contains tightly bound adenosine phosphate (ADP) and inorganic phosphate (Pi).\\n• Following an action potential, cytosolic Ca2+ is increased due to the Ca2+ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\xa0 Simultaneously\xa0Ca2+ influx happens through voltage-gated Ca2+ channels too.\\n• The free Ca2+ binds with\xa0troponin C. This binding will weaken the troponin I interaction with actin and exposes the actin-binding site for myosin.\\n• This results in the formation of myosin-actin cross-bridges.\\n• The\xa0cross-bridge between the head of the myosin and the active site of the actin causes a conformational change in the head and the\xa0power stroke\xa0occurs,\xa0moving the thin filament relative to the thick filament.\\n• Once the head of the cross-bridge tilts, the\xa0ADP and phosphate\xa0ion that were previously attached to the head are\xa0released\xa0and a new ATP molecule can bind in that place.\\n• The binding of the new ATP molecule\xa0to the free site on the myosin causes\xa0detachment of the head of the myosin\xa0from the actin.\\n• ATP is hydrolyzed and Pi released, causing a re-cocking\xa0of the myosin head and completing the cycle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-393d8f91c1","n":299,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Substances and the type of transport across a membrane are given below, choose the correct combination:\\n\\n1. Oxygen: Simple diffusion\\n2. Glucose: Facilitated diffusion\\n3. Sodium: Voltage-gated channel\\n4. Sodium: Secondary active transport\\n5. Calcium: Active transport","options":["1 and 2 are correct","1, 2, and 4 are correct","1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 are correct","1, 2, and 5 are correct"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 are correct\\n\\nAll the given combinations\xa01, 2, 3, 4, and 5 are correct.\\n\\n1) Oxygen\xa0is a non-polar molecule and hence can diffuse across the lipid membrane of the cells.\\n\\n2)\xa0Facilitated diffusion\xa0- Transporting the molecule from one side of the cell membrane to the other is done with the help of transport proteins called carriers.\xa0Molecules move from areas of high concentration to areas of low concentration. No energy input is required when carrier proteins move substances in the direction of their gradients. Glucose transport by the glucose transporter is an example, which moves glucose down its concentration gradient from the ECF to the cytoplasm of the cell.\\n\\n3) Voltage-gated channels are simple ion channels acting as transport proteins, gated by\xa0membrane potential alterations. Sodium transported via voltage-gated channels is an example.\\n\\n4) Transporting more than one substance together\xa0using a single transport protein against the gradient is known as secondary active transport.\xa0An example is SGLT 1,\xa0the cotransporter of sodium and glucose from the intestinal lumen into mucosal cells.\\n\\n5)Active transport - Transporting of substances against the gradients, and requires energy. Ca2+ ATPase that pumps out calcium out of the cells is an example.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-384b109b0e","n":300,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"The graph below shows oxygen dissociation curves. What does the curve marked as \'A\' indicate?","options":["Myoglobin","Carboxyhemoglobinemia","Methemoglobin","Fetal hemoglobin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Myoglobin\\n\\nThe curve \'A\' shows a hyperbolic curve that is characteristic of oxygen dissociation in myoglobin.\\n\\nMyoglobin is an iron-containing component of the skeletal muscle. Myoglobin can bind only to one oxygen molecule and hence lacks co-operative binding.\xa0It has a higher affinity to oxygen than hemoglobin and hence releases oxygen only at low PO2 values. This leads to a leftward shift in the myoglobin oxygen dissociation curve.\\n\\nMyoglobin is present in large quantities in muscles that are commonly used for sustained contraction. During such contractions, muscle blood supply is decreased due to compression. Myoglobin facilitates oxygen supply in these conditions.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Carbon monoxide (CO) reacts with hemoglobin to form carbon monoxyhemoglobin (carboxyhemoglobin or COHb).\xa0The affinity of hemoglobin for CO is 210 times its affinity for O2, and COHb liberates CO very slowly. An additional difficulty is that when COHb is present, the dissociation curve of the remaining HbO2 shifts to the left, decreasing the amount of O2 released.\\n\\nOption C: Hemoglobin normally has ferrous ion. When it is oxidized to ferric iron, it is called methemoglobin. It causes dark-coloured blood.\\n\\nOption D: Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) is structurally similar to hemoglobin A except for the gamma chains. It binds less avidly to 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate and hence has higher affinity for oxygen leading to left ward shift in the dissociation curve.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/0f74c84979ef5404be73.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-4a8a4947c4","n":301,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"The image shows changes in membrane potential during an action potential. The region marked between 4 to 5 is due to ________.","options":["Influx of Na+","Efflux of Na+","Influx of K+","Efflux of K+"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Efflux of K+\\n\\nThe region marked between 4 and 5 in the action potential curve indicates\xa0repolarization due to the efflux of K+\xa0ions across the cell membrane.\\n\\nThe sequence of events during the action potential\xa0occurs in the following sequence.\\n\\n1. Resting state:\xa0Both channels\xa0are\xa0closed.\\n\\n2.\xa0Na+\xa0channels open\xa0in response to depolarizing stimulus. Sodium enters the cell. The membrane reaches\xa0threshold potential.\\n\\n3. The sodium influx causes the\xa0opening\xa0of more\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0through a\xa0positive feedback\xa0mechanism.\xa0Rapid depolarization\xa0occurs.\\n\\n4. Sodium conductance decreases.\\n\\n5.\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0close\xa0and\xa0K+\xa0channels\xa0open. Due to\xa0K+\xa0efflux, repolarization\xa0occurs.\\n\\n6.\xa0Na+\xa0channels are\xa0reset\xa0and are ready to respond to another depolarizing stimulus (relative refractory period). During this phase, the\xa0K+\xa0channels remain open and\xa0slowly return\xa0to the\xa0closed\xa0state causing\xa0after-hyperpolarization.\\n\\n7. Both channels are closed. The membrane returns to\xa0RMP.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/31ce1fb59006bc97c3de.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-6ba56465ee","n":302,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"The parents notice that their child is tired and that he has shortness of breath on climbing stairs and while lying down. What is the first investigation that you would do?","options":["Echocardiogram","Chest x-ray","Spirometry","MRI to rule out enlarged heart"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Chest x-ray\\n\\nThe first investigation to be done for a child who feels shortness of breath on climbing stairs and in the supine position is a chest x-ray.\\n\\nThe child having shortness of breath on climbing stairs is known as dyspnea on exertion. The most common cause is congestive heart failure.\xa0The child feeling\xa0shortness of breath\xa0in the supine position is orthopnea. It is relieved on sitting or standing.\\n\\nRespiratory causes include asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disorder (COPD), pneumonia, and pulmonary embolism. Cardiovascular causes include congestive heart failure(CHF), pulmonary edema, valvular heart defect, and pulmonary hypertension.\\n\\nEvaluation begins with assessing the airway, breathing, and circulation of the patient. Oxygen saturation can appear normal at rest, and hence oxygen saturation during physical activity should be obtained. A chest x-ray is the first diagnostic test\xa0which can show pulmonary pathology or pumonary edema and other signs of CHF that could help in identifying the cause and further evaluation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: An echocardiogram can be done if the chest X-ray is abnormal. It is done to evaluate cardiac function, valvular function, and pericardial space.\\n\\nOption C: Spirometry is done when the chest x-ray is normal. It is done to determine lung function. Abnormal spirometry can indicate either an obstructive pathology such as asthma, or COPD or a restrictive disease such as interstitial fibrosis.\\n\\nOption D: Cardiac MRI (Magnetic resonance imaging)\xa0is an emerging modality done to look for cardiomegaly. It is for the accurate evaluation of left and right ventricular mass, size, and function. It is also used for localizing ischemic and non-ischemic causes such as myocarditis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a1a6fb8d1b","n":303,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"The second phase of saliva secretion involves:","options":["Active Na+ reabsorption, passive Cl- reabsorption, passive K+ secretion, active secretion of HCO3-","Passive Na+ reabsorption, active Cl- reabsorption, passive K+ secretion, active secretion of HCO3-","Passive Na+ secretion, active Cl- reabsorption, active K+ secretion, passive secretion of HCO3-","Active Na+ reabsorption, passive Cl- reabsorption, active K+ secretion, passive secretion of HCO3-"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Active Na+ reabsorption, passive Cl- reabsorption, active K+ secretion, passive secretion of HCO3-\\n\\nDuring the second phase of salivary secretion,\xa0Na+\xa0is actively reabsorbed,\xa0Cl-\xa0\xa0is reabsorbed passively,\xa0K+\xa0is secreted actively and\xa0HCO3-\xa0is secreted passively (part of the secretion may be active as well, but among the given options, option D is the most accurate with respect to all the ions).\\n\\nSalivary secretion occurs in two stages:\\n\\nThe\xa0first stage\xa0involves\xa0acini,\xa0which secrete ptyalin and/or mucin together with some ions. The composition of the ions is similar to that of the extra-cellular fluid.\\n\\nThe\xa0second stage\xa0involves the\xa0ducts.\xa0As the primary salivary secretion flows through the ducts, two active transport processes occur:\\n\\n• Na+\xa0is actively re-absorbed and\xa0K+\xa0is actively secreted, in exchange for Na+.\\n• HCO3-\xa0is secreted by the ductal epithelium into the lumen of the salivary ducts. Some of this secretion occurs by passive bicarbonate exchange for\xa0Cl-\xa0and partly active secretion.\\n\\nAs a result, the\xa0Na+\xa0and Cl-\xa0concentrations are\xa0lower\xa0than that of plasma, while\xa0K+\xa0and HCO3-\xa0concentrations are\xa0higher\xa0than that of plasma.\\n\\nComposition of ions:\\n\\n• Under resting conditions, the Na+ and Cl- concentrations in saliva are about 15 mEq/L each, which is 1/7th to 1/10th of the plasma concentrations.\\n• K+\xa0concentration is about\xa030 mEq/L, which is seven times higher than that of plasma.\\n• HCO3-\xa0concentration is\xa050 to 70 mEq/L, which is two to three times higher than that of the plasma.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3cee7f391d","n":304,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) is used to relieve pain. What is the mechanism behind it?","options":["Gate control theory","Conditioned pain modulation","Descending inhibition","Central inhibition"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Gate control theory\\n\\nGate theory of pain\xa0is the mechanism behind transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation\xa0(TENS).\xa0This method uses electrodes to activate Aα and Aβ fibres near the injury to relieve pain.\\nAt the site of sensory afferent termination, transmission in nociceptive pathways can be interrupted. This is done by the actions within the dorsal horn of the spinal cord. Pain subsiding when the site of injury is rubbed or shook is an example. Afferents of\xa0innocuous cutaneous mechanoreceptors emit collaterals that terminate in the dorsal horn. The responsiveness of dorsal horn neurons\xa0to their input from nociceptive afferent terminals is reduced by these afferents This is called the gate-control mechanism of pain modulation.\\n\\nThe given image depicts the gate control theory of pain:","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1a25036e15","n":305,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Transport of iron into enterocytes is done by which of the following proteins:","options":["Transferrin","Ferroportin","Hepcidin","Divalent metal transporter - 1"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Divalent metal transporter - 1\\n\\nTransport of iron into enterocytes is done by divalent metal transporter-1 protein.\\n\\nIron absorption occurs in the duodenum. Iron in our gut exists in 2 forms, they are nonheme and heme iron.\\n\\nLuminal nonheme iron is mostly in the Fe3+ (ferric) state and is reduced to Fe2+ (ferrous) iron by ferrireductases. Fe2+ iron is then transported across the apical membrane by divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1).\\n\\nGiven below is an image of iron absorption that shows divalent metal transporter-1 protein in intestinal mucosal cells.\\n\\nHeme iron is moved across the apical membrane into the cytoplasm through heme transporters.\\n\\nIn the duodenal cells, Fe2+ iron can follow one of two pathways: transport to the blood or storage as mucosal iron. The nonstorage form of Fe2+ iron is transported from the cytoplasm into circulation by the protein ferroportin\xa0(Option B).\\n\\nOnce in circulation, Fe2+ is again oxidized back to Fe3+ iron by hephaestin and ceruloplasmin. The newly absorbed Fe3+ iron binds rapidly to transferrin\xa0(Option A), which delivers iron to red cell progenitors in the marrow.\\n\\nHepcidin\xa0(Option C), is a protein secreted from the liver that plays a major role in the regulation of iron absorption. When the body’s iron stores are adequate, it downregulates the expression of ferroportin and thereby decreases iron absorption.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1c18d3a316","n":306,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Vessel A has a length of L and diameter of D and vessel B has 2L and 2D. Considering the flow is laminar through both the cylinders, the flow through B is how many times as much as the flow through A?","options":["4 times","8 times","16 times","32 times"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 8 times\\n\\nFlow through B will be 8 times\xa0more than the flow through A.\\n\\nAccording to Poiseuille\xa0Hagen formula,\\n\\nF=\xa0ΔP x\xa0π/8 x 1/n x r4/L\\n\\nF = flow\\n\\nΔP =\xa0pressure difference between two ends of the tube\\n\\nη = viscosity\\n\\nr = radius of the tube\\n\\nL = length of the tube\\n\\nHence flow directly varies to the 4th power of radius and inversely varies to length.\\n\\nFlow through B = (2r)4/2L = 8 times","img":false,"images":["/bimg/a94e4b1c3e4f7cd01d22.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-a5a6caac1e","n":307,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"What does the zero pressure indicate in the pressure-volume curve?","options":["Residual volume","Functional residual capacity","Tidal volume","Inspiratory reserve volume"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Functional residual capacity\\n\\nThe zero pressure in the pressure-volume curve indicates the functional\xa0residual capacity.\\n\\nThe pressure-volume relationship is basic to the understanding of the fundamental relationship between the compliance of the lungs and the chest wall. It is measured by a spirometer.\\n\\nThe functional residual capacity is the volume of the air that remains in the lungs after normal expiration. The normal value is about 2.5 liters.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Residual volume is the air left in the lungs\xa0after a maximal expiratory effort.\\n\\nOption C: Tidal volume is the amount of air that moves in during each inspiration or the amount of air that moves out with each expiration, during quiet breathing. The normal tidal volume is\xa0500–750 mL.\\n\\nOption D: Inspiratory reserve volume is the air inspired with a maximal inspiratory effort in addition to the tidal volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b4c618d7fb","n":308,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"What is the value of normal tissue oncotic pressure?","options":["12 mmHg","25 mmHg","32 mmHg","54 mmHg"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 12 mmHg\\n\\nThe value of normal tissue oncotic pressure is 12 mmHg. It refers to the interstitial osmotic pressure.\\n\\nThe interstitial oncotic pressure, while very small, is the pressure exerted by a small amount of protein present in the interstitium that tends to pull fluids out of the capillaries. It is different for each organ based on the level of protein components. It is mostly negligible,\xa0but sometimes can be as high as 6 mmHg in the brain. The values are also positive in the liver and kidneys.\\n\\nThe colloid osmotic pressure or oncotic pressure is the osmotic pressure exerted due to the presence of colloidal proteins like albumin and globulin within the plasma. The colloidal proteins are impermeable to the semi-permeable cell membrane and cannot cross the membrane. Thus, it exerts osmotic pressure, which pulls fluid into the capillaries.\\n\\nThe lowest number in the given options is 12 mmHg and, hence, is the best possible answer to this question.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1d94d98030","n":309,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Where is the signaling sequence in a type 2 membrane protein located?","options":["Inside the membrane at the N-terminal end","Inside the membrane at the C-terminal end","Outside the membrane at the N-terminal end","Outside the membrane at the C-terminal end"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inside the membrane at the N-terminal end\\n\\nThe signaling sequence in a type 2 membrane protein is located inside the membrane (transmembrane region).\\n\\nA membrane protein can be either integral protein (spans the thickness of the cell membrane) or peripheral\xa0protein (attached to the peripheral side of the membrane).\\n\\nIntegral membrane proteins or transmembrane proteins are embedded in the cell membrane, spanning through the entire thickness. They are anchored by hydrophobic interactions and require the use of detergent for solubility.\xa0 Examples are ligand-binding receptors for hormones or neurotransmitters, transport proteins like Na+-K+ ATPase, pores, ion channels, cell adhesion molecules, and GTP-binding proteins (G proteins).\\n\\nPeripheral proteins are attached only to one surface of the cell membrane and do not penetrate all the way through. They are often attached to integral proteins. Examples are cell surface receptors, enzymes, ankyrin.\\n\\nSignaling sequences are 16 to 30 amino acid residues in length and located at the N-terminal extension of the membrane proteins. After an appropriate trigger, they are either cleaved (and bind to MHC class I of the endoplasmic reticulum) or act as a membrane anchor (transporting proteins across the membrane).\\n\\nIntegral proteins can be\xa0divided into:\\n\\n• Type I integral proteins have a cleavable N-terminal signal sequence (towards the cytosol). Examples include histocompatibility antigens, glycophorin, and membrane immunoglobulin.\\n• Type II integral proteins have a non-cleavable\xa0N terminal combined signal/anchor sequence. Examples include transferrin receptors and glycosyl transferases.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b852cdf41a","n":310,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which among the following organs has the least arteriovenous oxygen difference?","options":["Brain","Kidney","Skin","Liver"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Kidney\\n\\nThe organ with the least arteriovenous oxygen difference is the kidney.\\n\\nThe\xa0arteriovenous oxygen difference\xa0is the amount of oxygen the tissues take up from the blood. If the amount of oxygen utilized by the tissues is more, the greater the arteriovenous oxygen difference.\\n\\nThe renal cortical blood flow is comparatively greater as the main function is to filter large volumes of blood through the glomeruli. Here, the oxygen extracted from the blood is relatively less. The\xa0arteriovenous oxygen difference for the kidney is only 14 mL/L of blood, which is very less when compared to the brain and heart.\\n\\nOrgans\\n\\nArteriovenous Oxygen Difference (mL/L)\\n\\nBrain\\n62\\n\\nKidney\\n14\\n\\nSkin\\n25\\n\\nLiver\\n34\\n\\nHeart muscles\\n114","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e9262a65ed","n":311,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which of the following can be used to calculate the cardiac output?\\n\\nA. ECHO.\\nB. Ventilation-perfusion scan.\\nC. Thermodilution.\\nD. Fick\'s principle","options":["A, B, C, D","A, D","B, C, D","A, C, D"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. A, C, D\\n\\nFick principle, thermodilution, and ECHO can be used to measure cardiac output.\\n\\nCardiac output is the amount of blood pumped by the heart into the aorta every minute. The average cardiac output is 5L/min and can be measured by various indirect methods.\\n\\nFick\'s principle uses the total amount of oxygen absorbed from the air by the lungs and the arteriovenous oxygen difference of blood to calculate the cardiac output using the following formula:\\n\\nCardiac output (L/min) = (O2 absorbed per minute by the lungs (mL/min))\xa0 /\xa0 (Arterio-venous O2 difference(mL/L of blood))\\n\\nThermodilution method\xa0employs cold saline which is infused into the right atrium through a double-lumen\xa0Swan–Ganz thermodilution catheter and the temperature change is measured at the pulmonary artery using a thermistor at the end of the catheter. The mean decrease in the temperature of blood is inversely proportional to the cardiac output.\\n\\nAn ECHO uses ultrasound waves from a transducer placed on the chest wall to measure the velocity of blood flowing from the left ventricle into the aorta. Stroke volume can be calculated from this.\xa0Cardiac Output = Heart Rate X Stroke Volume.\\n\\nThe other methods that can be used to measure cardiac output include impedance cardiography a.k.a. thoracic electrical bioimpedance,\xa0electromagnetic or ultrasonic flowmeter,and\xa0\xa0indicator dilution method.\\n\\nVentilation-perfusion scan\xa0is not a method of cardiac output measurement. It is used to assess pulmonary circulation.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b96bd3f83a","n":312,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which of the following primary second messengers is involved in the contraction of ciliary muscles while focusing on near objects?","options":["cAMP","cGMP","Na+ inflow through a channel","IP3"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. IP3\\n\\nIP3 is the primary second messenger involved in the\xa0contraction of ciliary muscles to focus on a near object.\\n\\nOn focusing on a near object, stimulation of the parasympathetic nerve fibers leads to the contraction of the bilateral ciliary muscle, via short ciliary nerves, which relaxes the lens ligaments. The lens becomes thicker and the increase in the refractive index allows the eye to focus on the near object. This is known as the accommodation reflex.\\n\\nIn the ciliary muscles of the eye, contraction is mediated via M3\xa0> M2\xa0receptors, while relaxation is mediated via the β2\xa0receptor.\\n\\nM3\xa0receptors act via the\xa0formation of inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG), leading to an increase in intracellular Ca2+\xa0and activation of protein kinase C, respectively. The increase in intracellular\xa0Ca2+\xa0sets up a chain reaction which eventually leads to contraction of the ciliary smooth muscle of the eye.\\n\\nThus, in the contraction of the ciliary muscle during focusing on a near object, the second messenger involved is the IP3-DAG pathway.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-cc6a919bac","n":313,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which of the following tissues are dependent on insulin for glucose uptake?","options":["Brain","Adipose tissue","Pancreas","Muscle"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Adipose tissue\\n\\nAdipose tissue is dependent on insulin for glucose uptake.\\n\\nThe uptake of glucose in skeletal muscles is also dependent on insulin but may be mediated via non-insulin-dependent mechanisms as well (such as muscle contractions).\xa0Therefore the best answer out of the given options is adipose tissue.\\n\\nGlucose uptake by skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and adipose tissues is via GLUT-4 which is insulin-dependent. In the presence of insulin, GLUT-4 receptors translocate to the cell membrane and hence, glucose uptake by the cell occurs.\\n\\nMost of the other GLUT transporters are not insulin sensitive and are constitutively expressed in the cell membrane. Ex: GLUT-1 in RBCs, brain and kidneys. (GLUT-3 is also present in the brain and kidneys)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-59875bb10f","n":314,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2022","text":"Which physiological adaptation does not happen at high altitudes?","options":["Pulmonary vasoconstriction","Polycythemia","Respiratory acidosis","Hypoxia"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Respiratory acidosis\\n\\nThe physiological adaptation that does not happen at high altitudes is respiratory acidosis. Respiratory alkalosis\xa0is the physiological adaptation seen at high altitudes due to hyperventilation. It is a method of acclimatization. Compensatory mechanisms that are carried out to increase altitude tolerance are called acclimatization.\\n\\nFollowing are the physiological changes that occur during acclimatization:\\n\\n• Hyperventilation leads to\xa0respiratory alkalosis\xa0and\xa0shift\xa0of the\xa0oxygen–hemoglobin dissociation curve\xa0to the\xa0left\xa0(and shifts to right after aclimitization).\\n• Increased erythropoietin\xa0secretion, leading to an increase in circulating red blood cells\\n• Increased number of mitochondria\\n• Increased myoglobin to facilitate the movement of O2 into the tissues.\\n• Increased tissue cytochrome oxidase\\n• Increased sympathetic tone\\n• Renal suppression of carbonic anhydrase and excretion of dilute alkaline urine brings the pH of the blood to normal.\\n• Decreased maximal exercise capacity (by ~1% for every 100 meters gained above 1500 meters)\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A-\xa0In response to the high altitude hypoxia, pulmonary vasoconstriction occurs due to which blood flow is distributed where it is most effective, leading to distribution of blood flow to the pulmonary areas in proportion to their alveolar O2 pressures.\\n\\nOption B-\xa0Polycythemia -\xa0Erythropoietin secretion increases on the ascent to high altitude which results in polycythemia\\n\\nOption D-\xa0Hypoxia\xa0- Total barometric pressure decreases as altitude increases, resulting in decreased\xa0Po2\xa0(partial pressure of oxygen) leading to hypoxic hypoxia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e6886a31b5","n":315,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Arrange the following bands in the sarcomere from the periphery to the center.\\n\\n1. Z line\\n2. M line\\n3. A band\\n4. H zone","options":["1,2,3,4","2,3,4,1","1,3,4,2","1,4,2,3"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1,3,4,2\\n\\nThe correct sequence of\xa0bands in the sarcomere\xa0from the periphery to the centre is:\xa0Z line→\xa0A band→\xa0H zone→\xa0M line.\\n\\nThe sarcomere is the contractile unit of the skeletal muscle fibre.\\n\\n• Z line\xa0provides attachment to actin filaments. The area between two adjacent Z lines is called the sarcomere. It is composed of thick and thin filaments:\\n\\n• Thick filament: Myosin\\n• Thin filaments: Actin, tropomyosin and troponin\\n\\n• I band is isotropic to light. It contains only\xa0actin\xa0filaments.\\n• A band is anisotropic to light. It contains myosin plus overlapped actin.\\n• H bands are the\xa0lighter areas in the centre of the A band. These are the areas where the the thick filaments do not overlap the thin filaments, when the muscle is relaxed.\\n• M line is present in the\xa0centre of H band. There are slender cross-connections that hold the thick filaments in proper array.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-bd6a14ef1e","n":316,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Assertion: RMP is dependent on proteins and phosphate ions.\\nReason: Diffusion potential can be calculated using Nernst Equation.\\n\\nRMP- Resting membrane potential","options":["The assertion is independently a true statement, but the reasons is independently a false statement.","The assertion is independently a false statement, but the reasons is independently a true statement.","Both assertion and reason are independently true and the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion","Both assertion and reason are independently true and the reason is the correct explanation for the assertion"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Both assertion and reason are independently true and the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion\\n\\nBoth assertion and reason are independently true and the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion.\\n\\nResting membrane potential (RMP) is the\xa0difference\xa0in the\xa0electrical potentials\xa0between the\xa0cytoplasm\xa0of the cell and the\xa0extracellular space. It represents an equilibrium situation where the\xa0concentration gradient\xa0of ions across the membrane is equal and opposite to the\xa0electrical gradients.\\n\\nResting membrane potential is primarily influenced by sodium (Na+) and potassium (K+) ions, along with negatively charged intracellular proteins and organic phosphates. In neurons, the concentration of potassium is higher inside the cell, while sodium ions exhibit the opposite distribution. The establishment of this concentration difference is facilitated by the Na, K ATPase pump. Potassium ions passively move out of the cell, while sodium ions move into the cell.\\n\\nThe Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation is utilized to calculate the resting membrane potential since multiple ions are involved. Among these ions, potassium, which has the greatest conductance across the membrane at rest, becomes the primary contributor to the resting membrane potential.\\n\\nGoldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation\\n\\nThe Nernst equation establishes a connection between the diffusion potential and the ion concentration difference across a membrane. The magnitude of the Nernst potential is determined by the ratio of the ion concentrations on the two sides of the membrane.\\n\\nNernst equation :\\n\\nEMF(millivolts) = [61/z] X log [Co / Ci]\\n\\n• EMF = electromotive force (Nernst potential)\\n• z = charge of ion\\n• Co = concentration outside\\n• Ci\xa0= concentration inside","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-63ee52b4a2","n":317,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"At which volume or capacity, pressure measured at the upper airway becomes zero?","options":["Residual volume","Functional residual capacity","Total lung capacity","Inspiratory capacity"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Functional residual capacity\\n\\nPressure measured at the upper airway becomes zero at functional residual capacity.\\n\\nThe curve of airway pressure obtained is plotted against the volume, which is the pressure-volume curve of the total respiratory system. This curve is used in understanding the fundamental relationship between the compliance of the lungs and the chest wall. It is measured by a spirometer.\\n\\nThe functional residual capacity is the volume of the air that remains in the lungs after normal expiration. The normal value is about 2.5 liters. At the lung volume of\xa0functional residual capacity (FRC), which is the relaxed state of the lungs, the pressure inside the lungs is effectively zero.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Residual volume is the air left in the lungs\xa0after a maximal expiratory effort.\\n\\nOption C: Total lung capacity\xa0is the sum of tidal volume, inspiratory reserve volume, expiratory reserve volume, and residual volume.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Inspiratory capacity\xa0is the maximum amount of air inspired from the end-expiratory level. It is the sum of tidal\xa0volume and\xa0inspiratory reserve volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0818707281","n":318,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Cells with prolific smooth endoplasmic reticulum secrete_______","options":["Eicosanoid hormones","Steroid hormones","Amine hormones","Peptide hormones"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Steroid hormones\\n\\nCells with prolific smooth endoplasmic reticulum secrete steroid hormones.\\n\\nThe endoplasmic reticulum contains\xa0a complex series of tubules in the cytoplasm of the cell.\\n\\n• Ribosomes are attached to the cytoplasmic side of the membrane in the\xa0rough(granular)\xa0endoplasmic reticulum.\xa0Protein synthesis and the initial folding of polypeptide chains with the formation of disulfide bonds are the functions of rough ER.\\n• Ribosomes are absent in the\xa0smooth (agranular) endoplasmic reticulum.\xa0Free ribosomes are found in the cytoplasm. It is the\xa0site of steroid synthesis in\xa0steroid-secreting cells and the site of detoxification processes in other cells.\\n\\nThe sarcoplasmic reticulum is a modified\xa0endoplasmic reticulum, that functions as an\xa0intracellular calcium store, which releases calcium into the cytoplasm stimulating\xa0cell contraction, proliferation, and migration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ef093f46b3","n":319,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"In the given cross-section of a muscle spindle, the structure marked below is innervated by which of the following motor nerve fibres?","options":["Gamma","Alpha","Beta","Delta"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Gamma\\n\\nIn the given cross-section of a muscle spindle, the structure marked shows intrafusal muscle fibres, which are innervated by gamma(γ) motor neurons.\\n\\nEach muscle spindle is 3 to 10 mm long and consists of 3 to 12 tiny intrafusal muscle fibers that are pointed at their ends and attached to the glycocalyx of the surrounding large extrafusal muscle fibers. There are two kinds of sensory endings in each spindle, a single primary (group Ia) ending and up to eight secondary (group II) endings.\\n\\nIntrafusal fibers are of 2 types: (1) nuclear bag fibers - subdivided as dynamic and static nuclear bag fibers and (2) nuclear chain fibers.\\n\\nEach intrafusal muscle fiber is a tiny skeletal muscle fiber. Since there are either few or no actin and myosin filaments in the centre of each of these fibres, the centre does not contract when the ends do. It performs the role of a sensory receptor instead. The end portions that do contract are excited by small gamma(γ) motor nerve fibers which help control basic muscle tone.\\n\\nThere are two types of γ motor neurons- (a) dynamic that supplies dynamic nuclear bag fibers and (b) static that supply static nuclear bag fibers and nuclear chain fibers.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Alpha (α) motor fibers supply the extrafusal muscle fibers which are the regular contractile units of the muscle. They are on an average 14 micrometers in diameter and these fibers branch many times after they enter the muscle and innervate the large skeletal muscle fibers at the motor endplate. A single alpha nerve fibre can stimulate anywhere between three to several hundred skeletal muscle fibres, which are collectively called the motor unit.\\n\\nOption C: Beta (β) motor fibers are for touch and pressure.\\n\\nOption D: Delta (δ) motor fibers are for pain and temperature.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/2c231dd0fa1fa5b97286.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-824cdff349","n":320,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Increase in blood pressure at the beginning of Valsalva maneuver is due to_________","options":["Increase in intrathoracic pressure adding to left ventricular pressure","Increase in intrathoracic pressure adding to aortic pressure","Increase in left atrial pressure","Increase in left ventricular volume"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Increase in intrathoracic pressure adding to aortic pressure\\n\\nIncrease in blood pressure at the beginning of the Valsalva maneuver is due to increase in intrathoracic pressure adding to aortic\xa0pressure.\\n\\nValsalva maneuver involves the performance of\xa0forced expiration against a closed glottis. It occurs during\xa0coughing, defecation, and heavy lifting. At the onset of straining, the blood pressure is raised because the\xa0increase in intrathoracic pressure is added to the pressure of the blood in the aorta. The pressure then falls as the high intrathoracic pressure compresses the veins and decreases the cardiac output.\\n\\nWhen the glottis is opened and intrathoracic pressure returns to its normal state, cardiac output is regained, but there is constriction in the peripheral vessels. As a result, blood pressure elevates beyond the normal range, which triggers the baroreceptors, leading to bradycardia and a subsequent decrease in pressure back to normal levels.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/f7a2f898b02b30763f89.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-cf049544b7","n":321,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Insulin-independent glucose transporter is present in all of the following except?","options":["Brain","Adipose tissue","RBC","Pancreas"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Adipose tissue\\n\\nInsulin-dependent (not independent) glucose transporter is found in the adipose tissue.\\n\\nGlucose uptake by skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle and adipose tissues is via GLUT-4 which is insulin-dependent.\\n\\nIn the cytoplasm of insulin-sensitive cells, the vesicles contain a pool of GLUT-4 molecules. When the insulin receptors of these cells are activated, the vesicles move rapidly to the cell membrane and fuse with it, inserting the transporters into the cell membrane.\\n\\nThe membrane patches containing transporters are endocytosed when insulin action stops, preparing the vesicles for subsequent insulin exposure. Phosphoinositol 3-kinase is activated by the insulin receptor, which causes the vesicles to migrate to the cell membrane\\n\\nMost of the other GLUT transporters are not insulin-sensitive and are constitutively expressed in the cell membrane. Ex: GLUT-1 in RBC\'s and brain, GLUT-2 in the beta cells of the pancreas and GLUT-3 present in the brain and kidneys.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fe7853a885","n":322,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Our body was built to identify toxic substance by the recognition of bitter taste. Which of the following substances is responsible for the bitter taste?","options":["Amino acids","Alkalaoids","Hydrogen ions","Aldehydes"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Alkalaoids\\n\\nAlkaloids cause an intensely bitter taste, that causes the rejection of the food.\\n\\nBitter taste sensations are caused by two\xa0particular classes of substances:\\n\\n• Long-chain organic substances that contain nitrogen.\\n• Alkaloids\\n\\nAlkaloids cause an intensely bitter taste, leading to the rejection of certain foods. They are found in many medicinal drugs such as quinine, caffeine, strychnine, and nicotine. The bitter taste sensation in many deadly toxins found in poisonous plants is often caused by alkaloids. This helps the person or animal to reject the food.\\n\\nSweet taste is caused by chemicals such as sugars, glycols, alcohols, aldehydes, ketones, amides, esters, some amino acids, some small proteins, sulfonic acids, halogenated acids, and inorganic salts of lead and beryllium.\\n\\nSour taste is caused by acids (Hydrogen ion concentration). The sour taste is directly proportional to the strength of the acid.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6c873b9320","n":323,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"The given graph shows the effect of PTH on blood calcium and phosphate levels in an experimental animal. How does PTH increase plasma calcium levels?","options":["By increasing osetoclastic activity","By decreasing renal excretion of calcium","By increasing osteoblastic activity","By increasing osteoclastic activity and decreasing renal excretion of calcium"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. By increasing osteoclastic activity and decreasing renal excretion of calcium\\n\\nThe parathyroid hormone increases the plasma calcium levels by increasing osteoclastic activity and decreasing renal excretion of calcium.\\n\\nBy regulating intestinal reabsorption, renal excretion, and the exchange of these ions between the extracellular fluid and bone, PTH offers a potent mechanism for controlling the concentrations of calcium and phosphate in extracellular fluid.\\n\\nThe parathormone (PTH)\xa0increases Ca+2 concentration by:\\n\\n• Increased reabsorption of Ca+2 by renal tubules, decreasing the urinary Ca+2 excretion\\n• Increasing bone resorption, leading to Ca+2 release into the ECF\\n• Decreased phosphate reabsorption by the renal tubules, increasing the urinary excretion of phosphate\\n• Conversion of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol which increases intestinal Ca2+ absorption\\n\\nActivation of the osteoclasts occurs due to activated osteoblasts and osteocytes that send secondary signals to the osteoclasts. Preosteoclast cells become mature osteoclasts when their receptors are activated by RANKL, a significant secondary signal. Activation of the osteoclastic system occurs in two stages: (1) immediate activation of the osteoclasts that are already formed and (2) formation of new osteoclasts.\\n\\nPTH administration decreases proximal tubular reabsorption of phosphate ions, which results in a rapid loss of phosphate in the urine. At the same time that PTH reduces phosphate reabsorption, it also increases calcium reabsorption through renal tubules. This ultimately leads to a decrease in the renal excretion of calcium.\\n\\nFurthermore, it increases the reabsorption of hydrogen and magnesium ions while decreasing the reabsorption of ions from amino acids, potassium, and sodium in a manner similar to how it affects phosphate. Distal tubules and the thick ascending loop of the Henle are the primary sites of the increased calcium reabsorption.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/42547e77d221984fb671.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-1de520cecb","n":324,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"The type of hypoxia based on the graph is_____ (Consider the plot in red as normal)","options":["Stagnant hypoxia","Histotoxic hypoxia","Hypoxic hypoxia","Anemic hypoxia"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Anemic hypoxia\\n\\nThe graph represents the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve that would be seen in anemic hypoxia and normal. The X-axis represents the partial pressure of oxygen(PO2). The Y-axis represents the percentage saturation between the hemoglobin and the oxygen.\\n\\nThe graph indicates two lines: a red line representing normal\xa0hemoglobin and a purple line representing reduced hemoglobin levels. The red line corresponds to an arterial blood oxygen content of approximately 19 mL/dL, and venous blood oxygen content of 14 mL/dL. On the other hand, the purple line represents an arterial blood oxygen content of 9 mL/dL and venous blood oxygen content of 4 mL/dL.\\n\\nThis scenario demonstrates a 50% decrease in oxygen saturation of hemoglobin, while the partial pressure of oxygen (PO2) remains within the normal range. This condition is characterized as anemic hypoxia.\\n\\nIn anemic hypoxia, the blood oxygen content is decreased.\xa0The patients might not experience any difficulty during rest because 2,3-DPG increases in the red blood cells. However, they may have difficulty during exercise because of a limited ability to increase O2 delivery to the active tissues.\\n\\nConditions that cause a right shift in the oxygen hemoglobin curve are: (Conditions with the increased requirement of O2 - metabolically active tissues)\\n\\n• Hypoxia\\n• Hypercarbia\\n• Decreased pH\\n• Increased temperature\\n• Increased\xa02,3-biphosphoglycerate\\n\\nConditions that cause a left shift in the oxygen hemoglobin curve\\n\\n• Increased oxygen\\n• Decreased carbon dioxide\\n• Increased pH\\n• Decreased temperature\\n• Decreased\xa02,3-biphosphoglycerate\\n• Fetal hemoglobin.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/64974b3f88a98b3fdddb.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-6f5aeffe40","n":325,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"The upstroke of action potential (phase 0) in the sinoatrial node is due to ______________ ?","options":["Sodium influx via voltage gated sodium channels","Calcium currents only","Potassium efflux","Influx of both sodium and calcium"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Calcium currents only\\n\\nThe upstroke of action potential (phase 0) in the sinoatrial node is due to calcium currents only. It occurs due to the influx of Ca2+ ions by the opening of voltage-gated L-type channels.\\n\\nThe action potential of the SA node differs from that of the atria, ventricles, and Purkinje fibres in the following ways: (1) In the absence of neural input, the SA node demonstrates automaticity and the ability to produce its own action potentials. (2) It has an unstable resting membrane potential. (3) It has no sustained plateau.\\n\\nThe resting membrane potential (RMP) of the SA node is -55 to -60 millivolts. Phases of this potential are:\\n\\n• Phase 0 (Upstroke wave)-\xa0 This\xa0is due to the influx of Ca2+ ions by the opening of voltage-gated L-type channels.\\n• Phase 3 (Downstroke wave)- This\xa0is due to the efflux of K+ leading to repolarization. (Option C)\\n• Phase 4 (Prepotential)- Because of ongoing K+ efflux, hyperpolarization sets in. This makes the channel more permeable to Na+ ions and since these Na+ channels are activated during hyperpolarization they are referred to as funny currents (Ih/ If). They form the first half of the pre-potential. The second half of the prepotential is completed by the opening of the voltage-gated T-(transient) type Ca2+ channel. The slope of Phase 4 in the SA node determines the heart rate.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d93de34e46","n":326,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following are incorrectly paired?","options":["Gastrin - G cells, Antrum","Secretin - S cells, Duodenum","CCK - I cells, Duodenum and jejunum","Motilin - M cells, Duodenum and Jejunum"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Motilin - M cells, Duodenum and Jejunum\\n\\nMotilin is secreted by the\xa0Mo cells(not M cells) in the stomach, small intestine, and colon\\n\\nMotilin is a\xa0gastrointestinal hormones that is secreted by enterochromaffin cells and Mo cells in the stomach, small intestine, and colon. It mediates its action via G-protein-coupled receptors on enteric neurons in the duodenum and colon. It produces contraction of smooth muscle in the stomach and intestines in the period between meals.\\n\\nM cells are microfold cells are located in the epithelium covering mucosa-associated lymphoid tissues\xa0of the small intestine such as the Peyer\'s patches (PPs). Their function is to transport\xa0luminal antigens to the underlying lymphoid follicles to initiate an immune response.\\n\\nGastrin is secreted by G cells of the antrum, duodenum, and jejunum. It stimulates gastric acid secretion and mucosal growth.\\n\\nSecretin is secreted by the S cells\xa0of\xa0the duodenum.\xa0Its primary function is to promote the secretion of bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice by the\xa0ductal cells of the pancreas and duodenum. This secretion is poor in enzymes.\\n\\nCholecystokinin (CCK) is secreted by I cells of the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum. It stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion, pancreatic bicarbonate secretion, gallbladder contraction, and growth of the exocrine pancreas. It also inhibits gastric emptying.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7464c86a6d","n":327,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following hormone acts via this pathway?","options":["Leptin","Calcitonin","Vasopressin","Aldosterone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Leptin\\n\\nThe given image shows the\xa0JAK-STAT signaling pathway, which is associated with tyrosine kinase receptors. Among the given hormones leptin acts via this pathway.\\n\\nHormones that use receptor tyrosine kinase/ JAK-STAT signaling include the following:\\n\\n• Growth hormone\\n• Fibroblast growth factor\\n• Hepatocyte growth factor\\n• Insulin\\n• Insulin-like growth factor-1\\n• Leptin\\n• Prolactin\\n• Vascular endothelial growth factor\\n\\nLeptin is a hormone that is secreted by fat cells. The physiological effects of leptin are\xa0regulating appetite and energy balance. The leptin receptor acts via\xa0tyrosine kinase of the Janus kinase (JAK) family, JAK2.\xa0Leptin binds to the extracellular part of the leptin receptor, which causes\xa0phosphorylation (P) and activation of the intracellular JAK2. This causes\xa0phosphorylation of signal transducer and activation of transcription (STAT) proteins, which results in the transcription of target genes and the synthesis of proteins.\\n\\nPhosphorylation of JAK2 also results in the activation of\xa0other intracellular enzyme pathways such as mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK) and phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (PI3K).\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nHormones such as adrenal (Aldosterone)and gonadal steroid hormones, thyroid hormones, retinoid hormones, and vitamin D, bind with protein receptors inside the cell rather than on the cell surface. These hormones are lipid soluble and they readily cross the cell membrane and interact with receptors in the cytoplasm or nucleus.\\n\\nVasopressin in the epithelial cells acts via V2 receptor. They use adenylyl cyclase as their second messenger system.\xa0Vasopressin in the\xa0vascular smooth muscles acts via\xa0V1 receptor that uses phospholipase C\xa0as the second messenger system.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/0f17fd1605d27e730002.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-14106a6a27","n":328,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following is responsible for fetal heart acceleration?","options":["Carotid body chemoreceptors","Aortic baroreceptors","Autonomic brain stem regulation","ANP"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Carotid body chemoreceptors\\n\\nAmongst the given options, the best answer for the site responsible for Fetal heart acceleration is Carotid body chemoreceptors>Aortic baroreceptors>Autonomic brain stem regulation.\\n\\nThough the brainstem is critical to mediating this reflex, the primary stimulus for initiating the changes in the heart rate is initiated by the carotid chemo and baro receptors, especially the chemoreceptor which is highly sensitive to the changes in O2 levels in the blood. It plays a permissive role on fetal heart rate acceleration.\\n\\nArterial chemoreceptors in the carotid arteries>aortic arch, respond to low pH levels and decreased oxygen saturation. These chemoreceptors detect instances of significant fall in blood oxygen levels, setting off the peripheral chemoreflex and heightening both parasympathetic and sympathetic neural activities. The activation of the parasympathetic branch leads to a swift decline in Fetal Heart Rate (FHR), a clinical manifestation identified as intrapartum decelerations. Concurrently, the sympathetic branch prompts significant peripheral vasoconstriction across a range of vascular territories, which results in the elevation of arterial blood pressure, ensuring continuous perfusion to essential organs.\\n\\nThe permissive action on the FHR acceleration comes in instances of alleviated hypoxemia, detected by the chemoreceptors in the carotid body and aortic arch, suppressing the peripheral chemoreflex, leading to a reduction in parasympathetic and sympathetic neural activities. Deactivation of the parasympathetic branch results in a swift increase in Fetal Heart Rate (FHR), typically seen clinically as an increase in intrapartum accelerations.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-af2fcc005b","n":329,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following is responsible for pneumoperitoneal bradycardia following CO2 insufflation during laparoscopic surgery?","options":["Decreased afterload","Vascular injury","Air embolism","Peritoneal stretching"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Peritoneal stretching\\n\\nVagal stimulation due to peritoneal stretching is responsible for bradycardia following CO2 insufflation during laparoscopic surgery.\\n\\nCarbon dioxide (CO2) and nitrous oxide (N2O) are used in pneumoperitoneum, a technique that enhances laparoscopic surgery by distending the abdominal cavity and splitting up its contents.\\n\\nThe physiologic effects of CO2 pneumoperitoneum can be divided into local and systemic effects:\\n\\nLocal Effects\\nSystemic effects\\n\\nPeritoneal distention\\nHypercarbia\\n\\nVagal reaction\\nAcidosis\\n\\nElevated diaphragm\\nIncreased afterload\\n\\nAltered venous return\\nIncreased catecholamines\\n\\nCO2 pneumoperitoneum is associated with the risk of pathophysiological cardiovascular changes such as severe bradycardia, arrhythmia, and cardiac arrest requiring cardiopulmonary resuscitation. These changes are known to be associated with vagal-mediated cardiovascular reflexes triggered by rapid peritoneum distension due to insufflation.\\n\\nThis can be managed by desufflation of the abdomen, the use of vagolytic medications (such as atropine), and sufficient volume replacement.\\n\\nMost people can tolerate the hemodynamic and metabolic effects of pneumoperitoneum for a brief duration, and most healthy people can tolerate them for a longer period of time. Long laparoscopic procedures can be challenging for patients whose cardiovascular systems are compromised.\\n\\nIn such cases, alternative gases like helium, neon\xa0and argon can be used. They do not cause any metabolic effects, but if they get direct access to the venous system, they can cause gas emboli because they are not as soluble in blood as CO2 and N2O.\\n\\nGas embolism is treated by positioning the patient in a left lateral decubitus position, head down, to trap the gas in the right ventricle\'s apex. The gas can then be aspirated out of the right ventricle using a rapidly placed central venous catheter.\\n\\nLimiting intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) below 12-15 mmHg during insufflation is also effective in preventing pathophysiological changes of pneumoperitoneum.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-932162fced","n":330,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following is responsible for the phase marked C to D in the given nerve action potential?","options":["Na+ influx","K+ influx","Na+ efflux","K+ efflux"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. K+ efflux\\n\\nThe phase C to D in the given nerve action potential is caused by K+ efflux as a result of the opening of the\xa0 K+ channels, resulting in repolarization.\\n\\nThe sequence of events occurring during an action potential is shown below\\n\\n• Point A represents the resting state where\xa0both channels\xa0are\xa0closed.\\n• Na+\xa0channels open in response to depolarizing stimulus resulting in Na+ influx (option A). The initiation of action potential occurs only after a certain potential is reached, which is called the threshold potential represented by point B.\\n• After this, the sodium influx causes the\xa0opening\xa0of more\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0through a\xa0positive feedback\xa0mechanism.\xa0Rapid depolarization\xa0occurs to reach point C.\\n• At point C,\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0close\xa0and\xa0K+\xa0channels\xa0open, causing\xa0repolarization due to\xa0K+\xa0efflux.\\n• Point D represents continued depolarization due to K+ efflux.\\n• At point E,\xa0Na+\xa0channels are\xa0reset\xa0and are ready to respond to another depolarizing stimulus. During this phase, the\xa0K+\xa0channels remain open and\xa0slowly return\xa0to the\xa0closed\xa0state, causing\xa0after-hyperpolarization. As a stronger stimulus is required to reach the threshold potential, the excitability of nerve fibre is reduced at point E.\\n• As\xa0both channels close, the membrane returns to resting membrane potential back to point A.\\n\\nPoint B-D is a part of the refractory period. During this period, the application of even a stronger stimulus will not elicit an action potential.\\n\\nThe refractory period is divided into an absolute refractory period and a relative refractory period. During the absolute refractory period, the nerve will not be excited no matter how strong a\xa0stimulus is given. However, during the relative refractory period, stronger than normal stimuli can cause excitation.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/fc484ab4676ed25cda78.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-55dbf35d53","n":331,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following options is false regarding the measurement of temperature in a febrile patient?","options":["Rectal temperature is ideal for measuring the core body temperature","Rectal temperature is lower than axillary temperature","Tympanic membrane temperature is lower than rectal temperature","Rectal temperature is higher than oral temperature."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Rectal temperature is lower than axillary temperature\\n\\nWith regards to the measurement of\xa0temperature in a febrile patient, rectal temperature is higher than axillary temperature.\\n\\nFor humans, the conventional normal value for oral temperature is 37\xb0C (98.6\xb0F).\\n\\nRectal temperature represents the core body temperature and is less affected by changes in environmental temperature. It remains relatively constant. In comparison, oral temperature is typically 0.5\xb0C lower than rectal temperature. However, the oral temperature can be influenced by various factors such as consuming hot or cold fluids, chewing gum, smoking, and breathing through the mouth.\\n\\nTympanic membrane thermometers measure radiant heat emitted by the tympanic membrane and the nearby ear canal, providing an absolute value of temperature. Tympanic membrane readings are generally 0.8\xb0C (1.6\xb0F) lower than rectal temperatures. The axillary temperature measured is lower than the rectal temperature.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ff3496e673","n":332,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following sodium transporters are present maximally in the early part of the PCT?\\n\\n1. Na-K-ATPase\\n2. Na-glucose cotransporter\\n3. Na-H antiporter\\n4. Na-HCO3 symporter","options":["2,3,4","2,4","1, 2, 3","1,4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 3\\n\\nThe sodium transporters present in the early part of PCT are Na-K-ATPase, Na+-glucose cotransporter (SGLT) and Na+-H+ antiporter.\\n\\nReabsorption of sodium in different parts of the nephron are:\\n\\n• Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT):\\n\\n• Nearly 60% of the filtered sodium is reabsorbed in the PCT. Solutes mainly absorbed in the proximal tubule are sodium, chloride, bicarbonate, phosphate, lactate, glucose, amino acids etc.\\n• Na+ is actively pumped from these cells into the interstitial space by Na-K ATPase in the basolateral membrane after travelling by cotransport or exchange via the tubular lumen into the tubular epithelial cells along its concentration and electrical gradients. It extrudes three Na+ in exchange for two K+ that are pumped into the cell.\\n• In early PCT, the main sodium channels used for reabsorption are Na+-glucose cotransporter (SGLT), Na+-H+ antiporter, Na+-amino acid cotransporter, Na+-phosphate cotransporter, etc.\\n• In distal PCT,\xa0due to high\xa0chloride concentration in the lumen, it favours NaCl reabsorption, driven by Cl− gradient.\\n• The total solute and water reabsorption are proportional to each other. Thus, the entire proximal tubule also reabsorbs nearly 60% of the filtered water (isosmotic reabsorption).\\n\\n• Loop of Henle:\\n\\n• The thin descending limb has no active transport of Na+ outside the lumen but the loop is permeable to water, making the tubular fluid\xa0hypertonic.\\n• A thick ascending limb\xa0reabsorbs nearly 30% of the Na+\xa0by Na–2Cl–K cotransporter, an example of secondary active transport. This loop is\xa0impermeable to water.\xa0Since some of the solutes are reabsorbed and water is not reabsorbed, it dilutes\xa0the tubular fluid.\\n\\n• Distal convoluted tubule: Approximately 7% of the filtered load of sodium chloride is reabsorbed in the early distal tubule. This occurs due to the presence of\xa0sodium chloride co-transporter.\xa0Even this segment dilutes the tubular fluid as it is impermeable to water.\\n• Cortical collecting tubule: The remaining 3% of the filtered Na+ is absorbed via ENaCs (epithelial sodium channels) in this duct. \xa0This portion is regulated by aldosterone to maintain homeostasis in Na+ balance.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b4debaf1e9","n":333,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following statements is false about male sexual development?","options":["At eight weeks the embryo is bipotent.","Wolffian duct develops into epidymis and vas deferens on either side of the body.","Mullerian inhibiting substance inhibits mullerian duct.","Dihydrotestosterone leads to the development of secondary sexual characters and external genitalia."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. At eight weeks the embryo is bipotent.\\n\\nThe embryo is bipotent\xa0till\xa0seven weeks of gestation, not eight.\\n\\nAt the seventh week, the embryo contains both\xa0male and female primordial genital ducts. In the case of a female fetus,\xa0uterine tubes and the uterus develops from\xa0the Mullerian duct. In case of a male fetus,\xa0the wolffian duct system on each side develops into the epididymis and vas deferens.\\n\\nTestosterone is secreted by the Leydig cells of the\xa0fetal testis and\xa0MIS (Mullerian inhibiting substance) is secreted by the Sertoli cells. MIS is an amino acid that belongs to the family of transforming growth factor β (TGF-β). MIS causes apoptosis of the Mullerian ducts on the side\xa0on which it is secreted.\\n\\nDihydrotestosterone, a metabolite of\xa0testosterone is responsible for the\xa0formation of male external genitalia and male secondary sex characteristics. Development of the vas deferens,\xa0epididymis, and other structures from the wolffian ducts is due to the action of testosterone.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-594aaef2af","n":334,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect with respect to the baroreceptors present in the carotid sinus?","options":["They need the support of electromechanical transducers","They are located in the deep smooth muscle layer","Afferent fibres pass via cranial nerve IX","They express several non-selective cation channels"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. They are located in the deep smooth muscle layer\\n\\nBaroreceptors are not located in the\xa0deep smooth muscle layer. They are located in the adventitia layer.\\n\\nBaroreceptors are mechanoreceptors which respond to stretch stimuli. They are located in the heart and adventitia of the blood vessels. There are two types of baroreceptors: high-pressure arterial baroreceptors and low-pressure volume receptors. Arterial baroreceptors are located within the carotid sinuses and the aortic arch.\\n\\nThe carotid sinus is a small dilation of the internal carotid artery just above the bifurcation of the common carotid into external and internal carotid branches. The afferent nerve fibres from the carotid sinus form a distinct branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), the carotid sinus nerve.\\n\\nAfferent signals from both the aortic and carotid sinus baroreceptors are sent to the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) and they create a negative feedback loop that keeps arterial blood pressure within normal limits.\\n\\nBy stimulating these arterial baroreceptors in response to vascular distension, elevated arterial blood pressure can be reflexively brought back to normal levels by decreasing peripheral sympathetic outflow and activating peripheral parasympathetic outflow.\\n\\nThree subsequent functional processes must take place in order to convert a mechanical change in the arterial vasculature into an excitatory signal:\\n\\n1. Vascular distension and the deformation of arterial baroreceptor terminals\\n2. Mechanoelectrical transduction converts the mechanical deformation of arterial baroreceptor nerve terminals into baroreceptor membrane depolarization. Mechanosensitive ion channels such as the epithelial Na+ channels (ENaCs), acid-sensing ion channels (ASICs) and transient receptor potential (TRP) ion channels are responsible for mechanoelectrical transduction in arterial baroreceptor nerve terminals.\\n3. Translation of membrane depolarization into action potentials via voltage-gated ion channels (Na+, Ca2+ and K+).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b876c68a81","n":335,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following statements is/are true about skeletal muscle?\\n\\n1. Extracellular calcium is not important for contraction.\\n2. Calcium is mainly released from mitochondria.\\n3. Calcium is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\\n4. Voltage-gated calcium channel on sarcolemma has no role in excitation-contraction coupling.","options":["Only 2","Only 3","3, 4","1, 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Only 3\\n\\nThe true statement about skeletal muscle is that the calcium is released from sarcoplasmic reticulum.\\n\\nExcitation-contraction coupling refers to the process by which the membrane depolarization of the muscle fiber (excitation) leads to the contraction of the muscle.\\n\\nFor the maximum contraction of the skeletal muscle, the current has to\xa0penetrate deeply into the muscle fiber to the vicinity of the separate myofibrils. This penetration of\xa0current is achieved by\xa0transmission of action potentials along transverse tubules (T tubules). The\xa0T tubules penetrate\xa0through the muscle fiber, from one side of the fiber to the other.\\n\\nSarcoplasmic reticulum contains\xa0calcium ions in high concentration\xa0within its vesicular tubules. These ions are released from each vesicle when an action potential occurs in the adjacent T tubule. (Statement 3)\\n\\nThis action potential when it reaches the\xa0T tubule, it is sensed by the\xa0dihydropyridine receptors linked to calcium release channels. Actvation of this receptor leads to the opening of\xa0calcium release channels that releases the calcium ions into the sarcoplasm surrounding the myofibrils and causing contraction. (Statement 4)\\n\\nIn skeletal muscles, extracellular calcium supply partially contributes for the\xa0maintenance of skeletal muscle contraction.(Statement 1)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0913fa5272","n":336,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Which of the following types of pain stimulus is associated with the Carrageenan-induced experimental pain model?","options":["Inflammatory stimulus","Acute noxious stimulus","Deep boring stimulus","Neuropathic stimulus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inflammatory stimulus\\n\\nThe Carrageenan-induced pain model also called the Carrageenan-Induced Paw Edema model is a well-known model of acute inflammation.\\n\\nIt is employed to evaluate the anti-inflammatory properties of a variety of synthetic and natural substances.\xa0A non-antigenic phlogistic polysaccharide derived from red seaweed, carrageenan is frequently added to processed foods as a thickening, stabilising, gelling, and emulsifying agent. The activation of the complement system and inflammatory mediators is caused by the sulphated sugars found in carrageenan.\\n\\nCarrageenan is injected subcutaneously into the plantar surface of the rat paw to cause inflammation. This process results in temporary inflammation and acute swelling, which peaks 3 to 5 hours after injection and subsides in 24 hours.\\n\\nPostcapillary venules dilate due to the action of carrageenan, causing inflammatory cells and fluid to exude. During this process, a number of proinflammatory mediators are released. The cyclooxygenase pathway\'s activation is commonly associated with the carrageenan model. A biphasic curve represents the edema caused by carrageenan.\\n\\nPart of the initial phase of inflammation caused by carrageenan is attributed to injection trauma and the release of acute phase mediators, particularly bradykinin, histamine, and serotonin. The second phase of carrageenan-induced inflammation, which happens about three hours after carrageenan is added, is mostly caused by prostaglandins.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9ab5a4ab10","n":337,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2023","text":"Windkessel effect is shown by all except ____","options":["Radial artery","Renal Artery","Aorta","Carotid artery"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Radial artery\\n\\nWindkessel effect is shown by all\xa0except the radial artery.\\n\\nThe windkessel effect is seen in the large elastic arteries like the\xa0aorta, carotid, and renal artery. The elasticity or windkessel effect is the exclusive property of large and elastic vessels. These vessels when stretched undergo elastic recoil.\\n\\nThe aorta is stretched during systole. This expansion causes temporary storage of blood, allowing more blood to enter the aorta. This buffer stock of blood is released during diastole, to maintain organ perfusion even during diastole.\\n\\nThe image given below shows the windkessel effect seen in the aorta.\\n\\nSeveral methods can be used to evaluate the elastic properties of the aortic Windkessel in living humans. One simple approach is to measure the pulse wave velocity along the aorta, where a higher velocity indicates increased stiffness and elastic resistance. Alternative methods involve assessing the ratio between pulse pressure and changes in aortic volume (delta P/delta V). These methods can be noninvasively performed using ultrasound or tomographic techniques.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f3f928f100","n":338,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"A 70-year-old man with a BP of 170/100mmHg. What is the reason for the increased pulse pressure in this patient?","options":["Decreased arterial compliance","Increased cardiac output","Increased sympathetic outflow","Increased myocardial contractility"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Decreased arterial compliance\\n\\nPulse pressure increases with age due to decreased arterial wall compliance.\\n\\nDiastolic blood pressure falls after 60 years of age due to increased arterial stiffness and decreased compliance, often associated with arteriosclerosis. As the left ventricle contracts against stiffer, less compliant arteries, systolic blood pressure also increases.\\n\\nAs a result of these two changes, pulse pressure increases (↑PP = ↑SBP - ↓DBP)\\n\\nThe factors that affect the pulse pressure:\\n\\n• the stroke volume output of the heart\\n• the compliance or total distensibility of the arterial walls\\n• the character of ejection from the heart during systole (less important)\\n\\nPulse pressure = Stroke volume/ arterial compliance\\n\\nWidened pulse pressure (> 40 mm Hg) occurs with several diseases such as:\\n\\n• Aortic regurgitation\\n• Aortic sclerosis\\n• Severe iron deficiency anaemia\\n• Arteriosclerosis\\n• Hyperthyroidism\\n\\nIn the majority of these cases, systolic pressures increase while diastolic pressures remain near normal.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-068e81f34e","n":339,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Choose the correct options from the given statements of the enteric nervous system-\\n\\n1. Vagus is the only afferent from the gut\\n2. Functions independent of the Brain and Spinal Cord\\n3. Myenteric plexus in the lamina propria\\n4. Exclusively under the influence of nerves\\n5. Only parasympathetic connects the enteric to the CNS","options":["1,2,3","1,4","1,3","2"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 2\\n\\nThe enteric nervous system functions independently of the brain and spinal Cord. (Statement 2)\\n\\nStatement 2: Functions independent of brain and spinal cord: The enteric nervous system is often referred to as the \\"second brain\\" because it can function independently of the central nervous system (CNS). It has its own reflex circuits and can control gut motility, secretion, and blood flow without input from the brain or spinal cord, although it can be influenced by them.\\n\\nStatement 1: The vagus is the only afferent from the gut: While the vagus nerve does provide significant afferent (sensory) input from the gut, other nerves, such as those from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, also contribute to afferent pathways. Therefore, the vagus is not the only afferent nerve.\\n\\nStatement 3: Myenteric plexus in the lamina propria: The myenteric plexus is located between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the gut, not in the lamina propria (which is part of the mucosal layer of the gut).\\n\\nStatement 4: Exclusively under the influence of nerves: The enteric nervous system is influenced by nerves, but it also responds to hormonal and immune system signals. Therefore, it is not exclusively under neural influence.\\n\\nStatement 5: Only parasympathetic connects enteric to CNS: Both parasympathetic and sympathetic connect the enteric nervous system to the central nervous system. Not just the parasympathetic.\\n\\nThe enteric nervous system consists of submucosal and myenteric plexi. This Plexi can function independently and is often referred to as the ‘little brain’. The enteric nervous system regulates gastrointestinal (GI) function and is connected to the central nervous system by parasympathetic and sympathetic fibers.\\n\\nThe myenteric (Auerbach’s) plexus is present between the longitudinal and circular muscle layers. It innervates both these layers along the entire length of the GI tract. It primarily controls GI motility. On stimulation, it has the following effects:\\n\\n• Increased tone of the GI wall\\n• Increased intensity and rate of rhythmic contractions\\n• Increased velocity of excitatory wave conduction\\n\\nSome of the myenteric plexus neurons primarily release vasoactive inhibitory peptide (VIP), which causes the relaxation of the GI sphincters.\\n\\nThe submucosal (Meissner’s) plexus is found between the mucosa and the circular muscle layers. It innervates the GI glands, endocrine cells, and submucosal blood vessels. It primarily controls GI secretions, absorption, and local blood flow.\\n\\nThe parasympathetic innervation of the plexi is from the vagus and pelvic nerves. They release either acetylcholine or substance P. It generally increases GI motility and secretion.\\n\\nThe sympathetic innervation is from the celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric sympathetic ganglia. It generally inhibits GI motility and stimulates sphincteric contraction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8891df9a2b","n":340,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"EOG, EEG, and EMG of a patient are given respectively from above downwards. Which stage of sleep does the marked area represent?\\n\\nEOG- Electroocoulography\\n\\nEEG- Electroencephalography\\n\\nEMG- Electromyography","options":["NREM I sleep","NREM II sleep","REM sleep","NREM III sleep"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. REM sleep\\n\\nREM sleep is represented by the marked area in the given polysomnography.\\n\\nThe three zones in the given image represent EOG, EEG, and EMG, respectively. In the marked area,\\n\\n• EOG shows Positive activity\\n• EEG shows Mixed waves\\n• EMG shows Minimal activity\\n\\nThis is characteristic of REM sleep.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/743893ef8802f9424249.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-ddb60f9764","n":341,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Erythropoietin is produced by:\\n\\n1. Kidney\\n2. Liver\\n3. Spleen\\n4. Lungs","options":["1 only","2 only","1,2","1,2,3,4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1,2\\n\\nErythropoietin is produced mainly by kidney and liver.\\n\\nIn adults, approximately 85% of erythropoietin is produced by the kidneys, while 15% is synthesized by the liver. Both of these organs possess the mRNA necessary for erythropoietin production. Although erythropoietin can be found in the spleen and salivary glands, these tissues lack the mRNA required for its synthesis, indicating that they do not produce the hormone.\\n\\nErythropoietin is secreted by interstitial cells in the peri-tubular capillary bed of the kidneys and by perivenous hepatocytes in the liver. It is also generated in the brain, where it provides a safeguarding effect against excitotoxic harm caused by hypoxia; and in the uterus and oviducts, where it is stimulated by estrogen and seems to facilitate estrogen-driven angiogenesis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6e13326aba","n":342,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Identify the correct option","options":["1-Z line, 2-A band, 3-H zone, 4-I band","1-A band, 2-Z line, 3-H zone, 4-I band","1-T tubule, 2-I band, 3-H zone, 4-A band","1-T tubule, 2- A band, 3-I band, 4-H zone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-Z line, 2-A band, 3-H zone, 4-I band\\n\\nThe correct marking is 1-Z line, 2-A band, 3-H zone, 4-I band.\\n\\nH zone or H band denotes the less refractile region in the centre of A band.\\n\\n• A band – Anisotropic / Dark band: Formed by thick filaments - Myosin\\n• I band – Isotropic / Light band: Formed by thin filaments – Actin, Troponin, Tropomyosin\\n• M line – Seen in the centre of H band: Due to central bulge of A band\\n• Z line – Seen in the centre of each I band: Narrow line with high refractile material appearing dark.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/048e8f3a015005e85509.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-25eb596520","n":343,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Identify the hormones marked in the image given","options":["A-Somatostatin, B-GHRH, C-GH, D-IGF-1","A-GHRH, B-GH, C-IGF-1, D-Somatostatin","A-GHRH, B-Somatostatin, C-GH, D-IGF-1","A-GH, B-GHRH, C-IGF-1, D-Somatostatin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. A-Somatostatin, B-GHRH, C-GH, D-IGF-1\\n\\nIn the given image, A: Somatostatin, B: GHRH, C: GH, D: IGF-1\\n\\n• Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH)\xa0secreted from the hypothalamus acts on the anterior pituitary to release growth hormone.\\n• Growth hormone (GH) increases the circulating levels of IGF -1 which exerts its effect on the tissues.\\n• Ghrelin promotes the secretion of growth hormone from the anterior pituitary.\\n• Somatostatin secreted from the hypothalamus is an inhibitory effect on the GH secretion from the anterior pituitary.\\n• Similarly, IGF -1 exerts negative feedback on GH secretion and positive feedback on somatostatin secretion, which in turn inhibits GH secretion.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/3aff30c2dc2320942cc7.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-10d6d13e7a","n":344,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Identify the marked structure.","options":["Intercalated disc","Connective tissue","Cytoskeletal condensation","Nerve fibre"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Intercalated disc\\n\\nThe given section shows cardiac muscle and the marked structure is an intercalated disc.\\n\\nIntercalated discs are specialized structures unique to cardiac muscle tissue. They are critical for the coordinated contraction of cardiac muscle cells. They are found at the junctions between adjacent cardiac muscle cells. They appear as dark, zigzagging lines under a microscope. They facilitate the rhythmic and coordinated contraction of the heart by ensuring efficient electrical and mechanical connections.\\n\\nOn microscopy, cardiac muscle can be identified by:\\n\\n• Presence of striations similar to the skeletal muscle.\\n• Presence of single and centrally placed nucleus. A perinuclear halo may be seen surrounding the nucleus.\\n• The junctions between adjoining cardiac myocytes are seen as dark staining transverse lines running across the muscle fibre. These lines are called intercalated discs.\\n• Cells within one fibre branch and join the cells in adjacent fibres.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption B: Connective Tissue: Found surrounding the cardiac muscle cells, providing structural support, but not at the cell junctions.\\n\\nOption C: Cytoskeletal condensation: Refers to cytoskeletal organization within a cell and is not visible as distinct structures in cardiac muscle histology.\\n\\nOption D: Nerve Fibre: Present in cardiac tissue to innervate the heart but appears as separate structures and not within cardiac muscle fibers.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/84fa474a80e4a48d1790.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-19bbac05df","n":345,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Match the following hormones with their receptors.\\n\\nHormone\\nReceptor\\n\\nA.Insulin\\n1. JAK/STAT\\n\\nB.Acetylcholine\\n2. Nuclear Receptor\\n\\nC.Growth Hormone\\n3. Tyrosine Kinase\\n\\nD.Thyroxine\\n4. Ligand gated","options":["A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4","A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4","A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2\\n\\nThe correct answer is A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2.\\n\\nHormone\\nReceptor\\n\\nA.Insulin\\n3. Tyrosine Kinase\\n\\nB.Acetylcholine\\n4. Ligand gated\\n\\nC.Growth Hormone\\n1. JAK/STAT\\n\\nD.Thyroxine\\n2. Nuclear Receptor\\n\\nInsulin binds to a receptor that has intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity. Upon activation, the receptor phosphorylates itself and downstream signaling proteins. (A-3)\\n\\nAcetylcholine acts on nicotinic receptors, which are ligand-gated ion channels. Binding of acetylcholine opens the channel, allowing ion flow (e.g., Na⁺ or K⁺). (B-4)\\n\\nGrowth hormone binds to its receptor, which lacks intrinsic kinase activity but recruits Janus Kinase (JAK) to phosphorylate downstream proteins and activate the STAT (Signal Transducer and Activator of Transcription) pathway. (C-1)\\n\\nThyroxine (and its active form, T3) binds to intracellular nuclear receptors. These hormone-receptor complexes act as transcription factors, regulating gene expression directly in the nucleus. (D-2)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6d55b83597","n":346,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Mechanical nociceptors and their response to a probe with a blunt object, pinprick, and toothed forceps with serrations are recorded as an output of a force transducer coupled to the stimulator. What could be inferred from this?","options":["There are no responses from the nociceptors when stimulated by blunt forceps","Pinching with toothed forceps causes stimulation of mechano-nociceptors, but no pain","Pricking with a needle does not stimulate nociceptors","Sharp pain sensation is carried by C fibres"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. There are no responses from the nociceptors when stimulated by blunt forceps\\n\\nThere are no responses from the nociceptors when stimulated by blunt forceps.\\n\\nMechanical nociceptors typically respond to noxious mechanical stimuli, such as sharp pressure or intense stretch. A blunt object, such as blunt forceps, may not provide a strong enough stimulus to activate these nociceptors, leading to no response.\\n\\nThe sharp painful sensation felt immediately upon injury is known as fast pain. The poorly localized dull aching pain is characteristic of slow pain. The nerve fibers carrying fast pain and slow pain are Aδ and C respectively.\\n\\nNociceptors are innervated by both Aδ and C fibers. Aδ fibers are myelinated and larger in diameter with a faster conduction velocity. C fibers are unmyelinated and conduct impulses at a slower rate. When nociceptors are activated by a noxious stimulus, activated Aδ fibers release glutamate and mediate fast pain. It is a rapid response that is responsible for localizing the noxious stimulus.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption B: Pinching with toothed forceps causes stimulation of mechanonociceptors, but no pain: Pinching with toothed forceps typically applies more localized pressure, which can activate mechanical nociceptors. However, it may still cause a painful sensation, not just a mechanical response. It is more likely that pain is perceived, even if the response is primarily mechanical.\\n\\nOption C: Pricking with a needle does not stimulate nociceptors: A needle prick is a sharp stimulus that activates nociceptors, particularly mechanical nociceptors in response to tissue damage.\\n\\nOption D: Sharp pain sensation is carried by C fibers: Sharp pain (due to a needle prick) is typically carried by A-delta fibers, which transmit fast, localized pain sensations. C fibers are responsible for carrying dull, burning pain that occurs later after the initial sharp pain.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/64cb8f7c3177b6e69d8d.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-099cbc7a09","n":347,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Mitochondrial sheaths are found in which part of the sperm?","options":["Tail","Middle piece","Principal piece","Connecting piece"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Middle piece\\n\\nMitochondrial sheaths are found in the middle piece of the sperm.\\n\\nThe middle piece is the segment of the sperm located just behind the head and neck.\\n\\nIt contains a spiral arrangement of mitochondria, forming the mitochondrial sheath around the central axoneme.\\n\\nThese mitochondria generate ATP, which provides the energy required for the flagellar movement of the sperm, enabling motility.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Tail: Comprises the middle piece, principal piece, and end piece and is primarily responsible for motility.\\n\\nOption C: Principle piece: Longest part of the tail which contains fibrous sheaths around the axoneme but lacks mitochondria.\\n\\nOption D: Connecting piece: Links the head of the sperm to the tail (neck region). Does not contain mitochondrial sheaths.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9e27533dd1","n":348,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Not seen in decompression sickness","options":["Vision loss","Hearing loss","Hemodilution","Coagulopathy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Hemodilution\\n\\nHemodilution is not seen in decompression sickness. Instead, hemoconcentration is seen in decompression sickness.\\n\\nHemodilution refers to the dilution of blood, usually due to excessive fluid intake or other medical conditions, but it is not a feature of decompression sickness.\\n\\nDecompression sickness (DCS) is a condition that occurs when a person ascends too quickly after being exposed to high-pressure environments (such as deep-sea diving), causing nitrogen bubbles to form in the bloodstream and tissues.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: Vision changes or loss can occur in DCS due to gas bubbles affecting the optic nerve or retinal circulation, leading to temporary or permanent vision impairment.\\n\\nOption B: Gas bubbles in DCS can also affect the auditory system, leading to hearing loss or tinnitus, a common feature of the condition.\\n\\nOption D: Decompression sickness can lead to coagulopathy, which refers to abnormal blood clotting due to the formation of nitrogen bubbles that disrupt the vascular system, potentially causing bleeding or clotting disorders.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d0982dd850","n":349,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors are most sensitive to?","options":["Hypercapnia","Hypoxia","Acidosis","Alkalosis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hypoxia\\n\\nPeripheral chemoreceptors are most sensitive to hypoxia.\\n\\nPeripheral Chemoreceptors are located in the carotid bodies (at the bifurcation of the common carotid arteries) and aortic bodies (near the aortic arch). They are sensitive to changes in pO2, pCO2, and pH of the blood. In response to changes in these parameters, they send impulses to the medullary respiratory centers and modify the duration and frequency of respiratory cycles.\\n\\nPeripheral chemoreceptors are most sensitive to a drop in partial pressure of oxygen (PaO₂) in arterial blood.\\n\\nMechanism:\\n\\nType I glomus cells in the carotid and aortic bodies have O2-sensitive K+ channels, which are inactivated in response to hypoxia. This reduces the K+ efflux, depolarizing the cell and causing Ca2+ inﬂux, via L-type voltage-gated calcium channels. The Ca2+ influx triggers action potentials and consequent excitation of the afferent nerve endings in the peripheral chemoreceptors.\\n\\nHypoxia (PaO₂ < 60 mmHg) strongly stimulates these receptors, leading to an increase in respiratory rate and depth to improve oxygenation.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nPeripheral chemoreceptors also respond to hypercapnia (Option A) and acidosis (Option C). However, these are primarily detected by central chemoreceptors, located in the medulla, which monitor CO₂ levels in cerebrospinal fluid.\\n\\nAlkalosis (Option D) causes a weaker response from peripheral chemoreceptors compared to hypoxia.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3983de8ab7","n":350,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Physiological dead space :","options":["Can be measured by spirometry","Is lesser than anatomical dead space","Represents inadequately ventilated alveoli","Represents inadequately perfused alveoli"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Represents inadequately perfused alveoli\\n\\nPhysiological dead space represents inadequately perfused alveoli.\\n\\nPhysiological dead space is the part of the respiratory system where no gas exchange occurs. It includes:\\n\\n1. Anatomic dead space: Air in the conducting airways (nose, trachea, bronchi) that doesn\'t reach the alveoli.\\n2. Alveolar dead space: Air in alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused by blood.\\n\\nTogether, it represents the total volume of air that doesn\'t participate in oxygen and carbon dioxide exchange.(Option B)\\n\\nAnatomical dead space refers to the volume of all the space of the respiratory system other than the alveoli that do not participate in gas exchange. The anatomical dead space is not altered with a change in ventilation.\\n\\nIn healthy individuals:\\n\\n• Anatomical dead space = 150 mL.\\n• Alveolar dead space is usually zero (increased in diseased conditions).\\n\\nAlveolar Dead space represents inadequately perfused alveoli (wasted ventilation). (Option C)\\n\\nIn normal individuals, physiological dead space remains unaltered and is equal to the anatomical dead space (volume of all the space of the respiratory system other than the alveoli that do not participate in gas exchange).\\n\\nDead space cannot be measured using spirometry. (Option A)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c6ed782abf","n":351,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"The O2 saturation with respect to PO2 is shown by the curve marked as 5. What happens to the oxygen Hb dissociation curve on transfusion of stored RBCs?","options":["2","3","4","1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 2\\n\\nWhen stored RBCs are transfused, the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve typically shifts to the left but still retains its sigmoid shape as represented by the curve marked as 2.\\n\\nThis is due to reduced levels of 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) in stored red blood cells. 2,3-BPG is a metabolite in RBCs that binds to hemoglobin and reduces its affinity for oxygen.\\n\\nDuring storage, metabolic processes in RBCs slow down, leading to a depletion of 2,3-BPG. Lower 2,3-BPG levels make hemoglobin hold onto oxygen more tightly, impairing oxygen delivery to tissues. Hence, this increases hemoglobin\'s affinity for oxygen, shifting the curve to the left.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption B and C: show a right shift to the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve.↑ in 2,3-BPG\xa0seen in increased anaerobic glycolysis leads to the right shift of the curve. Storage of blood causes depletion of 2,3-BPG, hence leading to left shift instead of right.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/a126a9091ad5a7c07157.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-d51f5a1b5e","n":352,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"What causes decrease in GFR?","options":["Decrease in glomerular coefficient","Decrease in glomerular oncotic pressure","Increase in glomerular hydrostatic pressure","Decrease of Bowman\'s hydrostatic pressure"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Decrease in glomerular coefficient\\n\\nDecrease in glomerular coefficient causes decrease in GFR.\\n\\nThe glomerular filtration coefficient (Kf) is a measure of how efficiently the glomerulus filters blood into the kidney tubules. It depends on two key factors:\\n\\n• Surface Area for Filtration: Greater surface area means higher filtration.\\n• Permeability of the Glomerular Membrane: Higher permeability leads to greater filtration.\\n\\nGlomerular filtration rate (GFR) is the volume of fluid filtered from the renal glomerular capillaries into the Bowman\'s capsule per unit time.\\n\\nThe GFR is determined by the sum of the hydrostatic and colloid osmotic forces across the glomerular membrane, which gives the net filtration pressure. It is dependent on Glomerular Kf (glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient) also.\\n\\nMathematically, it can be expressed as follows:\\n\\nGFR= Kf [PG + πB -PB- πG]\\n\\nForces favoring Filtration\\n\\n• PG: Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (Option C)\\n• πB: Bowman’s capsule oncotic pressure\\n\\nForces opposing Filtration\\n\\n• PB: Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure (Option D)\\n• πG: Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure (Option B)\\n\\nThe formula for glomerular filtration rate (GFR) incorporates Kf:\\n\\n• A high Kf means efficient filtration (e.g., in healthy kidneys)-indicates high GFR\\n• A low Kf {occurs when the surface area is reduced (e.g., due to glomerular damage) or the membrane becomes less permeable (e.g., in diabetic nephropathy)}- indicates low GFR.\\n\\nKf is essential for maintaining proper kidney function, as it directly affects how well the kidneys filter blood to form urine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-aa940bc689","n":353,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"What contributes to the latent period of muscle twitch?\\n\\n1. Neuromuscular transmission\\n2. Excitation contraction coupling\\n3. Reuptake of calcium back into sarcoplasmic reticulum\\n4. Transmission of impulse from nerve","options":["1,2,4","1,2,3","3,4","2,3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1,2,4\\n\\nNeuromuscular transmission, Excitation contraction coupling and transmission impulse from nerve coupling contribute to latent period of muscle twitch.\\n\\nThe latent period of a muscle twitch is the brief delay between the application of a stimulus (such as a nerve impulse) and the onset of muscle contraction. During this period, several physiological processes occur, but neuromuscular transmission and excitation-contraction coupling primarily contribute to this delay.\\n\\nNeuromuscular transmission: The action potential travels down the motor neuron to the neuromuscular junction. Acetylcholine (ACh) is released into the synaptic cleft and binds to receptors on the muscle fiber\'s sarcolemma, triggering an action potential in the muscle fiber. (Statement 1)\\n\\nExcitation-contraction coupling: The action potential propagates along the sarcolemma and into the transverse (T) tubules. This triggers the release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the cytosol. Calcium binds to troponin, initiating the interaction of actin and myosin filaments. (Statement 2)\\n\\nReuptake of calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum: This occurs during the relaxation phase, not the latent period. (Statement 3)\\n\\nTransmission of impulse from nerve: This is a part of neuromuscular transmission and is involved in the latent period. (Statement 4)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-141329eccd","n":354,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which among the following instruments is not used in the Sahli\'s method of hemoglobin estimation?","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. D\\n\\nOption D is RBC pipette, it is not used in Sahli\'s method of hemoglobin estimation. The instruments used are comparator with brown glass standard(Option A), graduated hemoglobin tube(Option B), Sahli\'s pipette (Option C)and stirrer.\\n\\nSahli\'s method of estimation of hemoglobin is based on colorimetric measurement of the intensity of color developed on the addition of dilute hydrochloric acid (N/10 HCl) reagent to the blood.\\n\\nIn Sahli\'s method, hemoglobin is converted to acid hematin with the action of dilute hydrochloric acid (N/10 HCl). The brown color of the acid hematin is matched to its intensity with a standard brown glass comparator in a visual colorimeter called Sahli’s colorimeter. It can be used to estimate oxyHb and reduced Hb.\\n\\nSahli\'s method is a simple bedside test and is cost-effective. However, it has some disadvantages, which include visual errors, the inability to measure other forms of hemoglobin like carboxyhemoglobin, methemoglobin, and sulfhemoglobin, fading of the comparator over time, and the color of acid hematin fading quickly.\\n\\nThe visual error can be reduced by taking the average of three readings; first, when the color is slightly darker than the standard; second, when the color exactly matches the standard; and third, when the color is slightly lighter than the standard.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/ad62d0746d8b3f15d9c2.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-9bd5b2fdec","n":355,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which blood component is not separated by centrifugation in the blood bank?","options":["Red blood cells","Platelets","Cryoprecipitate","Albumin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Albumin\\n\\nAlbumin is not separated by centrifugation in the blood bank.\\n\\nIn blood banks, centrifugation is used to separate the main components of blood into distinct layers for storage and transfusion purposes. These components include:\\n\\n1. Red Blood Cells (RBCs): Separated by centrifugation and stored for patients with anemia or significant blood loss. (Option A)\\n2. Platelets: Isolated through centrifugation of plasma-rich blood for use in patients with bleeding disorders. (Option B)\\n3. Cryoprecipitate: Derived from plasma by controlled freezing and thawing, which allows the collection of clotting factors (e.g., Factor VIII, fibrinogen, von Willebrand factor). (Option C)\\n\\nAlbumin is a plasma protein that is extracted through a process called fractionation, not simple centrifugation. This involves the use of chemical, temperature, and pH adjustments to isolate albumin from plasma.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-cca630bd1c","n":356,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which hormones are produced from the zones labelled in the image given?","options":["A-cortisol, B-aldosterone, C-androgens, D-epinephrine","A-epinephrine, B-cortisol, C-aldosterone, D-androgens","A-aldosterone, B-epinephrine, C-androgens, D-cortisol","A-aldosterone, B-cortisol, C-androgens, D-epinephrine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. A-aldosterone, B-cortisol, C-androgens, D-epinephrine\\n\\nOption D is the correct option. This is a section through an adrenal gland showing both the medulla and the zones of the cortex. Following are the zones of the cortex and medulla, and the hormones they secrete.\\n\\nA – Zona Glomerulosa \xa0– secretes mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)\\n\\nB – Zona Fasciculata \xa0– secretes glucocorticoids (cortisol, corticosterone)\\n\\nC – Zona Reticularis – \xa0secretes androgens (dehydroepiandrosterone)\\n\\nD – Adrenal Medulla – Produces Catecholamines: Norepinephrine, Epinephrine, and Dopamine\\n\\nOn histological examination, the different layers are identified as:\\n\\n• Outer zona glomerulosa - small, compact cells\\n• Central zona fasciculata - larger lipid-rich cells arranged in radial columns\\n• Inner zona reticularis - pigmented, compact cells\\n• Adrenal medulla - thin layer of large chromaffin cells","img":false,"images":["/bimg/f234d74839ac7fdbf5a3.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-2e28639046","n":357,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which is the most potent naturally occurring estrogen?","options":["Estradiol","Estriol","Estrone","Conjugated Estrogen"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Estradiol\\n\\nEstradiol is the most potent naturally occurring estrogen.\\n\\nClassic estrogens are C18 steroid compounds containing a phenolic ring. Only three estrogens are present in significant quantities in the plasma of the human female—β-estradiol, estrone, and estriol.\\n\\nThe principal estrogen secreted by the ovaries is β-estradiol.\\n\\nSmall amounts of estrone are also secreted, but most of this is formed in peripheral tissues from androgens secreted by the adrenal cortices and by ovarian thecal cells.\\n\\nEstriol is a weak estrogen; it is an oxidative product derived from both estradiol and estrone, with the conversion occurring mainly in the liver.\\n\\nThe estrogenic potency of β-estradiol is 12 times that of estrone and 80 times that of estriol. Hence, estradiol is the most potent natural form of estrogen.\\n\\nIn the liver, estradiol, estrone, and estriol are converted to inactive glucuronide and sulfate conjugates. All these compounds, along with other metabolites, are excreted in the urine.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7269269def","n":358,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which neurotransmitter is secreted by the SA node in response to increased blood pressure?","options":["Acetylcholine","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Dopamine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Acetylcholine\\n\\nAcetylcholine is released in response to increased blood pressure.\\n\\nIn response to raised BP, there is inhibition of sympathetic system and activation of parasympathetic system. Acetylcholine is the universal neurotransmitter of PNS.\\n\\nBaroreceptors are stretch receptors located in the heart and the adventitia of the blood vessels. They are stimulated by the stretching of the walls of the blood vessels when there is an increased volume or blood pressure in the vessel. They are important in the immediate management of blood pressure. They are of two types: carotid sinus and aortic arch receptors (high-pressure regions) and cardiopulmonary receptors (low-pressure regions).\\n\\nAn increase in blood pressure stimulates the baroreceptors. These signals are transmitted through small Hering’s nerves to the glossopharyngeal nerves and then to the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) in the medulla. These signals release glutamate and activate the caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM). This in turn inhibits the rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM) considered the vasoconstrictor center for the brain and the heart.\\n\\nSimultaneously, this pathway also excites the vagal parasympathetic center. The stimulation of the vagus results in a slowing of impulse generation in the SA node and a slowing of conduction velocity in the AV node.\\n\\nThese neural changes produce vasodilation, venodilation, hypotension, bradycardia, and a decrease in cardiac output.\\n\\n• NTS: Nucleus Tractus Solitarius\\n• CVLM: Caudal Ventrolateral Medulla\\n• RVLM : Rostral Ventrolateral Medulla (Vasomotor center)\\n• CVC : Cardiovagal center (Cardioinhibitory center)\\n• IML: intermediolateral nucleus","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3a132a7586","n":359,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of the following are properties of ventricular cardiac muscle?\\n\\n1. All or none phenomenon\\n2. Length tension relationship\\n3. Long refractory period\\n4. Tetanus","options":["1,2","1,2,3","1,2,4","1,2,3,4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 1,2,3\\n\\nAll or none phenomenon, length tension relationship and long refractory period applies to cardiac muscles. Cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized due to long refractory period.\\n\\nIn cardiac myocytes, depolarization proceeds rapidly and an overshoot of the zero potential is present. This is followed by a plateau before the membrane potential reaches the RMP (-90mV). This plateau phase lasts for 200 msec or more. Repolarization is therefore not complete until contraction is 50% completed.\\n\\nPhases of cardiac action potential\\n\\n• 0→ Depolarisation and overshoot-Due to opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels\\n• 1→ Initial rapid repolarization-Due to closure of Na+ channels and opening up of K+ channels\\n• 2→ Plateau phase - Due to slow but prolonged opening of Ca2+ channels\\n• 3→ Late rapid repolarization\\n• 4→ Baseline (RMP)\\n\\nPhases 3 and 4 are due closure of Ca2+ channels and a slow, delayed increase of K+ efflux through K+ channels.\\n\\nDuring phases 0 to 2 and about half of phase 3 of cardiac action potential, cardiac muscle cannot be excited again, that is, it is in its absolute refractory period. It remains relatively refractory until phase 4. Thus, cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6c5301e1ff","n":360,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of the following does not form the inner component of photoreceptors?","options":["Mitochondria","Membranous disc","Golgi apparatus","Endoplasmic reticulum"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Membranous disc\\n\\nAmong the given options, the membranous disc does not form the inner component of photoreceptors but instead forms the outer component.\\n\\nThe other three options i.e., mitochondria, Golgi apparatus, and endoplasmic reticulum form the inner component of photoreceptors.\\n\\nPhotoreceptors (rods and cones) are specialized neurons in the retina with distinct structural components:\\n\\n• Outer segment: Contains membranous discs that have photopigments (like rhodopsin in rods and photopsins in cones) essential for light detection.\\n\\n• Inner segment: Contains cellular organelles responsible for metabolism and protein synthesis, such as mitochondria, Golgi apparatus, and endoplasmic reticulum.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d53be4703d","n":361,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of the following does not stimulate catecholamine synthesis?","options":["Hyperglycemia","Smoking","Standing posture","Exercise"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Hyperglycemia\\n\\nHyperglycemia does not stimulate catecholamine synthesis. Instead, hypoglycemia stimulates catecholamine synthesis.\\n\\nCatecholamine synthesis is primarily stimulated by various physiological stressors and factors that activate the sympathetic nervous system, such as physical exertion(exercise), changes in posture, or certain external stimuli like smoking.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Smoking stimulates catecholamine release through the activation of the sympathetic nervous system, which increases catecholamines like adrenaline and norepinephrine.\\n\\nOption C: When a person stands up, it can lead to orthostatic hypotension, which triggers the release of catecholamines to help increase blood pressure and maintain adequate perfusion.\\n\\nOption D: Exercise stimulates the sympathetic nervous system and increases catecholamine production to support increased heart rate, blood pressure, and metabolic demands.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-46fae7e284","n":362,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of the following may lead to a decrease in GFR?","options":["Increase in renal blood flow","Decrease in glomerular oncotic pressure","Increase in glomerular hydrostatic pressure","Decrease in bowman capsule oncotic pressure"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Decrease in bowman capsule oncotic pressure\\n\\nA decrease in bowman\'s capsule oncotic pressure would lead to a decrease in GFR.\\n\\nGlomerular filtration rate (GFR) is the volume of fluid filtered from the renal glomerular capillaries into the Bowman\'s capsule per unit time.\\n\\nThe GFR is determined by sum of the hydrostatic and colloid osmotic forces across the glomerular membrane, which gives the net filtration pressure. It is dependant on Glomerular Kf (glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient) also.\\n\\nMathematically, it can be expressed as follows:\\n\\nGFR= Kf [PG + πB -PB- πG]\\n\\nForces favoring Filtration\\n\\n• PG: Glomerular hydrostatic pressure\\n• πB: Bowman’s capsule oncotic pressure\\n\\nForces opposing Filtration\\n\\n• PB: Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure\\n• πG: Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure\\n\\nFactors that can increase the GFR are as follows:\\n\\n• Increase in glomerular capillary filtration coefficient Kf: This coefficient depends on the hydraulic conductivity and surface area of the glomerular capillaries.\\n• Decrease in Bowman\'s capsule hydrostatic pressure: Conditions with urinary tract obstruction can lead to an increase in this pressure and a fall in GFR.\\n• Decrease in glomerular colloid osmotic pressure: Accumulation of plasma proteins that are not filtered from the blood contributes to this pressure and an increase reduces the further filtration and GFR. It is directly proportional to the following factors:Arterial plasma colloid osmotic pressure\\n• Filtration fraction","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-99cac95dc7","n":363,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of the following ovarian structures is responsible for the following finding?","options":["Secondary follicle","Theca interna","Granulosa cell","Corpus luteum"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Corpus luteum\\n\\nThe ovarian structure responsible for the following finding is the corpus luteum.\\n\\nThe histological image shows a corkscrew appearance of the glands, and this is characteristic of the secretory phase of the endometrium in the menstrual cycle. This phase is stimulated by progesterone, which is secreted by the corpus luteum.\\n\\nEndometrial Biopsy of the various stages of the ovarian cycle:\\n\\n1. Proliferative phase: The glands are simple, straight, and tubular glands. The lining of the glands shows pseudostratified columnar epithelium with mitotic figures, indicating active cell proliferation. The stroma is dense and compact, with numerous cells undergoing mitosis.\\n\\n2. Early secretory phase: The initially straight glands begin to coil as the endometrium transitions into the secretory phase. Subnuclear Vacuolation is seen in the epithelial cells of the endometrial glands, caused by the accumulation of glycogen at the base of the cells.\\n3. Late secretory phase: The glands become maximally tortuous and assume a distinctive \\"saw-tooth\\" appearance due to extreme coiling. They are filled with secretions rich in glycogen and glycoproteins, essential for supporting a potential embryo. The stroma is edematous.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption A: Secondary follicle\\n\\nThe secondary follicle is a more advanced stage of follicular development following the primary follicle. It contains a \xa0fluid-filled cavity (antrum) surrounded by two layers: theca interna and theca externa.\\n\\nOption B and C: Theca interna and Granulosa cell\\n\\nThe theca interna is a layer of stromal cells just outside the basal lamina of the granulosa cells. They are composed of cuboidal endocrine cells with lipid droplets and abundant smooth endoplasmic reticulum, reflecting its role in steroidogenesis.\\n\\nGranulosa cells line the interior of the follicle. Cells are organized in layers, initially single-layered in primary follicles and multilayered in secondary follicles.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/8e72c653d2db723304e1.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-0079d1191f","n":364,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding ABO blood grouping?","options":["Antibodies are naturally present since birth","The antigens are carbohydrate in nature","The antibodies are predominantly IgM","Antibodies are present only if antigens are absent"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Antibodies are naturally present since birth\\n\\nAntibodies are not present since birth. At 2 to 8 months after birth, an infant begins to produce agglutinins (antibodies).\\n\\nIn the absence of type A agglutinogen(antigen) in an individual\'s RBCs, antibodies called anti-A agglutinins form in the plasma. Similarly, when type B agglutinogen is lacking in the RBCs, antibodies referred to as anti-B agglutinins develop in the plasma. Type A blood carries type A agglutinogens and anti-B agglutinins, while type B blood contains type B agglutinogens and anti-A agglutinins. Type AB blood possesses both A and B agglutinogens but no agglutinins. On the other hand, type O blood, which lacks agglutinogens, does have both anti-A and anti-B agglutinins.\\n\\nThe A and B antigens are complex oligosaccharides that differ in their terminal sugar. The agglutinins are gamma globulins produced by the bone marrow and lymph gland cells. Most of them are IgM and IgG immunoglobulin molecules.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f74a668f52","n":365,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which of these drug transport systems do not require ATP?\\n\\n1. Simple diffusion\\n2. Paracellular\\n3. Facilitated diffusion\\n4. Antiport (Na/K ATPase)\\n5. Symport","options":["1,2,3","2,3,4","3,4,5","1,2,5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1,2,3\\n\\nParacellular transport, Simple Diffusion and Facilitated diffusion do not require ATP as they are all passive transport mechanisms.\\n\\n1: Simple diffusion : Simple diffusion is a passive transport mechanism where molecules (water, carbon dioxide, nitrogen) move from an area of high concentration to an area of low concentration and is not carrier mediated.\\n\\n2:\xa0Paracellular : Paracellular transport involves movement of molecules through tight junctions between cells without the need for energy (ATP).\\n\\n3: Facilitated diffusion : This also occurs along a concentration gradient, but involves carrier proteins to help move the drug across the membrane. No ATP is used because the process is still passive (driven by the concentration gradient).\\n\\n4: Antiport (Na/K ATPase): Antiport transport system actively moves ions (Na+, K+) against their concentration gradient using ATP in the opposite direction. It is a form of primary active transport.\\n\\n5: Symport : Two substances are transported in the same direction across a membrane. ATP is required to move substances against their gradients, often via a secondary active transport mechanism.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/9906c716c43b9172f12d.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-189df839f6","n":366,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"Which receptor contributes to static 2 point discrimination?","options":["Merkel cell","Meissner corpuscle","Pacinian corpuscle","All of them"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Merkel cell\\n\\nMerkel cell contributes to static 2 point discrimination.\\n\\nMerkel cells are responsible for static two-point discrimination. These cells are specialized for detecting pressure and texture and are involved in the detection of fine touch. They provide information about the spatial resolution of tactile stimuli, which is essential for static two-point discrimination, where the ability to differentiate between two distinct points of touch is tested.\\n\\nMerkel cells are expanded dendritic endings in the epidermis of glabrous (non-hairy) skin. They respond to sustained pressure and touch. They are slowly adapting receptors.\\n\\nStatic vs. Dynamic Two-Point Discrimination\\nStatic: Measures the ability to distinguish stationary points; mediated by Merkel cells (slow-adapting receptors).\\nDynamic: Measures the ability to distinguish moving points; mediated by Meissner corpuscles (fast-adapting receptors).\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Meissner corpuscle: Meissner corpuscles are dendrites encapsulated in the connective tissue beneath the epidermis of glabrous (non-hairy) skin. They respond to tap and flutter (low-frequency vibration - 5-40Hz). They are rapidly adapting receptors.\\n\\nOption C: Pacinian corpuscle: Pacinian corpuscles are the largest cutaneous mechanoreceptor. They are present in the dermis of glabrous (non-hairy) and hairy skin. A Pacinian corpuscle consists of a nerve ending encapsulated by concentric layers of connective tissue, giving it an onion-like appearance. They respond to fast vibration (60-500Hz) and deep pressure. They are rapidly adapting receptors.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3b0050a8da","n":367,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"INI-CET 2024","text":"While collecting a blood sample for serum calcium, which is easily alterable, which of the following should be prevented to measure it accurately?","options":["If a tourniquet is needed, tie and release as soon as the sample is collected.","Collecting after exercise.","Making a fist is not recommended.","Not collecting samples from patients immobilized for long periods of time"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Collecting after exercise.\\n\\nBlood collection after exercise should be prevented to measure serum calcium accurately.\\n\\nOption A: A prolonged tourniquet is usually avoided while drawing blood for calcium measurements since hemoconcentration also leads to an increase in serum calcium levels. Hence, the tourniquet should be released as soon as the sample is collected.\\n\\nOption C: Fist-clenching or forearm exercise can lead to falsely elevated ionized (free) calcium levels. Hence, making a fist while blood collection is not recommended.\\n\\nOption D: Staying immobilised for long periods of time increases bone resorption and increases calcium levels. Hence, do not collect blood samples for serum calcium estimation in patients who are immobilised for long periods.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7d790f8340","n":368,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"\'c\' wave in JVP is seen in","options":["Iso-volumetric contraction","Slow filling at end of diastole","End of systole","Start of diastole"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Iso-volumetric contraction\\n\\n\'c\' waves are produced by bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during the Iso-volumetric contraction of the right ventricle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-46bcf379e7","n":369,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Aldosterone secretion is maximally stimulated by______","options":["Hyperkalemia","ACTH","Hypernatremia","Exogenous steroid"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Hyperkalemia\\n\\nHyperkalemia\xa0and activation of\xa0renin-angiotensin system are the most potent\xa0stimulators of aldosterone secretion.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b2bcc558d2","n":370,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"All of the following are components responsible for the countercurrent mechanism in the kidney except _____.","options":["Sodium outflow in thick ascending limb","Water outflow in thin descending limb","Sodium outflow in thin ascending limb","Flow of tubular fluid from PCT to DCT"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sodium outflow in thin ascending limb\\n\\nThin ascending limb is not a component of the countercurrent mechanism.\\n\\nThe loop of Henle function as countercurrent multipliers and vasa recta as countercurrent exchangers.\\n\\nThe functioning of the loop of Henle depends upon:\\n\\n1. High permeability of the thin descending limb to water ( via aquaporin-1)\\n2. Active transport of Na+ and Cl- from the thick ascending limb\\n3. Inflow of tubular fluid from proximal tubule and outflow to the distal tubule.\\n\\nNote: The thin ascending limbs of juxtamedullary nephrons are permeable to Na+ and Cl- and hence do contribute passively to countercurrent multiplication. However, among the options provided, Option C is the best answer as the other options are directly responsible for the countercurrent mechanism.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-131cf7f7dd","n":371,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"All of the following are true regarding the actions of endothelin-1 except ______","options":["Bronchodilation","Vasoconstriction","Decreased GFR","Has inotropic effect"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Bronchodilation\\n\\nEndothelin-1\\n\\n• Potent vasoconstrictor agent.\\n• Produced by endothelial cells.\\n• They are secreted locally and act in a paracrine fashion.\\n\\nActions of Endothelin-1 (ET-1)\\n\\n• Potent vasoconstrictor.\\n• Platelet activation.\\n• Inotropic effect.\\n• Closure of ductus arteriosus.\\n• Have a role in the pathogenesis of hypertension, myocardial infarction, and congestive heart failure.\\n• In the brain, regulate the transport of substances across blood brain barrier (BBB).\\n• In the kidneys, they are involved in tubuloglomerular feedback→ ↓ GFR.\\n• In the lungs, it causes bronchoconstriction (mediated via ET-B receptors).\\n• In GIT, they are involved in the migration of myenteric plexus to distal colon (failure leads to Hirschsprung’s/megacolon).\\n• Deletion of both alleles of ET-1 gene→craniofacial abnormalities and respiratory failure at birth.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-124fef722a","n":372,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"cGMP is the second messenger of","options":["Somatostatin","Angiotensin II","ADH","NO (nitric oxide)"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. NO (nitric oxide)\\n\\nVasodilatory actions of NO are mediated by cGMP.\\n\\nNO is a hormone derived from the amino acid\xa0arginine.\xa0It\xa0activates\xa0guanylyl cyclases in vascular smooth muscle cells resulting in the conversion of cGTP to cGMP (2nd messenger). cGMP activates cGMP dependent protein kinase, which causes blood vessels to relax.\\n\\ncGMP is the second messenger for nitric oxide and atrial natriuretic factor.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b2de4d9b1e","n":373,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Glucose is absorbed in intestine by?","options":["Primary active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Simple diffusion","Secondary active transport"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Secondary active transport\\n\\nGlucose is absorbed in the intestine by secondary active transport (sodium co-transport).\\n\\nThe energy for glucose transport is provided indirectly, by the active transport of Na+ out of the cell. This maintains the concentration gradient across the luminal border of the cell so that more Na+ and consequently more glucose enter.\\n\\nThough both secondary active transport and facilitated diffusion play a role in glucose absorption, it is the initial active transport of sodium through the basolateral membranes of the intestinal epithelial cells that provide the eventual force for moving glucose through the membranes as well. So,\xa0secondary active transport is the single best answer for this question.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1b7c363f94","n":374,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"In which of the following conditions is oxygen therapy not useful?","options":["Asthma","Acute myocardial infarction","Pulmonary edema","Central respiratory depression"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Central respiratory depression\\n\\nOxygen therapy is not beneficial in central respiratory depression.\\n\\nHigh dose\xa0oxygen reduces the output of peripheral chemoreceptors. So, if given to patients who already have central respiratory depression,\xa0it will indirectly reduce the hypoxic drive to breathe even further, leading to a reduction in respiratory frequency and minute ventilation.\\n\\nNote: In acute severe asthma, a\xa0high concentration of oxygen should be given by face mask to achieve an oxygen saturation of >90%.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0f37bfcf5e","n":375,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Kidney responds to shock by _______.","options":["Increasing renal blood flow","Increasing afferent arteriole resistance","Increasing GFR","Both B and C"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Increasing afferent arteriole resistance\\n\\nKidney responds to shock by increasing afferent arteriolar resistance.\\n\\nDuring shock, both afferent and efferent arterioles in kidney are constricted, however, the efferent vessels are constricted much more.\\nThe glomerular filtration rate and the plasma renal flow is decreased during shock. As plasma renal flow is decreased to a greater extent, the filtration fraction increases. (Option 1 & 3)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0252fee45b","n":376,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Nuclear signalling pathways are all except","options":["Caveolins","NF-kB","Importins","Rat proteins"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Rat proteins\\n\\nRat proteins are not nuclear signalling pathways.\xa0Recombinant rat IFN- is a bioactive protein intended for use in cell culture applications.\\n\\nImportin:\\n\\n• Transporting protein molecules into nucleus by binding to specific recognition sequences, called Nuclear Localization Sequences (NLS).\\n• 2 subunits, importin α and importin β.\\n\\nCaveolins:\\n\\n• Family of integral membrane proteins that are principal components of caveolae membranes\\n• Involved in receptor-independent endocytosis.\\n• Acts as scaffolding proteins within caveolar membranes by compartmentalizing and concentrating signaling molecules.\\n\\nNF-kB\\n\\n• Part of a prototypical proinflammatory signaling pathway\\n• based on the role of NF-κB in the expression of proinflammatory genes including cytokines, chemokines, and adhesion molecules","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-61105c3c7e","n":377,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"The predominant organ producing insulin-like growth factor I is","options":["Liver","Pituitary gland","Pancreas","Adrenal glands"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Liver\\n\\nGrowth hormone acts on the\xa0liver to form small proteins called “somatomedins”\\n\\n• Somatomedins increases bone growth in all aspect\\n• Effects are similar to insulin, hence referred to as insulin-like growth factor (IGF).\\n• Four types available:\\n\\n• Most important is somatomedin C, specifically referred to as “insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1)\\n• Binds to carrier protein in blood\\n• Hence, longer duration of action than growth hormone.\\n• Half-life - about 20 Hrs (compared to growth hormone - 20 mins)\\n• Blood concentration of IGF-1, follows the levels of growth hormone.\\n\\n• Growth effects of GH are mostly attributed to somatomedin (rather than its direct effect on bones & peripheral tissues)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8f9f77b514","n":378,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Transport molecule for Fe2+ into the enterocytes is","options":["Ferroportin 1","Hephaestin","Divalent Metal Transporter 1","Divalent Metal Transporter 2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Divalent Metal Transporter 1\\n\\nTransport of\xa0Fe2+ into\xa0the\xa0enterocytes\xa0occurs via\xa0divalent metal transporter\xa01 (DMT1),\xa0through an active transport process.\\n\\nProtein DMT-1 (Divalent Metal Transporter-1) is expressed on the apical surface of enterocytes in the initial part of the duodenum. DMT-1 is not specific to iron & transports other substances like zinc, copper, cobalt & manganese.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2b6207ad16","n":379,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Va/Q = infinity means _________.","options":["Partial pressure of O2 becomes zero","No exchange of O2 & CO2","CO2 alone equilibrates with the venous blood","Partial pressure of both CO2 and O2 remain normal"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. No exchange of O2 & CO2\\n\\nVa /Q = infinity means\xa0No exchange of O2 & CO2.\\n\\n• Va represents the ventilation in alveoli.\\n• Q represents the blood flow through the alveolus.\\n• The ratio of Va and Q explains the respiratory exchange when there is an imbalance between alveolar ventilation and alveolar blood flow.\\n• Ventilation-perfusion ratio is considered normal if there is an equal amount of blood flow & ventilation through alveolus.\\n• When Va /Q ratio is infinity,\\n\\n• There is adequate ventilation but no perfusion.\\n• \xa0No exchange of gases through the respiratory membranes of corresponding alveoli.\\n• The alveolar air equilibrates the humidified inspired air\\n• No exchange of oxygen and carbon-di-oxide occurs.\\n• Partial pressures of the O2 and CO2 are 149 mmHg & 0 mm Hg respectively","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-535ed8c021","n":380,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"What is Prosopagnosia?","options":["Impairment of consciousness","Being unaware of one’s problems","Difficulty in identifying known faces","Failure to identify objects"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Difficulty in identifying known faces\\n\\nProsopagnosia is difficulty in identifying known faces.\\n\\nIn humans, storage and recognition of faces is more strongly represented in the right inferior temporal lobe in right-handed individuals, though the left lobe is also active. Damage to this area can cause prosopagnosia.\\n\\nPatients with this abnormality can recognize forms and reproduce them. They can recognize people by their voices, and many of them show autonomic responses when they see familiar as opposed to unfamiliar faces. However, they cannot identify the familiar faces they see. The left hemisphere is also involved, but the role of the right hemisphere is primary.\\n\\nThe presence of autonomic response to a familiar face in the absence of recognition has been explained by postulating the existence of a separate dorsal pathway for processing information about faces that leads to recognition at only a subconscious level.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\n• Autotopagnosia\xa0- inability to recognize or to orient any part of one\'s own body\\n• Simultagnosia\xa0- inability to recognize multiple elements in a visual presentation, one object or some elements of a scene can be appreciated but not the display as a whole\\n• Somatagnosia\xa0- inability to identify any part of the body, either one’s own or another’s body\\n• Tactile agnosia (astereognosis) - \xa0inability to recognize objects by touch, in the presence of intact cutaneous and proprioceptive hand sensation; caused by a lesion in the contralateral parietal lobe","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ddb6c28540","n":381,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2018","text":"Which of the following statements is false about Cuthbertson\'s metabolic ebb and flow responses to trauma?","options":["The \\"ebb\\" phase is associated with a decline in body temperature and oxygen consumption.","The \\"flow\\" phase occurs after resuscitation from a state of shock.","The \\"ebb\\" phase is aimed at reducing posttraumatic energy depletion.","The \\"ebb\\" phase leads to activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. The \\"ebb\\" phase leads to activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response.\\n\\nThe \\"flow\\" phase (not \\"ebb\\") leads to activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response.\\n\\nCuthbertson\'s ebb and flow response to trauma:\\n\\nThe \\"ebb\\"\xa0phase lasts for a brief duration and is associated with a decline in body temperature and oxygen consumption, aimed at reducing post traumatic energy depletion.\\n\\nThe\xa0\\"flow\\"\xa0phase occurs after resuscitation from a state of shock, which leads to an increased metabolic turnover, activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response. This hypercatabolic condition leads to a significantly increased oxygen consumption and energy expenditure.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1ef474532b","n":382,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"According to Herrington classification, the decerebrate rigidity is characterized by all except ______","options":["Rigidity occurs in all the muscles of the body","Increase in the rate of discharge of the ɣ efferent neuron","Increased excitability of the motor neuron pool","Decerebration produces no phenomenon akin to spinal shock"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Rigidity occurs in all the muscles of the body\\n\\nIn decerebrate rigidity, there is rigidity in extensor muscles of all the four extremities (not all muscles of the body).\\n\\nDecerebration\\n\\n• Complete transection of the brain stem between the superior and inferior colliculi permits the brain stem pathways to function independently of their input from higher brain structures. This is called a mid-collicular decerebration.\\n• This lesion interrupts all input from the cortex (corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts) and red nucleus (rubrospinal tract), primarily to distal muscles of the extremities.\\n• The excitatory and inhibitory reticulospinal pathways (primarily to postural extensor muscles) remain intact.\\n• The dominance of drive from ascending sensory pathways to the excitatory reticulospinal pathway leads to hyperactivity in extensor muscles in all four extremities which are called decerebrate rigidity.\\n• The excitatory input from the reticulospinal pathway activates γ-motor neurons which indirectly activate α-motor neurons (via Ia spindle afferent activity). This is called the gamma loop.\\n\\nOption 4. Spinal shock is different from decebrate rigidity as it is a transient state that can occur immediately after a spinal cord injury. It is characterized by a temporary loss or depression of spinal reflexes below the level of the injury.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c1358680c1","n":383,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"According to myogenic hypothesis of renal autoregulation the afferent arterioles contract in response to stretch-induced by______","options":["NO","Noradrenaline","Opening of calcium channels","Adenosine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Opening of calcium channels\\n\\nThe afferent arterioles contract in response to stretch induced by the opening of calcium channels. The mechanism is called myogenic autoregulation of renal blood flow and GFR.\\n\\nThe stretch of the vascular wall allows increased movement of calcium ions from the extracellular fluid into the cells, causing them to contract. This contraction prevents excessive stretch of the vessel. This prevents excessive increase of renal blood flow and GFR by raising vascular resistance.\\n\\nThis mechanism may be more important in protecting\xa0the kidney from hypertension-induced injury.\\n\\nAutoregulation has two main mechanisms:\\n\\n• Metabolic mechanism: Tissues produce vasodilatory metabolites that regulate flow e.g. prostaglandins, NO, CO2, H+ and K+\\n• Myogenic mechanism","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d92ba2c4c9","n":384,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Acid Citrate Dextrose (ACD), used to store blood, preserves the RBCs for a period of 21 days. The storage period when adding phosphate alone and when adding adenine and phosphate together to ACD would respectively be?","options":["35 days and 42 days","21 days and 35 days","28 days and 45 days","35 days and 28 days"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 21 days and 35 days\\n\\nThe storage period when adding phosphate alone to ACD would be 21 days and when adding adenine and phosphate together would be 35 days.\\n\\nWhen adding phosphate to ACD, Citrate Phosphate Dextrose (CPD) is formed – It has a storage period of 21 days. There is no change in the shelf life as phosphate only increases the 2,3 DPG levels of the stored blood.\\n\\nWhen adding both phosphate and adenine to ACD, Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine (CPDA-1) is formed. It has a storage period of 35 days. It increases the shelf life by improving ATP synthesis of RBCs.\\n\\nUsing additive solutions containing saline, adenine, and glucose, with or without mannitol (SAGM) decreases RBC hemolysis, thus increasing the storage period to 42 days.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-04c61f4a6b","n":385,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"ADH acts through which aquaporin channel?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 2\\n\\nThe antidiuretic hormone acts through aquaporin channel 2.\\n\\nADH binds to V2 receptors in the late distal tubules, collecting tubules, and collecting ducts, and stimulates the movement of an intracellular protein, called aquaporin-2 (AQP-2), to the luminal side of the cell membranes.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-851fee95bf","n":386,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Based on the agglutination test shown below, what is the blood group?","options":["B positive","A positive","O positive","AB positive"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. O positive\\n\\nBased on the agglutination test shown in the image, the blood group is O positive.\\n\\nAs agglutination is present only in \'Rh\' and not with \'A\' and \'B\' hence the blood group is O Rh positive.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/7ad15faa0b68e0f37b52.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-8ba8dd7058","n":387,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Brown sequard syndrome is characterized by _____","options":["Fine touch lost on the opposite side","Pain and temperature lost on the same side","Pain and temperature loss on the opposite side","Only pain lost on the same side"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pain and temperature loss on the opposite side\\n\\nHemisection of the spinal cord causes damage to ascending sensory (dorsal column pathway, ventrolateral spinothalamic tract) and descending motor (corticospinal tract) pathways, which is called the Brown- S\xe9quard syndrome.\\n\\nThe lesion to fasciculus gracilus or fasciculus cuneatus leads to ipsilateral loss of discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception below the level of the lesion.(fibers from the sacral cord are positioned most medially and those from the cervical cord are positioned most laterally.\xa0)\\n\\nThe loss of the spinothalamic tract leads to contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning one or two segments below the lesion.\\n\\nDamage to the corticospinal tract produces weakness and spasticity in certain muscle groups on the same side of the body.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-46d5e04511","n":388,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Functional residual capacity is:","options":["Volume expired after normal inspiration","Volume that remains after forced expiration","Expiratory reserve volume + residual volume","Tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Expiratory reserve volume + residual volume\\n\\nFunctional Residual Capacity (FRC) is expiratory reserve volume + residual volume.\\n\\nFrom the picture given below, (FRC = ERV + RV).\\n\\nLung Capacities:\\n\\nThe total lung capacity can be broken down into alternative capacities that help to define functioning lungs.\\n\\n• The vital lung capacity\xa0refers to the maximum amount of air expired from the fully inflated lung or maximum inspiratory level.\\n• The inspiratory capacity is the maximum amount of air inspired from the end-expiratory level.\\n• The functional residual capacity represents the volume of the air remaining in the lungs after expiration of a normal breath.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-98c1f66069","n":389,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Lesion in the medial lemniscus at the level of pons causes which of the following?","options":["Pain and temperature loss in the same side","Pain and temperature loss in the opposite side","Loss of tactile and proprioception on the same side","Loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side\\n\\nLesion in the medial lemniscus at the level of pons causes loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side.\\n\\nFibers of medial lemniscus ascend ipsilaterally in the dorsal columns of the spinal cord to the medulla, where they synapse in the gracilus and cuneate nuclei.\\n\\nThe second-order neurons from these nuclei cross the midline and ascend in the medial lemniscus to end in the contralateral ventral posterior lateral (VPL) nucleus and related specific sensory relay nuclei of the thalamus.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b8328ad599","n":390,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Liver synthesizes all of the following except:","options":["Albumin","Coagulation factors","Acute phase proteins","Immunoglobulins"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Immunoglobulins\\n\\nImmunoglobulins are not synthesized by the liver. They are produced by plasma cells.\\n\\nPlasma proteins synthesized by the liver are:\\n\\n• Albumin\\n• Coagulation/clotting factors\\n• Acute-phase proteins\\n• Steroid binding and other hormone-binding proteins","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-408c5bff19","n":391,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Testosterone production is mainly contributed by ______","options":["Leydig cells","Sertoli cells","Seminiferous tubules","Epidydmis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Leydig cells\\n\\nTestosterone production is mainly contributed by\xa0Leydig cells.\\n\\nTestosterone is synthesized from cholesterol in the Leydig cells and also from androstenedione secreted by the adrenal cortex.\\n\\nThe diagnosis of testosterone deficiency should be made only on the basis of clinical signs and symptoms consistent with androgen deficiency, and consistently subnormal serum testosterone concentrations at 8 to 10 AM on three occasions.\\n\\nTestosterone has been used to treat primary or secondary hypogonadism in adult men.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-7613dda0d3","n":392,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"What is the function of tubuloglomerular feedback?","options":["Regulate GFR","Decrease plasma sodium","Increase plasma volume","Decrease tubular secretion"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Regulate GFR\\n\\nTubuloglomerular feedback regulates glomerular filtration rate (GFR).\\n\\nAs the rate of ﬂow through the ascending limb of the loop of Henle and first part of the distal tubule increases, glomerular filtration in the same nephron decreases, and, conversely, a decrease in ﬂow increases the GFR.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b2a78bbafd","n":393,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which neurotransmitter is responsible for anterograde dilatation of the small intestine?","options":["VIP","Acetylcholine","Substance P","Serotonin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. VIP\\n\\nVIP is responsible for anterograde\xa0dilatation of the small intestine.\\n\\nPeristalsis involves the contraction of a segment of intestine behind the bolus of food and relaxation of a segment of the intestine in front of the bolus of food.\\n\\nCholinergic neurons passing in anterograde direction produce\xa0NO and VIP which cause relaxation of a segment of the intestine in front of the bolus.\\n\\nCholinergic neurons passing in retrograde direction produce\xa0substance P and acetylcholine which cause contraction of a segment of the intestine behind the bolus.\\n\\nNote: In achalasia cardia, there is decreased\xa0release of NO and VIP in the distal part of the esophagus.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0b5db0ed65","n":394,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following has the least conduction velocity?","options":["Postganglionic sympathetic fibers","Somatic motor fibers","Pre-ganglionic autonomic fibers","Touch and pressure fibers"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Postganglionic sympathetic fibers\\n\\nPostganglionic sympathetic (type C) has the least conduction velocity among the given options.\\n\\nTypes of nerve fibers:\\n\\nFiber type\\nFunction\\nFiber diameter (μm)\\nConduction velocity (m/s)\\n\\nAα\\nProprioception, somatic motor\\n12-20\\n70-120\\n\\nAβ\\nTouch, pressure\\n5-12\\n30-70\\n\\nAγ\\nMotor to muscle spindles\\n3-6\\n15-30\\n\\nAδ\\nPain, temperature\\n2-5\\n12-30\\n\\nB\\nPreganglionic autonomic\\n<3\\n3-15\\n\\nC, Dorsal root\\nPain, temperature\\n0.4-1.2\\n0.5-2\\n\\nC, Sympathetic\\nPost ganglionic sympathetic\\n0.3-1.3\\n0.7-2.3","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ccce43cc88","n":395,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following ions is involved in peripheral oxygen sensing chemoreceptors?","options":["Potassium","Calcium","Sodium","Chlorine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Potassium\\n\\nPotassium is involved in peripheral oxygen sensing chemoreceptors.\\n\\nType I glomus cells (carotid and aortic body) have O2-sensitive K+ channels, whose conductance is reduced in proportion to the degree of hypoxia.\\n\\nThis reduces the K+ efflux, depolarizing the cell and causing Ca2+ inﬂux.\\n\\nThe Ca2+ influx triggers action potentials and consequent excitation of the afferent nerve endings.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8c0f9d640e","n":396,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following is not a dietary fiber?","options":["Cellulose","Inulin","Pectin","Gums"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Gums\\n\\nGum\xa0is\xa0the best answer among the given options. Even though all of the given options are dietary fibers, the consumption of gums through diet is very low and hence, it is the best answer.\\n\\nDietary fibers consist of both soluble and insoluble forms and include lignin, pectin, inulin, cellulose, hemicellulose, etc.\\n\\nAdvantages of fibers:\\n\\n• Forms bulk of the stool and reduces stool transit time\\n• Reduces incidence of carcinoma colon, DM, CAD, diverticulitis","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-46b7664142","n":397,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following is true for decorticate rigidity?","options":["It is produced by the removal of cerebral cortex and basal ganglia","Flexion of lower limbs and extension of upper limbs occurs","Rigidity is pronounced","It is characterized by flexion of upper limbs and extension of lower limbs"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. It is characterized by flexion of upper limbs and extension of lower limbs\\n\\n• Removal of the cerebral cortex (decortication) produces decorticate rigidity.\\n• Characterized by flexion of the upper extremities at the elbow and extensor hyperactivity in the lower extremities.\\n• The flexion can be explained by active rubrospinal excitation of flexor muscles in the upper extremities; the hyperextension of lower extremities is due to the same changes that occur after midcollicular decerebration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-177f728687","n":398,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which of the following neurons predominantly fire during forceful expiration?","options":["Dorsal group of neurons","Ventral group of neurons","Pneumotaxic center","Chemoreceptors"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ventral group of neurons\\n\\nVentral groups of neurons fire, when forceful expiration is needed like during heavy exercise.\\n\\nDorsal group of neurons control the normal inspiration and expiration.\\n\\nPneumotaxic center limits the duration of inspiration.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-8e129ea99b","n":399,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2019","text":"Which part of the liver stores vitamin A?","options":["Ito cells","Hepatocytes","Endothelial cells","Kupffer cells"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Ito cells\\n\\nVitamin A is stored in the liver perisinusoidal stellate (Ito) cells as\xa0retinyl ester (retinol palmitate).\\n\\nKupffer cells, also known as stellate macrophages and Kupffer-Borowicz cells;\xa0are specialized macrophages located in the liver that form part of the mononuclear phagocyte system. These are present, lining the walls of the sinusoids, not the perisinusoidal space (Space of Disse).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f206b09bb6","n":400,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"A lady presents with vascular injury to the inferior frontal gyrus. Which functional area is likely to be affected?","options":["Motor speech area","Wernicke\'s area","Auditory area","Visual area"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Motor speech area\\n\\nBroca\'s motor speech area is likely to be affected in the vascular injury of the inferior frontal gyrus\\n\\nA lesion in this region leads to motor/Broca\'s aphasia.\xa0Speech is non-fluent and interrupted by many word-finding pauses. The speech is impoverished in function words that contribute to the syntax but is enriched in meaningful nouns and verbs. However, comprehension of speech is intact and the patient knows what he wants to say.\\n\\nFor example, a patient with Broca\'s aphasia may say \\"walk dog\\" instead of \\"I want to take the dog for a walk\\".","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-035ca5575c","n":401,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"A man falls into a deep sleep with one arm under his head. He finds he is not able to move this arm when he awakens. However, it tingles and pain sensation in the arm is still intact. What is the reason for the loss of motor function without loss of pain sensation is that in the nerves to his arm?","options":["B fibers are more susceptible to pressure than A fibers","A fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers","C fibers are more sensitive to pressure than A fibers","Motor nerves are more affected by sleep than sensory nerves"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers\\n\\nThe reason for the loss of motor function without loss of pain sensation is that in the nerves to his arm, A-fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers.\\n\\nSleeping for a long period with the arms under the head puts pressure on a nerve. It leads to the loss of conduction in large-diameter motor, touch, and pressure fibers, while pain sensation remains relatively intact. A-fibers (includes the motor fibers) are affected.\\n\\nThe susceptibility of the nerve fibers to pressure is\xa0A>B>C.\\n\\nThis is responsible for Saturday night or Sunday morning paralysis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a01a750afd","n":402,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"A person develops headache and breathlessness on a trekking expedition following rapid ascent to over 3000 metres above the sea level. Which of the following is not used in the treatment of this condition?","options":["IV digoxin","Descent to lower altitude","Administration of oxygen","Tablet acetazolamide"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. IV digoxin\\n\\nThe symptoms of headache and breathlessness following rapid ascent indicate acute mountain/altitude sickness and IV digoxin is not used to treat this condition.\\n\\nOn ascent to high altitudes, the low partial pressure of oxygen leads to hypoxia and systemic arteriolar dilatation. Consequently, the capillary pressure rises with increased transudation of fluid into the brain tissue and the following symptoms are seen:\\n\\n• Headache\\n• Irritation\\n• Insomnia\\n• Breathlessness\\n• Nausea and vomiting\\n\\nTreatment involves administration of oxygen, descent to a lower altitude and administration of acetazolamide. Acetazolamide counteracts hypoxia by stimulating respiration and also reduces the formation of cerebrospinal fluid, thereby reducing intracranial tension.\\n\\nPortable hyperbaric chambers are increasingly being used in the treatment of high-altitude illnesses when rapid descent is not possible. These chambers are available in armed forces hospitals in high altitude areas","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0ae5d8b059","n":403,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"C wave of JVP represents:","options":["Tricuspid bulge into right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction","Contraction of right atrium","Passive filling of right atrium","Passive emptying of right atrium"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Tricuspid bulge into right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction\\n\\n\'C\' wave in JVP\xa0represents tricuspid bulge into right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction.\\n\\nCharacteristics of JVP :\\n\\n• a wave\xa0corresponds to atrial systole which causes regurgitation of some blood into the great veins.\\n• c wave occurs due to the increase in atrial pressure caused by bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction.\\n• x descent occurs due to the fall in atrial pressure during ventricular systole due to pulling of valve leaflets.\\n• v wave is seen at the end of ventricular contraction and\xa0occurs due to the slow venous filling which causes an increase in atrial pressure.\\n• y descent\xa0occurs due to the passive emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-ab2c245d80","n":404,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"In which of the following reflexes, gastric emptying is delayed when acidic content enters the duodenum?","options":["Gastrocolic","Enterocolic","Gastroileal","Enterogastric"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Enterogastric\\n\\nIn\xa0the\xa0enterogastric reflex,\xa0gastric emptying is delayed when acidic content enters the duodenum.\\n\\nWhen the food enters the duodenum, multiple reflexes are initiated from the duodenal wall which passes back to the stomach. These reflexes slow or stop the stomach emptying if the volume of the chyme in the duodenum becomes too much. These reflexes are called enterogastric reflexes.\\n\\nThis reflex can be triggered\xa0due to the following reasons:\\n\\n• Presence of irritants and acids in duodenal chyme (most sensitive factor)\\n• Distension of duodenum due to the presence of an excessive volume of chyme\\n• Rapid entry of hypertonic or hypotonic fluids into the duodenum\\n• Presence of breakdown products of protein digestion and sometimes fat","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-473fd3126f","n":405,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Prolactin level is highest during :","options":["24 hours after ovulation","24 hours after delivery","REM Sleep","After running for 1 hour"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 24 hours after delivery\\n\\nAmong the given options, the prolactin level is highest 24 hours after delivery.\\n\\nThe maximum level of prolactin is seen during pregnancy and in the immediate postpartum period. During pregnancy, the secretion is increased reaching a peak at the time of delivery. After delivery, the plasma concentration falls to nonpregnant levels in a few weeks. Suckling produces surges of prolactin release.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5c41283773","n":406,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Respiratory distress syndrome is due to defective","options":["Type 1 pneumocytes","Type 2 pneumocytes","Alveolar capillary endothelial cells","Bronchial mucosal epithelial cells"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Type 2 pneumocytes\\n\\nRespiratory distress syndrome is due to defective\xa0type 2 pneumocytes which produce lung surfactants. The surfactants reduce the surface tension in the alveoli. A defect in type 2 pneumocytes or surfactant deficiency can lead to respiratory distress syndrome.\\n\\nThe primary alveoli develop and the production of the surfactant begins by week 20-24. However, it does not reach the lungs until later.\xa0The surfactant\xa0appears in the amniotic fluid between 28 and 32 weeks of gestation. Mature levels of the pulmonary surfactant are present usually after 35 weeks of gestation.\\n\\nAntenatal corticosteroids are given to increase production of surfactant.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-360fa004bf","n":407,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Respiratory distress syndrome is due to the deficiency of","options":["Dipalmitoyl Phosphatidylcholine","Phosphatidylserine","Phosphatidylethanolamine","Phosphatidylinositol"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Dipalmitoyl Phosphatidylcholine\\n\\nRespiratory distress syndrome is due to the deficiency of dipalmitoyl-phosphatidyl-choline(DPPC)\xa0/dipalmitoyl\xa0lecithin.\xa0It is the major pulmonary\xa0surfactant.\\n\\nClinical relevance-\xa0Test to\xa0assess fetal lung maturity is L/S ratio - lecithin/sphingomyelin ratio. A ratio of ≥ 2 is considered normal.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-99fbe9286f","n":408,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"The protein in the glomerular basement membrane responsible for charge-dependent filtration is?","options":["Fibronectin","Heparan sulfate","Collagen Type IV","Entactin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Heparan sulfate\\n\\nThe protein in the glomerular basement membrane responsible for charge-dependent filtration is heparan sulfate. It prevents filtration by repelling negatively charged proteins.\\n\\nCollagen type IV, fibronectin, entactin are structural components of basement membrane.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-e8792df6e8","n":409,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"V/Q = infinity holds true in which of the following conditions?","options":["Foreign body obstruction in the bronchus","Equal to dead space","When O2 and CO2 ratio is equal","The PO2 of alveolar air is 159 mmHg and PCO2 is 40 mmHg"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Equal to dead space\\n\\nVA\xa0/Q= infinity means that there is no exchange of O2 & CO2, which means the volume of the lung equals to dead space\xa0and the physiological effort of breathing is wasted.\\n\\nVA\xa0represents the ventilation in alveoli and Q represents the blood flow through the alveolus. The ratio of VA and Q explains the respiratory exchange when there is an imbalance between alveolar ventilation and alveolar blood flow. The ventilation-perfusion ratio is considered normal if there is an equal amount of blood flow & ventilation through the alveolus.\\n\\nVA\xa0/Q ratio is infinity when:\\n\\n• There is adequate ventilation but no perfusion.\\n• No exchange of gases ( O2 and CO2 )through the respiratory membranes of corresponding alveoli.\\n• The alveolar air equilibrates the humidified inspired air.\\n• Partial pressures of the O2 and CO2 are 149 mmHg & 0 mm Hg respectively.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b90c3ac0e1","n":410,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"What is the site of lesion in Wernicke\'s hemianopic pupil?","options":["Optic nerve","Optic tract","Optic radiation","Optic chiasma"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Optic tract\\n\\nThe site of lesion in Wernicke\'s hemianopic pupil is optic tract.\\n\\nWernicke hemianopic pupil is the light reflex, where, illumination of the blind half of the retina shows no pupillary reaction while stimulating the normal side of the retina shows a pupillary reaction. It is seen in lesions involving the optic tract. It is also seen in cases such as TB, syphilis and tumours of the optic thalamus.\\n\\nHomonymous hemianopia (HH) involves loss of vision on the same side of the visual field in both eyes. HH is characteristically seen in lesions involving the visual pathway posterior to the optic chiasma.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-1eafce7135","n":411,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"What makes CPDA superior than ACD for blood storage:","options":["The fall in 2, 3 DPG is less","Decreased release of O2","It has less P50","It is less acidic"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. The fall in 2, 3 DPG is less\\n\\nThe fall in 2, 3 DPG is less when the blood is stored in CPDA compared with ACD.\\n\\n2, 3 DPG (2,3-diphosphoglycerate) binds to\xa0deoxygenated haemoglobin. Therefore, an increase in 2,3 DPG causes increased release of O2\xa0to the tissues. The equilibrium\xa0between Hb and O2 can be understood from the following equation:\\n\\nHbO2 + 2,3-DPG ⇄ Hb−2,3-DPG + O2\\n\\nIn stored blood, the 2,3 DPG levels usually fall. As a result, stored blood releases less oxygen to the tissues than the normal circulating blood, thus limiting its use in hypoxic patients (Eg: anaemia).\\n\\nHowever, such a fall in 2,3 DPG is less when it is stored in a phosphate-containing solution like CPDA (Citrate, phosphate, dextrose, adenine), rather than ACD (Acid citrate dextrose). Therefore, the blood stored in CPDA, in comparison with blood stored in ACD has:\\n\\n• Higher 2,3 DPG concentration\\n• More amount of deoxygenated blood bound to 2,3 DPG\\n• Decreased affinity of Hb to the oxygen.\\n• Increased release of oxygen to the tissues.\\n• Raised P50 (PO2 at which Hb is half saturated)\\n• Shift in O2-Hb dissociation curve to the right","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-85e32f5f36","n":412,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Which of the following clotting factors would have decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residue in a patient on warfarin therapy?","options":["Factor 2","Factor 11","Tissue factor","Factor 5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Factor 2\\n\\nFactor 2 would have decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residue in a patient on warfarin therapy.\\n\\nActivation of coagulation factors II, VII, IX, X is done by carboxylation of their glutamate residues. This is done by the enzyme\xa0γ-glutamyl carboxylase with the reduced form of vitamin K as a co-factor.\\n\\nThis reduced form of vitamin K needs to be constantly recycled back from its oxidized form for the reaction to continue. This recycling is done by the enzyme\xa0vitamin K epoxide reductase. This enzyme is inhibited by warfarin.\\n\\nHence, warfarin indirectly inhibits the γ-carboxylation of vitamin K dependent clotting factors -\xa0Factors II, VII, IX, X\xa0by directly inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase. Thus leading to decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residue in these factors.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-2eb9488c89","n":413,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Which of the following conditions would have the highest levels of ACTH and cortisol?","options":["Early morning","Normal individuals with dexamethasone administration","Addison\'s disease","Cushing\'s disease"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cushing\'s disease\\n\\nAmong the given conditions, the levels of ACTH and cortisol will be highest in\xa0Cushing\'s disease.\\n\\nCushing’s disease is caused by an ACTH secreting pituitary adenoma. The increased ACTH causes increased cortisol production.\\n\\nNote: In normal physiologic state, ACTH levels are higher in the early morning but not as high as in Cushing\'s disease.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f9ac7f406a","n":414,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Which of the following has the highest oxygen concentration in fetal circulation?","options":["Inferior vena cava","Right atrium","Superior vena cava","Ascending aorta"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inferior vena cava\\n\\nInferior vena cava has the\xa0highest oxygen concentration in fetal circulation.\\n\\nThe blood gets oxygenated in the placenta and this passes to the umbilical vein. From the umbilical vein, 50% of the blood enters the hepatic circulation and 50% bypasses the liver to enter the inferior vena cava. From here, the blood enters the heart via the right atrium. The right atrium also receives deoxygenated blood from the superior vena cava. This mixed blood is distributed to the body via aorta.\\n\\nTherefore, inferior vena cava has the highest concentration of oxygen in the fetal circulation.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-724c5c6ec2","n":415,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2020","text":"Which of the following hormones use receptor tyrosine kinase signalling?","options":["Insulin","TSH","TRH","MSH"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Insulin\\n\\nInsulin\xa0uses the receptor tyrosine kinase signalling to exert its functions.\\n\\nOther hormones acting in a similar fashion includes\\n\\n• Growth hormone\\n• Growth factors like VEGF, PDGF, FGF, NGF\\n• Prolactin\\n• Leptin","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-f399fc8e46","n":416,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A 30-year-old man weighing 70 kg had a sodium level of 120 mEq/L. Calculate the sodium deficit.","options":["280 mEq","480 mEq","840 mEq","1400 mEq"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 840 mEq\\n\\nThe sodium deficit in the given scenario is 840 mEq.\\n\\nSodium deficit is calculated as 0.6 x body weight x (target plasma sodium concentration - starting plasma sodium concentration).\\n\\nSubstituting the given values in the formula :\\n\\nSodium deficit = 0.6 x 70 x (140 - 120) = 840 mEq.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-c2c0694109","n":417,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A body fluid sample is being studied and has Na: 10 mEq/L, K: 140 mEq/L, CI: 4 mEq/L. Identify the compartment from which the sample has been obtained.","options":["Interstitial","Intracellular fluid","Extracellular fluid","Plasma"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Intracellular fluid\\n\\nThe sample obtained is from the intracellular fluid (ICF) compartment. The ICF contains large quantities of potassium (K+) ions - 140 mEq/L . The ICF also has lesser amounts of sodium (Na+) and Chloride (Cl-) ions.\\n\\nThe extracellular fluid (ECF) compartment includes interstitial fluids and plasma. In contrast to the ICF, the ECF has an abundance of sodium, chloride,\xa0calcium and bicarbonate ions, and lesser amounts of potassium, phosphate and magnesium ions.\\n\\nThe image below depicts the proportion of the important anions and cations present in the intracellular and extracellular fluids.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d45da29ced","n":418,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A child with loose stools for one day presented to your OPD. On examination, signs of dehydration were present and the child was given ORS. It facilitates glucose absorption through which of the following receptors?","options":["SGLT1","SGLT2","GLUT4","Na- Ca transporter"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. SGLT1\\n\\nORS facilitates glucose absorption through SGLT 1 (Sodium-dependent glucose co-transporter - 1). In a child with diarrhea, when oral rehydration solution is given, there is coupled absorption of sodium and glucose through SGLT -1.\\n\\nGlucose transport may be active or passive.\\n\\nActive transport of glucose takes place through Sodium dependent glucose transporter (SGLT 1 and 2). SGLT 1 helps in glucose absorption in the small intestine and renal tubules.\xa0SGLT 2 primarily facilitates glucose absorption in the renal tubules.\\n\\nPassive absorption of glucose occurs through facilitated diffusion. This is carried out by Glucose transporters (GLUT) 1 -7.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0bbbedacf1","n":419,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"A middle-aged female patient presented with slowly progressive motor weakness, nuchal rigidity, and difficulty in swallowing and carrying out day-to-day activities. A provisional diagnosis of multiple sclerosis is suspected. Which of the following cells are affected in multiple sclerosis?","options":["Microglia","Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Oligodendrocytes\\n\\nOligodendrocytes are affected in multiple sclerosis.\\n\\nIn multiple sclerosis, there is patchy destruction of myelin in the central nervous system and the axons are demyelinated. This causes loss of conduction or delayed conduction in the demyelinated axons. Oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells are involved in the formation of myelin. Hence, they are affected by multiple sclerosis.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: Microglia are a part of the immune system. Microglia are scavenger cells that aid in the removal of cellular debris formed due to various diseases. They originate from macrophages outside of the nervous system and are unrelated to the neural cell types.\\n\\nOption C: There are three types of macroglia - astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and Schwann cells. Astrocytes are found throughout the brain and they induce capillaries to form the blood-brain barrier. The astrocytes may be fibrous or protoplasmic.\\n\\nOption D: Ependymal cells line the ventricular system. They are in contact with the cerebrospinal fluid and play a role in fluid homeostasis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-800459e435","n":420,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2021","text":"Beta 2 receptor action leading to hyperglycemia/ hypoglycemia is mediated by which of the following enzymes?","options":["Adenylyl cyclase","Guanylyl cyclase","Phospholipase C","Phospholipase A2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Adenylyl cyclase\\n\\nBeta 2 receptor action leading to hypoglycemia/ hyperglycemia is mediated by adenylyl cyclase. Catecholamines act through beta 2 receptors and they use\xa0adenylyl cyclase - cAMP second messenger system to stimulate the target tissues.\\n\\nOther hormones that use adenylyl cyclase - cAMP second messenger system are :\\n\\n• Adrenocorticotropic hormone\\n• Angiotensin II\\n• Corticotropin-releasing hormone\\n• Calcitonin\\n• Follicle-stimulating hormone\\n• Glucagon\\n• Growth hormone-releasing hormone\\n• Human chorionic gonadotropin\\n• Luteinizing hormone\\n• Parathyroid hormone\\n• Secretin\\n• Somatostatin\\n• Thyroid-stimulating hormone\\n• Vasopressin\\n\\nHormones exert their actions by stimulating cAMP production within the cells. When the hormone binds to the membrane receptor, the receptor is coupled to the G protein. This G protein can either stimulate (Gs protein) or inhibit (Gi protein) adenylyl cyclase that controls the conversion of cytoplasmic adenosine triphosphate to adenosine monophosphate (cAMP). The protein kinases dependent on cAMP are thus activated. This leads to a series of biochemical reactions enabling the hormone to exert its actions.\\n\\nGuanylyl cyclase is the enzyme that catalyzes the formation of cGMP.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6894eaa0c3","n":421,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"A 65-year-old man suffered from a stroke 2 days ago. He now presents with involuntary, violent, and flinging movements of the limbs on one side. What is the likely site of lesion in this patient?","options":["Subthalamic nuclei","Globus pallidus","Putamen","Caudate nucleus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Subthalamic nuclei\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario is suggestive of hemiballismus. A lesion in subthalamic nuclei is responsible for this condition.\\n\\nA\xa0lesion in the\xa0subthalamic nucleus\xa0causes\xa0sudden, violent, involuntary, and flinging movements confined to\xa0one side of the body,\xa0commonly affecting the proximal muscles of the limbs, a condition called\xa0hemiballismus.\xa0The movements are seen\xa0contralateral to the side of the lesion, i.e., involuntary movements of the right arm and leg are seen in the lesion of the left subthalamic nucleus and vice versa.\\n\\nBasal ganglia circuits have both\xa0direct\xa0and\xa0indirect\xa0pathways. The\xa0direct\xa0pathway\xa0stimulates\xa0the cortex to\xa0produce movement, whereas the\xa0indirect\xa0pathway\xa0inhibits\xa0it.\xa0The striatal activity in the\xa0direct pathway\xa0is mediated by\xa0D1 dopamine receptors\xa0and in the\xa0indirect pathway\xa0by\xa0D2 dopamine receptors.\\n\\nThe subthalamic nucleus is a part of the indirect pathway. Hence, a lesion at subthalamic nuclei leads to loss of inhibitory effect of the indirect pathway resulting in dopaminergic overactivity. This is responsible for features of hemiballismus.\\n\\nThis movement disorder is usually\xa0self-limiting\xa0and tends to resolve spontaneously after weeks or months.\xa0Dopamine antagonistic\xa0agents\xa0can be given to manage it as well.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: A lesion in the\xa0globus pallidus\xa0can give rise to\xa0athetosis. This involves spontaneous and\xa0continuous writhing movements\xa0of the hand, arm, neck, or face.\\n\\nOptions C and D: A lesion in the\xa0putamen and caudate nucleus\xa0can give rise to\xa0chorea.\xa0Chorea\xa0is defined as rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like nonpatterned involuntary movements involving distal or proximal muscle groups.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-4cdcc3a2c4","n":422,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"In a patient, the hypothalamic thermostat was reset from point A to point C as shown below. Which of the following happens in stage A compared to stage B?","options":["Shivering","Sweating","Increased blood flow to skin","Inhibition of chemical thermogenesis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Shivering\\n\\nIn the above scenario, the patient\'s hypothalamic thermostat has been reset from point A to a higher temperature (point C). The body responds to this change by using certain measures in stage A such as\xa0shivering.\\n\\nIn stage A the body activates various heat gain mechanisms\xa0to match the hypothalamic temperature setpoint. These include:\\n\\n• Shivering- the shivering center is located in the posterior hypothalamus. It is normally inhibited by signals from the heat center in the anterior hypothalamic-preoptic area but is excited by cold signals\xa0from the skin and spinal cord. \x7fA sudden increase in heat setpoint results in the activation of this\xa0center.\xa0It then transmits non-rhythmic signals through bilateral tracts through the lateral columns of the spinal cord, and finally to the anterior motor neurons. They increase the tone of the skeletal muscles throughout the body. As the tone rises above a certain critical level, shivering begins.\xa0During maximum shivering, body heat production can rise to four to five times normal.\\n• Vasoconstriction\xa0(decreased blood flow to skin) is mediated by increased sympathetic drive by the activation of the posterior hypothalamic sympathetic center.\\n• An increase in chemical thermogenesis in which heat production takes place in the brown fat by the mechanism of uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation. This is the predominant mechanism seen in neonates.\\n\\nIn stage B,\xa0the hypothalamic temperature setpoint is lower than the normal body temperature. Hence body activates various heat loss mechanisms to match the hypothalamic temperature setpoint. It includes vasodilatation of the skin blood vessel, sweating, and decreased thermogenesis.\\n\\nThe temperature of the body is regulated majorly by a nervous feedback mechanism that operates\xa0through temperature-regulating centers located in the hypothalamus. Minor regulation is done by the thermal receptors in the skin and the deep thermal receptors present in the spinal cord and abdominal viscera.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/7074583d1af3a702026f.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-1d757f5ffc","n":423,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"The image below depicts which of the following types of cell-to-cell signaling?","options":["Paracrine","Autocrine","Endocrine","Merocrine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Paracrine\\n\\nThe given image shows the\xa0paracrine cell-to-cell signaling mechanism. In paracrine signaling, the\xa0signaling molecules released by a cell only affect target cells in close proximity to it.\\n\\nAn example of paracrine signaling is the release of\xa0endothelin-1,\xa0a potent vasoconstrictor released by the endothelial cells.\\n\\nSynaptic signaling\xa0is a special case of paracrine signaling (for chemical synapses) between neurons and target cells.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: In the\xa0autocrine signaling\xa0mechanism, the cells respond to the signaling molecules that are released by themselves, which is the site of release and action within the same cell. Note that the signaling molecules are first secreted out of the cell and then act on the same cell through receptors. An example is the proliferation of activated T-cells by IL-2 released by the same cell after it interacts with antigen-presenting cells.\\n\\nOption C: In\xa0endocrine signaling,\xa0a signaling molecule, a hormone (for example insulin, FSH, LH), is usually carried by the blood from its site of release to act on its target at a distance.\\n\\nThe below image shows the different types of extracellular cell-to-cell communication mechanisms.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Merocrine is a type of glandular secretion and not an extracellular signaling mechanism.\xa0In this, the\xa0secretions\xa0are poured out by\xa0exocytosis and the\xa0cell remains intact. An\xa0example\xa0of the merocrine gland is the goblet cell and eccrine sweat gland.\\n\\nAn illustration depicting this mechanism is given below.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/012b8e6f1db48b5d888b.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-c961d0512b","n":424,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2022","text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the given cystometrogram?","options":["Segment Ia is due to residual urine","Segment Ib is due to Laplace law","Micturition fails to happen in segment II","The dotted line represents that micturition has occurred"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Segment Ib is due to Laplace law\\n\\nThe true statement regarding the given cystometrogram is that\xa0segment Ib is due to Laplace law.\\n\\nCystometrogram is a plot of intravesical pressure against the volume of fluid in the bladder. The curve shows three segments:\\n\\n• Ia-\xa0initial slight rise in pressure when the first increments in the volume are produced\\n• Ib-\xa0long, nearly flat segment as further increments are produced\\n• II-\xa0sudden, sharp rise in pressure as the micturition reflex is triggered (not failure).\\n• The dotted line-\xa0pressure-volume relation if the micturition had not occurred.\\n\\nThe flatness of segment Ib is a demonstration of the law of Laplace.\xa0The law of Laplace states that the pressure (P) in a spherical viscus is equal to twice the wall tension (T) divided by the radius (R). This is expressed as\xa0P= 2T/R.\xa0In the bladder, the tension increases as the bladder get filled. Since the radius also increases there is only a slight increase in pressure\xa0until the bladder is relatively full.\\n\\nThe\xa0first urge\xa0to\xa0void\xa0is felt at a bladder volume of about\xa0150 mL. A marked\xa0sense\xa0of\xa0fullness is felt at a bladder volume of 400ml.\\n\\nMicturition reflex\xa0is the result of sensory signals from the\xa0stretch receptors in the bladder wall, which are conducted to the sacral segments of the cord through the pelvic nerves and then efferent signals conducts back again to the bladder through the parasympathetic nerve fibers.\\n\\nThe micturition reflex pathway is given below.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/d6a34bab5316d9e13448.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-3c82163d05","n":425,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"A man was brought to the emergency after he suddenly became unconscious while working in the field. On examination, his temperature was 105 degrees Fahrenheit and his skin turgor was decreased. Which of the following would not be seen in the patient?","options":["Tachypnea","Hypotension","Sweating","Red hot skin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sweating\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette in a patient with signs of dehydration and\xa0a body temperature of 105\xb0F\xa0is highly suggestive of\xa0heatstroke.\xa0Sweating would not be seen in a patient with heatstroke.\\n\\nThe body has various mechanisms to maintain body temperature within a range of 36–37\xb0C. When the body temperature is elevated by exposure to high environmental temperatures or physical exertion, the central control area in the medial preoptic and anterior hypothalamic nuclei is activated. This engages the autonomic pathways that promote sweating and cutaneous vasodilation,\xa0causing the\xa0heat to dissipate and bring the body to normal temperature.\\n\\nHeatstroke\xa0occurs due to the failure of the heat regulation center\xa0of the hypothalamus. The person\xa0fails to lose heat by cutaneous circulation and sweating.\xa0It is likely to develop when the body temperature rises to a range of\xa0105\xb0F to 108\xb0F. The predisposing factors include high temperature, increased humidity, minor infections, old age, and physical activity.\\n\\nIt is a medical emergency that leads to homeostatic imbalance.\xa0The symptoms include dizziness, abdominal distress, vomiting, delirium, and eventually leading to loss of consciousness. On examination, the skin is dry, hot, and flushed, with a complete absence of sweating\xa0(anhidrosis). Other signs seen in heat stroke are tachycardia,\xa0Kussmaul-type\xa0rapid deep breathing\xa0(tachypnea), hypotension,\xa0cardiac failure,\xa0dizziness, muscle cramping, confusion, and seizures.\\n\\nHeatstroke can cause damage to multiple organ systems, including the brain, kidneys, and liver, which can lead to life-threatening complications. It is essential to initiate rapid cooling and supportive care measures to prevent permanent injury or death. The cooling methods used to treat heatstroke include:\\n\\n• Cold water immersion: The patient is immersed in a tub of cold water, which is the most effective way to lower body temperature.\\n• Evaporative cooling: The patient\'s body is sprayed with water while fans blow air over the body to help facilitate evaporation and cooling.\\n• Applying cold packs: Applying ice packs to the neck, armpits, and groin can help cool the body by targeting areas where large blood vessels are located close to the skin surface.\\n• Misting and fanning: Spraying the patient with cool water and using a fan to circulate air can help promote evaporative cooling.\\n\\nPreventive measures for heatstroke include staying hydrated, taking regular breaks in shaded areas, and avoiding strenuous activities during the hottest part of the day.\\n\\nOther Options\\n\\n• Option A: Tachypnea - Rapid, deep breathing (Kussmaul breathing) can be seen in heatstroke as the body tries to eliminate excess heat through increased respiratory rate.\\n• Option B: Hypotension - Dehydration and vasodilation in an attempt to dissipate heat can result in hypotension in heatstroke patients.\\n• Option D: Red hot skin - Heatstroke patients often present with hot, flushed skin as a result of increased blood flow to the skin surface and the body\'s inability to dissipate heat effectively.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-d1ba74db3a","n":426,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"A patient came to the hospital after a road traffic accident. He had severe muscle injury and his serum K+ level was found to be 5.5 mEq/L. What will happen to the resting membrane potential in this patient?","options":["No change","Becomes more negative","Becomes more positive","First becomes more positive then negative"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Becomes more positive\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario, the patient has hyperkalemia. It would result in the resting membrane potential becoming more positive.\\n\\nThe resting membrane potential represents an equilibrium situation where the concentration gradient\xa0of ions across the membrane is equal and opposite to the electrical gradients.\xa0At a normal potassium concentration of 3.5–5.0 mEq/L, the equilibrium potential for potassium is −90 mV. This is close to the resting membrane potential of neurons, which is about –70 mV.\\n\\nA\xa0decrease in the extracellular sodium concentration reduces the size of the action potential but has little effect on the resting membrane potential. However, the intracellular and extracellular potassium (K+) concentrations are the major determinants of the RMP.\\n\\nThis is because\\n\\n• At rest, there are more open K+ channels compared to Na+ channels; therefore, the membrane permeability of K+ is greater.\\n• The RMP is close to the equilibrium potential of K+.\\n\\nIn hyperkalemia, the extracellular level of K+ is increased. Thus, the RMP moves closer to the threshold to elicit an action potential, leading to a more excitable nerve. This means the RMP has become more positive.\\n\\nIn hypokalemia, the extracellular level of K+\xa0is reduced. Thus, the RMP is reduced or becomes more negative and the nerve is hyperpolarized.\\n\\nHyperkalemia can lead to various clinical manifestations depending on the severity of the condition. Mild to moderate hyperkalemia may be asymptomatic or cause nonspecific symptoms such as fatigue, muscle weakness, and paresthesias. Severe hyperkalemia can lead to life-threatening complications such as cardiac arrhythmias and paralysis. The management of hyperkalemia includes treating the underlying cause, administering medications to stabilize the cardiac membrane (e.g., calcium gluconate), promoting the movement of potassium into cells (e.g., insulin and glucose, beta-agonists), and enhancing the removal of potassium from the body (e.g., diuretics, ion-exchange resins, or hemodialysis).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9fb354d0bc","n":427,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"A patient met with a road traffic accident and developed a cervical spine injury. The fracture fragment had pierced the lateral aspect of the dorsal column tract. Which of the following findings is seen in this patient?","options":["Absence of ipsilateral lower limb proprioception","Absence of fine motor movement of fingers","Absence of ipsilateral arm proprioception","Absence of contralateral lower limb proprioception"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Absence of ipsilateral arm proprioception\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario of transection of the spinal cord in the lateral aspect of the dorsal column\xa0would affect the\xa0fasciculus cuneatus. This lesion would result in\xa0absence of ipsilateral proprioception of the arm.\\n\\nThe fibers in the dorsal column are organized in such a way that the sacral segments\xa0are positioned most medially and those from the cervical segments are positioned most laterally.\\n\\nThese are called\\n\\n• Fasciculus gracilis - carrying sensations from\xa0the ipsilateral\xa0lower limb\\n• Fasciculus cuneatus -\xa0carrying sensations from\xa0the\xa0ipsilateral\xa0upper limb.\\n\\nThis patient with an injury to the lateral part of the cervical cord\'s dorsal column tract will have damaged\xa0fasciculus cuneatus.\xa0This would result in loss of\xa0touch, vibration, and proprioception\xa0in the arm of the same side of the lesion.\\n\\nThe extent of neurological deficits resulting from a spinal cord injury depends on the level of the injury and the severity of the damage. Injuries may be classified as complete or incomplete, depending on whether there is a total or partial loss of sensory and motor function below the level of injury. The key to solving this question lies in recognizing that the question mentions that the damage is to the lateral aspect of the dorsal column tract.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Absence of ipsilateral lower limb\xa0proprioception - This would be seen if the fasciculus gracilis was affected, which is responsible for carrying sensations from the ipsilateral lower limb. However, in this case, the injury is to the lateral part of the dorsal column, affecting the fasciculus cuneatus.\\n\\nOption B: Absence of fine motor movement of fingers - This would be a result of damage to the corticospinal tract, which carries motor information. The question is specifically focused on the dorsal column tract, which is responsible for carrying sensory information.\\n\\nOption D: Absence of contralateral lower limb\xa0proprioception - Proprioception is carried by the dorsal column pathway, and it is affected ipsilaterally. Damage to the lateral part of the dorsal column would not result in contralateral loss of proprioception.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-00311e5f33","n":428,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"Identify the correctly matched pair of substances with their renal clearance from the graph given below.\\n\\nPAH - Para-aminohippurate","options":["A - Glucose, B - PAH, C - Bicarbonate and D - Inulin","A - Glucose, B - Bicarbonate, C - Inulin and D - PAH","A - PAH, B - Inulin, C - Glucose and D - Bicarbonate","A - Inulin, B - Glucose, C - Bicarbonate and D - PAH"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. A - Glucose, B - Bicarbonate, C - Inulin and D - PAH\\n\\nThe correctly matched pair of substances and their renal clearance is A - Glucose, B - Bicarbonate, C - Inulin and D - PAH.\\n\\nThe concentration of the substance changes as it passes through the tubules. The tubular cells may\xa0remove the substance from the filtrate (tubular reabsorption) or add more of the substance to the filtrate (tubular secretion) or both. The amount of the substance finally\xa0excreted\xa0equals the amount filtered plus the net amount transferred out\xa0by the tubules.\\n\\nPAH is filtered by the glomeruli and secreted by the tubular cells. Its concentration\xa0increases on moving through the length\xa0of the nephron due to secretion into tubules. It is represented by curve D.\\n\\nInulin is freely filtered\xa0by glomeruli and does not undergo any reabsorption or secretion. Hence, its concentration does not change throughout the length of the nephron and its excretion is constant and equal to the glomerular filtration rate. It is represented by curve C.\\n\\nThe majority of bicarbonate\xa0reabsorption occurs in the proximal tubule and a small amount of it flows into the distal tubules and collecting ducts.\xa0Its reabsorption occurs in\xa0all parts of the tubules, except the descending and ascending thin limbs of the loop of Henle. It is represented by curve B.\\n\\nSubstances like amino acids or glucose\xa0are\xa0reabsorbed\xa0along with sodium in the early portion of the proximal tubule.\xa0They have a graph that is sloping down as shown in curve A.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/46c96a31c832c89bc024.png"]},{"bankId":"qb-5598c376ce","n":429,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"Identify the marked region in the given spirometry.","options":["Functional residual capacity","Tidal volume","Expiratory reserve volume","Expiratory capacity"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Functional residual capacity\\n\\nThe marked area in the\xa0spirometry shows the total amount of air present in the lung after normal expiration. This is called functional residual capacity.\\n\\nThe various volumes and capacities in a spirometry are shown below.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/d9d3b280e993741bbec5.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-807eeac4bc","n":430,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"Identify the true statement regarding the given nerve action potential curve.","options":["Threshold point is at A","At point E, the nerve is more excitable","Point C to D is due to opening of Na+ and closure of K+ channels","Point B to D is refractory period"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Point B to D is refractory period\\n\\nThe correct statement regarding the given nerve action potential curve is point B to D is the refractory period.\\n\\nThe sequence of events occurring during an action potential is shown below\\n\\n• Point A represents the resting state where\xa0both channels\xa0are\xa0closed.\\n• Na+\xa0channels open\xa0in response to depolarizing stimulus and sodium enters the cell. The initiation of action potential occurs only after a certain potential is reached, which is called the\xa0threshold potential represented by\xa0point B. (Option A)\\n• After this, the sodium influx causes the\xa0opening\xa0of more\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0through a\xa0positive feedback\xa0mechanism.\xa0Rapid depolarization\xa0occurs to reach point C.\\n• At point C,\xa0Na+\xa0channels\xa0close\xa0and\xa0K+\xa0channels\xa0open, causing\xa0repolarization due to\xa0K+\xa0efflux. (Option C)\\n• Point D represents continued repolarization due to potassium efflux.\\n• At point E,\xa0Na+\xa0channels are\xa0reset\xa0and are ready to respond to another depolarizing stimulus. During this phase, the\xa0K+\xa0channels remain open and\xa0slowly return\xa0to the\xa0closed\xa0state, causing\xa0after-hyperpolarization. As a stronger stimulus is required to reach the threshold potential, the excitability of nerve fiber is reduced at point E. (Option B)\\n• As\xa0both channels close, the membrane returns to resting membrane potential back to point A.\\n\\nPoint B-D is a part of the refractory period. During this period, the application of even a stronger stimulus will not elicit an action potential.\\n\\nThe refractory period is divided into an absolute refractory period and a relative refractory period. During the absolute refractory period, the nerve will not be excited no matter how strong a\xa0stimulus is given. However, during the relative refractory period, stronger than normal stimuli can cause excitation.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/da1f8b3da9bcf9a0ee26.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-d30b7ac9ce","n":431,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"Identify the true statement regarding the point marked on the myocardial action potential curve.","options":["Due to opening of Na+ and closure of fast K+ channels","Due to slow but prolonged opening of Ca2+ channels","Due to closure of Na+ and opening of slow K+ channel","Due to Na+/K+ ATPase"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Due to slow but prolonged opening of Ca2+ channels\\n\\nThe myocardial action potential curve marked above represents the\xa0plateau phase, which\xa0is due to the slower and prolonged opening of calcium channels.\\n\\nTwo types of voltage-gated Ca2+ channels (T- and L-types) are present in myocardial cells. The opening of the slower L-type Ca2+ channels is mostly responsible for changes in the action potential. The action potential curve of cardiac muscles consists of 5 phases, as shown below.\\n\\nPhase 0: Depolarization -\xa0Opening of voltage-gated sodium channels (fast sodium channels) leads to sodium influx.\\n\\nPhase 1: Initial repolarization -\xa0Closure of Na+ channels and opening up of K+ channels leads to potassium efflux.\\n\\nPhase 2: Plateau -\xa0Slower but prolonged opening of voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, leading to calcium influx and reduced permeability of K+ channels, causes the graph to plateau\\n\\nPhase 3: Rapid Repolarization -\xa0The closure of Ca2+\xa0channels and opening of K+ at the end of the plateau phase causes repolarization.\\n\\nPhase 4: Resting membrane potential -\xa0Is reached at the end of phase 3","img":false,"images":["/bimg/a547255807527cbab0e4.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-b23705ba30","n":432,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2023","text":"Identify the type of transport across the cell membrane given below.","options":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Primary active transport","Secondary active transport"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Facilitated diffusion\\n\\nThe given image shows the transport of a molecule across a membrane with the help of a carrier protein. This form of transport is called facilitated diffusion.\\n\\nWhen carrier proteins help in the movement of substances in the direction of their chemical or electrical gradients (downhill movement), no energy input is required and the process is called facilitated diffusion.\\n\\nGlucose transporters or\xa0GLUTs are examples of transport proteins in the cell membrane responsible for the transfer of glucose into the cells by facilitated diffusion. Facilitated diffusion involves the passage of molecules or ions inside the cell membrane by binding to a carrier/ transport protein.\\n\\nOther options -\\n\\nOption A. Simple diffusion -\xa0It is along the concentration gradient but is not carrier-mediated.\xa0In the cell, examples of molecules that can use simple diffusion to travel in and out of the cell membrane are water, oxygen, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen. They pass directly through the cell membrane along the concentration gradient, without the use of energy.\\n\\nOption C.\xa0Primary active transport - It is a form of cellular transport requiring energy in the form of\xa0hydrolysis of ATP\xa0to ship and transfer molecules against gradient differences.\xa0The important examples of primary active transport include sodium-potassium ATPase, proton pump, and calcium ATPase.\\n\\nOption D. Secondary active transport -\xa0In this form of transport,\xa0two or more substances are transported together across the membrane with help of a carrier molecule. As one of the substances (e.g., sodium) diffuses down its electrochemical gradient, the energy released is used to drive another substance (e.g., glucose) against the gradient. It does not require energy directly from ATP.","img":false,"images":["/bimg/965248833e6a01e6b106.jpg"]},{"bankId":"qb-fafaeb9aa6","n":433,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A 30-year-old female went on vacation and fell asleep while sitting on a beach. After some time she woke with sunburn. Later when she went back home, she experienced pain while taking a warm bath (40\xb0C). Which of the following receptors is responsible for the pain?","options":["Innocuos thermal receptor - allodynia","Innocuos thermal receptor - hyperalgesia","Thermal receptor - allodynia","Thermal receptor - hyperalgesia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Innocuos thermal receptor - allodynia\\n\\nThe receptors responsible for this are innocuous thermal receptors which have perceived ordinarily non-noxious stimulus (i.e., warm bath) as severe pain also known as allodynia.\\n\\nIn this case, the warm bath at 40\xb0C (typically not painful) causes pain due to sunburn, which sensitizes the skin. After sunburn, the skin becomes more sensitive, and thermal receptors that usually detect warmth now trigger pain signals in response to non-painful thermal stimuli, leading to allodynia.\\n\\nInnocuous thermoreceptors, which detect non-harmful temperature changes(cold and warm), are specialized nerve endings found on the skin. Cold receptors are located on the dendritic endings of Aδ and C fibers, while warm receptors are situated on C fibers. The specific thermosensitive molecular channels mediating innocuous heat stimuli is transient receptor potential (TRP) cation channel TRPV1 and TRPM8 cation channel for innocuous cold stimuli.\\n\\nThermal hyperalgesia(increased response to noxious stimulation)\xa0is mediated by activation of NMDA receptors.\xa0 After tissue or nerve injury, C-fibers discharges release glutamate and neurokinins from primary afferent terminals, leading to the release of peptides (e.g., substance P; calcitonin gene-related peptide, CGRP). As a result, a voltage-dependent magnesium block on NMDA receptors is removed allowing for an influx of calcium that triggers a cascade of intracellular events leading to the development and maintenance of hyperalgesia.(Option D)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fdbb23d44a","n":434,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A 50-year-old patient presents with a chronic tingling sensation in the forearm. A neurological examination is done and appears to be normal. Which of the following receptors are correctly matched?","options":["Merkel cells-slow vibration","Meissner\'s corpuscles-stretch","Pacinian corpuscles-fast vibration","Ruffini corpuscles-sustained pressure"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pacinian corpuscles-fast vibration\\n\\nPacinian corpuscles are responsible for sensing fast vibration. The chronic tingling sensation is mediated by pressure-sensitive Aβ fibers and low-conduction velocity C fibers.\\n\\nPacinian corpuscles are the most sensitive mechanoreceptors in the somatosensory system. They are responsive to high-frequency (30–500 Hz) vibratory stimuli and deep pressure. They are also examples of rapidly adapting receptors.\\n\\nChronic tingling causing conditions include Diabetes, carpal tunnel syndrome, ulnar nerve palsy, radial nerve palsy, tarsal tunnel syndrome, sciatica, vitamin B1,B6,B12 deficiencies, and alcoholism.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: The Merkel cells are expanded dendritic endings in the epidermis of glabrous skin and respond to sustained pressure and touch.\\n\\nOption B: Meissner\'s corpuscle senses tap and flutter. They are present in dermal papillae. They are rapidly adapting receptors.\\n\\nOption D: Ruffini\'s corpuscles are enlarged dendritic endings with elongated capsules in the dermis of glabrous and hairy skin. They respond to stretch and fluttering vibrations. They are slowly adapting receptors.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-28f4bb44f3","n":435,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A 60-year-old patient came with a puncture wound on the left chest wall. Radiological investigations confirmed pneumothorax. Which of the following is correct regarding this condition?","options":["Lung volume increases, thoracic volume increases","Lung volume decreases, thoracic volume increases","Lung volume increases, thoracic volume decreases","lung volume decreases, thoracic volume decreases"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lung volume decreases, thoracic volume increases\\n\\nIn pneumothorax, lung volume is decreased and thoracic volume is increased.\\n\\nBoth lungs and the chest wall (thoracic cage) are elastic structures. Under normal conditions, the elastic tendency of the lungs to collapse is exactly balanced by the elastic tendency of the chest wall to expand.\\n\\nIn pneumothorax, air is introduced into the pleural space, so the pleural pressure, which is normally subatmospheric, becomes equal to atmospheric pressure.\xa0The chest wall springs outward and the lungs collapse. This increases the thoracic volume and decreases the lung volume.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-624c850569","n":436,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"A patient who underwent right limb amputation 2 years back, now presents with severe pain in the limb (Phantom limb pain). On radiological investigations, What is the finding seen?","options":["Projection of adjacent fibres to overlap the right somatosensory cortex","Expansion of left somatosensory cortex","Projection of adjacent fibres to overlap the left somatosensory cortex","Expansion of right somatosensory cortex"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Projection of adjacent fibres to overlap the left somatosensory cortex\\n\\nIn the given scenario of phantom limb pain, on the radiological investigation of the brain projection of adjacent fibres to overlap the left somatosensory cortex can be seen. This pain is based on cortical plasticity.\\n\\nCortical structures are capable of reorganization after the loss of a body part. The cortical representation of the neighbouring parts spreads into the cortical area that was formerly occupied by the representation of the amputated part. This is called cortical plasticity leads to the phenomenon of phantom limb pain. The sensory cortical area occupied by the right limb is the left somatosensory cortex.\\n\\nCortical plasticity / Phantom limb: It is the sensation that an amputated or missing limb is still attached. Plasticity is the ability of the brain to adapt to such a condition by reorganization of thalamic and cortical structures.\\n\\nThe converse also holds true: if the cortical area representing a limb is removed, the somatosensory map of the limb moves to the surrounding cortex.\\n\\nDiagnosis and Management :\\n\\n• PET scan is used to document these plastic changes in cortex.\\n• Use of epidural analgesia during amputation surgery decreases incidence of phantom pain.\\n• Spinal cord stimulation also helps in reducing phantom pain. (interferes with ascending impulses to brain)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-b55161af98","n":437,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"Arrange the sequence of steps involved at neuromuscular junction endplate potential.","options":["Acetylcholine release - Calcium entry- Na+ entry","Calcium entry- Acetylcholine release- Na+ entry","Na+ entry - Calcium entry - Acetylcholine release","Na+ entry - Acetylcholine release - Calcium entry"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Calcium entry- Acetylcholine release- Na+ entry\\n\\nThe sequence of steps involved at neuromuscular junction endplate potential is Calcium entry- Acetylcholine release- Na+ entry.\\n\\nEvents occurring at the Chemical Synapse:\\n\\nThe action potential arriving at the end of the motor neuron increases the permeability of its endings to Ca2+\\n\\n↓\\n\\nCa2+ enters the endings and triggers a marked increase in exocytosis of the acetylcholine-containing synaptic vesicles\\n\\n↓\\n\\nThe binding of acetylcholine to nicotinic receptors increases the Na+ and K+ conductance\\n\\n↓\\n\\nThe resultant influx of Na+ produces a local depolarizing potential, the endplate potential\\n\\n↓\\n\\nThe current created by this local potential depolarizes the adjacent muscle membrane to its firing level\\n\\n↓\\n\\nAction potentials are generated on either side of the endplate and are conducted away from the endplate in both directions along the muscle fiber. This muscle action potential, in turn, initiates muscle contraction.\\n\\nAcetylcholine is then removed from the synaptic cleft by acetylcholinesterase, which is present in high concentration at the neuromuscular junction.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3152a4caec","n":438,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"In a type 2 diabetes mellitus patient, serum insulin levels are found to be raised. He is advised to exercise to improve insulin resistance. Which of the following GLUT will help to improve insulin resistance after exercise ?","options":["GLUT 1","GLUT 2","GLUT 5","GLUT 4"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. GLUT 4\\n\\nThere is an increase in the number of GLUT 4 receptors in response to exercise. This increases the insulin dependent glucose uptake into the cells, contributing to a decrease in insulin resistance.\\n\\nGLUT-4 is an insulin-dependent GLUT. Other GLUTs are not insulin-sensitive and are constitutively expressed in the cell membrane.\\n\\nInsulin resistance is a condition in which the cells in muscles, fat, and liver are less responsive to insulin and have difficulty absorbing glucose from the blood. The pancreas then produces more insulin to facilitate the entry of glucose into the cells.\\n\\nAn arbitrary but clinically useful benchmark considers patients insulin resistant if they require greater than 1 unit/kilogram/day of exogenous insulin to maintain glycemic control. Patients requiring greater than 200 units of\xa0exogenous insulin per day are considered severely insulin resistant.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: \xa0GLUT 1 receptors are present in the red blood cell, brain, kidney, colon, retina, and placenta. It is responsible for glucose uptake.\\n\\nOption B: GLUT-2 is found in the liver, pancreatic β cell, small intestine, and kidney. Its function is the rapid uptake or release of glucose.\\n\\nOption C: GLUT-5 is present in the small intestine and is involved in the absorption of fructose.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-5e331ba1e6","n":439,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"Renal compensation in metabolic acidosis takes time because:","options":["Initially the pH of tubular fluid increases","Low pH normally decreases bicarbonate excretion","It takes hours to days for kidney to synthesize enzymes that produce NH4+","Other ions in the tubule interfere with buffer action"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. It takes hours to days for kidney to synthesize enzymes that produce NH4+\\n\\nCompensation in metabolic acidosis happens late in the kidney because it takes hours to days for it to synthesize enzymes that produce NH4+(ammonium ions).\\n\\nAcid-base balance equation: CO2 + H2O -----> H2CO3 -----> H+(acid) and HCO3-(base)\\n\\n• First line of defence (in seconds): Buffers ( phosphate and ammonium )\\n• Second line of defence (in Minutes): Lungs\\n• Third line of defence (in hours - days): Kidney\\n\\nThe kidneys regulate the acid-base balance by two major mechanisms: the reabsorption of bicarbonate and the generation of new bicarbonate via ammonia production and distal nephron acid secretion. Urinary NH4+ is produced primarily in the proximal tubule cells by converting glutamine to glutamate.\\n\\nRenal failure leads to a buildup of acids due to loss of excretory function of the kidney. The decreased glomerular filtration rate also leads to diminished excretion of the renal buffers phosphate and ammonium. Thus, bicarbonate reabsorption decreases, resulting in metabolic acidosis.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-6c943aa251","n":440,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"Sour taste is mediated by which of the following receptors?","options":["T1R1 GPCR","TRPV1 channel","T1R3 GPCR","Metabolic receptor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. TRPV1 channel\\n\\nSour taste is mediated by the TRPV1 channel. The TRPV1 (Transient Receptor Potential Vanilloid 1) channel is involved in detecting the acidity of foods, which contributes to the perception of sour taste.\\n\\nIt is a cation channel that allows the influx of various cations, including sodium (Na⁺), calcium (Ca\xb2⁺), and magnesium (Mg\xb2⁺), in response to changes in temperature and the presence of acidic conditions or capsaicin\\n\\nTaste Receptors\\n\\n• Humans have five established basic tastes: sweet, salt, sour, bitter and\xa0umami.\\n• The taste receptors for these five basic tastes are of two major types:\\n\\n• Ligand-gated channels (ionotropic receptors): for salt and sour taste\\n• G-Protein Coupled Receptors (metabotropic receptors): for sweet, bitter and umami\\n\\n• Salt and sour taste are mediated through epithelial sodium channels (ENaC)\\n\\n• Na+\xa0movement through ENaC →\xa0salt taste\\n• H+\xa0movement through ENaC →\xa0sour taste\\n\\n• Sweet, bitter, and umami are sensed via\xa0GPCRs\\n\\n• T1R2 and T1R3\xa0families→\xa0sweet\xa0taste\\n• T2R family\xa0→\xa0bitter taste\\n• T1R1, T1R3, and Metabotropic glutamate receptor (mGluR4) → Umami (Option A and C)","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-fbce2174d4","n":441,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"The functioning of the thyroid involves the transport of iodine from the blood to the thyroid follicle. Iodine is transported from the follicle to the colloid. Which pump or channel is for transport of iodine from the follicle to the colloid?","options":["Sodium iodine symporter","Calmodulin","Claudin","Pendrin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Pendrin\\n\\nThe channel needed for the transport of iodine from the follicle to the colloid is Pendrin.\\n\\nSteps in synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormone:\\n\\n• Iodide uptake:\xa0The basolateral sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) actively takes up\xa0I-(iodide) from the circulation into the follicular cells.\xa0It is then transported into the colloid through a pendrin (chloride-iodide) transporter\\n• Thyroglobulin synthesis: Thyroglobulin (Tg), is a large glycoprotein rich in tyrosine residues. It is synthesized by follicular cells.\\n• Iodination of Tg: This is regulated by the\xa0thyroid peroxidase (TPO) enzyme. TPO has three functions:\\n• Oxidation: TPO oxidizes I- to I2\xa0(iodine)\xa0\xa0in the presence of H2O2\\n• Organification: TPO links tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin with iodine (I2) to form monoiodotyrosine (MIT), diiodotyrosine (DIT)\\n• Coupling reactions: TPO combines iodinated tyrosine residues to make\xa0MIT + DIT = T3 (triiodothyronine),\xa0DIT + DIT = T4 (tetraiodothyronine)\\n• Storage: Thyroid hormones are bound to thyroglobulin and stored in the follicular lumen as colloids. The colloid has hormone reserves for 2-3 months\\n• Release:\xa0Iodinated thyroglobulin is taken up by the thyroid follicles via endocytosis and fuses with lysosomes.\xa0It is cleaved by lysosomal proteolytic enzymes into MIT, DIT, T3, and T4.\xa0T4 (80%) and T3 (20%) are released into the blood\\n• Peripheral T3 conversion: T4 undergoes deiodination in extrathyroidal tissue to form T3, which is the active form of the hormone.\\n\\nPendred syndrome: Pendrin is present in the thyroid gland and inner ear. The mutation causes goiter and sensorineural hearing loss.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: Sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) is responsible for transporting iodine from the bloodstream into the thyroid follicular cell, not into the colloid. It works by bringing in iodine along with sodium from the bloodstream into the follicle.\\n\\nOption B: Calmodulin is structurally and functionally related to troponin. In place of troponin, smooth muscle cells contain a large amount of the regulatory protein calmodulin; it is different in the manner in which it initiates contraction. Calmodulin initiates contraction by activating the myosin light chain kinase.\\n\\nOption C: Claudin is a transmembrane protein involved in the formation of tight junctions.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-a7f53c0879","n":442,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"The patient presented with alcoholic gait, and abnormal eye movements (nystagmus) after RTA. Which lobe of the cerebellum is likely affected?\\n\\nRTA - Road Traffic Accident","options":["Flocculonodular Lobe","Dentate nucleus","Anterior lobe","Interposed nucleus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Flocculonodular Lobe\\n\\nThe flocculonodular lobe is involved in this patient with an alcoholic gait and abnormal eye movements (nystagmus) following an RTA.\\n\\nThe cerebellum has three parts based on function.\\n\\n• Vestibulocerebellum (or flocculonodular lobe): The oldest part of the cerebellum which contains nodulus in the vermis and the flanking flocculus. It has vestibular connections and is concerned with equilibrium and gait.\xa0 Loss of it can cause disturbance of equilibrium and postural movements, especially during rapid movements.\\n\\n• Spinocerebellum:\xa0The region that receives proprioceptive input from the body as well as a copy of the “motor plan” from the motor cortex. By comparing the plan with performance, it smooths and coordinates movements that are ongoing. The superior vermis is a part of the spinocerebellum. Loss of it can cause problems with coordination.\\n• Cerebrocerebellum:\xa0They are the newest from a phylogenetic point of view, reaching their greatest development in humans. They interact with the motor cortex in planning and programming movements.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: The Dentate nucleus(largest) is one of the 4 deep cerebellar nuclei others being, Emoliform, Globose, and Fastigial nuclei. These receive input from climbing fibers and are the only motor output from the cerebellar cortex.\\n\\nOption C: Anterior lobe is a part of the Paleo/Spinocerebellum responsible for the coordination of movement and regulation of muscle tone whose lesion results in hypotonia.\\n\\nOption D: Interposed nuclei / Nucleus interpositus is the term used to describe the cerebellar nuclei, Emboliform, and Globose.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-3116c78dbc","n":443,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"The resting membrane potential(RMP) of the nerve is -70mV. Increased permeability of which ion is responsible for the RMP?","options":["Calcium","Sodium","Potassium","Chloride"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Potassium\\n\\nIncreased permeability of potassium ions(K+) is responsible for the RMP of a nerve as there are more K+ channels open at rest, facilitating the flow of potassium ions across the membrane.\\n\\nResting membrane potential (RMP) is the difference in the electrical potentials between the cytoplasm of the cell and the extracellular space.\\n\\nAt rest, there are more open K+ channels compared to Na+ channels, thus the membrane permeability of K+ is greater. As a result of this, the intracellular and extracellular K+ concentrations are the major determinants of the RMP. Furthermore, the RMP is close to the equilibrium potential of K+ in general.\\n\\nClinical Significance: In hyperkalemia, the extracellular level of K+ is increased. Thus the RMP moves closer to the threshold to elicit an action potential leading to a more excitable nerve. The excitable nerve, stimulates the muscle repeatedly causing fasciculations, exhaustion of muscle, and weakness. In hypokalemia, the extracellular level of K+ is reduced. Thus the RMP is reduced and the neuron is hyperpolarized.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-26a7e924ac","n":444,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"To prevent polyspermy, which reaction occurs in the zona pellucida of the oocyte after fertilization?","options":["Acrosomal reaction","Increased membrane potential of the oocyte","Cortical reaction","Decreased membrane potential of the oocyte"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cortical reaction\\n\\nTo prevent polyspermy, the reaction in the zona pellucida of the oocyte after fertilisation is the cortical reaction.\\n\\nThe chronological steps of fertilisation:\\n\\nAcrosomal reaction: After a sperm reaches the oocyte and binds to the zona pellucida, the acrosome reaction occurs. The acrosome, an enzyme-rich organelle in the anterior of the sperm head, fuses at many points with the plasma membrane of the oocyte. Then those fused membranes are sloughed off from the head, exposing the acrosomal enzymes. The enzymes digest a path through the zona pellucida, enabling the sperm to advance.\\n\\nEventually, the sperm meets and fuses with the secondary oocyte plasma membrane.\\n\\nThis contact results in weak membrane depolarisation(decreased membrane potential) and a calcium wave.\\n\\nCortical reaction: The calcium wave triggers the release of lysosomal enzymes from the cortical granules lining the oolemma. These enzymes hydrolyse the ZP3 receptor on the zona pellucida. Digestion of ZP3 and alteration of zona pellucida (zona reaction) prevents other sperms from binding, thus creating a block to polyspermy.","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-9a73dfba3b","n":445,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"What are the effects of posterior lesions in a Brodmann\'s area 22 in a stroke patient?","options":["Auditory function","Receptive Speech Function","Expressive Speech Function","Visual functions"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Receptive Speech Function\\n\\nA posterior lesion in Brodmann\'s area 22, which is located in the superior temporal gyrus, typically affects receptive speech function. It is the sensory speech area.\\n\\nThe patient will most likely have Wernicke’s aphasia due to the damage to Wernicke’s area - area 22.\\n\\nIn\xa0Wernicke\'s aphasia,\xa0there\xa0is difficulty in finding words leading to poorly understood content for the listener. These patients also find it difficult to repeat sentences and name things properly. It is a type of\xa0fluent aphasia\xa0in\xa0which the\xa0speech is easy and fluent but there are difficulties related to the output of language like paraphasia (substitution of similar-sounding, non-words, or incorrect words).\xa0The speech is full of jargon and neologisms (non-existent words) that make little sense.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Auditory Function is primarily mediated by Brodmann area 41, which is located in the primary auditory cortex within the superior temporal gyrus.\\n\\nOption C: Expressive Speech Function is associated with Brodmann areas 44 and 45 (Broca\'s area), which are involved in language production and speech articulation. Lesions here can cause difficulty in producing coherent speech (Broca\'s aphasia).\\nOption D: Visual function primarily associated with Brodmann areas 17, 18, and 19 (the primary and secondary visual cortices).","img":false},{"bankId":"qb-0904af6f7f","n":446,"subject":"Physiology","topic":"NEET 2024","text":"Which of the following is incorrect about iron absorption?","options":["Ferrireductase convert Fe2+ to Fe 3+","RBC protophyrin is increased in deficiency","Iron excretion is only through skin, gut and urothelial cells","Body cannot compensate if more than 10 RBC/HPF per day is lost in urine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Ferrireductase convert Fe2+ to Fe 3+\\n\\nFerrireductase is an enzyme that converts Fe3+ (ferric iron) to Fe2+ (ferrous iron),\xa0 not the other way around. Ferrous is the form of iron that is more readily absorbed in the gut.\\n\\nAlmost all the iron absorption occurs in the duodenum.\\n\\nMost of the iron in the diet is in the ferric (Fe3+) form, whereas it is the ferrous (Fe2+)\xa0form that is absorbed.\\n\\nBody iron stores are controlled by intestinal absorption as there is no exit to iron from the body. Divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1) transports non-heme iron into the enterocyte. Iron absorption in the duodenum is regulated by hepcidin release from the liver in response to increases in intrahepatic iron levels. It binds to ferroportin leading to endocytosis and degradation within the enterocyte. This prevents the transfer of iron from enterocyte to plasma resulting in iron trapping within duodenal cells in the form of mucosal ferritin. The trapped iron is lost as these cells are sloughed.\xa0The iron transported by ferroportin into the plasma is transferred to other parts with the help of transferrin and stored as serum ferritin and hemosiderin. The image depicting iron absorption and storage is given below:\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: RBC protoporphyrin is increased in deficiency, increased protoporphyrin levels in red blood cells can indicate iron deficiency or disorders of heme synthesis.\\n\\nOption C: Iron is primarily excreted through the gut, skin, and urinary tract, but the body generally has very limited mechanisms for iron excretion compared to iron absorption and recycling.\\n\\nOption D: The body’s iron homeostasis is finely tuned, and a significant loss of red blood cells, such as more than 10 ml per day, can overwhelm the body’s ability to compensate and maintain iron balance.","img":false}]}')}}]);